window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"UR-1":{"unit":"UR-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-01-01","title":"Development of the Kidneys and Urinary Tract","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the urinary system from intermediate mesoderm to a working kidney, ureter, bladder, and urethra. It covers the three successive kidneys, the reciprocal induction that builds nephrons and collecting ducts, renal ascent, ureteric bud splitting, partition of the cloaca, and the fate of the allantois. Board questions usually name one finding, such as oligohydramnios, urine at the umbilicus, a low fused kidney, or constant dribbling in a girl, and ask which developmental step failed. Each anomaly in this chapter follows directly from a normal step, so learning the sequence lets you predict the defect rather than memorize it.","prerequisites":["Trilaminar disc and the subdivisions of mesoderm (paraxial, intermediate, lateral plate)","Embryonic folding and formation of the hindgut, cloaca, and allantois","Basic nephron structure from Bowman capsule to collecting duct","Origin of the abdominal aorta branches and the position of the inferior mesenteric artery","Sources and normal volume of amniotic fluid"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive embryology (RE-1)","how":"The mesonephric duct that gives rise to the ureteric bud becomes the male genital ducts, and the urogenital sinus forms both the bladder and parts of the reproductive tract."},{"to":"Respiratory development","how":"Lung growth depends on adequate amniotic fluid, so failure of fetal urine output causes pulmonary hypoplasia."},{"to":"Renal anatomy (UR-2, UR-3)","how":"The adult arrangement of calyces, collecting ducts, renal arteries, and the trigone reflects the embryonic origin of each part."},{"to":"Urinary tract infection and reflux nephropathy","how":"Short intravesical ureters and obstructed drainage from developmental errors set up stasis, reflux, ascending infection, and scarring."},{"to":"Genetics","how":"Mutations in genes that control induction, such as RET and WT1, and syndromes such as Turner syndrome, link single genes and karyotype to renal anomalies."}],"terms":[{"term":"Intermediate mesoderm","def":"The strip of mesoderm between paraxial and lateral plate mesoderm that forms the nephrogenic cord and gonads."},{"term":"Nephrogenic cord","def":"The longitudinal ridge of intermediate mesoderm that gives rise to the pronephros, mesonephros, and metanephros."},{"term":"Mesonephric (Wolffian) duct","def":"The excretory duct of the mesonephros, which sprouts the ureteric bud and in males becomes the genital ducts."},{"term":"Ureteric bud","def":"An outgrowth of the caudal mesonephric duct that forms the ureter, renal pelvis, calyces, and collecting ducts."},{"term":"Metanephric mesenchyme","def":"The caudal intermediate mesoderm (metanephric blastema) that forms the nephron from Bowman capsule to the distal convoluted tubule."},{"term":"Reciprocal induction","def":"A two-way exchange of signals in which each of two tissues directs the development of the other."},{"term":"Cloaca","def":"The endoderm-lined chamber at the end of the hindgut that receives both the allantois and the mesonephric ducts before it is partitioned."},{"term":"Urorectal septum","def":"A wedge of mesoderm that divides the cloaca into the urogenital sinus in front and the anorectal canal behind."},{"term":"Urachus","def":"The fibrous cord left when the allantois closes, running from the bladder apex to the umbilicus as the median umbilical ligament."},{"term":"Oligohydramnios","def":"Abnormally low amniotic fluid volume, which in the second half of pregnancy usually signals low fetal urine output or ruptured membranes."},{"term":"Potter sequence","def":"The chain of fetal compression and pulmonary hypoplasia that follows severe oligohydramnios of any cause."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-01-01-C01","obj":"UR-1.1","title":"Three kidneys in sequence: pronephros, mesonephros, metanephros","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The urinary system arises from intermediate mesoderm, the strip of mesoderm between the somites and the lateral plate. As the embryo folds, this tissue separates from the somites and forms a longitudinal ridge on each side of the posterior body wall, the nephrogenic cord. Three kidney systems form from this cord in a head to tail order, and the timing overlaps, so the cranial system regresses while the next one develops below it."},{"type":"p","text":"The pronephros appears early in the fourth week in the future neck as a few solid cell clusters or vestigial tubules. It never filters blood in humans and is gone by the end of the fourth week. The mesonephros appears in the thoracic and lumbar region late in the fourth week, as the pronephros regresses. Its tubules acquire a capillary tuft at their medial end, forming a true glomerulus inside a Bowman capsule, and their lateral ends drain into the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct, which runs caudally to open into the cloaca. The mesonephros may produce small amounts of urine as an interim kidney, then largely degenerates by the end of the second month."},{"type":"p","text":"The mesonephric duct matters long after its kidney fails, for two reasons. First, in the fifth week it sprouts the ureteric bud, which starts the permanent kidney. Second, in males under testosterone the duct persists, and a few mesonephric tubules near the testis become the efferent ductules, while the duct forms the epididymis, ductus deferens, seminal vesicle, and ejaculatory duct; in females they leave only small remnants such as Gartner duct cysts. This shared origin is why urinary and genital anomalies often occur together."},{"type":"table","title":"The three kidney systems","cols":["Feature","Pronephros","Mesonephros","Metanephros"],"rows":[["Appears","Early week 4","Late week 4","Week 5"],["Region","Cervical","Thoracic and upper lumbar","Sacral (pelvic), then ascends"],["Function","None in humans","Brief, as an interim kidney","Permanent kidney; urine from about weeks 10 to 12"],["Fate","Regresses by end of week 4","Mostly gone by end of month 2; duct persists","Adult kidney"],["Lasting derivative","None","Ureteric bud from the duct; male genital ducts","Nephrons and, with the bud, the collecting system"]],"caption":"Each system arises from intermediate mesoderm caudal to the last; only the metanephros survives as a kidney."},{"type":"p","text":"The metanephros, the permanent kidney, appears in the fifth week in the sacral region from two sources described in the next section: the ureteric bud and the metanephric mesenchyme at the caudal end of the nephrogenic cord. It begins producing urine at about weeks 10 to 12. That urine passes into the amniotic cavity, and from about the middle of pregnancy fetal urine is the main source of amniotic fluid. The fetus does not need its kidneys for excretion, because the placenta clears waste, but it does need their urine to maintain amniotic fluid volume. This distinction explains why a fetus without kidneys can grow to term with normal blood chemistry yet be born with lungs too small to survive."},{"type":"p","text":"New nephrons continue to form until about weeks 34 to 36, when nephrogenesis ends; no new nephrons are made after that, although existing ones enlarge and mature after birth. A term infant has roughly one million nephrons per kidney, with wide individual variation. Preterm birth, growth restriction, or obstruction during this window can therefore leave a permanently reduced nephron number."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-01-01-C02","obj":"UR-1.2","title":"Reciprocal induction: collecting system and nephron","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The permanent kidney forms only when two tissues meet. In the fifth week the ureteric bud grows dorsally from the caudal mesonephric duct, near its entry into the cloaca, and pushes into the metanephric mesenchyme, which condenses around its tip as a cap. Neither tissue can build a kidney alone, and each instructs the other, which is why the process is called reciprocal induction."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Reciprocal induction between bud and mesenchyme","steps":["WT1 makes the mesenchyme competent to respond","Mesenchyme secretes GDNF, which binds RET on the bud","Bud tip grows and branches into the mesenchyme","Bud tips secrete WNT9B and FGF signals","Mesenchyme condenses and survives around each new tip","Condensed mesenchyme keeps secreting GDNF"],"caption":"Each bud branch induces new nephrons, and the induced mesenchyme drives further branching."},{"type":"p","text":"The ureteric bud forms the collecting system, the passages that carry finished urine. Its stalk becomes the ureter and its expanded tip the renal pelvis. The tip divides repeatedly: the first generations of branches form the major calyces, the next few coalesce into minor calyces, and later generations elongate into the collecting ducts and papillary ducts of the medulla."},{"type":"p","text":"The metanephric mesenchyme forms the nephron, the part that makes urine. Around each new collecting duct tip, mesenchymal cells condense and undergo a mesenchyme to epithelium transition, forming a renal vesicle that becomes a comma shaped and then an S shaped tubule. Capillaries invade one end, which folds around them as the double walled Bowman capsule with podocytes on its inner layer. The tubule then lengthens into the proximal convoluted tubule, loop of Henle, and distal convoluted tubule, and its distal end fuses with the adjacent collecting duct. The boundary between the two tissues therefore lies at the junction of the distal convoluted tubule with the collecting duct."},{"type":"table","title":"Embryonic origin of adult kidney parts","cols":["Embryonic source","Adult structures","Function"],"rows":[["Ureteric bud","Ureter, renal pelvis, major and minor calyces, collecting ducts","Carries and modifies finished urine"],["Metanephric mesenchyme","Bowman capsule (including podocytes), proximal tubule, loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubule","Filters plasma and processes filtrate"],["Invading endothelium","Glomerular capillaries","Filtration surface"],["Metanephric stroma","Interstitial cells and mesangium","Support; signals that pattern branching"]],"caption":"Plumbing comes from the bud; the filtering and processing nephron comes from the mesenchyme."},{"type":"p","text":"If the bud fails to form, fails to reach the mesenchyme, or cannot respond to GDNF (for example with loss of function RET variants), no branching occurs, the unstimulated mesenchyme dies by apoptosis, and no kidney forms. This is renal agenesis. Partial or disordered induction instead produces renal dysplasia, a kidney of cysts and primitive tubules with little function. Unilateral agenesis is usually silent: the single kidney enlarges by compensatory hypertrophy, the ipsilateral ureter is absent, and genital duct anomalies may accompany it because the mesonephric duct is shared."},{"type":"p","text":"Bilateral agenesis means no fetal urine, and lung growth fails because it needs surrounding amniotic fluid."},{"type":"flow","title":"Bilateral renal agenesis to Potter sequence","steps":["Ureteric buds absent or fail to induce mesenchyme","No nephrons, so no fetal urine","Oligohydramnios from the second trimester","Uterine compression: flat face, low set ears, limb deformities","Too little fluid for lung growth: pulmonary hypoplasia","Respiratory failure at birth"],"caption":"The fatal problem is pulmonary hypoplasia, not uremia, because the placenta handles excretion before birth."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A fetus has bilateral renal agenesis. What would its serum creatinine be just before birth, and why does it still die soon after delivery?","explain":"Creatinine is near the maternal value because the placenta clears waste; death follows from pulmonary hypoplasia caused by oligohydramnios, with renal failure developing only after the cord is cut.","check":"The paragraph on bilateral agenesis and the Potter sequence flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-01-01-C03","obj":"UR-1.3","title":"Ascent of the kidneys, pelvic kidney, and horseshoe kidney","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The metanephros forms in the pelvis, with its hilum facing forward. Between about weeks 6 and 9 it comes to lie in the upper lumbar region against the suprarenal gland. This ascent is mostly relative: the lumbar and sacral parts of the body grow rapidly and the body curvature lessens, so the caudal body grows away from the kidneys. During ascent each kidney rotates medially by about 90 degrees, so the hilum comes to face anteromedially."},{"type":"p","text":"As the kidneys rise, they are supplied in turn by arteries arising from progressively higher levels: first branches near the common iliac arteries, then successively higher aortic branches. The lower vessels normally regress as new ones arise above. A vessel that fails to regress remains as an accessory renal artery, which is common (about one person in four). Each is an end artery to its segment, so ligation or occlusion causes infarction of that segment, and an accessory vessel to the lower pole that crosses in front of the ureter can compress it and cause hydronephrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"On their way up, the kidneys pass through the narrow fork formed by the umbilical arteries. If one kidney fails to pass, it stays in the pelvis near the common iliac artery as a pelvic kidney, supplied by low vessels, often malrotated, and with a short ureter. If the two are pressed together in the fork, their lower poles fuse across the midline, producing a horseshoe kidney, the most common renal fusion anomaly (roughly 1 in 400 to 1 in 600 people)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Formation of a horseshoe kidney","steps":["Both kidneys pass through the umbilical arterial fork","Lower poles meet and fuse across the midline","Isthmus lies in front of the aorta and IVC","Isthmus catches under the root of the inferior mesenteric artery","Ascent stops; kidneys stay low in the lumbar region","Fused poles block normal medial rotation"],"caption":"The inferior mesenteric artery, arising from the aorta at about L3, is the structure that halts ascent of the fused kidney."},{"type":"p","text":"The fused isthmus, made of functioning renal tissue or fibrous tissue, lies anterior to the aorta and inferior vena cava. When the rising kidney reaches the inferior mesenteric artery, the isthmus hooks beneath it and cannot pass, so the kidneys remain at about the level of the lower lumbar vertebrae. The superior mesenteric artery arises higher and is not involved. Because rotation also fails, the renal pelves face forward and the ureters must pass anterior to the isthmus, which kinks them at the ureteropelvic junction."},{"type":"p","text":"Most horseshoe kidneys cause no symptoms and are found incidentally, and renal function is usually normal. The complications follow from the anatomy: angled, high inserting ureters drain poorly, so ureteropelvic junction obstruction, hydronephrosis, stasis, stones, and infection are more frequent. The low position anterior to the spine also makes the isthmus vulnerable to blunt abdominal trauma. Horseshoe kidney is associated with Turner syndrome (45,X), trisomy 18, and a modestly higher risk of Wilms tumor, so its discovery should prompt attention to these associations."},{"type":"table","title":"Disorders of position and fusion","cols":["Anomaly","Mechanism","Key findings"],"rows":[["Pelvic kidney","One kidney fails to pass the umbilical arterial fork","Low pelvic mass, low arterial supply, short ureter, malrotation"],["Horseshoe kidney","Lower poles fuse; isthmus caught under inferior mesenteric artery","Low midline fused kidney, anterior pelves, obstruction, stones, infection"],["Accessory renal artery","Lower embryonic arteries fail to regress","End artery; lower pole vessel can compress ureter"],["Malrotation","Medial rotation incomplete","Hilum faces anteriorly; often accompanies ectopia or fusion"]],"caption":"Each anomaly corresponds to one failed part of ascent: movement, vessel regression, separation, or rotation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-01-01-C04","obj":"UR-1.4","title":"Bifid and duplex ureters, ectopic orifices, obstruction, and reflux","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ureteral duplication is one of the most common congenital anomalies of the urinary tract. Its form depends on when and how the ureteric bud divides. If a single bud splits early, before it enters the metanephric mesenchyme, the two branches drain separate upper and lower parts of the kidney but join to form one ureter before the bladder: a bifid (partial) ureter with one orifice. If two separate buds arise from the mesonephric duct, each forms a complete ureter with its own orifice: a duplex (complete) ureter. In both, the kidney has upper and lower pole moieties, each with its own pelvis."},{"type":"p","text":"Where each ureteric orifice ends up follows from how the caudal mesonephric duct is absorbed into the developing bladder wall. As the duct is taken up, the ureteric bud acquires its own opening into the bladder and is carried upward and laterally, while the duct remnant moves downward toward the urethra. In a complete duplication, the bud that arises closer to the bladder (which drains the lower pole) is absorbed first and is carried farther upward and laterally. The bud that arises higher on the duct (which drains the upper pole) is absorbed later, stays with the duct longer, and opens lower and more medially, sometimes outside the bladder altogether. This relationship is the Weigert-Meyer rule."},{"type":"table","title":"Complete ureteral duplication (Weigert-Meyer rule)","cols":["Feature","Upper pole ureter","Lower pole ureter"],"rows":[["Orifice position","Lower and more medial (inferomedial)","Higher and more lateral (superolateral)"],["Intramural tunnel","Long, or orifice ectopic outside the trigone","Short and nearly perpendicular"],["Typical problem","Obstruction, ureterocele, ectopic insertion","Vesicoureteral reflux"],["Consequence","Hydroureteronephrosis of the upper pole, often dysplastic","Recurrent infection and scarring of the lower pole"]],"caption":"The two ureters cross as they descend, so the upper pole ureter ends lower in the urinary tract."},{"type":"p","text":"Reflux and obstruction are opposite failures of the ureterovesical junction. Normally the ureter runs obliquely through the bladder wall in a long submucosal tunnel; as the bladder fills and contracts, its pressure flattens this tunnel and acts as a flap valve that prevents backflow. A laterally placed lower pole orifice has a short, straight tunnel, so the valve fails and urine refluxes into the ureter during voiding, carrying bacteria to the renal pelvis and causing pyelonephritis and reflux scarring. An upper pole orifice that ends low or ectopically often has a narrowed opening or a ureterocele, a cystic dilation of the terminal ureter, and the result is obstruction and dilation of that moiety."},{"type":"p","text":"An ectopic ureter opens outside its normal site on the trigone, and the clinical result depends on sex because the possible sites differ. In a girl the orifice can lie below the external urethral sphincter, in the distal urethra, vestibule, or vagina, so urine from that moiety leaks continuously while she also voids normally from the bladder. In a boy, ectopic orifices stay on derivatives of the mesonephric duct or the urethra above the external sphincter (bladder neck, prostatic urethra, seminal vesicle, ductus deferens, or ejaculatory duct), so boys are not incontinent but may present with infection, obstruction, or epididymitis."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: dribbling with normal voiding","text":"A 5 year old girl voids normally but her underwear is always damp. Step 1: constant wetness despite normal voiding means some urine bypasses the bladder and its sphincter. Step 2: that implies a ureter opening below the external sphincter. Step 3: in girls, ectopic ureters usually come from a duplex system, and by the Weigert-Meyer rule the upper pole ureter is the one that ends low and medial. Step 4: imaging would therefore show a duplex kidney with a dilated, often poorly functioning upper pole draining into the urethra or vagina. A boy with the same anomaly would not leak, because his ectopic sites all lie above the external sphincter."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-01-01-C05","obj":"UR-1.5","title":"Partition of the cloaca and remnants of the allantois","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cloaca is the endoderm lined chamber at the end of the hindgut. It receives the allantois, a diverticulum that extends into the connecting stalk, and the two mesonephric ducts, and it is closed ventrally by the cloacal membrane, where endoderm meets ectoderm without intervening mesoderm. Between about the fourth and seventh weeks, the urorectal septum, a wedge of mesoderm in the angle between the allantois and hindgut, grows toward the cloacal membrane and divides the cloaca in two."},{"type":"flow","title":"Partition of the cloaca","steps":["Urorectal septum grows caudally toward the cloacal membrane","Cloaca splits into urogenital sinus and anorectal canal","Cloacal membrane divides into urogenital and anal membranes","Septum tip forms the perineal body","Membranes rupture, opening the urogenital sinus and anus"],"caption":"Partition is complete by about week 7; incomplete septation leaves fistulas between the rectum and the urinary or genital tract."},{"type":"p","text":"The urogenital sinus lies in front and forms the bladder and urethra; the anorectal canal lies behind and forms the rectum and upper anal canal. The sinus has three parts. The upper, vesical part becomes the bladder and is continuous at its apex with the allantois. The pelvic part becomes the prostatic and membranous urethra in males and the urethra in females. The phallic part becomes the spongy (penile) urethra in males and the vestibule in females. As the bladder grows, the caudal mesonephric ducts are absorbed into its posterior wall and form the trigone, with the ureters opening at its upper corners. The mesodermal lining of the trigone is soon replaced by endodermal epithelium, so the whole bladder lining is endodermal in the adult."},{"type":"p","text":"As the bladder enlarges, the lumen of the allantois is normally obliterated, leaving a thick fibrous cord, the urachus, which in the adult is the median umbilical ligament running from the bladder apex to the umbilicus along the inner abdominal wall. Persistence of all or part of the lumen produces a urachal anomaly, and the site of persistence predicts the finding."},{"type":"table","title":"Urachal anomalies","cols":["Anomaly","What stays open","Finding"],"rows":[["Patent urachus (urachal fistula)","The whole lumen, bladder to umbilicus","Clear urine leaking from the umbilicus, more when the bladder fills or strains"],["Urachal cyst","A middle segment, closed at both ends","Midline lower abdominal mass between umbilicus and bladder; may become infected"],["Urachal sinus","The umbilical end only","Intermittent discharge at the umbilicus"],["Vesicourachal diverticulum","The bladder end only","Outpouching at the bladder dome; may hold stagnant urine"]],"caption":"A patent vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct, by contrast, drains bowel contents rather than urine at the umbilicus."},{"type":"p","text":"Two further points connect this section to others. A patent urachus is more likely when the bladder outlet is obstructed, as with posterior urethral valves, because sustained high bladder pressure keeps the channel open. Urachal remnants can also undergo glandular metaplasia, so cancers arising in them at the bladder dome are typically adenocarcinomas rather than the urothelial carcinomas that arise elsewhere in the bladder."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn has clear fluid leaking from the umbilicus that worsens when he cries. What structure failed to close, and how could you tell this fluid from the drainage of a patent vitelline duct?","explain":"The allantois failed to obliterate (patent urachus), so bladder urine reaches the umbilicus; vitelline duct drainage is bowel content, not urine.","check":"The urachal anomalies table and its caption."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-01-01-C06","obj":"UR-1.6","title":"Posterior urethral valves and lower tract obstruction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Posterior urethral valves are obstructing membranous folds in the posterior (prostatic) urethra, typically just distal to the verumontanum. They occur only in males, because they form in the male posterior urethra in relation to the entry of the mesonephric duct derivatives, and they are the most common cause of congenital bladder outlet obstruction in boys (about 1 in 5,000 to 8,000 male births). The folds act like a one way valve: urine flowing out balloons them into the lumen and blocks the stream, while a catheter passed retrograde slips through easily."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the obstruction sits below the bladder, the entire urinary tract above it is exposed to high pressure. The detrusor hypertrophies and becomes trabeculated as it pushes against the blocked outlet, and the bladder distends. The posterior urethra above the valves dilates. Raised bladder pressure is transmitted up both ureters, which dilate and become tortuous, and both renal pelves and calyces dilate: bilateral hydroureteronephrosis. Vesicoureteral reflux often coexists because the high pressure distorts the ureterovesical junctions."},{"type":"flow","title":"Posterior urethral valves in the fetus","steps":["Valves obstruct the male posterior urethra","Bladder pressure rises; detrusor thickens and bladder distends","Posterior urethra dilates above the valves","Backpressure dilates both ureters, pelves, and calyces","High pressure during nephrogenesis causes renal dysplasia","Fetal urine output falls: oligohydramnios","Pulmonary hypoplasia and Potter features"],"caption":"Outlet obstruction affects both kidneys at once, unlike a unilateral ureteropelvic junction obstruction."},{"type":"p","text":"The damage begins before birth. Obstruction during nephrogenesis distorts branching and induction, so the kidneys may be dysplastic and the total nephron number reduced, a loss that is not reversed when the obstruction is relieved. As the kidneys fail, fetal urine output falls, oligohydramnios follows, and the same Potter sequence seen in renal agenesis can develop, with pulmonary hypoplasia as the main threat to life. The severity of oligohydramnios and how early it appears therefore track the severity of renal injury."},{"type":"p","text":"These mechanisms explain the diagnostic findings. Prenatal ultrasound in a male fetus shows a distended, thick walled bladder, a dilated posterior urethra (often called the keyhole sign), bilateral hydroureteronephrosis, and reduced amniotic fluid. After birth, an infant may have a palpable bladder, a weak or dribbling stream, urinary tract infection, and rising serum creatinine; a voiding cystourethrogram shows the dilated posterior urethra above the valves. Damaged tubules often cannot concentrate urine or acidify it well, so polyuria and acidosis can persist even after the outlet is opened."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing congenital obstruction by level","cols":["Level","Example","Distribution","Bladder","Amniotic fluid"],"rows":[["Ureteropelvic junction","Incomplete canalization, crossing lower pole vessel","Usually unilateral pelvis and calyces; normal ureter","Normal","Usually normal"],["Ureterovesical junction","Ureterocele, ectopic or narrowed orifice","Ureter and kidney on the affected side","Normal","Usually normal"],["Bladder outlet or urethra","Posterior urethral valves","Bilateral ureters and kidneys","Distended, thick walled","Often reduced"]],"caption":"Dilation stops at the level of obstruction; only a block below the bladder affects both kidneys and the bladder together."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: locating the obstruction","text":"A fetal ultrasound at 24 weeks shows a male fetus with a large, thick walled bladder, dilation of both ureters and both renal pelves, and markedly low amniotic fluid. Step 1: both kidneys are affected, so the block lies where the two systems share a path, at or below the bladder. Step 2: the bladder itself is distended and thickened, which places the block below it, at the outlet or urethra. Step 3: in a male fetus the classic cause is posterior urethral valves. Step 4: low amniotic fluid means fetal urine output has fallen, which signals kidney injury and predicts pulmonary hypoplasia. A unilateral ureteropelvic junction obstruction would leave the bladder and amniotic fluid normal."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and anatomy explain the adult urinary tract. The split between bud and mesenchyme defines where the nephron ends and the collecting duct begins. The arterial ladder of ascent explains accessory renal arteries, and the absorption of the mesonephric duct into the bladder explains the trigone, the position of the ureteric orifices, and the shared urinary and genital anomalies in both sexes. Genetics links induction failure to RET and WT1, and karyotype abnormalities such as Turner syndrome to fusion anomalies."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology explain the consequences. The fetus relies on the placenta for excretion and on its own kidneys for amniotic fluid, so renal failure before birth shows up as oligohydramnios and pulmonary hypoplasia rather than uremia. After birth, obstruction and reflux cause stasis, ascending infection, and scarring, and any loss of nephrons during nephrogenesis is permanent because no new nephrons form after about week 36."}],"summary":["Pronephros (week 4, nonfunctional), mesonephros (late week 4, interim), and metanephros (week 5, permanent) arise in sequence from intermediate mesoderm.","The metanephros makes urine from about weeks 10 to 12, and fetal urine is the main source of amniotic fluid from mid pregnancy.","GDNF from metanephric mesenchyme acting on RET of the ureteric bud drives branching, and bud signals induce nephron formation.","The bud forms the ureter, pelvis, calyces, and collecting ducts; the mesenchyme forms Bowman capsule through the distal convoluted tubule.","Failed induction causes renal agenesis; bilateral agenesis causes oligohydramnios and Potter sequence with fatal pulmonary hypoplasia.","Horseshoe kidney forms when the lower poles fuse, and its isthmus is trapped under the inferior mesenteric artery; it is linked to Turner syndrome.","Early bud splitting gives a bifid ureter; two buds give a duplex ureter, in which the upper pole ureter obstructs or is ectopic and the lower pole ureter refluxes.","An ectopic ureter below the external sphincter in a girl causes constant dribbling with normal voiding.","The urorectal septum divides the cloaca into urogenital sinus and anorectal canal; failed obliteration of the allantois leaves a patent urachus or urachal cyst.","Posterior urethral valves in boys cause a distended thick walled bladder, bilateral hydronephrosis, renal dysplasia, and oligohydramnios."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, kidneys and ureters; Pelvis and perineum, urinary bladder and urethra"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, congenital and developmental anomalies; The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Fetal and Neonatal Physiology"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Urinary System"}]},"UR-10":{"unit":"UR-10","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-10","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-10-01","title":"Immune Responses in Urinary Disease","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the immune system injures the kidney and how the urinary tract defends itself. It sorts glomerular disease by mechanism (anti-GBM antibody, immune complexes, or ANCA), shows how serum complement reveals which pathway a disease consumes, and compares the vasculitides by vessel size. It then covers the mechanical and mucosal defenses against urinary infection, immune attack on and escape by renal cell carcinoma, and the serology of hepatitis B vaccination. Board questions usually give an immunofluorescence pattern, a complement result, an ANCA type, or a serology panel and ask for the disease and its mechanism, so the reasoning here is built around those findings.","prerequisites":["Glomerular filtration barrier: fenestrated endothelium, glomerular basement membrane (GBM), podocytes, and mesangium","Hypersensitivity types I to IV and the effector cells of each","Classical, alternative, and lectin complement pathways","Antigen processing on MHC class I and class II and T cell help for B cells","Nephritic versus nephrotic syndrome (UR-12 chapters)"],"connections":[{"to":"Glomerular disease (UR-12 chapters)","how":"The immune mechanisms here explain the light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and electron microscopy findings of each glomerulonephritis."},{"to":"Respiratory system","how":"Goodpasture syndrome, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, and microscopic polyangiitis link pulmonary hemorrhage or airway granulomas to glomerulonephritis."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal system","how":"IgA vasculitis causes bowel wall hemorrhage and intussusception, and chronic hepatitis B drives membranous nephropathy and polyarteritis nodosa."},{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Uropathogenic E. coli adhesins defeat urinary defenses, and streptococcal infection triggers poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis."},{"to":"Oncology and tumor immunology","how":"VHL-driven renal cell carcinoma illustrates T cell and NK cell surveillance and escape through MHC class I loss and PD-L1."}],"terms":[{"term":"Glomerular basement membrane (GBM)","def":"The fused basal lamina of glomerular endothelium and podocytes, built on a type IV collagen network of alpha-3, alpha-4, and alpha-5 chains."},{"term":"Immune complex","def":"An antigen bound to antibody, which can circulate and deposit in tissue or form in place on a fixed or planted antigen."},{"term":"Linear immunofluorescence","def":"Smooth, continuous antibody staining along the GBM, produced when antibody binds an evenly distributed basement membrane antigen."},{"term":"Granular immunofluorescence","def":"Lumpy, discontinuous staining produced by discrete immune complex deposits."},{"term":"Pauci-immune","def":"Showing little or no immunoglobulin or complement on immunofluorescence, typical of ANCA-associated glomerulonephritis."},{"term":"ANCA","def":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies, autoantibodies against neutrophil granule enzymes, mainly proteinase 3 (PR3) and myeloperoxidase (MPO)."},{"term":"Crescent","def":"A collection of proliferating parietal epithelial cells and infiltrating macrophages in Bowman space after fibrin leaks through a ruptured glomerular capillary."},{"term":"C3 nephritic factor","def":"An autoantibody that stabilizes the alternative pathway C3 convertase (C3bBb), causing persistent C3 consumption."},{"term":"Vesicoureteral reflux","def":"Retrograde flow of bladder urine into the ureter and renal pelvis because the ureterovesical junction fails to close."},{"term":"Missing self","def":"NK cell killing of a cell that lacks the MHC class I molecules that normally engage NK inhibitory receptors."},{"term":"PD-1 and PD-L1","def":"An inhibitory receptor on activated T cells and its ligand, whose binding suppresses T cell activation and effector function."},{"term":"Anti-HBs","def":"Antibody to hepatitis B surface antigen, which neutralizes the virus and indicates immunity."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-10-01-C01","obj":"UR-10.1","title":"Three immune mechanisms of glomerular injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The glomerulus is a common target of immune injury because it filters a large plasma volume under high pressure through a charged basement membrane. Three mechanisms account for most immune glomerulonephritis, and each leaves a recognizable immunofluorescence pattern, because the pattern reflects where the antigen sits."},{"type":"h","text":"Type II: anti-GBM antibody"},{"type":"p","text":"In anti-GBM disease, IgG autoantibodies bind the noncollagenous (NC1) domain of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen. This antigen is part of the basement membrane itself and is spread evenly along every capillary loop, so bound IgG, usually with C3, outlines the GBM as a smooth linear ribbon. Antibody binding activates complement and Fc receptor bearing leukocytes, which break capillary walls. Fibrin then leaks into Bowman space, where parietal epithelial cells proliferate and monocytes and macrophages migrate in to form crescents, the lesion of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN). The same alpha-3 chain is present in alveolar basement membrane, where the epitope is normally hidden behind intact endothelium. Injury that exposes it, such as smoking, inhaled hydrocarbons, or infection, allows antibody to cause alveolar hemorrhage. Glomerulonephritis plus pulmonary hemorrhage with anti-GBM antibody is Goodpasture syndrome."},{"type":"h","text":"Type III: immune complex disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Immune complexes either circulate and lodge in the glomerulus or form in place when antibody meets an antigen fixed in or planted on the glomerulus. Either way they are discrete clumps, so immunofluorescence is granular. Location shapes the disease. Subendothelial and mesangial deposits face the blood, recruit neutrophils and monocytes, and cause nephritic inflammation, as in lupus nephritis and IgA nephropathy. Subepithelial deposits lie outside the reach of circulating leukocytes; in membranous nephropathy they injure podocytes through the complement membrane attack complex (C5b-9) and cause nephrotic proteinuria with little inflammation. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is nonetheless nephritic: its large subepithelial humps are accompanied by mesangial and subendothelial deposits and an intense neutrophil infiltrate."},{"type":"h","text":"Pauci-immune ANCA disease"},{"type":"p","text":"In ANCA-associated glomerulonephritis, autoantibodies activate neutrophils inside glomerular capillaries (mechanism in C03). Neutrophil proteases and oxygen radicals cause necrosis and crescents, yet little immunoglobulin remains, so immunofluorescence is weak or negative. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis are the examples."},{"type":"table","title":"Immune mechanisms of glomerulonephritis","cols":["Mechanism","Antigen and effector","Immunofluorescence","Examples"],"rows":[["Type II, anti-GBM","IgG to NC1 domain of alpha-3 type IV collagen","Linear IgG and C3 along GBM","Anti-GBM disease; Goodpasture syndrome with lung hemorrhage"],["Type III, immune complex","Circulating or in situ antigen and antibody; complement and leukocytes","Granular, location varies","PSGN, lupus nephritis, IgA nephropathy, membranous nephropathy, immune complex MPGN"],["Pauci-immune","ANCA-activated neutrophils","Little or none","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis"]],"caption":"All three can produce crescentic RPGN, which is classified as type I (anti-GBM), type II (immune complex), or type III (pauci-immune) by this same immunofluorescence pattern."},{"type":"flow","title":"From anti-GBM antibody to Goodpasture syndrome","steps":["IgG binds alpha-3 NC1 domain of type IV collagen","Linear deposition along glomerular basement membrane","Complement and neutrophils break capillary walls","Fibrin enters Bowman space; crescents form","Lung injury exposes alveolar basement membrane epitope","Alveolar hemorrhage with hemoptysis plus RPGN"],"caption":"One antigen in two basement membranes links the kidney and lung lesions."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A biopsy shows crescents in most glomeruli but only faint, scattered immunoglobulin staining. Which serum test is most likely to be positive, and which is likely negative?","explain":"Weak staining means pauci-immune disease, so ANCA (PR3 or MPO) is the likely positive test. Anti-GBM antibody would give bright linear IgG, and an immune complex disease would give granular deposits.","check":"The immunofluorescence column of the mechanism table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-10-01-C02","obj":"UR-10.2","title":"Serum complement in glomerular disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Serum complement falls when a disease consumes it faster than the liver makes it, and the pattern of C3 and C4 shows which pathway is being used. The classical pathway starts when C1q binds IgG or IgM in immune complexes; C1 cleaves C4 and C2, forming the C3 convertase C4b2a. The alternative pathway bypasses C4: C3b with factor B, cleaved by factor D, forms the C3 convertase C3bBb, stabilized by properdin. Both converge on C3. Classical activation therefore lowers C4 and C3, while alternative activation lowers C3 with normal C4."},{"type":"p","text":"In PSGN, nephritogenic streptococcal antigens (such as streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin B and a plasmin receptor) form complexes that mainly activate the alternative pathway, so C3 is low and C4 is usually normal. C3 returns to normal within about 6 to 8 weeks as the antigen is cleared. Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis (MPGN) also lowers C3, but it stays low. In the C3 glomerulopathies (including dense deposit disease), C3 nephritic factor or a defect in factor H keeps the alternative pathway running, so C3 falls and C4 stays normal. Immune complex MPGN, often from chronic hepatitis C with cryoglobulins, uses the classical pathway and can lower C4 as well."},{"type":"p","text":"Lupus nephritis is driven by IgG complexes of DNA and nucleosomes, so classical pathway consumption lowers both C3 and C4, and levels fall further during flares. Inherited deficiency of C1q, C2, or C4 predisposes to lupus because these proteins help clear apoptotic debris and immune complexes. Immunofluorescence in lupus often shows IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q together, the full house pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"IgA nephropathy, anti-GBM disease, and ANCA vasculitis keep normal serum complement. IgA does not activate the classical pathway, and the local alternative and lectin activation it causes in the mesangium is too small to lower serum levels. Anti-GBM disease and ANCA disease injure the kidney through antibody bound to tissue or activated neutrophils rather than through large circulating complex loads."},{"type":"table","title":"Complement patterns","cols":["Disease","C3","C4","Main pathway"],"rows":[["Poststreptococcal GN","Low, normal by about 8 weeks","Usually normal","Alternative"],["MPGN, C3 glomerulopathy","Low and persistent","Normal","Alternative (C3 nephritic factor, factor H defect)"],["MPGN, immune complex type","Low","Often low","Classical"],["Lupus nephritis","Low","Low","Classical"],["IgA nephropathy, anti-GBM disease, ANCA vasculitis","Normal","Normal","No significant serum consumption"]],"caption":"Low C4 points to classical pathway immune complexes; isolated low C3 points to alternative pathway activation."},{"type":"example","title":"Using the timing of C3","text":"A 9-year-old has cola-colored urine, edema, and hypertension 2 weeks after a sore throat. C3 is low, C4 normal, and anti-streptolysin O is raised. The 2-week latency, isolated low C3, and streptococcal serology fit PSGN, and C3 should normalize by about 8 weeks. If C3 is still low at 12 weeks, PSGN no longer explains it. Persistent isolated low C3 with normal C4 suggests continuing alternative pathway activation, as in C3 glomerulopathy with C3 nephritic factor, and biopsy would show MPGN pattern with dominant C3 staining. Had C4 also been low, lupus or immune complex MPGN would be the concern."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-10-01-C03","obj":"UR-10.3","title":"ANCA vasculitis and polyarteritis nodosa","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vasculitides are classified by the size of vessel they involve, and vessel size predicts which organs are hit. The ANCA-associated vasculitides affect small vessels (capillaries, venules, arterioles, and small arteries), so they damage glomeruli and alveolar capillaries. Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) affects medium muscular arteries and spares these capillary beds."},{"type":"p","text":"ANCA are directed against PR3 or MPO, enzymes normally stored in neutrophil granules. Cytokines such as TNF released during an infection prime neutrophils to move these enzymes to the cell surface. ANCA then bind them and, through Fc receptors, activate the neutrophil to degranulate, produce oxygen radicals, and release neutrophil extracellular traps next to the endothelium. The result is necrotizing vasculitis with few deposits. On indirect immunofluorescence of ethanol-fixed neutrophils, PR3-ANCA gives granular cytoplasmic staining (c-ANCA) and MPO-ANCA stains around the nucleus (p-ANCA), because MPO redistributes during fixation; specific PR3 and MPO immunoassays now confirm the target."},{"type":"flow","title":"How ANCA injure small vessels","steps":["Infection releases TNF and other cytokines","Primed neutrophils express PR3 or MPO on surface","ANCA bind antigen and engage Fc receptors","Neutrophils degranulate and release oxygen radicals","Necrotizing injury to capillary and arteriolar walls","Pauci-immune crescentic GN and alveolar capillaritis"],"caption":"Injury comes from activated neutrophils rather than deposited antibody, so immunofluorescence is weak."},{"type":"p","text":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) adds necrotizing granulomatous inflammation of the upper and lower respiratory tract to the vasculitis. Granulomas in the nose and sinuses cause chronic sinusitis, mucosal ulcers, and septal collapse (saddle nose); in the lung they form nodules that may cavitate; the kidney shows focal necrotizing pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis. Most patients with active GPA have PR3-ANCA. Microscopic polyangiitis (MPA) produces the same necrotizing small vessel vasculitis in kidney and lung, with glomerulonephritis and pulmonary capillaritis, but without granulomas and without destructive upper airway disease; it is usually MPO-ANCA positive."},{"type":"p","text":"PAN is a transmural necrotizing inflammation of medium and small muscular arteries with fibrinoid necrosis. Lesions are segmental and of different ages in the same patient. Because it spares arterioles, capillaries, and venules, glomerulonephritis and lung involvement are absent and ANCA is negative. Weakening of the renal and interlobar artery walls produces microaneurysms seen on angiography, and narrowing and thrombosis cause renal infarcts and renin-mediated hypertension. About a third of classic cases are linked to chronic hepatitis B, through immune complexes containing viral antigen. Other targets include mesenteric arteries (abdominal pain, bleeding) and the vasa nervorum (mononeuritis multiplex)."},{"type":"table","title":"GPA, MPA, and PAN compared","cols":["Feature","GPA","MPA","PAN"],"rows":[["Vessel size","Small","Small","Medium muscular arteries"],["ANCA","PR3 (c-ANCA) in most","MPO (p-ANCA) in most","Negative"],["Granulomas","Yes, upper and lower airway","No","No"],["Upper airway","Sinusitis, ulcers, saddle nose","Spared","Spared"],["Lung","Nodules, cavities, hemorrhage","Capillaritis, hemorrhage","Spared"],["Kidney","Pauci-immune crescentic GN","Pauci-immune crescentic GN","Glomeruli spared; microaneurysms, infarcts, renin-driven hypertension"]],"caption":"Glomerulonephritis means small vessel disease; renal artery aneurysms without GN mean medium vessel disease."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 45-year-old with hepatitis B has hypertension, abdominal pain after meals, foot drop, and a bland urinalysis. Why is there no glomerulonephritis even though the kidney is involved?","explain":"This is PAN, which inflames medium muscular arteries and spares glomerular capillaries. Renal involvement is ischemic (infarcts and renin release), not glomerular inflammation, so red cell casts are absent.","check":"The vessel size row and the PAN paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-10-01-C04","obj":"UR-10.4","title":"IgA vasculitis in children","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura) is the most common systemic vasculitis of childhood, mostly between about 3 and 15 years of age. It often follows an upper respiratory infection, and it is an immune complex small vessel vasculitis with IgA-dominant deposits in skin, gut, joints, and glomeruli."},{"type":"p","text":"The central abnormality is IgA1 with too little galactose on the O-linked sugars of its hinge region. Mucosal infection increases production of this galactose-deficient IgA1, and IgG or IgA autoantibodies recognize the exposed hinge sugars and form complexes. These complexes deposit in small vessel walls and in the glomerular mesangium, activate complement locally through the alternative and lectin pathways, and recruit neutrophils. Serum C3 and C4 stay normal."},{"type":"flow","title":"From respiratory infection to IgA vasculitis","steps":["Upper respiratory infection stimulates mucosal IgA1","Galactose-deficient IgA1 exposes hinge region sugars","Autoantibodies bind it and form IgA complexes","Complexes deposit in skin, gut, joint, mesangial vessels","Local complement activation recruits neutrophils","Purpura, arthralgia, abdominal pain, hematuria"],"caption":"The same complexes cause the skin, gut, and kidney lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Each manifestation follows from small vessel inflammation. In the skin, leukocytoclastic vasculitis of dermal venules lets red cells escape into an inflamed, edematous dermis, so the purpura is raised and palpable. It concentrates on the buttocks and the extensor surfaces of the legs, where gravity raises venous pressure. Platelet count and coagulation are normal, which separates it from thrombocytopenic purpura. Arthralgia or arthritis, usually of the knees and ankles, is transient and nonerosive. In the gut, vasculitis of the bowel wall causes colicky pain, edema, and bleeding; an edematous or hemorrhagic segment of wall can act as a lead point and telescope into the next segment, so intussusception occurs, often confined to the small bowel."},{"type":"p","text":"The kidney lesion is mesangial IgA deposition with mesangial proliferation, identical on biopsy to IgA nephropathy, and it presents with hematuria, sometimes with proteinuria. Most children recover, but renal involvement determines long-term outcome, and adults with IgA vasculitis have more severe kidney disease. IgA nephropathy is best thought of as the kidney-limited form of the same process. A useful timing distinction is that IgA nephropathy causes hematuria within a day or two of a mucosal infection, while PSGN appears 1 to 3 weeks after pharyngitis."},{"type":"table","title":"IgA vasculitis, IgA nephropathy, and PSGN","cols":["Feature","IgA vasculitis","IgA nephropathy","PSGN"],"rows":[["Typical patient","Child","Young adult","Child"],["Relation to infection","Follows upper respiratory infection","Hematuria within 1 to 2 days","1 to 3 weeks after pharyngitis"],["Features beyond hematuria","Purpura, arthralgia, abdominal pain, intussusception","Usually none","Edema and hypertension from salt and water retention"],["Immunofluorescence","Mesangial IgA","Mesangial IgA","Granular IgG and C3, subepithelial humps"],["Serum complement","Normal","Normal","Low C3"]],"caption":"IgA vasculitis and IgA nephropathy share the kidney lesion; the vasculitis adds skin, joint, and gut involvement."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-10-01-C05","obj":"UR-10.5","title":"Urinary tract defenses and what defeats them","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Above the distal urethra, urine is normally sterile. Most urinary tract infections are ascending: bowel flora such as uropathogenic E. coli colonize the periurethral area, enter the bladder, and may climb the ureter to the kidney. The urinary tract resists this mainly by mechanics, supported by mucosal and innate immune defenses."},{"type":"p","text":"The most important defense is flushing. Urine flows one way from kidney to urethra, and the bladder empties almost completely at each void, so bacteria that enter are diluted by new urine and expelled before they can multiply to large numbers. The ureterovesical junction prevents backflow. Each ureter runs obliquely through the bladder wall in a submucosal tunnel, so as the bladder fills and during voiding, rising pressure flattens the tunnel against the detrusor and closes it like a flap valve. Ureteral peristalsis also carries urine forward."},{"type":"p","text":"Mucosal defenses add to this. A glycosaminoglycan layer coats the urothelium and resists adherence. Uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein), made by the thick ascending limb, binds the type 1 fimbriae of E. coli and carries bacteria out in urine. When bacteria invade superficial umbrella cells, those cells are shed into the urine with their passengers, and the underlying cells regenerate. Urothelial Toll-like receptor 4 detects lipopolysaccharide and triggers IL-8 release, which recruits neutrophils (pyuria), and antimicrobial peptides and secretory IgA limit growth. A short female urethra is one reason infection is more common in women."},{"type":"cycle","title":"How stasis sustains infection","steps":["Obstruction or poor emptying leaves residual urine","Bacteria in residual urine multiply between voids","Higher bacterial load overwhelms mucosal defenses","Inflammation and edema further impair drainage"],"caption":"Any break in flushing lets the bacterial count rise with each cycle."},{"type":"p","text":"Each classic risk factor removes a defense. Obstruction from stones, prostatic enlargement, urethral stricture, or posterior urethral valves causes stasis and residual urine above the block, and an obstructed infected kidney cannot clear bacteria. Neurogenic bladder, as in diabetic neuropathy or spinal cord injury, leaves residual urine for the same reason. Vesicoureteral reflux, most often primary from a congenitally short intravesical ureter in children, carries infected bladder urine to the renal pelvis, converting cystitis into pyelonephritis and renal scarring; urine refluxes into the open ducts of compound papillae, which cluster at the poles, so scars favor the poles. An indwelling catheter bypasses the urethra, keeps the bladder neck open, leaves residual urine below the balloon, and becomes coated with biofilm, where bacteria are shielded from flow, antibodies, and neutrophils. In pregnancy, progesterone relaxes ureteral smooth muscle and the enlarging uterus compresses the ureters at the pelvic brim (more on the right), so the upper tracts dilate and drain slowly; asymptomatic bacteriuria therefore progresses to pyelonephritis more often than in nonpregnant women."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors and the defense each removes","cols":["Condition","Defense lost","Typical result"],"rows":[["Obstruction (stone, prostate, valves, stricture)","One-way flow, complete emptying","Stasis, hydronephrosis, pyelonephritis"],["Neurogenic bladder","Complete emptying","Residual urine, recurrent cystitis"],["Vesicoureteral reflux","Flap-valve ureterovesical junction","Pyelonephritis and renal scarring in children"],["Indwelling catheter","Urethral barrier, complete emptying, mucosal contact","Biofilm infection, often polymicrobial"],["Pregnancy","Ureteral peristalsis and drainage","Bacteriuria progressing to pyelonephritis"]],"caption":"Each risk factor is easiest to remember by the specific defense it breaks."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-10-01-C06","obj":"UR-10.6","title":"Immune surveillance and escape in renal cell carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal cell carcinoma, most often the clear cell type driven by VHL loss, is usually heavily infiltrated by T cells and is regarded as an immunogenic tumor. It illustrates the two arms of antitumor immunity, CD8+ T cells and NK cells, and the ways tumors escape both."},{"type":"p","text":"Every nucleated cell displays fragments of its own proteins on MHC class I. Cytosolic proteins are degraded by the proteasome, the peptides are pumped into the endoplasmic reticulum by TAP, loaded onto class I heavy chains with beta-2 microglobulin, and carried to the surface. Tumor cells present abnormal peptides this way: mutated proteins (neoantigens) and normal proteins that are overexpressed or reexpressed. Naive CD8+ T cells are first activated in lymph nodes by dendritic cells that take up tumor material and cross-present it on class I, with costimulation through CD28 and B7. Activated cytotoxic T cells then recognize the same peptide and class I complex on the tumor through the T cell receptor, with CD8 binding the class I molecule, and kill by perforin and granzymes and by Fas ligand. Their interferon-gamma raises class I expression on nearby tumor cells."},{"type":"p","text":"NK cells need no prior sensitization. Their inhibitory receptors (killer cell immunoglobulin-like receptors and CD94/NKG2A) recognize self MHC class I and block killing. A cell that loses class I removes this brake, and if it also displays stress ligands such as MICA and MICB for the activating receptor NKG2D, the NK cell kills it with perforin and granzymes. NK cells also kill antibody-coated cells through CD16 (antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity)."},{"type":"p","text":"Tumors escape by turning these mechanisms against themselves. Mutation or silencing of HLA genes, beta-2 microglobulin, or TAP lowers class I and hides tumor peptides from CD8+ T cells. That should invite NK cell attack, which tumors avoid by shedding NKG2D ligands or by an immunosuppressive environment. Tumor cells and infiltrating myeloid cells also express PD-L1, often induced by interferon-gamma from the very T cells attacking them. PD-L1 binds PD-1 on activated T cells, recruits phosphatases that damp T cell receptor and CD28 signaling, and drives T cell exhaustion. Regulatory T cells, TGF-beta, and VEGF, which is overproduced in VHL-deficient tumors and impairs dendritic cell maturation, add further suppression. Antibodies that block PD-1 or PD-L1 work by removing this brake, which is the mechanistic basis of checkpoint inhibition."},{"type":"table","title":"Recognition and escape","cols":["Effector","What it recognizes","How it kills","How the tumor escapes"],"rows":[["CD8+ cytotoxic T cell","Tumor peptide on MHC class I","Perforin, granzymes, Fas ligand","Loses MHC class I, beta-2 microglobulin, or TAP; expresses PD-L1"],["NK cell","Missing MHC class I plus stress ligands (MICA, MICB)","Perforin, granzymes; ADCC through CD16","Sheds NKG2D ligands; suppressive environment"]],"caption":"Losing MHC class I trades T cell attack for NK cell attack, so successful tumors usually need more than one escape route."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A tumor clone acquires a beta-2 microglobulin mutation. Which effector cell loses the ability to kill it, and which gains it?","explain":"Without beta-2 microglobulin, MHC class I cannot reach the surface, so CD8+ T cells can no longer recognize tumor peptide. NK cell inhibitory receptors are no longer engaged, so NK cells can kill the clone if activating ligands are present.","check":"The NK cell paragraph and the recognition table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-10-01-C07","obj":"UR-10.7","title":"Hepatitis B vaccination and membranous nephropathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The hepatitis B vaccine contains recombinant hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) produced in yeast and adsorbed to an aluminum adjuvant. HBsAg self-assembles into virus-like particles that carry the antigen in repeated arrays but contain no viral DNA and no core protein, so they cannot cause infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Because HBsAg is a protein, the response is T cell dependent. Dendritic cells take up the particles, present HBsAg peptides on MHC class II, and activate CD4+ helper T cells. B cells whose receptors bind HBsAg internalize it, present peptides to these helper cells, and receive CD40 ligand and cytokine signals. In germinal centers the B cells switch from IgM to IgG and undergo affinity maturation, producing long-lived plasma cells and memory B cells. The resulting antibody, anti-HBs, binds the surface antigen on the viral envelope and neutralizes the virus before it can infect hepatocytes. A titer of at least 10 mIU/mL after the full series is regarded as protective."},{"type":"flow","title":"From vaccine to protective anti-HBs","steps":["Recombinant HBsAg particles with alum adjuvant","Dendritic cells present HBsAg on MHC class II","CD4+ helper T cells activated","HBsAg-specific B cells receive CD40L and cytokines","Germinal centers: class switch to IgG, affinity maturation","Plasma cells secrete anti-HBs; memory B cells persist"],"caption":"A protein antigen with T cell help produces durable, high-affinity IgG."},{"type":"p","text":"Serology separates vaccination from infection because the vaccine contains only surface antigen. Natural infection exposes the immune system to the whole virus, including core antigen, so it produces anti-HBc. A vaccinated person makes anti-HBs but never anti-HBc. Anti-HBs with anti-HBc means recovery from past infection, and HBsAg with anti-HBc means current infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Hepatitis B serology patterns","cols":["Pattern","HBsAg","Anti-HBs","Anti-HBc"],"rows":[["Vaccine immunity","Negative","Positive","Negative"],["Resolved past infection","Negative","Positive","Positive"],["Acute infection","Positive","Negative","Positive (IgM)"],["Chronic infection","Positive","Negative","Positive (IgG)"],["Never infected, not immune","Negative","Negative","Negative"]],"caption":"Anti-HBc is the marker of exposure to the virus; anti-HBs alone is the vaccine signature."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic hepatitis B causes membranous nephropathy, most often in children in endemic regions. Viral antigens, especially HBeAg, reach the glomerulus and combine with antibody in the subepithelial space, forming granular IgG and C3 deposits. Because these deposits lie outside the reach of circulating leukocytes, injury comes from complement membrane attack complexes damaging podocytes, which causes nephrotic proteinuria with little inflammation. Unlike primary membranous nephropathy, this secondary form usually lacks antibodies to the phospholipase A2 receptor. Preventing infection removes the antigen source, and in regions with universal infant vaccination, hepatitis B associated membranous nephropathy in children has declined."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a hepatitis B panel","text":"A nursing student has HBsAg negative, anti-HBs 85 mIU/mL, and anti-HBc negative. HBsAg negative excludes current infection. Anti-HBs above 10 mIU/mL shows protective antibody. Anti-HBc negative shows the immune system has never seen core antigen, so the antibody did not come from infection; this is vaccine immunity. A classmate has HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, and anti-HBc positive. The same anti-HBs now sits beside anti-HBc, so this is immunity after a resolved natural infection. A third student positive only for anti-HBc needs further testing, since this pattern can mean the window period, remote infection with waning anti-HBs, or a false positive."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and immunology together explain where immune disease strikes. The type IV collagen alpha-3 chain shared by glomerular and alveolar basement membranes links the kidney and lung in Goodpasture syndrome, and the capillary beds of the glomerulus and alveolus explain why small vessel ANCA vasculitis produces pulmonary-renal syndromes while medium vessel PAN spares both. Serum complement then separates classical from alternative pathway disease. Neither test stands alone, because complement can be normal early in lupus and ANCA can be negative in a minority of pauci-immune disease, so biopsy immunofluorescence remains the reference."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology runs through the chapter. Streptococcal and respiratory infections trigger PSGN and IgA disease, hepatitis B supplies the antigen for membranous nephropathy and PAN, and E. coli adhesins test urinary defenses that are mostly mechanical, so anatomy and urodynamics are the main predictors of infection risk. Renal cell carcinoma shows that the same MHC class I pathway that presents viral peptides also presents tumor peptides, and that tumors survive by the same inhibitory checkpoints that normally prevent immune damage to healthy tissue."}],"summary":["Linear IgG along the GBM is type II anti-GBM disease (alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen); with lung hemorrhage it is Goodpasture syndrome.","Granular deposits are type III immune complex disease (PSGN, lupus, IgA nephropathy, membranous); little or no staining is pauci-immune ANCA disease.","Subendothelial and mesangial deposits cause nephritic inflammation; subepithelial deposits injure podocytes through C5b-9 and cause nephrotic proteinuria.","Low C3 with normal C4 means alternative pathway activation (PSGN, C3 glomerulopathy); low C3 and C4 means classical pathway activation (lupus).","PSGN C3 normalizes by about 8 weeks; persistently low C3 suggests MPGN. IgA nephropathy, anti-GBM disease, and ANCA vasculitis keep normal complement.","GPA is PR3-ANCA with airway granulomas and GN; MPA is MPO-ANCA with GN and capillaritis but no granulomas; PAN is ANCA negative, spares glomeruli, and causes renal microaneurysms.","IgA vasculitis follows respiratory infection in children and causes palpable purpura, arthralgia, abdominal pain, intussusception, and mesangial IgA GN with normal platelets.","Flushing by one-way flow, complete emptying, and the flap-valve ureterovesical junction is the main urinary defense; obstruction, reflux, catheters, and pregnancy each break it.","CD8+ T cells kill tumor cells presenting peptide on MHC class I; NK cells kill cells missing class I; tumors escape by losing class I and expressing PD-L1.","Anti-HBs alone is vaccine immunity because the vaccine contains only HBsAg; preventing hepatitis B prevents its membranous nephropathy."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, glomerular diseases and mechanisms of glomerular injury"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, vasculitis"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity Disorders; Immunity to Tumors; Effector Mechanisms of Humoral Immunity"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Hepatitis Viruses; Enteric Gram-Negative Rods (Enterobacteriaceae)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal Disease, glomerulonephritis and urinary tract infection"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and perineum, ureters and urinary bladder"}]},"UR-11":{"unit":"UR-11","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-11","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-11-01","title":"Urinary Pathogens and Infection-Related Kidney Disease","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the organisms that infect the urinary tract and the ways infection injures the kidney. It explains how uropathogenic Escherichia coli ascends and attaches, why other organisms appear in particular hosts, how Proteus builds struvite stones, how to read a urinalysis by mechanism, how gonorrhea and chlamydia cause urethritis, which infections cause which glomerular lesions, and how tuberculosis and Schistosoma haematobium damage the urinary tract. Board questions usually give a host, a urinalysis, a culture clue, or a biopsy pattern and ask you to name the organism or explain the finding, so the focus is on virulence factors, laboratory identification, and immune mechanisms.","prerequisites":["Gram stain, bacterial cell wall structure, and basic culture media (MacConkey, blood agar)","Anatomy of the urethra, bladder, ureters, renal pelvis, and calyces","Glomerular filtration barrier and the nephritic versus nephrotic patterns","Complement pathways and type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity","Granuloma formation and cell-mediated immunity"],"connections":[{"to":"Renal pathology (glomerular and tubulointerstitial disease)","how":"Pyelonephritis, poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, membranous nephropathy, membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, and FSGS reappear there as lesion patterns; this chapter supplies the infectious causes."},{"to":"Kidney stones","how":"Struvite calculi are the stone type caused directly by infection, and they contrast with calcium, uric acid, and cystine stones that form in acidic or normal urine."},{"to":"Reproductive system pathogens","how":"Gonorrhea and chlamydia that cause urethritis also ascend to cause epididymitis, cervicitis, and pelvic inflammatory disease."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"Antigenic variation, IgA protease, complement consumption, cryoglobulins, and granulomas are general immune mechanisms seen here in a urinary setting."},{"to":"Respiratory pathogens (tuberculosis)","how":"Renal tuberculosis is seeded through the blood during lung infection and reactivates years later."}],"terms":[{"term":"Uropathogenic E. coli (UPEC)","def":"Strains of E. coli carrying adhesins and other virulence genes that let them colonize and invade the urinary tract."},{"term":"Fimbriae (pili)","def":"Hairlike surface protein fibers whose tip adhesins bind specific host cell receptors."},{"term":"Cystitis","def":"Infection and inflammation of the bladder, causing dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic pain."},{"term":"Pyelonephritis","def":"Infection of the renal pelvis and parenchyma, usually by ascent from the bladder, causing fever and flank pain."},{"term":"Urease","def":"A bacterial enzyme that hydrolyzes urea to ammonia and carbon dioxide."},{"term":"Struvite","def":"Magnesium ammonium phosphate, a crystal that precipitates in alkaline urine and forms infection stones."},{"term":"Pyuria","def":"White blood cells, mainly neutrophils, in the urine above the normal number."},{"term":"Sterile pyuria","def":"Pyuria with no growth on routine urine culture."},{"term":"Elementary body","def":"The small, metabolically inactive, infectious extracellular form of Chlamydia."},{"term":"Reticulate body","def":"The larger, metabolically active, intracellular form of Chlamydia that divides by binary fission."},{"term":"Cryoglobulins","def":"Immunoglobulins that precipitate in the cold and redissolve on warming, and that can deposit in vessels and glomeruli."},{"term":"Caseating granuloma","def":"A granuloma with central cheeselike necrosis, typical of Mycobacterium tuberculosis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-11-01-C01","obj":"UR-11.1","title":"Uropathogenic Escherichia coli and fimbrial adhesion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Escherichia coli is a gram-negative, facultatively anaerobic rod that ferments lactose, so it forms pink colonies on MacConkey agar. It causes most urinary tract infections (UTIs), about 75 to 95% of uncomplicated community cystitis. The infecting strain is almost always the patient's own fecal flora, which colonizes the perineum and periurethral area and then ascends the urethra. The female urethra is short (about 4 cm) and close to the anus, which is why UTIs are far more common in women; intercourse, catheters, obstruction, and vesicoureteral reflux add further risk."},{"type":"p","text":"The urinary tract normally clears bacteria. Voiding washes them out, urine has a high urea concentration and often a low pH, the urothelium is coated with a protective glycosaminoglycan layer, and uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) secreted by the thick ascending limb binds bacterial adhesins and acts as a decoy. To cause disease, a strain must attach firmly enough to resist the flow of urine. Uropathogenic strains (UPEC) do this with fimbriae, and the receptor each fimbria binds decides where in the tract the infection settles."},{"type":"table","title":"The two key E. coli fimbriae","cols":["Feature","Type 1 fimbriae","P fimbriae"],"rows":[["Tip adhesin","FimH","PapG"],["Host receptor","Mannose residues on uroplakins of bladder umbrella cells","Gal-Gal (galactose alpha 1,4 galactose) on P blood group glycolipids of upper tract and renal epithelium"],["Adhesion blocked by mannose","Yes (mannose sensitive)","No (mannose resistant)"],["Disease linked","Cystitis","Pyelonephritis and urosepsis"]],"caption":"Type 1 fimbriae anchor E. coli in the bladder; P fimbriae let it attach in the upper tract and kidney."},{"type":"p","text":"Type 1 fimbriae do more than attach. FimH binding triggers uptake into superficial umbrella cells, where bacteria multiply as intracellular communities shielded from antibodies and neutrophils, and can persist in quiescent reservoirs that seed recurrent cystitis. P fimbriae bind receptors that are dense on renal epithelium, so P-fimbriated strains are overrepresented in pyelonephritis. Other UPEC factors include alpha hemolysin (a pore-forming toxin), siderophores such as aerobactin that capture iron, a polysaccharide (K) capsule that resists phagocytosis, and lipopolysaccharide (LPS), whose lipid A drives fever and the neutrophil response."},{"type":"flow","title":"From fecal flora to pyelonephritis","steps":["Fecal E. coli colonizes the periurethral area","Ascends the urethra into bladder urine","Type 1 fimbriae bind mannosylated uroplakins: cystitis","Reflux or obstruction carries bacteria up the ureter","P fimbriae bind Gal-Gal receptors on renal epithelium","Neutrophils fill tubules and interstitium: pyelonephritis"],"caption":"Ascending infection. Each level of the tract requires its own adhesin, and stasis or reflux helps bacteria climb."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow the level of infection. Cystitis inflames the bladder mucosa and irritates its sensory nerves, giving dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic pain without fever. Pyelonephritis is a parenchymal infection: neutrophils fill the tubules and interstitium while glomeruli are relatively spared, LPS triggers cytokine release with fever, and stretching of the renal capsule causes flank pain and costovertebral angle tenderness. Neutrophils packed inside tubules are molded into white blood cell casts (C04). Hematogenous seeding of the kidney is much less common and suggests organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-11-01-C02","obj":"UR-11.2","title":"Other urinary pathogens and their host settings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most of the remaining urinary pathogens are predicted by the host. Young, otherwise healthy women get E. coli and Staphylococcus saprophyticus. Patients with catheters, recent instrumentation, hospital stays, structural abnormalities, or repeated antibiotic exposure get Klebsiella, Enterococcus, Pseudomonas, Proteus (C03), and other resistant gram-negative rods. Recognizing each organism depends on a few laboratory features tied to its structure."},{"type":"p","text":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus is a gram-positive coccus in clusters, catalase positive (like all staphylococci) and coagulase negative. It is the second most common cause of cystitis in young sexually active women. It is separated from the other common coagulase-negative species, Staphylococcus epidermidis, by novobiocin resistance: S. saprophyticus grows up to a novobiocin disk, while S. epidermidis is inhibited. Surface adhesins bind uroepithelium; it produces urease but does not reduce nitrate."},{"type":"p","text":"Klebsiella pneumoniae is a nonmotile, lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod with a thick polysaccharide capsule. The capsule blocks complement deposition and phagocytosis and makes colonies large and mucoid. It causes UTIs mainly in hospitalized, catheterized, and diabetic patients, and it is weakly urease positive."},{"type":"p","text":"Enterococcus faecalis and Enterococcus faecium are gram-positive cocci in pairs and short chains that carry the Lancefield group D antigen. They are catalase negative and tolerate harsh conditions, growing in 40% bile and 6.5% NaCl. They are normal gut flora that cause UTIs after instrumentation and in hospital, partly because their penicillin-binding proteins have low affinity for cephalosporins, so cephalosporin use selects for them. They do not reduce nitrate, so the nitrite test is negative."},{"type":"p","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is an aerobic, non-lactose-fermenting, oxidase-positive gram-negative rod that produces the blue-green pigment pyocyanin and a grapelike odor. Its exopolysaccharides, including alginate, form biofilm on catheter surfaces, where bacteria are protected from host defenses and antimicrobials, making it a classic catheter-associated pathogen."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the common urinary pathogens","cols":["Organism","Gram stain and lab clues","Key virulence feature","Typical host"],"rows":[["Escherichia coli","Gram-negative rod, lactose fermenter, oxidase negative","Type 1 and P fimbriae, LPS, capsule","Most community UTIs"],["Staphylococcus saprophyticus","Gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase positive, coagulase negative, novobiocin resistant","Urothelial adhesins, urease","Young sexually active women"],["Klebsiella pneumoniae","Gram-negative rod, lactose fermenter, mucoid, nonmotile","Polysaccharide capsule","Hospital, catheter, diabetes"],["Proteus mirabilis","Gram-negative rod, non-lactose fermenter, swarming","Urease, flagella","Recurrent UTI, struvite stones"],["Enterococcus species","Gram-positive cocci in pairs and chains, catalase negative, bile and salt tolerant, nitrite negative","Hardiness, intrinsic cephalosporin resistance","Instrumentation, hospital"],["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Gram-negative rod, non-lactose fermenter, oxidase positive, blue-green pigment","Alginate biofilm","Indwelling catheters"]],"caption":"Lactose fermentation, oxidase, catalase, coagulase, and novobiocin separate the organisms; the host setting narrows the list before any test is run."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 21-year-old woman has dysuria and frequency. Urinalysis shows pyuria with a negative nitrite, and culture grows catalase-positive, coagulase-negative cocci. What single disk test identifies the organism, and why was the nitrite negative?","explain":"Novobiocin resistance identifies Staphylococcus saprophyticus rather than S. epidermidis. Nitrite was negative because this gram-positive organism does not reduce nitrate to nitrite.","check":"The S. saprophyticus paragraph in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-11-01-C03","obj":"UR-11.3","title":"Proteus mirabilis, urease, and struvite stones","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Proteus mirabilis is a gram-negative rod that does not ferment lactose (pale colonies on MacConkey agar) and produces hydrogen sulfide. Its two defining features are abundant urease and vigorous motility from many peritrichous flagella. It causes a minority of community UTIs but is overrepresented in complicated, recurrent, and catheter-associated infection, and it is the classic cause of infection stones."},{"type":"p","text":"Urease hydrolyzes urea, which is present in urine at high concentration, into two molecules of ammonia and one of carbon dioxide. Ammonia (NH3) takes up hydrogen ions to form ammonium (NH4+), consuming free protons and raising urine pH, often above about 7.2. In alkaline urine, the fraction of phosphate in the trivalent form (PO4 3-) rises, and ammonium is now abundant. Magnesium, ammonium, and phosphate combine and exceed their solubility, so magnesium ammonium phosphate (struvite) precipitates, usually together with calcium phosphate (carbonate apatite). Struvite crystals look like coffin lids on urine microscopy."},{"type":"flow","title":"How urease builds a struvite stone","steps":["Proteus urease splits urea into NH3 and CO2","NH3 binds H+ to form NH4+","Urine pH rises, often above about 7.2","More phosphate is trivalent PO4 3-; NH4+ is abundant","Mg2+, NH4+, and PO4 3- precipitate as struvite","Crystals grow around bacteria into a staghorn calculus"],"caption":"Infection drives stone formation through chemistry: urease alkalinizes urine until struvite becomes insoluble."},{"type":"p","text":"Struvite stones can grow quickly and branch to fill the renal pelvis and several calyces, forming a staghorn calculus. Bacteria live inside the stone matrix and biofilm, where host defenses and drugs reach them poorly, so infection persists and stones regrow from any fragment left behind. The stone obstructs urine flow, which in turn promotes further infection and can lead to chronic pyelonephritis or xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis. Ammonia is also directly toxic to the urothelium and damages its protective glycosaminoglycan layer. Because struvite stones follow infection, they are more common in women and in people with catheters, neurogenic bladder, or urinary diversion."},{"type":"p","text":"Other urease producers can do the same: Klebsiella, Staphylococcus saprophyticus, Morganella, Providencia, and Ureaplasma urealyticum. E. coli rarely produces urease, so a stone with persistently alkaline urine points away from it. A contrast is useful: uric acid and cystine stones form in acidic urine, and calcium oxalate forms largely independently of urine pH, whereas struvite and calcium phosphate form in alkaline urine."},{"type":"p","text":"In the laboratory, Proteus swarms. Its flagella let it spread across the surface of blood agar in thin concentric waves rather than forming discrete colonies, and this swarming pattern, with a non-lactose-fermenting, urease-positive, hydrogen sulfide-producing rod, identifies it. The same motility helps it ascend the ureters and spread along catheters."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-11-01-C04","obj":"UR-11.4","title":"Reading the urinalysis in infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A urinalysis has a chemical part (the dipstick) and a microscopic part. Each finding reports a different event: neutrophils have entered the urine, bacteria with a particular enzyme have been in the bladder for some hours, or inflammation is occurring inside the renal tubules. Reading the pattern rather than any single result is what places the infection and suggests the organism."},{"type":"p","text":"Leukocyte esterase is an enzyme from neutrophil granules. A positive pad means neutrophils, intact or lysed, are present, which is pyuria; on microscopy this corresponds to more than about 10 white cells per microliter of uncentrifuged urine or more than about 5 per high-power field of spun sediment. Pyuria shows inflammation but not its cause or level."},{"type":"p","text":"Nitrite is not normally in urine. Dietary nitrate is filtered and excreted, and Enterobacterales such as E. coli, Klebsiella, and Proteus carry nitrate reductase, which converts it to nitrite. The conversion takes time, about 4 hours of urine sitting in the bladder. A positive nitrite is therefore quite specific for bacteriuria with these gram-negative rods, but its sensitivity is limited. It is negative with organisms that lack the enzyme (Enterococcus and Staphylococcus saprophyticus), often negative with Pseudomonas, and falsely negative with frequent voiding, dilute urine, or low dietary nitrate. A negative nitrite never excludes UTI."},{"type":"p","text":"Casts form only in the tubules, where uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) gels and molds whatever cells are in the lumen. White blood cell casts therefore place neutrophils inside the kidney, as in pyelonephritis (or acute interstitial nephritis), and distinguish it from cystitis, in which white cells come from the bladder and no casts form. Red blood cell casts point instead to glomerular bleeding, as in glomerulonephritis (C06). Bacteria on microscopy and many squamous epithelial cells must be interpreted together: squamous cells suggest the sample was contaminated by perineal or vaginal flora."},{"type":"p","text":"Sterile pyuria means pyuria with no growth on routine culture. The organism is present but does not grow on standard media, or no organism is involved. Infectious causes include Chlamydia trachomatis (obligate intracellular), Neisseria gonorrhoeae (fastidious, needs enriched selective media), Mycoplasma genitalium, and Mycobacterium tuberculosis (slow growing, needs mycobacterial culture). Noninfectious causes include interstitial nephritis, stones, and recent antibiotic use. Culture confirms UTI: the classic threshold for a clean-catch midstream sample is 100,000 colony-forming units per mL, with lower counts accepted in symptomatic patients and catheter samples."},{"type":"table","title":"Expected urinalysis patterns","cols":["Condition","Leukocyte esterase","Nitrite","WBC casts","Routine culture"],"rows":[["E. coli cystitis","Positive","Usually positive","Absent","Grows E. coli"],["E. coli pyelonephritis","Positive","Usually positive","Present","Grows E. coli"],["Enterococcus or S. saprophyticus cystitis","Positive","Negative","Absent","Grows organism"],["Chlamydial or gonococcal urethritis","Often positive","Negative","Absent","No growth (sterile pyuria)"],["Renal tuberculosis","Positive","Negative","Usually absent","No growth (sterile pyuria); acid urine"]],"caption":"Leukocyte esterase marks neutrophils, nitrite marks nitrate-reducing gram-negative rods, casts mark the kidney, and a negative routine culture with pyuria points to organisms that need special methods."},{"type":"example","title":"Placing an infection from the urinalysis","text":"A 30-year-old woman has fever of 39 C (102.2 F), right flank pain, and costovertebral angle tenderness. Dipstick: leukocyte esterase positive, nitrite positive, pH 6. Microscopy: many neutrophils, white cell casts, few squamous cells. Leukocyte esterase shows pyuria. Positive nitrite shows a nitrate-reducing gram-negative rod has been in the bladder for hours. The casts place neutrophils inside the tubules, so this is pyelonephritis, not cystitis, and few squamous cells argue against contamination. Acidic pH makes a urease producer less likely. The best answer is E. coli, very likely carrying P fimbriae. If the pH had been 8 with coffin-lid crystals, Proteus would be the leading choice."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-11-01-C05","obj":"UR-11.5","title":"Gonococcal and chlamydial urethritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Urethritis is inflammation of the urethra, usually sexually transmitted, causing dysuria and urethral discharge. It is divided into gonococcal and nongonococcal urethritis; Chlamydia trachomatis is the most common nongonococcal cause, and Mycoplasma genitalium, Ureaplasma, and Trichomonas vaginalis cause others. The two main organisms differ in structure, and those differences explain both the symptoms and the testing."},{"type":"p","text":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative diplococcus with flattened adjacent sides (kidney bean pairs). It is oxidase positive, ferments glucose but not maltose (unlike N. meningitidis), lacks a polysaccharide capsule, and is fastidious, needing enriched, selective media such as Thayer-Martin agar with added CO2. Type IV pili attach it to columnar epithelium of the urethra and cervix. The pilin gene recombines with silent gene copies, so the pilus antigens keep changing (antigenic variation), and Opa proteins switch on and off (phase variation). Antibodies raised against one variant fail against the next, so immunity does not last, reinfection is common, and no vaccine exists. IgA1 protease cleaves IgA at the hinge region, removing the main mucosal antibody, and lipooligosaccharide provokes an intense neutrophil response. In men this produces purulent discharge and dysuria about 2 to 7 days after exposure; Gram stain of the discharge shows diplococci inside neutrophils. Infection in women is often asymptomatic and can ascend to cause pelvic inflammatory disease. People with terminal complement deficiency (C5 to C9) are prone to disseminated infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium that depends on host cell ATP. Its envelope lacks a conventional peptidoglycan layer, its rigidity coming instead from disulfide cross-linked outer membrane proteins, so it does not stain on Gram stain. It alternates between two forms (figure below). Serovars D to K infect columnar epithelium of the urethra, cervix, and conjunctiva; A to C cause trachoma, and L1 to L3 cause lymphogranuloma venereum. Because it multiplies quietly inside cells and provokes milder inflammation, chlamydia causes scant watery or mucoid discharge or none at all, has a longer incubation of about 1 to 3 weeks, and often ascends unnoticed to cause epididymitis or pelvic inflammatory disease. Coinfection with gonorrhea is common."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Chlamydia developmental cycle","steps":["Elementary body attaches to columnar epithelial cell","Enters by endocytosis; blocks phagolysosome fusion","Converts to reticulate body inside an inclusion","Reticulate bodies divide by binary fission using host ATP","Reticulate bodies condense back into elementary bodies","Cell ruptures or extrudes inclusion, releasing elementary bodies"],"caption":"The small elementary body survives outside cells and infects; the larger reticulate body replicates. The cycle takes about 2 to 3 days."},{"type":"table","title":"Gonorrhea and chlamydia compared","cols":["Feature","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Chlamydia trachomatis D to K"],"rows":[["Organism","Gram-negative diplococcus, extracellular and within neutrophils","Obligate intracellular bacterium"],["Key virulence","Pili with antigenic variation, Opa proteins, IgA1 protease, lipooligosaccharide","Elementary and reticulate bodies, inhibition of phagolysosome fusion"],["Incubation","About 2 to 7 days","About 1 to 3 weeks"],["Discharge","Purulent, often copious","Scant, watery, or absent"],["Gram stain of discharge","Intracellular gram-negative diplococci","Neutrophils without organisms"],["Routine urine culture","No growth","No growth"],["Preferred diagnosis","Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT)","Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT)"]],"caption":"Both give urethritis with sterile pyuria; the discharge, Gram stain, and timing differ."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis of both relies on nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT), which amplifies organism DNA or RNA and is the most sensitive method. Suitable samples are first-catch urine (the first portion of the stream, which carries urethral cells) in men and a vaginal or endocervical swab in women, and one sample is usually tested for both organisms."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-11-01-C06","obj":"UR-11.6","title":"Infections that cause glomerular disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Infection can injure the glomerulus without any organism reaching it. Microbial antigens and the antibodies against them form immune complexes, either in the circulation or in situ in the glomerulus. Where the complexes lodge predicts the lesion. Subendothelial and mesangial deposits lie on the blood side of the basement membrane, attract neutrophils and macrophages, and produce a nephritic picture (hematuria, red cell casts, hypertension, reduced filtration). Subepithelial deposits sit beyond the basement membrane, away from circulating leukocytes; in membranous nephropathy they injure podocytes through complement membrane attack and cause nephrotic-range proteinuria, whereas the larger subepithelial humps of poststreptococcal disease form during an acute exudative nephritis. A virus can also damage podocytes directly."},{"type":"p","text":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) follows infection with nephritogenic strains of group A Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes), about 1 to 3 weeks after pharyngitis or 3 to 6 weeks after impetigo. The delay is the time needed to make antibody. Streptococcal antigens (the leading candidates are the cationic proteinase SpeB and nephritis-associated plasmin receptor) deposit in the glomerulus and combine with antibody, activating complement mainly through the alternative pathway, so serum C3 falls while C4 is usually normal. Glomeruli are enlarged and hypercellular with neutrophils; immunofluorescence shows granular IgG and C3; electron microscopy shows subepithelial humps. Typically a child develops cola-colored urine, periorbital edema, and hypertension. Elevated antistreptolysin O titers support a throat source and anti-DNase B a skin source. Most children recover fully, and C3 normalizes within about 6 to 8 weeks. Unlike rheumatic fever, PSGN follows skin as well as throat infection."},{"type":"flow","title":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","steps":["Pharyngitis or impetigo with nephritogenic group A strain","Latent period of 1 to 6 weeks while antibody forms","Streptococcal antigen and antibody deposit in glomeruli","Complement activation, mainly alternative pathway; low C3","Neutrophil influx and endocapillary proliferation","Hematuria, red cell casts, edema, hypertension, oliguria"],"caption":"A type III hypersensitivity reaction: the kidney is injured by immune complexes and complement, not by the bacteria themselves."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic hepatitis B causes membranous nephropathy, especially in children. Hepatitis B antigens (often HBeAg) form subepithelial complexes, the basement membrane thickens with spikes on silver stain, and the patient is nephrotic. Hepatitis B surface antigen complexes can instead deposit in medium-sized arteries, producing polyarteritis nodosa. Chronic hepatitis C drives clonal B cells to make cryoglobulins (mixed cryoglobulinemia: a monoclonal IgM rheumatoid factor bound to polyclonal IgG and viral antigen). These deposit beneath the endothelium and activate the classical pathway, lowering C4 more than C3, and cause membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis (MPGN) with mesangial interposition and a double-contoured (tram track) basement membrane, often with palpable purpura and arthralgia. HIV infects glomerular epithelial cells, which dedifferentiate and proliferate while the capillary tuft collapses: collapsing focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (HIV-associated nephropathy), with heavy proteinuria, enlarged echogenic kidneys, and rapid loss of function, seen mainly in people of African ancestry carrying APOL1 risk variants."},{"type":"table","title":"Infections and their glomerular lesions","cols":["Infection","Lesion","Deposit or mechanism","Complement","Main picture"],"rows":[["Group A Streptococcus","Poststreptococcal GN","Subepithelial humps; granular IgG and C3","Low C3, normal C4","Nephritic"],["Hepatitis B","Membranous nephropathy","Subepithelial immune complexes; GBM spikes","Usually normal","Nephrotic"],["Hepatitis C","MPGN with mixed cryoglobulinemia","Subendothelial cryoglobulin complexes; tram track GBM","Low C4 and often low C3","Mixed nephritic and nephrotic"],["HIV","Collapsing FSGS (HIVAN)","Direct podocyte infection and proliferation","Normal","Nephrotic, rapid progression"]],"caption":"Deposit location and complement pattern let you work backward from a biopsy or lab panel to the infection."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients are nephritic. One has low C3 with normal C4 three weeks after a sore throat; the other has low C4, palpable purpura, and positive cryoglobulins. Which complement pathway is consumed in each, and which infection is behind each?","explain":"The first is poststreptococcal GN, consuming complement mainly through the alternative pathway. The second is hepatitis C with cryoglobulinemic MPGN; IgM rheumatoid factor and IgG complexes activate the classical pathway, which uses up C4.","check":"The PSGN and hepatitis C paragraphs and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-11-01-C07","obj":"UR-11.7","title":"Renal tuberculosis and urinary schistosomiasis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal tuberculosis is not an ascending infection. During primary pulmonary infection, Mycobacterium tuberculosis spreads through the blood and seeds well-perfused organs, including the renal cortex. Cell-mediated immunity contains the bacilli: macrophages activated by interferon gamma from CD4 Th1 cells form granulomas, and the centers undergo caseous necrosis. The lesions can stay dormant for years and reactivate, often with no active lung disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Reactivated lesions enlarge into the medulla and papillae, cause papillary necrosis, and rupture into the calyces. Bacilli then travel down the collecting system, so ulceration and fibrosis follow the urine: calyceal deformity, ureteral strictures with hydronephrosis, and a small contracted bladder; in men, spread to the epididymis and prostate. Advanced disease can leave a nonfunctioning calcified kidney. Urine shows pyuria and often hematuria, usually with acidic pH, but routine culture is negative because the bacilli grow only on mycobacterial media over weeks. This is the classic sterile pyuria. Diagnosis uses acid-fast staining, mycobacterial culture of several early morning urine samples, and NAAT."},{"type":"p","text":"Schistosoma haematobium is a blood fluke (trematode) found mainly in Africa and the Middle East. Unlike other flukes, the sexes are separate, and the adult male holds the female in a groove along its body. The species is defined by its location: adults live in the vesical venous plexus around the bladder, whereas Schistosoma mansoni and Schistosoma japonicum live in mesenteric veins and injure the gut and liver."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Schistosoma haematobium life cycle","steps":["Terminal-spined eggs pass in urine into fresh water","Eggs hatch into ciliated miracidia","Miracidia infect freshwater snails (Bulinus)","Snails release fork-tailed cercariae","Cercariae penetrate intact human skin","Larvae travel by blood through lungs to the liver","Paired adults migrate to the vesical venous plexus","Females lay eggs that cross the bladder wall"],"caption":"Humans are the definitive host where adults mate; the snail is the intermediate host where the parasite multiplies asexually. Infection follows wading or swimming in contaminated fresh water."},{"type":"p","text":"Disease comes from the eggs, not the adult worms, which evade immunity. Each egg carries a terminal spine and secretes enzymes as it works through the bladder wall into the urine. Many eggs become trapped in tissue, where their antigens provoke Th2-driven granulomas rich in eosinophils, which explains blood eosinophilia. The granulomas ulcerate the mucosa, causing painless terminal hematuria, then heal by fibrosis and calcification. Fibrosis of the bladder and lower ureters leads to obstruction, hydronephrosis, and secondary bacterial infection. Years of inflammation drive squamous metaplasia of the urothelium, and with chronic irritation, oxidative DNA damage, and nitrosamines from secondary infection, this progresses to squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder, in contrast to the urothelial carcinoma typical elsewhere. Diagnosis is by finding terminal-spined eggs in urine."},{"type":"table","title":"Renal tuberculosis and Schistosoma haematobium","cols":["Feature","Renal tuberculosis","Schistosoma haematobium"],"rows":[["Route to urinary tract","Hematogenous from lung","Skin penetration, then venous migration to bladder plexus"],["Tissue reaction","Caseating granulomas (Th1, macrophages)","Egg granulomas with eosinophils (Th2), fibrosis, calcification"],["Urine","Sterile pyuria, hematuria, acid pH","Hematuria, terminal-spined eggs"],["Late complications","Strictures, contracted bladder, calcified kidney","Obstruction, hydronephrosis, squamous cell carcinoma of bladder"]],"caption":"Both are chronic granulomatous diseases that scar the urinary tract, but they arrive by different routes and leave different clues in the urine."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Most urinary infection is a contest between adhesion and flow. Anatomy (a short female urethra, reflux, obstruction, catheters) decides whether bacteria can reach and stay in the tract, microbiology decides which adhesins, enzymes, and capsules a given organism brings, and the host response decides the findings: neutrophils give leukocyte esterase and, inside tubules, white cell casts, while granulomatous immunity in tuberculosis and schistosomiasis produces scarring instead of pus. The urinalysis is the bridge, because each result is a mechanistic report on where inflammation is and what kind of organism is present."},{"type":"p","text":"Infection also injures the kidney indirectly. Poststreptococcal GN, hepatitis B membranous nephropathy, and hepatitis C cryoglobulinemic MPGN are immune complex diseases whose biopsy appearance depends on where the complexes lodge and which complement pathway they activate, while HIV injures podocytes directly. The limits matter: these patterns are typical, not exclusive. Membranous nephropathy is more often primary (autoantibody to the podocyte phospholipase A2 receptor) than viral, MPGN has many noninfectious causes, and a nitrite-negative urine or a sterile culture does not exclude infection."}],"summary":["Uropathogenic E. coli from the patient's own fecal flora ascends the urethra; type 1 fimbriae bind mannosylated uroplakins (cystitis) and P fimbriae bind Gal-Gal receptors (pyelonephritis).","Staphylococcus saprophyticus is the novobiocin-resistant, coagulase-negative cause of cystitis in young sexually active women; Klebsiella has a mucoid capsule, Enterococcus is a hardy gram-positive, and Pseudomonas forms catheter biofilm.","Proteus urease turns urea into ammonia, alkalinizes urine, and precipitates magnesium ammonium phosphate as staghorn calculi that shelter bacteria; swarming on blood agar identifies it.","Leukocyte esterase marks neutrophils; nitrite marks nitrate-reducing gram-negative rods and is negative with Enterococcus and S. saprophyticus; WBC casts place infection in the kidney.","Sterile pyuria points to organisms that do not grow on routine media: Chlamydia, gonococcus, and Mycobacterium tuberculosis.","Gonococcal pilus antigenic variation and IgA1 protease prevent lasting immunity; chlamydia alternates between the infectious elementary body and the replicating reticulate body, and NAAT diagnoses both.","Poststreptococcal GN is an immune complex nephritis after a latent period with low C3; hepatitis B causes membranous nephropathy, hepatitis C causes cryoglobulinemic MPGN with low C4, and HIV causes collapsing FSGS.","Renal tuberculosis is blood-borne, forms caseating granulomas, and scars the collecting system; Schistosoma haematobium eggs cause hematuria, fibrosis, and squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric gram-negative rods; Pseudomonads"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci; The Streptococci, Enterococci, and Related Genera"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Neisseriae, chlamydiae, mycobacteria, and medical parasitology (schistosomes)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney: glomerular diseases, pyelonephritis, and urolithiasis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System: schistosomiasis and bladder carcinoma"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity; Immunity to Microbes"}]},"UR-12-A":{"unit":"UR-12-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01","title":"Glomerular Diseases and Renal Failure","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how injury to the glomerular filtration barrier produces the two classic clinical patterns, nephritic and nephrotic syndrome, and how biopsy findings, complement levels, and urine sediment identify the specific disease. It then follows glomerular and tubular injury to its end points, acute kidney injury and chronic kidney disease. Board questions give a urinalysis, a set of labs, or a biopsy description and ask you to name the lesion, its cause, or its complication. If you can place an immune deposit on the filter and predict what that location does to inflammation, protein loss, and filtration, most of these questions become reasoning rather than recall.","prerequisites":["Structure of the glomerular filtration barrier: fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, podocytes, and mesangium (UR-2, UR-3)","Determinants of glomerular filtration rate and Starling forces across glomerular and systemic capillaries","Complement activation by the classical, lectin, and alternative pathways","Type II and type III hypersensitivity and immune complex deposition","Tubular handling of sodium, potassium, urea, and acid"],"connections":[{"to":"Renal physiology (UR-4 to UR-8)","how":"Glomerular pressure, tubuloglomerular feedback, and tubular sodium handling explain hyperfiltration, prerenal indices, and the volume and electrolyte disturbances of renal failure."},{"to":"Immunology (IM)","how":"Most glomerulonephritis is antibody mediated, and the complement pathway each disease activates predicts whether serum C3 and C4 fall."},{"to":"Microbiology and urinary pathogens (UR-11)","how":"Group A streptococci, hepatitis B and C, and HIV cause specific glomerular lesions, while pyelonephritis is recognized in the same sediment by WBC casts."},{"to":"Endocrine and cardiovascular pathology","how":"Diabetes and hypertension are the leading causes of chronic kidney disease, and kidney failure in turn worsens hypertension, heart failure, and bone mineral balance."},{"to":"Hematology (HE)","how":"Plasma cell dyscrasias cause AL amyloid, nephrotic syndrome causes hypercoagulability, and chronic kidney disease causes anemia and platelet dysfunction."}],"terms":[{"term":"Nephritic syndrome","def":"Glomerular inflammation producing hematuria with RBC casts, reduced GFR, oliguria, azotemia, hypertension, and proteinuria usually below 3.5 g/day."},{"term":"Nephrotic syndrome","def":"Loss of the protein barrier producing proteinuria above 3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminemia, edema, hyperlipidemia, and lipiduria."},{"term":"Azotemia","def":"Elevation of blood urea nitrogen and creatinine from reduced glomerular filtration."},{"term":"Uremia","def":"The clinical syndrome of advanced renal failure, caused by retained nitrogenous and other solutes and the metabolic and endocrine failures of the kidney."},{"term":"Podocyte effacement","def":"Flattening and fusion of podocyte foot processes with loss of slit diaphragms, seen on electron microscopy in proteinuric diseases."},{"term":"Subepithelial deposit","def":"Immune deposit between the basement membrane and the podocyte, on the urinary side of the filter."},{"term":"Subendothelial deposit","def":"Immune deposit between the endothelium and the basement membrane, on the blood side of the filter, where it attracts leukocytes."},{"term":"Crescent","def":"A proliferation of parietal epithelial cells and macrophages with fibrin in Bowman space that compresses the glomerular tuft."},{"term":"Cast","def":"A cylindrical mold of the tubule lumen made of uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) that traps cells or debris present in the tubule."},{"term":"Fractional excretion of sodium (FENa)","def":"The percentage of filtered sodium that appears in the urine, calculated as (urine Na times plasma creatinine) divided by (plasma Na times urine creatinine), times 100."},{"term":"C3 nephritic factor","def":"An autoantibody that stabilizes the alternative pathway C3 convertase (C3bBb), causing continuous C3 consumption."},{"term":"Glomerulosclerosis","def":"Replacement of glomerular capillaries by collagen and matrix, either in segments of some glomeruli (focal segmental) or diffusely, as in diabetes."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C01","obj":"UR-12-A.1","title":"Nephritic versus nephrotic syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The glomerular filtration barrier has three layers. Fenestrated endothelium faces the blood, the glomerular basement membrane (GBM) of type IV collagen, laminin, and negatively charged heparan sulfate sits in the middle, and podocyte foot processes joined by slit diaphragms (built from nephrin and podocin) face Bowman space. Together they restrict filtration by size and charge, so normal urine contains less than about 150 mg of protein per day and less than 30 mg of albumin. Glomerular diseases present in two broad patterns that depend on which part of the barrier is injured and whether inflammation is present."},{"type":"p","text":"Nephritic syndrome is inflammatory. Immune deposits in the mesangium or on the blood side of the GBM activate complement, and C5a and Fc receptor binding recruit neutrophils and monocytes. Proliferating glomerular cells and leukocytes crowd the capillary lumens, which reduces the filtering surface and lowers GFR, so blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine rise and urine output falls. Breaks in the inflamed capillary wall let red cells into the urine, where they are molded into RBC casts. Because the tubules are intact and keep reabsorbing sodium while filtration falls, salt and water are retained, producing hypertension and edema. Protein leaks through the damaged wall, but the low GFR keeps the total usually below 3.5 g/day."},{"type":"p","text":"Nephrotic syndrome is a failure of the protein barrier with little inflammation. Podocyte injury or deposits on the urinary side of the GBM disrupt the slit diaphragms, and proteinuria exceeds 3.5 g/day in adults (in children, above about 40 mg/m2 per hour). Serum albumin falls below about 3 g/dL (reference about 3.5 to 5.0 g/dL), and edema, hyperlipidemia, and lipiduria with fatty casts follow. Hematuria is slight and GFR is often normal at first."},{"type":"p","text":"Deposit location is the most useful rule. Mesangial and subendothelial deposits are exposed to circulating leukocytes and complement, so they cause nephritic disease; subepithelial deposits are separated from the blood by the GBM, so complement injures the podocyte without a leukocyte influx and the result is nephrotic. Some diseases, notably lupus nephritis and membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, produce a mixed nephritic and nephrotic picture."},{"type":"table","title":"Nephritic and nephrotic syndrome compared","cols":["Feature","Nephritic","Nephrotic"],"rows":[["Main lesion","Inflammation and proliferation in the glomerulus","Podocyte or slit diaphragm injury, little inflammation"],["Proteinuria","Usually under 3.5 g/day","Over 3.5 g/day"],["Hematuria","Yes, dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts","Minimal or absent"],["GFR","Reduced; oliguria and azotemia","Often preserved early"],["Blood pressure","Hypertension from sodium retention","Often normal early"],["Edema","Mild, often periorbital","Marked, generalized"],["Lipids and casts","Normal lipids; RBC casts","Hyperlipidemia; fatty casts and oval fat bodies"],["Typical deposits","Mesangial, subendothelial","Subepithelial or none"]],"caption":"The pattern reflects which layer of the filter is injured and whether leukocytes are recruited."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a child with acute glomerulonephritis become hypertensive even though the problem is in the glomerulus, not the vessels?","explain":"Inflammation lowers GFR, but the tubules still reabsorb sodium avidly, so filtered sodium falls further than intake. The positive sodium balance expands extracellular volume, which raises blood pressure and causes edema.","check":"The paragraph on how nephritic injury lowers GFR."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C02","obj":"UR-12-A.2","title":"Urine sediment as a map of the nephron","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Casts form when uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein), secreted by the thick ascending limb, gels in the distal tubule and collecting duct. Low flow, concentrated urine, and an acidic pH favor gelling. Anything trapped in the matrix was in the tubule lumen when the cast formed, so a cellular cast proves that the cells came from the kidney rather than from the bladder or urethra. Hyaline casts contain matrix alone and are nonspecific: they appear in concentrated urine, after exercise, and in prerenal states."},{"type":"p","text":"Red cells localize bleeding. Glomerular red cells are dysmorphic, with blebs and irregular outlines (acanthocytes), because they are squeezed through breaks in the GBM and then exposed to changing osmolality and pH along the tubule. RBC casts are specific for glomerular bleeding. Uniform (isomorphic) red cells without casts point to the collecting system, ureter, or bladder, as with stones, tumors, or cystitis. A dipstick positive for heme with no red cells on microscopy suggests myoglobin from rhabdomyolysis or free hemoglobin from hemolysis."},{"type":"p","text":"WBC casts mean leukocytes entered the tubules, which happens in tubulointerstitial inflammation: acute pyelonephritis, with bacteriuria and fever, and acute interstitial nephritis, often a drug hypersensitivity with eosinophils. Necrotic tubular cells slough into the lumen in acute tubular necrosis (ATN) and break down into the coarse muddy brown granular casts of that disease. Fatty casts and oval fat bodies are tubular cells and casts loaded with filtered lipoproteins, showing a Maltese cross under polarized light. Waxy casts are casts that degenerated during prolonged stasis in dilated, low-flow tubules, so broad waxy casts indicate advanced chronic kidney disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Urinary casts and what they localize","cols":["Finding","Where it forms and why","Classic disease"],"rows":[["Hyaline cast","Uromodulin alone in concentrated, slow urine","Normal, dehydration, prerenal state"],["RBC cast, dysmorphic RBCs","Red cells pass a damaged GBM","Glomerulonephritis"],["WBC cast","Leukocytes infiltrate tubules and interstitium","Pyelonephritis, interstitial nephritis"],["Muddy brown granular cast","Necrotic tubular cells degenerate in the lumen","Acute tubular necrosis"],["Fatty cast, oval fat body","Filtered lipid taken up by tubular cells","Nephrotic syndrome"],["Waxy cast, broad cast","Old casts in dilated, low-flow tubules","Chronic kidney disease"],["Isomorphic RBCs, no casts","Bleeding below the glomerulus","Stones, tumor, cystitis"]],"caption":"A cast places its contents inside the tubule, so the sediment identifies which compartment of the kidney is injured."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C03","obj":"UR-12-A.3","title":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is the prototype of immune complex (type III hypersensitivity) nephritic disease. It follows infection with nephritogenic strains of group A beta-hemolytic Streptococcus pyogenes, and it is most common in children between about 5 and 12 years old. The latent period is about 1 to 3 weeks after pharyngitis and about 3 to 6 weeks after impetigo. This delay is the time needed to make antibody against streptococcal antigens, so the kidney becomes involved after the infection has resolved."},{"type":"p","text":"The main nephritogenic antigens are streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin B (SpeB) and a nephritis-associated plasmin receptor. They lodge in the glomerulus or circulate as immune complexes, and complement is activated mainly by the alternative pathway. Serum C3 therefore falls while C4 is usually normal, and C3 returns to normal within about 8 weeks. A low C3 that persists beyond that suggests another disease, such as membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis or lupus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","steps":["Pharyngitis or impetigo with a nephritogenic group A strain","Antibodies form over 1 to 6 weeks","Antigen-antibody complexes deposit in glomeruli","Complement activation, mainly alternative pathway; serum C3 falls","Neutrophils and monocytes infiltrate; glomerular cells proliferate","GFR falls; hematuria, oliguria, sodium retention"],"caption":"The latent period reflects antibody production, which separates PSGN from disease caused directly by infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Light microscopy shows enlarged, hypercellular glomeruli packed with neutrophils and monocytes, a diffuse proliferative and exudative pattern. Immunofluorescence shows granular (lumpy-bumpy) IgG and C3 along capillary walls and in the mesangium. Electron microscopy shows large subepithelial humps, the distinctive finding, along with smaller subendothelial and mesangial deposits. The clinical picture follows the nephritic mechanism: cola-colored urine from hemoglobin altered in acidic urine, periorbital edema where loose tissue lets fluid collect, hypertension, and oliguria."},{"type":"p","text":"Evidence of recent infection supports the diagnosis. Antistreptolysin O titers rise after pharyngitis, while anti-DNase B is more reliable after skin infection because streptolysin O is inactivated by skin lipids. Most children recover completely; adults more often develop persistent disease or rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis. Unlike acute rheumatic fever, which follows only pharyngitis, PSGN can follow either throat or skin infection."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C04","obj":"UR-12-A.4","title":"IgA nephropathy and how it differs from PSGN","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"IgA nephropathy (Berger disease) is the most common primary glomerulonephritis worldwide. Its basis is a defect in O-glycosylation of the IgA1 hinge region, which produces galactose-deficient IgA1. IgG and IgA autoantibodies recognize the exposed hinge, and the resulting complexes deposit in the mesangium. There they stimulate mesangial cell proliferation and matrix production and activate complement through the alternative and lectin pathways. Because activation is local rather than systemic, serum C3 stays normal."},{"type":"p","text":"Mucosal infections of the upper respiratory or gastrointestinal tract increase production of the abnormal IgA. Because the antibodies and complexes already circulate, more deposition occurs almost at once, so episodes of gross hematuria appear within about 1 to 2 days of the infection (synpharyngitic hematuria) rather than after weeks. Between episodes, microscopic hematuria and mild proteinuria often persist. Liver disease and celiac disease are secondary associations because they impair IgA clearance or increase mucosal IgA production. IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura) produces the same mesangial lesion together with palpable purpura, arthralgia, and abdominal pain."},{"type":"p","text":"The disease is often slowly progressive, and a minority of patients reach end-stage kidney disease over about two decades. Biopsy shows mesangial proliferation on light microscopy, dominant mesangial IgA with C3 on immunofluorescence, and mesangial deposits on electron microscopy."},{"type":"table","title":"IgA nephropathy versus poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","cols":["Feature","IgA nephropathy","PSGN"],"rows":[["Timing after infection","1 to 2 days","1 to 3 weeks (throat), 3 to 6 weeks (skin)"],["Why the timing differs","Abnormal IgA and antibodies already circulate","New antibody must be made"],["Serum C3","Normal","Low, recovers by about 8 weeks"],["Typical patient","Young adult with recurrent episodes","Child after a single infection"],["Immunofluorescence","Mesangial IgA","Granular IgG and C3 in capillary walls"],["Electron microscopy","Mesangial deposits","Subepithelial humps"],["Course","Recurrent; slow progression in some","Usually resolves in children"]],"caption":"Timing and complement level are the two quickest discriminators."},{"type":"example","title":"Two patients with cola-colored urine","text":"A 22-year-old man has dark urine 2 days after a sore throat; C3 is normal, and he had a similar episode last year. A 7-year-old boy has dark urine, periorbital edema, and blood pressure of 135/90 mm Hg about 3 weeks after impetigo; C3 is low. In the man, the short interval means the injurious antibody already existed, and the normal C3 means complement was not consumed systemically, which fits IgA nephropathy with mesangial IgA. In the boy, the delay matches the time to form new antibody, and the low C3 reflects alternative pathway consumption by immune complexes, which fits PSGN; anti-DNase B is the better test after skin infection, and subepithelial humps would be expected on electron microscopy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C05","obj":"UR-12-A.5","title":"Rapidly progressive (crescentic) glomerulonephritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN) is a clinical syndrome of nephritic disease with loss of renal function over days to weeks. Its morphologic correlate is crescents in most glomeruli, often defined as more than half. RPGN is not one disease; it is the common end result of any injury severe enough to rupture glomerular capillary walls."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a crescent forms","steps":["Severe injury ruptures the glomerular capillary wall","Plasma proteins and fibrin leak into Bowman space","Fibrin and cytokines stimulate parietal epithelial cells","Parietal cells proliferate; monocytes and macrophages enter","Crescent compresses the tuft and blocks filtration","Untreated crescents fibrose; nephron loss is permanent"],"caption":"Cellular crescents become fibrocellular and then fibrous, which is why RPGN can cause irreversible renal failure within weeks."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunofluorescence separates three types by the pattern of immunoglobulin along the GBM. In type I, anti-GBM disease, autoantibodies bind the noncollagenous (NC1) domain of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen. The antigen is spread evenly along the membrane, so IgG and C3 appear as a smooth linear stain. The same antigen is present in alveolar basement membrane, so pulmonary hemorrhage with hemoptysis may accompany the nephritis (Goodpasture syndrome); smoking and inhaled hydrocarbons that expose the lung antigen are recognized triggers."},{"type":"p","text":"In type II, immune complex RPGN, deposits are discrete and the stain is granular. Any immune complex nephritis can become crescentic, including PSGN, lupus nephritis, IgA nephropathy, and IgA vasculitis. In type III, pauci-immune RPGN, little or no immunoglobulin is found. Most cases are renal involvement by antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (ANCA) small-vessel vasculitis. Cytokines prime neutrophils to express proteinase 3 (PR3) or myeloperoxidase (MPO) on their surface; ANCA binding then activates them to degranulate and release reactive oxygen species against the glomerular endothelium, causing necrotizing lesions without immune deposits."},{"type":"table","title":"Immunofluorescence classification of RPGN","cols":["Type","Immunofluorescence","Mechanism","Examples and serology"],"rows":[["I: anti-GBM","Linear IgG and C3","Antibody to alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen","Anti-GBM disease; Goodpasture syndrome with lung hemorrhage"],["II: immune complex","Granular","Circulating or in situ immune complexes","PSGN, lupus, IgA nephropathy, IgA vasculitis; complement often low"],["III: pauci-immune","Little or none","ANCA-activated neutrophils injure vessels","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (PR3-ANCA, c-ANCA); microscopic polyangiitis (MPO-ANCA, p-ANCA); eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (MPO-ANCA in some)"]],"caption":"The immunofluorescence pattern reflects the antigen: continuous membrane antigen gives a linear stain, discrete complexes give a granular one, and neutrophil-mediated injury leaves little immunoglobulin."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C06","obj":"UR-12-A.6","title":"Lupus nephritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal involvement is one of the most important determinants of outcome in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). Antibodies against double-stranded DNA and nucleosomes form immune complexes that deposit in the glomerulus or form there in situ, and they activate complement by the classical pathway. Immunofluorescence typically shows a full house pattern of IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q. Where the deposits sit decides the clinical picture, which is why lupus can be nephritic, nephrotic, or both."},{"type":"p","text":"Diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis (ISN/RPS class IV) is the most common class on biopsy and the most severe. Large subendothelial deposits thicken capillary walls into rigid wire loops visible on light microscopy, and because they face the blood they recruit leukocytes and drive proliferation in at least half of the glomeruli. Patients have hematuria with RBC casts, hypertension, a falling GFR, and often heavy proteinuria; crescents may develop. Membranous lupus nephritis (class V) has subepithelial deposits, which injure podocytes without inflammation and produce nephrotic syndrome, much like primary membranous nephropathy. Classes can coexist and can change over time."},{"type":"table","title":"ISN/RPS classes of lupus nephritis","cols":["Class","Pattern","Deposits","Usual presentation"],"rows":[["I","Minimal mesangial","Mesangial only, normal light microscopy","Normal urine or minimal findings"],["II","Mesangial proliferative","Mesangial","Mild hematuria or proteinuria"],["III","Focal (under 50 percent of glomeruli)","Subendothelial in affected segments","Nephritic, variable severity"],["IV","Diffuse (50 percent or more)","Subendothelial wire loops","Nephritic, often with nephrotic-range proteinuria"],["V","Membranous","Subepithelial","Nephrotic syndrome"],["VI","Advanced sclerosing (90 percent or more sclerosed)","Scarred glomeruli","Chronic kidney disease"]],"caption":"Classes III and IV are proliferative and nephritic; class V is nephrotic."},{"type":"p","text":"Serology tracks the mechanism. Antinuclear antibody is sensitive but not specific. Anti-dsDNA is highly specific for SLE, and its titer tends to rise with active nephritis because these antibodies form the complexes that injure the glomerulus. Classical pathway activation consumes both C3 and C4, so low complement with rising anti-dsDNA points to an active renal flare. Anti-Smith antibody is also specific for SLE but does not track activity. A low C4 helps separate lupus from PSGN, where C4 is usually normal."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C07","obj":"UR-12-A.7","title":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis (MPGN) is a pattern of injury rather than a single disease. Glomeruli are hypercellular and lobular. Mesangial cells and leukocytes extend into the capillary wall between the endothelium and the GBM (mesangial interposition), and the endothelium lays down a new layer of basement membrane inside the original one. Silver or PAS stains then show a double contour, the tram-track appearance. Because the injury is both proliferative and damages the barrier, MPGN may present as nephritic syndrome, nephrotic syndrome, or a mix, and it tends to progress slowly to renal failure."},{"type":"p","text":"Current classification uses immunofluorescence. In immune complex MPGN, immunoglobulin and C3 deposit together, mainly in subendothelial and mesangial sites, after chronic antigen exposure. Hepatitis C, often with mixed cryoglobulinemia, is the classic cause, and hepatitis B, chronic bacterial infections such as endocarditis, autoimmune disease, and monoclonal gammopathies are others. These complexes activate the classical pathway, so C3 and often C4 are low. This group largely corresponds to the older type I."},{"type":"p","text":"In C3 glomerulopathy, C3 deposits with little or no immunoglobulin because the alternative pathway is uncontrolled. Dense deposit disease (formerly type II MPGN) is the best-known form, with ribbon-like, very dense deposits within the lamina densa of the GBM. Most patients have C3 nephritic factor, an IgG autoantibody that binds the alternative pathway C3 convertase (C3bBb) and protects it from inactivation by factor H. The convertase then cleaves C3 continuously, so serum C3 is very low while C4 is normal. Genetic defects in factor H produce the same effect, and dense deposit disease is associated with partial lipodystrophy."},{"type":"table","title":"Two mechanisms behind the MPGN pattern","cols":["Feature","Immune complex MPGN","Dense deposit disease (C3 glomerulopathy)"],"rows":[["Immunofluorescence","Immunoglobulin and C3","C3 dominant, little immunoglobulin"],["Complement pathway","Classical","Alternative, uncontrolled"],["Serum C3, C4","C3 low, C4 often low","C3 very low, C4 normal"],["Electron microscopy","Subendothelial and mesangial deposits","Dense ribbon-like deposits in the lamina densa"],["Associations","Hepatitis C and B, cryoglobulinemia, chronic infection, SLE","C3 nephritic factor, factor H defects, partial lipodystrophy"]],"caption":"Both share mesangial interposition and tram-track basement membrane duplication."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C08","obj":"UR-12-A.8","title":"Diabetic nephropathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Diabetic nephropathy is the leading cause of end-stage kidney disease in developed countries, and it arises from hemodynamic and metabolic injury acting together. Early in diabetes, filtered glucose increases proximal sodium and glucose cotransport, so less sodium chloride reaches the macula densa. Tubuloglomerular feedback reads this as low flow and dilates the afferent arteriole, while angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole. Glomerular capillary pressure and GFR rise above normal (hyperfiltration), and the kidneys enlarge."},{"type":"p","text":"The metabolic arm is nonenzymatic glycation. Glucose binds proteins without enzymes and forms advanced glycation end products that cross-link collagen, stimulate matrix synthesis through TGF-beta, and trap plasma proteins. GBM thickening, detectable by electron microscopy within a few years of onset, is the earliest structural change. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis affects both the afferent and the efferent arteriole; efferent involvement is characteristic of diabetes and keeps glomerular pressure high. Mesangial matrix expands first diffusely and then forms round, PAS-positive nodules at the periphery of the tuft, the Kimmelstiel-Wilson lesion of nodular glomerulosclerosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Progression of diabetic nephropathy","steps":["Hyperglycemia raises proximal sodium and glucose reabsorption","Afferent dilation and efferent constriction cause hyperfiltration","Glycation end products thicken the GBM and expand mesangium","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of afferent and efferent arterioles","Moderately increased albuminuria, 30 to 300 mg/day","Overt then nephrotic-range proteinuria; Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules","Glomerulosclerosis, falling GFR, end-stage kidney disease"],"caption":"Hemodynamic stress and matrix accumulation reinforce each other; albuminuria is the first clinical sign."},{"type":"p","text":"Clinically, the first sign is moderately increased albuminuria (30 to 300 mg/day, traditionally called microalbuminuria), which a standard dipstick does not detect. Proteinuria then becomes overt and often reaches the nephrotic range, hypertension develops, and GFR declines after the period of hyperfiltration. The role of glomerular pressure is confirmed by the observation that blocking angiotensin II formation or action dilates the efferent arteriole, lowers glomerular pressure, and reduces albuminuria. Diabetes also predisposes to pyelonephritis and renal papillary necrosis, which add tubulointerstitial injury. Unlike most causes of chronic kidney disease, diabetic kidneys are often normal or large in size even when function is poor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C09","obj":"UR-12-A.9","title":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) is sclerosis of part of the tuft (segmental) in some glomeruli (focal). Juxtamedullary glomeruli are often affected first, so a small biopsy that samples only superficial cortex can miss the lesion. Its root cause is podocyte injury. Podocytes are terminally differentiated cells with very limited ability to replace themselves, so when they are lost, bare segments of GBM adhere to Bowman capsule, and those segments collapse and scar. Immunofluorescence is usually negative apart from nonspecific trapping of IgM and C3 in scarred areas, and electron microscopy shows foot process effacement."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary FSGS is thought to involve a circulating factor that injures podocytes. Genetic forms involve slit diaphragm and podocyte cytoskeletal proteins such as nephrin and podocin, and APOL1 risk variants, common in people of West African ancestry, help explain why FSGS is a leading cause of nephrotic syndrome in Black adults. Compared with minimal change disease, proteinuria is less selective, hematuria and hypertension are more common, response to corticosteroids is poor, and many patients progress to renal failure."},{"type":"p","text":"Secondary FSGS has several mechanisms. Obesity, sickle cell disease (with anemia-driven hyperfiltration), and loss of nephron mass overload the remaining glomeruli, as shown in the cycle below. Heroin use, especially in the past, is associated with FSGS, although adulterants may be responsible. HIV causes the collapsing variant, HIV-associated nephropathy, in which HIV infects podocytes and tubular cells and viral gene products make podocytes dedifferentiate and proliferate while the capillary tuft collapses. Tubules dilate into microcysts filled with casts, endothelial cells show tubuloreticular inclusions, the kidneys are large and echogenic, and heavy proteinuria progresses rapidly to end-stage disease. APOL1 variants also raise this risk."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Hyperfiltration and nephron loss","steps":["Nephrons are lost or demand rises (obesity, sickle cell)","Remaining glomeruli hyperfilter at high pressure","Podocytes stretch, detach, and are not replaced","Bare basement membrane adheres to Bowman capsule and scars","More nephrons are lost, increasing load on the rest"],"caption":"This adaptive mechanism explains secondary FSGS and the steady progression of many chronic kidney diseases once nephron mass falls."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C10","obj":"UR-12-A.10","title":"Consequences of nephrotic syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The complications of nephrotic syndrome follow from what the urine loses and how the liver responds. Edema has two components. Low albumin lowers plasma oncotic pressure, so fluid moves into the interstitium by Starling forces (underfill). In many patients the kidney also retains sodium directly, partly because filtered plasminogen is converted to plasmin, which cleaves and activates epithelial sodium channels in the collecting duct (overfill). The relative weight of these two mechanisms varies, and it explains why some nephrotic patients have normal or high plasma volume."},{"type":"p","text":"Hyperlipidemia arises because the liver responds to low oncotic pressure by increasing synthesis of lipoproteins, including apolipoprotein B-containing VLDL and LDL, while catabolism falls because lipoprotein lipase activity is reduced. Filtered lipid appears as oval fat bodies and fatty casts. Hypercoagulability comes from urinary loss of antithrombin III, a small protein that crosses the damaged filter, combined with increased hepatic synthesis of fibrinogen and other clotting factors and platelet hyperaggregability. Renal vein thrombosis is the classic result, most often in membranous nephropathy, and pulmonary embolism and deep vein thrombosis also occur."},{"type":"p","text":"Infection risk rises because IgG and complement factors such as factor B are lost in the urine, weakening opsonization of encapsulated bacteria. Children with nephrotic syndrome are therefore prone to Streptococcus pneumoniae peritonitis in ascitic fluid. Other carrier proteins are lost as well, including vitamin D-binding protein, thyroxine-binding globulin, and transferrin, which alters measured total hormone and iron levels."},{"type":"p","text":"Renal vein thrombosis presents with flank pain, hematuria, and a sudden increase in proteinuria. In men, a new left-sided varicocele may appear because the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanisms of nephrotic complications","cols":["Complication","Mechanism","Result"],"rows":[["Edema","Low oncotic pressure plus primary renal sodium retention","Generalized edema, ascites, pleural effusion"],["Hyperlipidemia","More hepatic lipoprotein synthesis, less lipoprotein lipase activity","High LDL and cholesterol; lipiduria, fatty casts"],["Hypercoagulability","Urinary antithrombin III loss; more hepatic clotting factors","Renal vein thrombosis, deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism"],["Infection","Urinary loss of IgG and factor B","Pneumococcal peritonitis, cellulitis"],["Carrier protein loss","Loss of vitamin D-binding protein, thyroxine-binding globulin, transferrin","Low measured total levels"]],"caption":"Each complication is either a urinary loss or a hepatic response to that loss."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If the liver increases synthesis of many proteins in nephrotic syndrome, why does serum albumin stay low while cholesterol rises?","explain":"Albumin is small enough to be lost in the urine nearly as fast as it is made, while large lipoproteins such as LDL are too big to cross even a damaged filter, so they accumulate. Reduced lipoprotein lipase activity adds to the rise.","check":"The paragraph on hyperlipidemia and hypercoagulability."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C11","obj":"UR-12-A.11","title":"Minimal change disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Minimal change disease (MCD) is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in young children, with a peak at about 2 to 6 years of age. Its name describes the biopsy. Light microscopy shows normal glomeruli, immunofluorescence shows no immune deposits, and only electron microscopy reveals the lesion: diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes. Proximal tubular cells may contain reabsorbed lipid, which gave the disease its older name, lipoid nephrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The cause is podocyte dysfunction without immune complex deposition. A circulating factor, linked to abnormal T-cell function, is thought to alter the podocyte and its slit diaphragm, and antibodies against nephrin have been reported in some patients. The classic teaching is that loss of the negatively charged polyanions of the filter removes charge selectivity, so the small, negatively charged albumin crosses while larger proteins such as IgG are largely held back. This selective albuminuria is typical of MCD and contrasts with the nonselective proteinuria of FSGS and other structural lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the podocytes are functionally altered rather than destroyed, the lesion is reversible. More than about 90 percent of children have complete remission of proteinuria with corticosteroids, and this response is itself a diagnostic feature that supports the immune basis of the disease. Hematuria, hypertension, and reduced GFR are uncommon, and progression to renal failure is rare. Relapses are common, however."},{"type":"p","text":"Secondary MCD is recognized in adults. The classic association is Hodgkin lymphoma, presumably through cytokines produced by the tumor and its reactive T cells, and nephrotic syndrome may resolve when the lymphoma is controlled. NSAIDs can cause MCD together with acute interstitial nephritis, and allergies and infections have been linked to relapses. When an adult presents with nephrotic syndrome and a biopsy shows only foot process effacement, these causes should be considered, and FSGS should be kept in mind because a small biopsy can miss segmental scars."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C12","obj":"UR-12-A.12","title":"Membranous nephropathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Membranous nephropathy is a major cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults, usually after age 40. In primary disease, IgG autoantibodies, mostly of the IgG4 subclass, bind phospholipase A2 receptor (PLA2R), a protein on the podocyte surface. About 70 to 80 percent of primary cases have anti-PLA2R antibodies, and a smaller group has antibodies to thrombospondin type-1 domain-containing 7A. The complexes form in situ beneath the podocyte, producing subepithelial deposits."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the deposits sit on the urinary side of the GBM, circulating leukocytes cannot reach them, and the disease is not inflammatory. Instead, complement is activated to the membrane attack complex (C5b-9), which inserts into the podocyte, injures its cytoskeleton and slit diaphragm, and causes heavy, nonselective proteinuria. Light microscopy shows uniformly thickened capillary walls without increased cellularity. The podocyte lays down new basement membrane between the deposits, which a silver stain shows as spikes, and the matrix later covers them (the spike and dome pattern). Immunofluorescence shows granular IgG and C3 along capillary walls, and electron microscopy shows the subepithelial deposits with effaced foot processes."},{"type":"p","text":"About a quarter of cases are secondary, with the deposits formed by other antigens. Causes include SLE (class V lupus nephritis), hepatitis B, solid tumors such as carcinomas of the lung, colon, and breast, and drugs including NSAIDs, penicillamine, and gold. Serum anti-PLA2R is usually negative in secondary disease, which helps separate the two groups. Membranous nephropathy carries the highest risk of renal vein thrombosis among the nephrotic diseases. Its course is variable: some patients remit, some have persistent proteinuria, and some progress to renal failure."},{"type":"table","title":"Biopsy findings in the main nephrotic lesions","cols":["Disease","Light microscopy","Immunofluorescence","Electron microscopy"],"rows":[["Minimal change disease","Normal","Negative","Diffuse foot process effacement"],["FSGS","Segmental sclerosis in some glomeruli","Negative or nonspecific IgM and C3 in scars","Foot process effacement"],["Membranous nephropathy","Thick capillary walls; spikes on silver stain","Granular IgG and C3","Subepithelial deposits"],["Diabetic nephropathy","Mesangial expansion; Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules","Linear IgG trapped along GBM (nonspecific)","Thick GBM, mesangial matrix increase"],["Amyloidosis","Acellular eosinophilic deposits; Congo red positive","Light chain (AL) or negative (AA)","Nonbranching fibrils about 8 to 10 nm wide"]],"caption":"Light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and electron microscopy each answer a different question: structure, immune content, and exact deposit location."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C13","obj":"UR-12-A.13","title":"Renal amyloidosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Amyloid is protein misfolded into a cross beta-pleated sheet that polymerizes into insoluble, nonbranching fibrils. The fibrils resist proteolysis and accumulate in the extracellular space. In the kidney they deposit first in the mesangium and along capillary walls and arterioles. Expanding deposits replace the normal matrix of the filter and separate the podocytes from the basement membrane, so the barrier fails and nephrotic-range proteinuria follows, usually with little hematuria. As deposits enlarge, capillary lumens are obliterated, glomeruli become acellular masses, and GFR falls. The kidneys are often normal or enlarged in size because the deposits add bulk."},{"type":"p","text":"Two forms dominate. AL amyloid is made of immunoglobulin light chains, more often lambda than kappa, produced by a clonal plasma cell population. It occurs in primary amyloidosis and in a minority of patients with multiple myeloma, and serum or urine immunofixation detects the monoclonal protein. AA amyloid is made from serum amyloid A, an acute phase protein that the liver produces under the influence of IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha. Chronic inflammation keeps serum amyloid A high, so AA amyloid follows rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease (especially Crohn disease), chronic osteomyelitis, tuberculosis, bronchiectasis, and familial Mediterranean fever."},{"type":"p","text":"The beta-pleated structure explains the stain. Congo red dye molecules align along the regular fibrils, so deposits appear salmon pink to red under ordinary light and show apple-green birefringence under polarized light. Electron microscopy shows randomly arranged nonbranching fibrils about 8 to 10 nm in diameter. Biopsy of abdominal fat or rectal mucosa can confirm systemic amyloidosis without a kidney biopsy."},{"type":"table","title":"AL and AA amyloid","cols":["Feature","AL amyloid","AA amyloid"],"rows":[["Precursor protein","Immunoglobulin light chain","Serum amyloid A"],["Source","Clonal plasma cells","Liver, driven by IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha"],["Settings","Primary amyloidosis, multiple myeloma","Rheumatoid arthritis, Crohn disease, chronic infection, familial Mediterranean fever"],["Shared findings","Congo red apple-green birefringence; nephrotic syndrome","Congo red apple-green birefringence; nephrotic syndrome"]],"caption":"The two forms differ in precursor protein but share the fibril structure and staining."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C14","obj":"UR-12-A.14","title":"Acute kidney injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute kidney injury (AKI) is an abrupt fall in GFR over hours to days, defined by consensus criteria as a rise in serum creatinine of at least 0.3 mg/dL within 48 hours, a rise to 1.5 times baseline within 7 days, or urine output below 0.5 mL/kg per hour for 6 hours. Reference values are about 0.6 to 1.2 mg/dL for creatinine and 7 to 20 mg/dL for BUN. Causes are grouped by where the problem lies."},{"type":"p","text":"Prerenal AKI is reduced renal perfusion, from hypovolemia, heart failure, sepsis with vasodilation, or cirrhosis, with kidneys that are structurally intact. The kidney responds appropriately: angiotensin II, aldosterone, and ADH drive avid sodium and water reabsorption, so urine sodium is low, FENa is under 1 percent, and urine osmolality is high (over about 500 mOsm/kg). Slow tubular flow and ADH-stimulated urea transport in the medullary collecting duct increase urea reabsorption, while creatinine is not reabsorbed, so the BUN to creatinine ratio exceeds 20. A diuretic given before sampling raises FENa despite low perfusion; fractional excretion of urea, under about 35 percent in prerenal states, is less affected."},{"type":"p","text":"Intrinsic AKI is damage to the kidney itself. The most common form is acute tubular necrosis, from prolonged ischemia or toxins such as aminoglycosides, radiocontrast, myoglobin, and ethylene glycol. Injured tubular cells cannot reabsorb sodium or concentrate the urine, so FENa exceeds 2 percent, urine osmolality approaches that of plasma (under about 350 mOsm/kg), and the BUN to creatinine ratio stays near the normal 10 to 15. Sloughed cells form muddy brown casts and obstruct tubules, and backleak of filtrate through the denuded epithelium further lowers effective GFR. Glomerulonephritis, interstitial nephritis, and vascular disease are other intrinsic causes."},{"type":"p","text":"Postrenal AKI is obstruction of urine outflow, such as prostatic enlargement, bilateral ureteral stones, or tumor. Back pressure in Bowman space opposes filtration, but total GFR falls enough to cause AKI only when both kidneys, or a single functioning kidney, are blocked. Imaging shows hydronephrosis. Early indices can resemble prerenal disease, but prolonged obstruction damages tubules and FENa rises."},{"type":"table","title":"Urinary indices in acute kidney injury","cols":["Index","Prerenal","Intrinsic (ATN)","Postrenal"],"rows":[["BUN to creatinine ratio","Over 20","Under about 15","Over 15 early, variable"],["FENa","Under 1 percent","Over 2 percent","Under 1 percent early, over 2 percent later"],["Urine osmolality (mOsm/kg)","Over about 500","Under about 350","Under about 350 when prolonged"],["Urine sodium (mEq/L)","Under 20","Over 40","Over 40 when prolonged"],["Sediment","Bland, hyaline casts","Muddy brown granular casts","Bland; possible hematuria from stones"]],"caption":"Prerenal indices show intact tubules conserving sodium and water; ATN indices show tubules that can do neither."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating FENa","text":"A man with vomiting has plasma Na 140 mEq/L, plasma creatinine 2.0 mg/dL, BUN 50 mg/dL, urine Na 10 mEq/L, and urine creatinine 100 mg/dL. FENa equals (urine Na times plasma creatinine) divided by (plasma Na times urine creatinine), times 100: (10 times 2.0) divided by (140 times 100) is 20 divided by 14,000, or about 0.14 percent. The BUN to creatinine ratio is 25. Both indices show tubules conserving sodium and urea, so this is prerenal AKI from volume loss. If the same patient later had FENa of 3 percent, a ratio of 12, and muddy brown casts, prolonged hypoperfusion would have progressed to acute tubular necrosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient in prerenal AKI received a loop diuretic an hour before the urine sample. What happens to FENa, and why?","explain":"Loop diuretics block sodium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb, so FENa rises above 1 percent even though the tubules are healthy and perfusion is low. The fractional excretion of urea, which is less affected by these diuretics, is more reliable; a value under about 35 percent suggests prerenal disease.","check":"The prerenal paragraph and the indices table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-01-C15","obj":"UR-12-A.15","title":"Chronic kidney disease and uremia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is a GFR below 60 mL/min per 1.73 m2, or markers of kidney damage such as albuminuria, persisting for more than 3 months. Diabetes is the leading cause and hypertension the second; glomerulonephritis, polycystic kidney disease, and chronic tubulointerstitial disease follow. Hypertension damages the kidney through hyaline arteriolosclerosis that narrows afferent arterioles and causes ischemic glomerulosclerosis (benign nephrosclerosis). Whatever the cause, nephron loss leads the survivors to hyperfilter, which drives further sclerosis. Most chronically damaged kidneys are small and scarred, with waxy casts in the sediment, while diabetes, amyloidosis, polycystic disease, and HIV-associated nephropathy keep the kidneys normal or large."},{"type":"p","text":"Uremia results from retained urea and other nitrogenous solutes, together with the loss of the kidney's endocrine and regulatory functions. Uremic toxins cause fibrinous pericarditis and encephalopathy, which ranges from poor concentration to asterixis, seizures, and coma. They also impair platelet adhesion and aggregation, in part by disrupting von Willebrand factor binding, so bleeding time is prolonged while the platelet count, PT, and PTT are normal. Anorexia, nausea, pruritus, and peripheral neuropathy are common."},{"type":"p","text":"Electrolyte disorders follow the failure of excretion. Potassium is secreted by the distal nephron, and when GFR falls very low the remaining nephrons cannot excrete the daily load, so serum potassium rises above the normal range of about 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L; acidosis adds to this by shifting potassium out of cells. Fewer nephrons produce less ammonia, so acid excretion falls, causing a normal anion gap acidosis at first and a high anion gap acidosis later as sulfate, phosphate, and urate are retained. Inability to excrete sodium causes volume overload, edema, and hypertension, and the hypertension is worsened by renin release from ischemic nephrons. Anemia is normocytic and results mainly from erythropoietin deficiency, because the peritubular interstitial cells that produce it are lost to fibrosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Renal osteodystrophy and secondary hyperparathyroidism","steps":["Falling GFR retains phosphate","FGF23 rises and inhibits 1-alpha-hydroxylase","Loss of renal mass further lowers calcitriol","Less intestinal calcium absorption; serum calcium falls","Low calcium, high phosphate, and low calcitriol stimulate PTH","Secondary hyperparathyroidism increases bone resorption","Osteitis fibrosa cystica, osteomalacia, vascular calcification"],"caption":"Phosphate retention and calcitriol deficiency together drive parathyroid hyperplasia and the bone and mineral disorder of CKD."},{"type":"table","title":"Consequences of chronic kidney disease","cols":["Consequence","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Uremic pericarditis and encephalopathy","Retained nitrogenous toxins"],["Bleeding tendency","Uremic platelet dysfunction; normal count, prolonged bleeding time"],["Hyperkalemia","Too few nephrons to secrete potassium; acidosis shifts potassium out of cells"],["Metabolic acidosis","Less ammonia production; retained sulfate and phosphate"],["Volume overload, hypertension","Sodium retention plus renin from ischemic nephrons"],["Anemia","Erythropoietin deficiency"],["Renal osteodystrophy","Phosphate retention, low calcitriol, secondary hyperparathyroidism"]],"caption":"Each consequence reflects a lost excretory, regulatory, or endocrine function of the kidney."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Glomerular disease is applied immunology on a known anatomy. The filter has three layers, and the location of injury on that structure predicts the clinical syndrome: mesangial and subendothelial deposits face the blood, recruit leukocytes, and cause nephritic disease, while subepithelial deposits and pure podocyte injury cause nephrotic disease. Complement links the immunology to the laboratory: classical pathway activation (lupus, immune complex MPGN) lowers C3 and C4, alternative pathway activation (PSGN, dense deposit disease) lowers C3 alone, and local mesangial activation (IgA nephropathy) leaves both normal. Microbiology supplies many of the antigens, including streptococcal proteins, hepatitis B and C, and HIV, and biochemistry explains diabetic glycation and amyloid fibril structure."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology carries these lesions to renal failure. Lost filtering surface lowers GFR, intact or failing tubules determine the urinary indices in AKI, and surviving nephrons hyperfilter until they too scar, which is why many different diseases converge on the same CKD picture. These rules have limits. Many diseases cross the nephritic and nephrotic boundary, numerical cutoffs for FENa, urine osmolality, and the BUN to creatinine ratio are guides that diuretics, contrast, and myoglobin can distort, and the definitive diagnosis of most glomerular diseases still requires biopsy with all three methods of microscopy."}],"summary":["Nephritic syndrome is inflammation with hematuria, RBC casts, low GFR, hypertension, and proteinuria under 3.5 g/day; nephrotic syndrome is barrier failure with proteinuria over 3.5 g/day, low albumin, edema, hyperlipidemia, and lipiduria.","Mesangial and subendothelial deposits recruit leukocytes and cause nephritic disease; subepithelial deposits and podocyte injury cause nephrotic disease.","A cast proves its contents came from the tubule: RBC casts mean glomerulonephritis, WBC casts interstitial inflammation, muddy brown casts ATN, fatty casts nephrotic syndrome, and waxy casts CKD.","PSGN appears weeks after infection with low C3 and subepithelial humps; IgA nephropathy appears within days with normal C3 and mesangial IgA.","RPGN is defined by crescents; immunofluorescence divides it into linear anti-GBM, granular immune complex, and pauci-immune ANCA types.","Class IV lupus nephritis has subendothelial wire loops and is nephritic; class V is membranous and nephrotic; low C3 and C4 with rising anti-dsDNA signal activity.","MPGN shows tram-track GBM duplication; immune complex MPGN links to hepatitis C and B, and dense deposit disease links to C3 nephritic factor with very low C3.","Diabetic nephropathy combines hyperfiltration, glycation, and efferent hyaline arteriolosclerosis, progressing from albuminuria to Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules and renal failure.","MCD is selective albuminuria with normal light microscopy and steroid response; FSGS is podocyte loss with scarring and poor response; membranous nephropathy is anti-PLA2R subepithelial disease; amyloid shows apple-green birefringence.","Prerenal AKI conserves sodium (FENa under 1 percent, BUN to creatinine over 20); ATN cannot (FENa over 2 percent); CKD causes uremia, hyperkalemia, acidosis, volume overload, anemia, and renal osteodystrophy."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Kidney diseases: acute kidney injury and chronic kidney disease"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Glomerular filtration and renal blood flow"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity disorders and the complement system"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal Disease"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg Medical Microbiology","where":"Streptococci, poststreptococcal diseases"}]},"UR-12-B":{"unit":"UR-12-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-02","title":"Tubulointerstitial Disease: Acute Tubular Necrosis and Fanconi Syndrome","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the renal tubule fails. Acute tubular necrosis (ATN) is the most common cause of intrinsic acute kidney injury, and it follows either loss of perfusion or direct toxic injury to tubular cells. Fanconi syndrome is a generalized failure of proximal tubule transport that spills glucose, amino acids, phosphate, and bicarbonate into the urine. Board questions give a setting (shock, crush injury, a new drug, a child with rickets) and a urine or serum pattern, then ask for the segment, the mechanism, the phase, or the cause. If you can explain why the outer medulla is vulnerable and why proximal transporters fail together, the lab patterns follow.","prerequisites":["Nephron segments and their transporters, especially the proximal tubule and thick ascending limb (UR-2, UR-6)","Renal blood flow, medullary oxygen supply, and tubuloglomerular feedback (UR-5)","Renal handling of sodium, potassium, phosphate, and bicarbonate (UR-6, UR-7)","Anion gap and the classification of metabolic acidosis (UR-7)","Cell injury from ATP depletion and oxidative stress (general pathology)"],"connections":[{"to":"Glomerular diseases (UR-12-A)","how":"Prerenal azotemia, ATN, and glomerular disease are separated by urine sediment, FENa, and the BUN to creatinine ratio."},{"to":"Renal circulation (UR-5)","how":"Countercurrent oxygen exchange in the vasa recta keeps the outer medulla hypoxic, which is why its segments die first in ischemia."},{"to":"Acid-base and potassium regulation (UR-7)","how":"ATN produces an anion gap acidosis with hyperkalemia, while Fanconi syndrome produces a normal anion gap acidosis with potassium wasting."},{"to":"Cardiovascular conduction (CV-7)","how":"Hyperkalemia from oliguric ATN depolarizes cardiac cells, giving peaked T waves, a wide QRS, and fatal arrhythmias."},{"to":"Urinary genetics and biochemistry (UR-8, UR-9)","how":"Cystinosis, Wilson disease, and ethylene glycol metabolism link inborn errors and toxic metabolites to tubular failure."}],"terms":[{"term":"Acute kidney injury (AKI)","def":"An abrupt fall in GFR over hours to days, recognized by a rising serum creatinine, a falling urine output, or both."},{"term":"Acute tubular necrosis (ATN)","def":"Acute kidney injury caused by injury and death of tubular epithelial cells, from ischemia or nephrotoxins."},{"term":"Oliguria","def":"Urine output below about 400 mL/day in an adult (or below about 0.5 mL/kg/h), too little to excrete the daily solute load."},{"term":"Muddy brown granular cast","def":"A pigmented cast of degenerating tubular cells and debris in a uromodulin matrix, characteristic of ATN."},{"term":"Uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein)","def":"A glycoprotein secreted by the thick ascending limb that forms the matrix of all urinary casts."},{"term":"Fractional excretion of sodium (FENa)","def":"The percentage of filtered sodium that appears in the urine, calculated as (urine Na x plasma creatinine) / (plasma Na x urine creatinine) x 100."},{"term":"Tubuloglomerular feedback","def":"Afferent arteriolar constriction triggered when the macula densa senses increased NaCl delivery, lowering GFR of that nephron."},{"term":"Tubulorrhexis","def":"Rupture of the tubular basement membrane, typical of ischemic rather than toxic tubular necrosis."},{"term":"Rhabdomyolysis","def":"Breakdown of skeletal muscle that releases myoglobin, potassium, phosphate, urate, and creatine kinase into plasma."},{"term":"Fanconi syndrome","def":"Generalized proximal tubule dysfunction with urinary loss of glucose, amino acids, phosphate, bicarbonate, urate, and low-molecular-weight proteins."},{"term":"Proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis","def":"Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis caused by a lowered proximal threshold for bicarbonate reabsorption."},{"term":"Cystinosis","def":"Autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disease caused by loss of the cystine transporter cystinosin, the most common inherited cause of Fanconi syndrome in children."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-02-C01","obj":"UR-12-B.1","title":"Ischemic and nephrotoxic acute tubular necrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"ATN is acute kidney injury caused by damage to tubular epithelial cells. Despite the name, much of the injury is sublethal: many cells lose function and detach without dying, which is why the lesion can recover completely. The two causes are ischemia (shock, sepsis, hemorrhage, or a prolonged prerenal state) and nephrotoxins, which reach the tubule through filtration and uptake."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the injury concentrates in two segments"},{"type":"p","text":"The straight part of the proximal tubule (the S3 segment) and the medullary thick ascending limb both lie in the outer medulla. Countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta lets oxygen diffuse from descending to ascending vessels, so oxygen tension in this region is low even in health. Both segments also carry heavy ATP-dependent transport: the proximal tubule reabsorbs about two thirds of filtered sodium and relies on oxidative phosphorylation with little glycolytic reserve, and the thick ascending limb drives NaCl uptake through the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter powered by basolateral Na/K-ATPase. High demand with marginal supply means these cells fail first when perfusion falls. The proximal tubule is also the main target of toxins because it takes up filtered substances by endocytosis and organic ion transporters."},{"type":"flow","title":"From ischemia to a falling GFR","steps":["Renal blood flow falls in shock or sepsis","ATP falls in S3 and thick ascending limb cells","Cytoskeleton breaks down; Na/K-ATPase moves to apical membrane","Cells lose integrin attachment and slough into lumen","Debris and uromodulin form casts that obstruct tubules","Filtrate leaks back across denuded tubular wall","More NaCl at macula densa constricts afferent arterioles","GFR falls; oliguria and azotemia follow"],"caption":"Loss of polarity stops vectorial sodium transport; obstruction, back leak, and vasoconstriction together lower GFR. Endothelial injury and inflammation add further vasoconstriction."},{"type":"p","text":"GFR falls by three linked mechanisms. Casts raise intratubular pressure and oppose filtration. Filtrate leaks back into the interstitium through the bare basement membrane. Because injured proximal cells reabsorb less sodium, more NaCl reaches the macula densa, and tubuloglomerular feedback constricts the afferent arteriole. Endothelial injury favors endothelin over nitric oxide, adding vasoconstriction."},{"type":"table","title":"Ischemic versus nephrotoxic ATN","cols":["Feature","Ischemic","Nephrotoxic"],"rows":[["Typical setting","Shock, sepsis, hemorrhage, major surgery, prolonged prerenal state","Pigments, aminoglycosides, contrast, cisplatin, ethylene glycol"],["Distribution","Patchy, focal necrosis at many points along the nephron, with skip areas","More extensive and continuous, mainly in the proximal tubule"],["Basement membrane","Often ruptured (tubulorrhexis)","Usually intact"],["Regeneration","Slower where the basement membrane is lost","Usually orderly along the intact scaffold"],["Urine sediment","Muddy brown granular casts, tubular epithelial cells","Same, plus agent clues (pigment casts, oxalate crystals)"]],"caption":"Both forms share the cast and the high FENa; the difference lies in the pattern of necrosis and the integrity of the basement membrane."},{"type":"p","text":"Sloughed cells and debris become trapped in uromodulin secreted by the thick ascending limb, forming pigmented, muddy brown granular casts in the distal tubule and collecting duct. Their appearance in the sediment is direct evidence of tubular injury, which separates ATN from prerenal azotemia, where the sediment is bland or shows only hyaline casts. Red cell casts point to glomerulonephritis and white cell casts to interstitial inflammation or pyelonephritis, so the cast type localizes the lesion."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If hypotension lowered oxygen delivery to the whole kidney equally, why would the glomeruli and cortical collecting ducts survive while the S3 segment dies?","explain":"Survival depends on the balance of oxygen supply and demand. The S3 segment and medullary thick ascending limb sit in the hypoxic outer medulla and spend large amounts of ATP on transport; glomeruli filter passively and cortical segments have richer oxygen supply and lower demand.","check":"The paragraph on why injury concentrates in two segments."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-02-C02","obj":"UR-12-B.2","title":"The three phases of acute tubular necrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"ATN follows the life cycle of the tubular cell: injury, a period while the tubule is denuded, and regeneration. Each phase has a characteristic urine output and electrolyte pattern because each reflects how much filtration and tubular transport remain."},{"type":"table","title":"Phases of ATN","cols":["Phase","Approximate duration","Urine output","Main problems and mechanism"],"rows":[["Initiation","About 36 hours","Begins to fall","The ischemic or toxic insult; GFR starts to drop and creatinine begins to rise"],["Maintenance","Days to a few weeks (often 1 to 3 weeks)","Oliguria, or normal volume in nonoliguric ATN","Low GFR: rising BUN and creatinine, hyperkalemia, anion gap metabolic acidosis, volume overload, hyperphosphatemia, uremia"],["Recovery","Days to weeks","Rising, often polyuric (up to about 3 L/day)","Regenerating tubules cannot yet reabsorb: loss of water, sodium, and potassium; hypokalemia and volume depletion; susceptibility to infection"]],"caption":"Hyperkalemia belongs to maintenance and hypokalemia to recovery. Creatinine lags behind GFR, so it keeps rising early in recovery."},{"type":"h","text":"Maintenance: why each abnormality appears"},{"type":"p","text":"With GFR low and the tubules obstructed, the kidney cannot excrete its daily load of potassium, acid, salt, and water. Potassium accumulates because filtration and distal flow are low, and acidosis shifts potassium out of cells in exchange for hydrogen ions; tissue breakdown in sepsis or crush injury adds more. Hyperkalemia (above 5.0 mEq/L; reference about 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L) is the most dangerous abnormality. It speeds repolarization (peaked T waves) and partially depolarizes cardiac cells, inactivating fast Na+ channels so conduction slows (widened QRS) and risking ventricular fibrillation or asystole."},{"type":"p","text":"Metabolic acidosis arises because the injured kidney excretes too little ammonium and titratable acid and retains sulfate, phosphate, and other unmeasured anions, so the anion gap rises. Retained sodium and water expand extracellular volume, causing edema, hypertension, and pulmonary edema. Retained phosphate lowers ionized calcium, and urea and other nitrogenous wastes accumulate (azotemia), producing uremic symptoms if the phase is prolonged."},{"type":"flow","title":"Recovery phase polyuria","steps":["Surviving cells proliferate and reline the basement membrane","GFR returns before transport capacity matures","Retained urea is filtered and acts as an osmotic diuretic","Medullary solute gradient has washed out","Immature cells reabsorb little sodium, water, or potassium","Polyuria with sodium and potassium loss"],"caption":"Filtration recovers faster than tubular reabsorption, so a large filtered load passes through a tubule that cannot yet reclaim it."},{"type":"p","text":"Nonoliguric ATN, common with aminoglycosides and some other nephrotoxins, still lowers GFR, but enough tubular reabsorption is lost that urine volume stays normal. It generally reflects less severe injury. Normal urine volume therefore does not exclude ATN; the rising creatinine and the sediment establish it."},{"type":"table","title":"Prerenal azotemia versus ATN","cols":["Finding","Prerenal azotemia","ATN"],"rows":[["FENa","Below 1%","Above 2%"],["Urine sodium","Below about 20 mEq/L","Above about 40 mEq/L"],["Urine osmolality","Above about 500 mOsm/kg","About 300 to 350 mOsm/kg (near plasma)"],["BUN to creatinine ratio","Above about 20:1","Below about 15:1"],["Sediment","Bland or hyaline casts","Muddy brown granular casts, tubular cells"]],"caption":"Intact prerenal tubules avidly reabsorb sodium, water, and urea; necrotic tubules cannot, so urine approaches plasma in concentration."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating and interpreting FENa","text":"A man becomes oliguric two days after septic shock. Plasma sodium is 135 mEq/L, plasma creatinine 4.0 mg/dL, urine sodium 60 mEq/L, and urine creatinine 40 mg/dL. FENa = (urine Na x plasma creatinine) / (plasma Na x urine creatinine) x 100 = (60 x 4.0) / (135 x 40) x 100 = 240 / 5400 x 100, or about 4.4%. A value above 2% means the tubules are wasting sodium despite low perfusion, so this is ATN, not prerenal azotemia. Had the tubules been intact, aldosterone and angiotensin II would have driven FENa below 1%. Note that a loop or thiazide diuretic raises FENa on its own, and early pigment or contrast injury can give a low FENa, so the value is read with the sediment."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-02-C03","obj":"UR-12-B.3","title":"Nephrotoxic causes and the heme pigment dipstick","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Nephrotoxins injure the proximal tubule because it concentrates filtered substances: it reabsorbs most of the filtrate, takes up proteins and cationic drugs by megalin-mediated endocytosis, and secretes organic ions through basolateral transporters."},{"type":"table","title":"Nephrotoxins that cause ATN","cols":["Toxin","Setting","Mechanism of tubular injury","Clue"],"rows":[["Myoglobin","Rhabdomyolysis: crush injury, extreme exertion, seizures, immobilization, some drugs","Heme iron oxidant injury, obstructing pigment casts, and nitric oxide scavenging with vasoconstriction","Dark urine, dipstick blood with few RBCs, very high CK"],["Hemoglobin","Intravascular hemolysis, such as an ABO-incompatible transfusion","Same heme mechanisms once haptoglobin is saturated","Pink plasma, low haptoglobin, high LDH, dipstick blood with few RBCs"],["Aminoglycosides","Antibiotic courses, worse with volume depletion or kidney disease","Megalin uptake into proximal cells and accumulation in lysosomes, which rupture","Onset after about 5 to 7 days, often nonoliguric, magnesium and potassium wasting"],["Iodinated contrast","Imaging; highest risk with diabetic nephropathy, chronic kidney disease, or volume depletion","Medullary vasoconstriction and hypoxia plus direct tubular toxicity","Creatinine rises within 24 to 48 hours, peaks at about 3 to 5 days"],["Cisplatin","Chemotherapy","Organic cation uptake into S3 cells; DNA and oxidant injury","Hypomagnesemia from tubular magnesium wasting"],["Ethylene glycol","Antifreeze ingestion","Alcohol dehydrogenase begins its oxidation to glycolic acid and oxalate; oxalate precipitates as calcium oxalate in tubules","High anion gap acidosis, high osmolal gap, envelope or needle-shaped crystals, low calcium"]],"caption":"Drugs appear here only as causes of tubular injury."},{"type":"h","text":"Pigment nephropathy"},{"type":"p","text":"Myoglobin is a small monomer (about 17 kDa) that circulates largely unbound, so it is filtered and cleared quickly and plasma stays clear. Hemoglobin is normally bound to haptoglobin, which keeps it out of the filtrate; once haptoglobin is saturated, free hemoglobin dissociates into dimers small enough to be filtered, and the plasma turns pink. Both pigments injure the tubule the same way. Heme iron catalyzes free radical formation and lipid peroxidation in proximal cells, heme precipitates with uromodulin in acidic, concentrated urine to form obstructing casts, and free heme scavenges nitric oxide, constricting renal vessels. Volume depletion from fluid sequestered in injured muscle adds an ischemic component."},{"type":"p","text":"Rhabdomyolysis also releases intracellular contents: potassium, phosphate, urate, organic acids, and creatine kinase (CK), which is typically more than five times the upper reference limit and often far higher. Early hypocalcemia occurs because calcium phosphate deposits in damaged muscle; calcium may later rise above normal as these deposits mobilize during recovery."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rhabdomyolysis to a heme-positive dipstick","steps":["Muscle injury releases myoglobin, potassium, phosphate, and CK","Myoglobin is freely filtered at the glomerulus","Heme iron injures proximal tubule cells","Pigment casts obstruct; nitric oxide loss constricts vessels","Urine is red-brown; dipstick reads heme","Microscopy shows few or no red cells"],"caption":"The dipstick reacts to heme, not intact cells."},{"type":"p","text":"The dipstick blood pad uses the peroxidase-like activity of heme to oxidize a chromogen. It cannot tell whether heme comes from intact red cells, free hemoglobin, or myoglobin. Microscopy settles the question: hematuria shows red cells (3 or more per high-power field), whereas pigmenturia shows few or none. Plasma color then separates the two pigments."},{"type":"table","title":"Interpreting a dipstick positive for blood","cols":["Source","RBCs on microscopy","Plasma","Supporting findings"],"rows":[["Hematuria","Present","Clear","Red cell casts if glomerular"],["Hemoglobinuria","Few or none","Pink","Low haptoglobin, high LDH and indirect bilirubin"],["Myoglobinuria","Few or none","Clear","Very high CK, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia"]],"caption":"Microscopy separates cells from pigment; plasma color separates hemoglobin from myoglobin."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A runner collapses after a race with cola-colored urine. The dipstick is 3+ for blood but microscopy shows 0 to 2 RBCs per high-power field, and the plasma is clear. What pigment is present and why is the plasma not pink?","explain":"Myoglobin. The dipstick detects heme, not cells. Small, poorly bound myoglobin is filtered and cleared before it can color plasma; free hemoglobin beyond haptoglobin capacity accumulates and turns plasma pink.","check":"The pigment nephropathy paragraphs and the dipstick table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-02-C04","obj":"UR-12-B.4","title":"Fanconi syndrome: generalized proximal tubule failure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fanconi syndrome is generalized dysfunction of the proximal tubule, the segment that reclaims nearly all filtered glucose and amino acids, most filtered phosphate, and about 80% of filtered bicarbonate. Its transporters share two requirements. Most are sodium-coupled secondary active transporters driven by the low intracellular sodium that basolateral Na/K-ATPase creates, and that pump depends on ATP from the cell's abundant mitochondria. The tubule also recycles filtered low-molecular-weight proteins through megalin and cubilin endocytosis. Anything that depletes ATP, damages mitochondria, or clogs the lysosomal and endocytic system therefore disables many transporters at once."},{"type":"table","title":"What the proximal tubule loses and the consequence","cols":["Substance lost","Normal transport","Consequence"],"rows":[["Glucose","SGLT2 and SGLT1 with sodium","Glucosuria with normal plasma glucose"],["Amino acids","Sodium-coupled amino acid transporters","Generalized aminoaciduria"],["Phosphate","NaPi-IIa cotransport","Hypophosphatemia; rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults"],["Bicarbonate","Apical Na+/H+ exchange (NHE3) with carbonic anhydrase","Proximal (type 2) RTA, normal anion gap acidosis"],["Urate","Proximal urate reabsorption","Hypouricemia"],["Low-molecular-weight proteins","Megalin and cubilin endocytosis","Tubular proteinuria (for example beta-2 microglobulin)"],["Water and potassium (indirectly)","Proximal solute reabsorption; distal sodium delivery","Polyuria, dehydration, hypokalemia"]],"caption":"Isolated losses suggest a single transporter defect instead."},{"type":"p","text":"Glucose spills despite normal plasma glucose because the tubule's maximum reabsorptive capacity has fallen. In diabetes mellitus the tubule is normal and glucose appears only when the filtered load exceeds the renal threshold of about 180 to 200 mg/dL."},{"type":"p","text":"Bicarbonate loss explains the acidosis. When the proximal threshold falls, filtered bicarbonate above it escapes into the urine and serum bicarbonate declines, usually settling at about 12 to 20 mEq/L (reference about 22 to 28 mEq/L). Once the filtered load drops below the new threshold, the intact distal nephron can acidify the urine below pH 5.5. Chloride is retained in place of bicarbonate, so the acidosis is hyperchloremic with a normal anion gap. Potassium is wasted because extra sodium and bicarbonate reach the collecting duct, raising flow and lumen negativity, and aldosterone released in response to volume loss increases secretion further."},{"type":"flow","title":"From proximal injury to bone disease","steps":["Toxin, crystal, or metabolite injures proximal cells","ATP and sodium-coupled transport fail","Phosphate is lost in urine","Proximal 1-alpha-hydroxylase fails; calcitriol low or inappropriately normal","Chronic acidosis releases bone buffer","Rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults"],"caption":"Phosphate wasting, low calcitriol, and acidosis together impair bone mineralization."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of Fanconi syndrome","cols":["Category","Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Inherited","Cystinosis (autosomal recessive, CTNS gene)","Cystine accumulates and crystallizes in lysosomes; most common inherited cause in children"],["Inherited","Wilson disease","Copper accumulates in proximal tubule cells"],["Inherited","Galactosemia, hereditary fructose intolerance, tyrosinemia type 1","Accumulated metabolites impair proximal cell energy metabolism"],["Acquired","Multiple myeloma","Filtered light chains are endocytosed and crystallize in proximal cells"],["Acquired","Heavy metals (lead, cadmium, mercury)","Mitochondrial and transporter injury"],["Acquired","Drugs (tenofovir, ifosfamide, outdated tetracycline)","Mitochondrial toxicity or toxic degradation products"]],"caption":"Child: think inborn error, especially cystinosis. Adult: think myeloma, heavy metals, or drugs."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a Fanconi pattern","text":"A 2-year-old has growth failure, polyuria, and bowed legs. Plasma glucose is 85 mg/dL but urine glucose is positive. Serum sodium is 138, chloride 112, and bicarbonate 15 mEq/L, so the anion gap is 138 minus (112 + 15) = 11, which is normal (reference about 8 to 12). Serum phosphate and potassium are low, urine amino acids are increased, and a morning urine pH is 5.2. Glucosuria with normal plasma glucose and aminoaciduria point to the proximal tubule. A normal anion gap acidosis with acidic urine at steady state fits proximal RTA, because the distal nephron still acidifies once bicarbonate falls below the lowered threshold. Phosphate wasting explains the rickets, and cystinosis is the leading cause at this age."},{"type":"p","text":"Single-transporter disorders must be kept apart. Familial renal glucosuria (SGLT2 defect) spills only glucose, cystinuria loses only cystine and the dibasic amino acids and forms stones, and Hartnup disease loses only neutral amino acids; generalized proximal failure requires the combination."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology explain where the tubule fails. Countercurrent oxygen exchange that serves concentration also leaves the outer medulla hypoxic, so the S3 segment and thick ascending limb die first in ischemia, and the proximal tubule's endocytic and secretory machinery makes it the target of filtered toxins, light chains, and metals. Biochemistry links the two conditions: both disable energy-dependent sodium-coupled transport in tubular cells, acutely and often with cell death in ATN, chronically and usually without it in Fanconi syndrome. Ethylene glycol metabolism, lysosomal cystine storage, and copper accumulation link toxic and inborn chemistry to the same cell."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and the laboratory tie mechanism to diagnosis. Casts show where injury occurred, FENa shows whether tubules still reclaim sodium, the anion gap separates retained acid (ATN) from lost bicarbonate (Fanconi), and the dipstick with microscopy separates cells from pigment. These tests have limits: FENa can mislead with diuretics or early pigment injury, creatinine lags behind GFR, and dipstick heme never identifies its source."}],"summary":["ATN is the most common cause of intrinsic acute kidney injury, caused by ischemia or nephrotoxins.","The S3 proximal tubule and medullary thick ascending limb are most vulnerable because they combine heavy ATP-dependent transport with low outer medullary oxygen.","GFR falls through cast obstruction, back leak of filtrate, and afferent constriction from tubuloglomerular feedback; muddy brown granular casts are the signature.","Ischemic ATN is patchy with basement membrane rupture; nephrotoxic ATN is more continuous along the proximal tubule with the basement membrane intact.","ATN has FENa above 2%, urine osmolality near plasma, and a BUN to creatinine ratio below about 15; prerenal azotemia has FENa below 1%.","Maintenance phase brings oliguria, hyperkalemia (the main lethal threat), anion gap acidosis, and volume overload; recovery brings polyuria with sodium, potassium, and water loss.","Heme pigments give a dipstick positive for blood without red cells; plasma is clear in myoglobinuria and pink in hemoglobinuria.","Aminoglycoside injury appears after about a week and is often nonoliguric; contrast raises creatinine within 1 to 2 days; cisplatin wastes magnesium; ethylene glycol leaves calcium oxalate crystals.","Fanconi syndrome is generalized proximal failure: glucosuria with normal plasma glucose, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and bicarbonaturia with a normal anion gap proximal RTA.","Cystinosis is the leading inherited cause of Fanconi syndrome in children; myeloma light chains, heavy metals, and drugs such as tenofovir cause it in adults."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, section on acute tubular injury"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Renal tubular reabsorption and secretion; acute kidney injury and chronic kidney disease"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of organic solutes and acid-base handling by the proximal tubule"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal Disease, acute kidney injury"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Metabolism of monosaccharides and amino acids (galactosemia, fructose intolerance, tyrosinemia)"}]},"UR-12-C":{"unit":"UR-12-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-03","title":"Obstructive Urinary Disease: Hydronephrosis and Renal Calculi","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains what happens when urine cannot leave the kidney. It covers the causes of hydronephrosis at each age, how back pressure lowers filtration and destroys the papillae and cortex, why one blocked kidney can go unnoticed while two cause acute kidney injury, and why relief can be followed by a large diuresis. It then explains how urine chemistry, especially pH, decides which kidney stone forms, and how a moving stone produces renal colic, obstruction, and infection. Board questions usually give an age, a sex, a urine pH, a crystal, or a radiograph and ask for the cause, stone type, or complication, so each topic is taught from the mechanism outward.","prerequisites":["Starling forces across the glomerular capillary","Countercurrent concentration and ADH action on aquaporin-2","Anatomy of the kidney, ureter, bladder, and male urethra","Urinary acidification and ammonium excretion","Calcium regulation by PTH and vitamin D"],"connections":[{"to":"Acute tubular necrosis (UR-12-B)","how":"Postrenal AKI must be separated from intrinsic tubular injury, and long obstruction itself injures tubules."},{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Urease-producing bacteria such as Proteus create the alkaline urine that precipitates struvite."},{"to":"Endocrinology","how":"Primary hyperparathyroidism causes hypercalciuria and calcium stones."},{"to":"Biochemistry","how":"Purine breakdown to uric acid, ethylene glycol metabolism to oxalate, and the dibasic amino acid transporter explain three stone types."},{"to":"Reproductive pathology","how":"Prostatic enlargement and cervical carcinoma are major causes of obstruction in older adults."}],"terms":[{"term":"Hydronephrosis","def":"Dilation of the renal pelvis and calyces, with parenchymal atrophy when caused by obstruction to outflow."},{"term":"Obstructive uropathy","def":"Structural or functional damage to the kidney caused by obstruction of urine flow."},{"term":"Postrenal acute kidney injury","def":"An abrupt fall in total GFR caused by obstruction of both upper tracts, a solitary kidney, or the bladder outlet."},{"term":"Postobstructive diuresis","def":"A large output of urine, salt, and water after relief of bilateral obstruction."},{"term":"Supersaturation","def":"A solute concentration above its solubility limit, allowing crystals to nucleate and grow."},{"term":"Hypercalciuria","def":"Excess urinary calcium excretion, the most common metabolic abnormality in calcium stone formers."},{"term":"Struvite","def":"Magnesium ammonium phosphate, which precipitates in alkaline urine produced by urease-producing bacteria."},{"term":"Staghorn calculus","def":"A branched stone that fills the renal pelvis and extends into several calyces."},{"term":"Cystinuria","def":"An autosomal recessive defect in proximal tubular and intestinal transport of cystine and the dibasic amino acids."},{"term":"Renal colic","def":"Severe flank pain radiating toward the groin caused by acute distension of the ureter and pelvis, usually by a stone."},{"term":"Pyonephrosis","def":"Pus within an obstructed, infected renal collecting system."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-03-C01","obj":"UR-12-C.1","title":"Causes of hydronephrosis and how obstruction destroys the kidney","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hydronephrosis is dilation of the renal pelvis and calyces; when caused by obstruction it brings progressive parenchymal atrophy. A block at or below the bladder neck dilates both upper tracts, while a block at the ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) or along one ureter dilates only the kidney above it. Vesicoureteral reflux widens the collecting system without obstruction, and the partly compressive dilation of pregnancy rarely damages the kidney."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of hydronephrosis by age and sex","cols":["Who","Cause","Level and side","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Male fetus and newborn","Posterior urethral valves","Prostatic urethra; bilateral, with a thick-walled distended bladder","Mucosal folds block outflow; severe cases cause oligohydramnios and pulmonary hypoplasia (Potter sequence)"],["Fetus, infant, child (more often boys, left side)","UPJ obstruction, the most common congenital cause","UPJ; usually unilateral","Aperistaltic proximal ureteral segment or crossing lower pole vessel"],["Young and middle-aged adults","Calculi","One ureter, at a natural narrowing","Stone lodges in the lumen"],["Pregnant women","Physiologic hydronephrosis","Both ureters, right more than left","Progesterone relaxes ureteral muscle; the dextrorotated uterus compresses the right ureter at the pelvic brim, and the sigmoid colon cushions the left"],["Older men","Benign prostatic hyperplasia, prostatic carcinoma","Bladder outlet; bilateral","Urethral compression; bladder wall thickens before the upper tracts dilate"],["Women, mainly middle-aged and older","Pelvic tumors, especially cervical carcinoma","Distal ureters; one or both","Compression or invasion of the ureters"],["Any age","Neurogenic bladder, strictures, retroperitoneal fibrosis","Variable","Failed emptying or encasement"]],"caption":"Each age and sex group carries its own anatomic risk."},{"type":"h","text":"Why back pressure lowers GFR"},{"type":"p","text":"Filtration continues when outflow is blocked; filtrate escapes into the interstitium, lymphatics, and veins, so the pelvis keeps distending. Pressure is transmitted back through the collecting ducts to Bowman space. Because GFR equals Kf multiplied by (glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space pressure minus glomerular oncotic pressure), every rise in Bowman space pressure subtracts directly from net filtration pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"The kidney first defends filtration with prostaglandin and nitric oxide mediated afferent dilation. Within hours angiotensin II and thromboxane constrict preglomerular vessels, so renal blood flow and GFR fall together. Persisting obstruction recruits macrophages, and angiotensin II and TGF-beta drive tubular apoptosis and interstitial fibrosis."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a small pressure change, a large GFR change","text":"Using about 60 mmHg glomerular capillary pressure, 18 mmHg Bowman space pressure, and 32 mmHg mean oncotic pressure, net filtration pressure is 60 minus 18 minus 32, or 10 mmHg. If obstruction raises Bowman space pressure to 25 mmHg, net pressure falls to 3 mmHg, and with Kf unchanged that kidney's GFR falls by about 70% although its glomeruli are normal. Afferent dilation raising glomerular pressure to 65 mmHg restores net pressure only to 8 mmHg. Net filtration pressure is a small difference between large forces, so modest back pressure has a large effect."},{"type":"h","text":"Papillary and cortical atrophy"},{"type":"p","text":"Injury begins in the medulla. The papillae project into the calyces and take the full pelvic pressure, and the distended pelvis compresses the low-pressure vasa recta. Papillary ischemia comes first, so the earliest loss is tubular: urine cannot be concentrated, and distal H+ and K+ secretion fails, sometimes causing hyperkalemic distal renal tubular acidosis. Calyces lose their cupped papillary impressions and become blunted. Cortical tubules then atrophy and are replaced by fibrosis while glomeruli survive until late, leaving dilated calyces within a thin cortical rim. Partial or intermittent obstruction produces the largest kidneys because filtration continues long enough to distend the system. Function largely recovers if complete obstruction is relieved within about 1 to 2 weeks, and little returns after several weeks."},{"type":"flow","title":"From obstruction to parenchymal atrophy","steps":["Outflow blocked; filtration continues","Pelvic and Bowman space pressure rise; net filtration falls","Early afferent dilation partly preserves GFR","Angiotensin II and thromboxane cut blood flow and GFR","Compressed vasa recta: papillary ischemia, concentrating defect","Fibrosis; cortex thins to a rim"],"caption":"Medullary injury precedes cortical scarring."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-03-C02","obj":"UR-12-C.2","title":"Unilateral versus bilateral obstruction and postobstructive diuresis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Serum creatinine reflects total GFR from both kidneys. When one kidney is obstructed, the other raises its single-nephron GFR, so total GFR falls by much less than half and creatinine stays normal or rises only slightly. A congenital UPJ obstruction or a slowly growing pelvic tumor can therefore destroy one kidney silently and be found only on imaging, or present with infection, hematuria, or hypertension. An acute stone hurts because of sudden distension, not because of lost function."},{"type":"p","text":"Bilateral obstruction, obstruction of a solitary functioning kidney, or a block at the bladder outlet removes all filtration reserve and causes postrenal acute kidney injury (AKI). Complete obstruction produces anuria. Partial obstruction may produce oliguria, or a normal or even high output of dilute urine because the injured medulla cannot concentrate; output alternating between anuria and polyuria suggests intermittent obstruction. Urea and creatinine rise, and failed distal secretion causes hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis. Early on, slow tubular flow increases urea reabsorption, so the BUN to creatinine ratio may exceed about 15 to 20 and the urine can resemble prerenal urine; as tubules are damaged, sodium excretion rises and urine osmolality approaches that of plasma. A distended bladder points to a block below the bladder neck."},{"type":"table","title":"Unilateral compared with bilateral obstruction","cols":["Feature","Unilateral","Bilateral, solitary kidney, or bladder outlet"],"rows":[["Serum creatinine","Normal or slightly raised","Rises steadily"],["Urine output","Normal","Anuria if complete; oliguria or fluctuating output if partial"],["Potassium and acid-base","Usually normal","Hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis"],["Typical causes","Stone, UPJ obstruction, one-sided pelvic tumor","Prostatic enlargement, posterior urethral valves, neurogenic bladder, bilateral ureteral encasement"],["Hypertension, if present","Renin-mediated, from the ischemic kidney","Volume-mediated, from salt and water retention"],["Diuresis after relief","Minimal; the other kidney maintains balance","Can be marked"]],"caption":"Creatinine and urine output detect obstruction only when no normal kidney remains to compensate."},{"type":"h","text":"Postobstructive diuresis"},{"type":"p","text":"After bilateral obstruction is relieved, urine output can exceed about 200 mL/h for several hours or about 3 L per day. Part of this diuresis is appropriate: urea, sodium, and water retained during obstruction are excreted, and the retained urea acts as an osmotic diuretic. Part reflects tubular injury: collecting duct cells have downregulated aquaporin-2 and respond poorly to ADH, sodium transporters along the nephron are reduced, the medullary solute gradient has washed out, and natriuretic peptides released during volume expansion keep promoting sodium loss. The result can be volume depletion, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, and either hyponatremia or hypernatremia depending on free water loss. Once the retained load is cleared, a healthy kidney ends the diuresis; an injured one may keep wasting salt and water."},{"type":"flow","title":"Mechanism of postobstructive diuresis","steps":["Bilateral obstruction relieved","Retained urea is filtered and drives osmotic diuresis","Low aquaporin-2: poor response to ADH","Fewer Na+ transporters; medullary gradient washed out","Large volume of dilute urine rich in Na+ and K+","Risk of volume depletion, hypokalemia, and sodium disorders"],"caption":"Appropriate excretion of retained solute and true tubular dysfunction combine after relief."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 74-year-old man with prostatic enlargement has a bladder palpable to the umbilicus, creatinine 5.8 mg/dL, and potassium 6.1 mmol/L. After the bladder is drained, what happens to his urine output and serum potassium over the next day?","explain":"Output rises sharply as retained urea causes osmotic diuresis and injured collecting ducts ignore ADH. Potassium falls as distal flow and K+ excretion increase, and can overshoot into hypokalemia.","check":"Compare with the postobstructive diuresis paragraph and flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-03-C03","obj":"UR-12-C.3","title":"Kidney stones classified by composition and urine pH","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A stone forms when urine is supersaturated with a salt, so crystals nucleate, grow, and aggregate faster than flow clears them. Supersaturation rises with high excretion of the solute, low urine volume, and a urine pH that lowers solubility. Inhibitors oppose this: citrate binds calcium in a soluble complex, and proteins such as uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) block aggregation. Chronic acidosis increases proximal citrate reabsorption, and the resulting hypocitraturia removes a key inhibitor. Many calcium oxalate stones grow on Randall plaques, papillary calcium phosphate deposits that erode through the urothelium."},{"type":"p","text":"Urine pH organizes the classification. Phosphate is less soluble as pH rises, because more of it exists as HPO4 2- and PO4 3-, so calcium phosphate and struvite need alkaline urine. Uric acid (pKa about 5.5) is mainly the poorly soluble undissociated acid below that pH, so uric acid stones need acidic urine. Cystine also precipitates in acidic urine and dissolves well only above a pH of about 7.5. Calcium oxalate solubility barely changes with pH."},{"type":"table","title":"Kidney stone types","cols":["Stone","Urine pH","Main causes","Crystal","Plain radiograph"],"rows":[["Calcium oxalate (most common; calcium stones about 75 to 80%)","Any","Hypercalciuria, enteric hyperoxaluria, ethylene glycol, hypocitraturia","Envelope or dumbbell","Radiopaque"],["Calcium phosphate","Alkaline","Distal (type 1) RTA, primary hyperparathyroidism","Wedge-shaped prisms","Radiopaque"],["Struvite (about 10 to 15%)","Alkaline, often above about 7.2","Urease producers: Proteus, Klebsiella, Staphylococcus saprophyticus","Coffin lid","Radiopaque; often staghorn"],["Uric acid (about 5 to 10%)","Acidic, below about 5.5","Gout, tumor lysis, insulin resistance, chronic diarrhea","Rhomboid or rosette","Radiolucent; seen on CT"],["Cystine (about 1 to 2%)","Acidic","Cystinuria","Hexagonal","Faintly radiopaque; may be staghorn"]],"caption":"Phosphate stones need alkaline urine, uric acid and cystine acidic urine; calcium oxalate forms at any pH."},{"type":"p","text":"Most calcium stone formers have idiopathic hypercalciuria with a normal serum calcium. In primary hyperparathyroidism the filtered calcium load rises so much that urinary calcium increases despite PTH-driven distal reabsorption. In ileal disease, unabsorbed fatty acids bind dietary calcium, leaving oxalate free for colonic absorption, and ethylene glycol is metabolized to oxalate. Distal renal tubular acidosis favors calcium phosphate through alkaline urine, hypercalciuria from bone buffering, and hypocitraturia."},{"type":"p","text":"Struvite forms only with infection by urease producers; E. coli generally lacks urease. Uric acid stones contain no calcium, phosphate, or sulfur, so they are radiolucent on plain films; persistently acidic urine is the dominant cause, and tumor lysis or high urate excretion in gout adds solute. Cystinuria is a defect of the apical transporter for cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine in the proximal tubule and gut; only cystine, the least soluble, forms stones. Stones begin young, recur, and show hexagonal crystals and a positive cyanide-nitroprusside test."},{"type":"flow","title":"How urease produces a struvite staghorn","steps":["Urease-producing bacteria infect the urine","Urease splits urea into NH3 and CO2","NH3 binds H+ to form NH4+; urine pH rises","Mg2+, NH4+, and phosphate precipitate as struvite","Stone grows around surviving bacteria into a staghorn"],"caption":"Infection creates the chemistry, and the stone shelters the infection."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reasoning from radiograph and pH","text":"A 52-year-old man with gout has renal colic. A plain radiograph shows no stone, noncontrast CT shows a 6 mm ureteral stone, and urine pH is 5.0. A radiolucent stone excludes the radiopaque calcium oxalate, calcium phosphate, and struvite stones. Cystine is faintly radiopaque. At pH 5.0 uric acid is in its poorly soluble form, so the stone is uric acid. By contrast, recurrent infection, urine pH 8.0, coffin-lid crystals, and a branched radiopaque stone indicate struvite."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child with distal renal tubular acidosis and an adult with cystinuria both form stones. Which stone does each form, and at what urine pH?","explain":"Distal RTA cannot acidify urine and causes hypercalciuria and hypocitraturia: calcium phosphate in alkaline urine. Cystine is least soluble in acidic urine, so cystine stones form at low pH.","check":"Compare with the pH paragraph and stone table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-03-C04","obj":"UR-12-C.4","title":"Renal colic and the complications of calculi","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal colic is pain from acute obstruction of the upper urinary tract, usually a stone moving from the pelvis into the ureter. Sudden distension of the ureter, pelvis, and renal capsule stimulates stretch receptors, and ureteral smooth muscle contracts harder against the stone, while local prostaglandins sensitize nerve endings. Despite the name, the pain is often a steady severe ache with waves of exacerbation as peristalsis pushes against the obstruction. The patient typically cannot lie still, unlike a patient with peritonitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Visceral afferents from the kidney and ureter travel with sympathetic fibers to roughly the T11 to L2 spinal segments, so pain is referred to the skin of those segments. A high stone causes flank and loin pain; as it descends, pain moves to the lower abdomen and groin and into the ipsilateral testis or labium majus. A stone at the ureterovesical junction irritates the bladder, adding urgency, frequency, and dysuria, sometimes with pain at the tip of the penis. Nausea and vomiting reflect shared autonomic pathways through the celiac and renal plexuses. Hematuria, gross or microscopic, comes from mucosal abrasion and is present in most but not all patients, so its absence does not exclude a stone."},{"type":"table","title":"Where stones lodge in the ureter","cols":["Narrowing","Location","Typical pain"],"rows":[["Ureteropelvic junction","Where the funnel-shaped pelvis joins the ureter","Flank and costovertebral angle"],["Pelvic brim","Where the ureter crosses the iliac vessels near the common iliac bifurcation","Lower abdomen radiating to the groin"],["Ureterovesical junction","Oblique passage through the bladder wall; narrowest point","Groin, scrotum or labium, with urgency and dysuria"]],"caption":"Stones stop where the lumen narrows; the ureterovesical junction is the most common site of impaction."},{"type":"h","text":"Complications"},{"type":"p","text":"A stone that blocks the ureter produces acute hydronephrosis above it, with the pressure effects described in C01. Small stones usually pass, but the chance falls as size increases, and stones larger than about 5 to 10 mm often remain impacted. Persistent impaction destroys that kidney progressively, often silently once the pain settles, because the other kidney maintains the creatinine."},{"type":"p","text":"Infection above a stone is the most dangerous complication. Normal urine flow flushes bacteria out; stagnant urine above an obstruction lets them multiply, and raised pelvic pressure pushes bacteria and endotoxin into the renal veins and lymphatics. The infected obstructed system can become pyonephrosis, a perinephric abscess, or urosepsis, so fever with an obstructing stone signals a life-threatening infection. Struvite stones harbor bacteria inside them, so the infection persists while the stone remains. Long-standing staghorn calculi with chronic Proteus infection are associated with xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis, and chronic irritation can cause squamous metaplasia and, rarely, squamous cell carcinoma of the renal pelvis."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Stone, stasis, and infection","steps":["Stone narrows the outflow","Urine stagnates above the stone","Bacteria multiply instead of being flushed out","Urease organisms alkalinize urine; struvite precipitates","Stone enlarges and obstruction worsens"],"caption":"With urease-producing organisms, obstruction and infection reinforce each other until the stone becomes a staghorn."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have the same 7 mm stone at the ureterovesical junction. One is afebrile; the other has a temperature of 39.2 °C (102.6 °F). Why is the second patient in much greater danger?","explain":"Trapped urine cannot clear bacteria, and high pelvic pressure drives organisms into the bloodstream, so an infected obstructed kidney can progress to pyonephrosis and urosepsis within hours.","check":"Compare with the infection paragraph and the stone, stasis, and infection cycle."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy explains where and in whom obstruction occurs: the male urethra and prostate, the right ureter at the pelvic brim in pregnancy, the three ureteral narrowings, and the T11 to L2 referral of ureteral pain. Physiology explains the damage: Bowman space pressure subtracts from filtration, the vasa recta and papillae fail first, and creatinine reflects both kidneys together. Biochemistry and microbiology explain stone type: solubility depends on pH, purine breakdown supplies uric acid, ethylene glycol supplies oxalate, a single transporter defect causes cystinuria, and bacterial urease converts urea into the alkaline chemistry of struvite. Pathology ties these together as blunted calyces, a thin cortical rim, and staghorn stones with chronic infection."},{"type":"p","text":"These patterns have limits. Dilation is not proof of obstruction, because reflux and pregnancy widen the collecting system without destroying it, and severe early obstruction can cause little dilation. A normal creatinine does not exclude obstruction of one kidney. Many stones are mixed, and a plain radiograph that misses a uric acid stone does not exclude a stone."}],"summary":["Hydronephrosis is dilation of the pelvis and calyces; obstruction adds progressive atrophy of the parenchyma.","Posterior urethral valves (newborn boys), UPJ obstruction (congenital), stones (young adults), pregnancy (right more than left), prostatic enlargement (older men), and pelvic tumors (women) are the classic causes.","Back pressure raises Bowman space pressure and lowers net filtration pressure; later angiotensin II and thromboxane cut renal blood flow.","Papillae and vasa recta fail first, so a concentrating defect precedes cortical thinning; partial obstruction causes the most dilation.","Unilateral obstruction can stay silent because the other kidney keeps creatinine normal; bilateral or outlet obstruction causes postrenal AKI with oliguria or anuria, hyperkalemia, and acidosis.","Postobstructive diuresis combines osmotic diuresis from retained urea with ADH resistance and sodium wasting from injured tubules.","Calcium oxalate is the most common stone and forms at any pH; calcium phosphate and struvite need alkaline urine; uric acid and cystine need acidic urine.","Urease splits urea to ammonia, alkalinizing urine and precipitating struvite staghorns that shelter bacteria.","Uric acid stones are radiolucent on plain films; cystine stones show hexagonal crystals.","Renal colic is steady severe pain with waves, radiating from flank to groin with hematuria; stones lodge at the UPJ, pelvic brim, and ureterovesical junction, and infection above a stone can cause urosepsis."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, sections on obstructive uropathy (hydronephrosis) and urolithiasis"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Glomerular filtration, renal blood flow, and their control"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Urine concentration and dilution; transport of urea and organic solutes"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, kidneys and ureters, including ureteric constrictions and referred pain"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal Disease, nephrolithiasis and urinary obstruction"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric gram-negative rods, Proteus"}]},"UR-12-D":{"unit":"UR-12-D","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-04","title":"Inflammatory Urinary Tract Diseases: Interstitial Nephritis, Pyelonephritis, and Interstitial Cystitis","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers inflammation that targets the renal interstitium, tubules, papillae, and bladder rather than the glomeruli: drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis, analgesic nephropathy with papillary necrosis, chronic and xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis, and interstitial cystitis. Board questions usually give a drug history, a scarred or enlarged kidney, a urine sediment, or sterile bladder pain and ask for the lesion, its mechanism, and its effect on renal function. The unifying idea is that inflammation between the tubules impairs tubular work first, so concentrating ability fails, the urine fills with white cells instead of red cell casts, and glomeruli are spared until late.","prerequisites":["Nephron segments, the renal interstitium, and the urine concentrating mechanism (countercurrent multiplication and the vasa recta)","Renal blood supply, including the low-flow, hypoxic medulla","Hypersensitivity types I to IV and the hapten concept","Urinalysis and urine sediment: pyuria, casts, and their sites of formation","Acute pyelonephritis, urinary obstruction, and kidney stones (UR-12-C)"],"connections":[{"to":"Tubulointerstitial disease and acute kidney injury (UR-12-B)","how":"Acute interstitial nephritis is the immune counterpart of acute tubular necrosis, and the urine sediment separates the two."},{"to":"Obstructive urinary diseases (UR-12-C)","how":"Obstruction and stones set up chronic pyelonephritis, xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis, and papillary necrosis."},{"to":"Immunology (hypersensitivity reactions)","how":"Drug-induced interstitial nephritis is a hapten-driven, mainly T cell mediated (type IV) reaction with eosinophil recruitment."},{"to":"Hematology (sickle cell disease)","how":"Sickling in the hypoxic, hypertonic medulla explains papillary necrosis and the concentrating defect in sickle cell disease and trait."},{"to":"Microbiology (urinary pathogens)","how":"Urease-producing Proteus links struvite staghorn stones to chronic destructive infection of the kidney."}],"terms":[{"term":"Tubulointerstitial nephritis","def":"Inflammation that primarily involves the renal tubules and the interstitium between them, with the glomeruli spared or affected only late."},{"term":"Acute interstitial nephritis (AIN)","def":"Acute kidney injury caused by inflammatory cells and edema in the renal interstitium, most often a hypersensitivity reaction to a drug."},{"term":"Hapten","def":"A small molecule that becomes immunogenic only after it binds a host protein."},{"term":"White blood cell (WBC) cast","def":"A cylinder of leukocytes embedded in Tamm-Horsfall protein that forms in the tubule lumen and therefore proves inflammation within the kidney."},{"term":"Sterile pyuria","def":"White cells in the urine with a negative routine bacterial culture."},{"term":"Renal papillary necrosis","def":"Ischemic coagulative necrosis of the renal papillary tips, the most poorly perfused part of the kidney."},{"term":"Vesicoureteral reflux (VUR)","def":"Retrograde flow of bladder urine into the ureter and renal pelvis because the ureterovesical valve fails."},{"term":"Intrarenal reflux","def":"Passage of refluxed urine from the calyx into the collecting ducts through open papillary duct orifices."},{"term":"Thyroidization","def":"Dilated atrophic tubules filled with eosinophilic hyaline casts that resemble thyroid follicles, typical of chronic pyelonephritis."},{"term":"Xanthoma cell","def":"A macrophage with foamy, lipid-filled cytoplasm."},{"term":"Interstitial cystitis (bladder pain syndrome)","def":"Chronic bladder pain with urgency and frequency in the absence of infection or another identifiable cause."},{"term":"Hunner lesion","def":"A reddened, inflamed area of bladder mucosa seen at cystoscopy in a subset of patients with interstitial cystitis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-04-C01","obj":"UR-12-D.1","title":"Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The renal interstitium is the connective tissue space between tubules and peritubular capillaries. Inflammation there injures tubules and compresses peritubular blood flow, so tubular function and glomerular filtration rate (GFR) fall even though the glomeruli themselves are normal. Drugs are the most common cause of acute interstitial nephritis (AIN) in adults; infections and systemic immune diseases cause most of the rest."},{"type":"h","text":"Mechanism: a drug hypersensitivity in the interstitium"},{"type":"p","text":"The drug or one of its metabolites acts as a hapten. It is secreted or filtered into the tubules, binds proteins of tubular cells or the tubular basement membrane, and makes them immunogenic. Sensitized T cells then drive a delayed, mainly type IV reaction that recruits macrophages and eosinophils. Raised serum IgE and tissue eosinophils in some patients suggest an added type I component. Because the reaction is immune rather than toxic, it is not dose dependent, it begins after a sensitization period of days to weeks (often about 2 weeks), and it recurs quickly on re-exposure to the same or a related drug."},{"type":"table","title":"Drugs that commonly cause acute interstitial nephritis","cols":["Drug class","Examples"],"rows":[["Beta-lactam antibiotics","Penicillins (methicillin is the classic example), ampicillin, cephalosporins"],["Other antimicrobials","Sulfonamides, including trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole; rifampin"],["Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)","Ibuprofen, naproxen, and others"],["Proton pump inhibitors","Omeprazole and related drugs"],["Diuretics","Thiazides, furosemide"],["Others","Allopurinol"]],"caption":"Any new drug started in the preceding weeks is a suspect; these are the classes tested most often."},{"type":"flow","title":"From new drug to acute kidney injury","steps":["New drug started","Drug binds tubular proteins and acts as a hapten","Sensitized T cells recruit macrophages and eosinophils","Interstitial edema and tubulitis","Tubular dysfunction and fall in GFR","Pyuria, WBC casts, rising creatinine"],"caption":"Removing the drug removes the antigen; persistent inflammation organizes into interstitial fibrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Microscopically the interstitium shows edema and an infiltrate of lymphocytes and macrophages with prominent eosinophils, and inflammatory cells invade the tubular epithelium (tubulitis). Some drugs, such as methicillin and thiazides, produce small non-necrotizing granulomas. The glomeruli are normal, with one exception: NSAIDs can cause AIN together with a minimal change pattern of podocyte injury and nephrotic-range proteinuria."},{"type":"h","text":"Manifestations explained by mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"Systemic hypersensitivity produces fever, a maculopapular rash, and blood eosinophilia, but the full triad appears in only a minority of patients, and NSAID-induced AIN, which may appear only after months of use, often lacks all three. Leukocytes cross into the tubule lumen, giving sterile pyuria, and become trapped in Tamm-Horsfall protein to form WBC casts, which place the inflammation inside the kidney. Microscopic hematuria is common. Injured tubules fail to reclaim filtered low molecular weight protein, giving mild proteinuria, and may waste sodium or cause hyperkalemia or a normal anion gap acidosis. Edema can enlarge the kidneys. Urine eosinophils are neither sensitive nor specific. Creatinine usually returns toward baseline over weeks once the drug is stopped; prolonged exposure leaves interstitial fibrosis and chronic kidney disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Urine sediment in common causes of acute kidney injury","cols":["Condition","Urine culture","Typical sediment","Distinguishing clue"],"rows":[["Drug-induced AIN","Negative","WBCs, WBC casts, sometimes eosinophils","New drug days to weeks earlier; rash, fever, eosinophilia"],["Acute pyelonephritis","Positive","WBCs, WBC casts, bacteria","Fever, flank pain, costovertebral angle tenderness"],["Acute tubular necrosis","Negative","Muddy brown granular and epithelial cell casts","Ischemia or a direct nephrotoxin"],["Glomerulonephritis","Negative","Dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts","Hypertension and edema"]],"caption":"WBC casts localize inflammation to the kidney; RBC casts localize bleeding to the glomerulus."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has pyuria and WBC casts, a negative culture, and no rash or eosinophilia, and has taken an NSAID daily for 4 months. Can this be AIN?","explain":"Yes. AIN is defined by interstitial inflammation, not by the systemic triad, and NSAID-induced AIN often appears after months without fever, rash, or eosinophilia, sometimes with minimal change proteinuria.","check":"The paragraph on manifestations and the NSAID exception in the histology paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-04-C02","obj":"UR-12-D.2","title":"Chronic analgesic nephropathy and renal papillary necrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The renal papilla is the tip of a medullary pyramid, where collecting ducts empty into a minor calyx. Its only blood supply is the vasa recta, long hairpin capillaries that descend from efferent arterioles of juxtamedullary glomeruli. Medullary flow is a small fraction of renal blood flow and moves slowly, which preserves the countercurrent concentration gradient but leaves the papilla hypoxic and hypertonic even in health. Medullary interstitial cells make vasodilating prostaglandins, mainly PGE2, that help sustain this flow, especially when renal perfusion falls."},{"type":"h","text":"How combination analgesics destroy the papilla"},{"type":"p","text":"Analgesic nephropathy follows years of heavy use of combination analgesics, classically phenacetin with aspirin and caffeine. Phenacetin is metabolized to acetaminophen, which the countercurrent system concentrates in the papilla. There its reactive metabolites exhaust glutathione and cause oxidative injury. Aspirin inhibits cyclooxygenase, removing prostaglandin vasodilation from an already marginal circulation. The combination is therefore more damaging than either drug alone. The papillae die first; the cortex that drains through each necrotic papilla then undergoes tubular atrophy and interstitial fibrosis, giving chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Analgesic nephropathy","steps":["Years of combination analgesic use","Acetaminophen concentrated in the papilla by countercurrent","Reactive metabolites exhaust glutathione; oxidative injury","Aspirin blocks prostaglandin vasodilation; papillary ischemia","Papillary necrosis","Overlying cortex atrophies and scars"],"caption":"Toxic injury and ischemia converge on the most poorly perfused tissue in the kidney."},{"type":"p","text":"Grossly, the necrotic papillae are shrunken and yellow-brown and may calcify or slough, and the cortex above them forms depressed scars. Microscopy shows coagulative necrosis of the papillae with ghost outlines of tubules, and tubular atrophy, interstitial fibrosis, and chronic inflammation in the overlying cortex."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical picture follows the anatomy. Loss of the inner medulla destroys the concentration gradient early, causing polyuria and nocturia. Inflamed tissue sheds white cells, giving sterile pyuria, and hematuria is common. A sloughed papilla can enter the ureter and cause colic, gross hematuria, or obstruction, and superimposed infection is frequent. Creatinine rises slowly, and hypertension may develop. Long-term phenacetin use also increases the risk of urothelial carcinoma of the renal pelvis."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of renal papillary necrosis","cols":["Cause","Why the papilla dies"],"rows":[["Analgesic nephropathy","Concentrated toxic metabolites plus loss of prostaglandin vasodilation"],["Diabetes mellitus","Small vessel disease of the medulla, often combined with acute pyelonephritis"],["Sickle cell disease or trait","Red cells sickle in the hypoxic, hypertonic, acidic medulla and occlude the vasa recta"],["Severe acute pyelonephritis","Intense inflammation and edema compress papillary blood flow"],["Urinary obstruction","Raised pelvic pressure lowers medullary blood flow, often with infection"]],"caption":"Every cause attacks the same weak point: the slow, low-oxygen blood supply of the papillary tip."},{"type":"example","title":"Hematuria in sickle cell trait","text":"A 22-year-old man with sickle cell trait has painless gross hematuria and passes a small piece of tissue. His first morning urine is dilute. Step 1: in sickle cell trait less than half of each red cell's hemoglobin is hemoglobin S, so sickling elsewhere needs extreme conditions. Step 2: the papilla is the exception; low oxygen tension, high osmolality, and low pH there promote polymerization of hemoglobin S. Step 3: sickled cells occlude the slow vasa recta, so the papillary tip infarcts and can slough, causing bleeding. Step 4: vasa recta loss also dissipates the medullary gradient, so the urine cannot be concentrated even before papillae slough. The same slow, hypoxic medullary circulation is why analgesics and diabetes also target the papilla."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-04-C03","obj":"UR-12-D.3","title":"Chronic pyelonephritis: reflux nephropathy and chronic obstruction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chronic pyelonephritis is chronic tubulointerstitial inflammation and scarring that involves the calyces and pelvis as well as the parenchyma. Calyceal involvement is what separates it from other chronic tubulointerstitial diseases. It develops by two main routes: reflux nephropathy, which begins in childhood, and chronic obstruction with recurrent infection."},{"type":"h","text":"Reflux nephropathy"},{"type":"p","text":"Normally the ureter crosses the bladder wall obliquely, and this intravesical segment is compressed when the bladder contracts, acting as a one-way flap valve. A congenitally short intravesical segment, or bladder outlet obstruction and neurogenic bladder that raise voiding pressure, lets urine reflux up the ureter. Sterile reflux at normal bladder pressures causes little injury; most damage comes when infected bladder urine reaches the pelvis and then enters the parenchyma. That entry depends on papillary shape. Simple papillae are convex with slit-like duct openings that close under pressure, whereas compound papillae, concentrated at the upper and lower poles, are flat or concave with wide openings that stay open, so intrarenal reflux and the worst scars occur at the poles."},{"type":"flow","title":"Reflux nephropathy","steps":["Incompetent ureterovesical valve","Infected bladder urine refluxes into the renal pelvis","Intrarenal reflux through polar compound papillae","Acute pyelonephritis destroys papillae and tubules","Cortex contracts into a scar over a blunted calyx","Hypertension, polyuria, and progressive chronic kidney disease"],"caption":"Repeated episodes, often beginning in early childhood, add scar to scar."},{"type":"p","text":"In chronic obstructive pyelonephritis, stones, prostatic enlargement, strictures, or congenital anomalies cause stasis, and stagnant urine permits recurrent infection above the obstruction. Back pressure also injures the medulla directly. The process is unilateral or bilateral depending on the level of the obstruction."},{"type":"h","text":"Morphology"},{"type":"p","text":"The kidneys are irregularly and often asymmetrically scarred. Each scar is a coarse, discrete corticomedullary depression lying directly over a dilated, blunted, deformed calyx, because the infection destroyed the papilla and the cortex that drains into it. Microscopically there is uneven interstitial fibrosis with lymphocytes and plasma cells, and neutrophils during flares. Tubules are atrophic or dilated, and the dilated ones fill with eosinophilic hyaline casts that resemble thyroid colloid (thyroidization). The calyceal wall is chronically inflamed and fibrotic, and glomeruli show periglomerular fibrosis. Thyroidization occurs in other chronic tubular diseases, so it supports the diagnosis only alongside the gross pattern."},{"type":"table","title":"Patterns of renal scarring","cols":["Feature","Chronic pyelonephritis","Benign nephrosclerosis","Analgesic nephropathy"],"rows":[["Distribution","Coarse, irregular, often asymmetric","Symmetric, evenly shrunken kidneys","Scars over necrotic papillae, usually bilateral"],["Surface","Broad U-shaped scars","Fine, diffuse granularity","Depressed cortical scars"],["Calyces","Blunted and deformed under scars","Normal","Deformed where papillae slough"],["Primary target","Pelvis, calyces, tubules, interstitium","Arterioles (hyaline arteriolosclerosis)","Papillae, then tubules and interstitium"]],"caption":"Calyceal deformity under a coarse scar points to pyelonephritis; uniform fine granularity points to vascular disease."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow from tubulointerstitial loss. Destruction of the medulla causes polyuria and nocturia, scarring and ischemia stimulate renin release and hypertension, and nephron loss slowly reduces GFR. Proteinuria is mild at first, but surviving nephrons hyperfilter and develop secondary focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, which brings heavier proteinuria and faster progression. Bacteriuria and WBC casts appear during flares. Reflux nephropathy is an important cause of hypertension and chronic kidney disease in children and young adults, and it can be clinically silent until those complications appear."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two kidneys are equally small. One has a coarse polar scar over a blunted calyx; the other has a finely granular surface and normal calyces. Which disease made each?","explain":"Infection that destroys a papilla shrinks the cortex draining through it and deforms the calyx, giving coarse focal scars over abnormal calyces (chronic pyelonephritis). Hyaline arteriolosclerosis narrows small vessels throughout the cortex, giving uniform fine scarring with intact calyces (benign nephrosclerosis).","check":"The morphology paragraph and the scarring table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-04-C04","obj":"UR-12-D.4","title":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis is an uncommon, chronic, destructive form of pyelonephritis that is almost always unilateral. Its usual setting is long-standing obstruction by a stone, often a staghorn calculus, with infection by Proteus or Escherichia coli. Proteus links the two: its urease splits urea into ammonia, alkalinizing the urine and precipitating struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate), which grows into a branching stone that fills the pelvis and calyces and harbors bacteria within it."},{"type":"flow","title":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","steps":["Urease-producing Proteus infects the urine","Ammonia alkalinizes urine; struvite staghorn stone forms","Obstruction prevents drainage; infection persists","Parenchyma is destroyed; macrophages ingest lipid debris","Foamy macrophage nodules replace the kidney","Enlarged, nonfunctioning kidney with a mass"],"caption":"Obstruction converts a treatable infection into chronic, nonresolving inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the obstructed kidney cannot drain, suppurative inflammation never resolves and steadily destroys the parenchyma. Macrophages engulf lipid released from necrotic cell membranes and fill with it, becoming foamy xanthoma cells. Grossly the kidney is enlarged and its parenchyma is replaced by yellow-orange nodules and abscess cavities arranged around dilated calyces containing the stone. Microscopically sheets of foamy macrophages mix with plasma cells, lymphocytes, neutrophils, and multinucleated giant cells. The process can extend into the perinephric fat and occasionally forms fistulas."},{"type":"p","text":"Patients present with flank pain, fever, malaise, weight loss, a palpable mass, and a history of recurrent urinary infection, often with anemia and leukocytosis. Imaging shows an enlarged kidney that does not function, with a stone and a mass. Renal cell carcinoma can produce every one of these features, so the lesion is a classic mimic. The resemblance extends to the microscope, where pale foamy macrophages can look like the clear cells of clear cell renal cell carcinoma. Immunohistochemistry separates them: macrophages express the histiocyte marker CD68, whereas carcinoma cells express epithelial markers such as cytokeratin and PAX8. Malakoplakia, another macrophage disorder of the urinary tract linked to defective bacterial killing, is distinguished by Michaelis-Gutmann bodies, which xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis lacks."},{"type":"table","title":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis versus clear cell renal cell carcinoma","cols":["Feature","Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","Clear cell renal cell carcinoma"],"rows":[["Cause","Obstruction with chronic infection","Neoplasm, often with VHL gene loss"],["Stone","Usually present, often staghorn","Not characteristic"],["Signs of infection","Recurrent urinary infection, pyuria, fever","Usually absent; fever can occur as a paraneoplastic sign"],["Pale cells","Lipid-laden macrophages (CD68 positive)","Tumor cells with lipid and glycogen (cytokeratin and PAX8 positive)"],["Other cells","Plasma cells, neutrophils, giant cells","Delicate branching vascular network"]],"caption":"Both present as a renal mass with pale cells; the stone, the infection, and the inflammatory mixture favor xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-04-C05","obj":"UR-12-D.5","title":"Interstitial cystitis (bladder pain syndrome)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The bladder is lined by urothelium whose superficial umbrella cells are joined by tight junctions and covered by uroplakin plaques and a glycosaminoglycan layer. Together these form one of the tightest barriers in the body, keeping urea, potassium, and acid out of the bladder wall. Afferent nerves in the wall signal fullness, mainly through the pelvic nerves to sacral segments S2 to S4, and the same fibers can signal pain when they are sensitized."},{"type":"p","text":"Interstitial cystitis, also called bladder pain syndrome, is chronic pain, pressure, or discomfort perceived as coming from the bladder, accompanied by urgency and frequency, in the absence of infection or another identifiable cause. It is much more common in women and often coexists with other chronic pain disorders, such as irritable bowel syndrome and fibromyalgia. Its cause is not established, but the leading explanations interact in a self-sustaining loop."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Proposed self-sustaining loop in interstitial cystitis","steps":["Defective glycosaminoglycan layer; leaky urothelium","Urine potassium enters the bladder wall","Sensory nerves depolarize; mast cells release mediators","Neurogenic inflammation and nerve sensitization","Further urothelial injury; barrier stays leaky"],"caption":"Barrier failure, mast cell activation, and neural sensitization are linked hypotheses, not a proven sequence."},{"type":"p","text":"Patients fall into two broad groups at cystoscopy. A minority have Hunner lesions (formerly Hunner ulcers), reddened mucosal areas with small vessels radiating toward a central scar, which may crack and bleed when the bladder is distended. Biopsies from this group show chronic inflammation with lymphocytes and plasma cells, granulation tissue, and increased mast cells in the detrusor, and advanced cases develop transmural fibrosis and a small, contracted bladder. The larger group has a normal-looking bladder or only petechial hemorrhages after distention, a nonspecific finding."},{"type":"p","text":"The symptoms follow from a sensitized bladder. Pain rises as the bladder fills and eases after voiding, so patients void often and in small volumes, day and night, to keep pain down. Urgency here is a pressure to relieve pain rather than fear of leakage, which helps separate it from overactive bladder. Dyspareunia and pelvic floor pain are common. Urinalysis and culture are repeatedly negative, so the diagnosis is made by excluding conditions that cause the same symptoms."},{"type":"table","title":"Conditions that resemble interstitial cystitis","cols":["Condition","Key distinguishing finding"],"rows":[["Bacterial cystitis","Positive culture, pyuria, and nitrite; symptoms resolve when infection clears"],["Urothelial carcinoma in situ","Frequency and urgency with hematuria; malignant cells on urine cytology or biopsy"],["Bladder or distal ureteral stone","Hematuria and a stone on imaging"],["Overactive bladder","Urgency driven by fear of leakage, often with incontinence, without pain"],["Radiation or drug-induced cystitis","History of pelvic radiation, cyclophosphamide, or ketamine use"]],"caption":"Interstitial cystitis is a diagnosis of exclusion; carcinoma in situ is the most dangerous condition to miss."},{"type":"example","title":"Why a sterile urine does not rule out disease","text":"A 45-year-old woman has 2 years of suprapubic pain that worsens as her bladder fills and eases after voiding, with frequency and nocturia. Five cultures are negative. Step 1: negative cultures exclude bacterial cystitis, but not every inflammatory or neoplastic cause. Step 2: carcinoma in situ also causes frequency and urgency with sterile urine, so urine cytology and cystoscopy are needed. Step 3: if cytology is negative and cystoscopy shows a red patch with radiating vessels, a Hunner lesion is likely; biopsy showing chronic inflammation and mast cells, without malignant cells, completes the exclusion. Step 4: the pattern of pain with filling and relief after emptying points to bladder sensory sensitization rather than detrusor overactivity."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology explain where these diseases strike. The papilla depends on slow vasa recta flow and prostaglandin vasodilation, so analgesics, sickling, diabetes, and inflammation kill it first and concentrating ability fails early. Papillary shape decides where reflux enters the kidney, and the oblique ureterovesical course decides whether reflux happens at all. Immunology explains drug-induced AIN as hapten-driven T cell hypersensitivity, and microbiology explains why Proteus urease builds the staghorn stones behind xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology ties them together as tubulointerstitial disease: inflammation between tubules produces pyuria, WBC casts, tubular proteinuria, and polyuria rather than the RBC casts and heavy proteinuria of glomerular disease, until secondary glomerulosclerosis develops late. The hypersensitivity triad is often absent, thyroidization is not specific, foamy cells can mimic carcinoma, and interstitial cystitis has no confirmatory test, so each diagnosis rests on the full pattern rather than a single finding."}],"summary":["Drug-induced AIN is a hapten-driven, mainly T cell mediated reaction days to weeks after a drug, with interstitial edema, lymphocytes, eosinophils, and spared glomeruli.","AIN gives sterile pyuria, WBC casts, and rising creatinine; fever, rash, and eosinophilia occur together in only a minority, and NSAID cases may add minimal change proteinuria.","WBC casts localize inflammation to the kidney (AIN or pyelonephritis); RBC casts localize disease to the glomerulus.","The papilla is the most poorly perfused part of the kidney, which is why it dies first from analgesics, diabetes, sickle cell disease or trait, severe pyelonephritis, and obstruction.","Analgesic nephropathy combines concentrated acetaminophen metabolites, glutathione depletion, and loss of prostaglandin vasodilation, and it raises the risk of urothelial carcinoma of the renal pelvis.","Chronic pyelonephritis arises from reflux or obstruction with recurrent infection and produces coarse, asymmetric scars over blunted calyces, worst at the poles in reflux.","Thyroidization (dilated tubules filled with eosinophilic casts) with calyceal deformity is the classic pattern; hypertension, polyuria, and secondary FSGS follow.","Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis is unilateral destruction by foamy macrophages behind an obstructing stone, usually with Proteus, and mimics clear cell renal cell carcinoma.","Interstitial cystitis is chronic bladder pain that rises with filling, with urgency, frequency, and negative cultures, mostly in women; a minority have Hunner lesions, and it is a diagnosis of exclusion."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, sections on tubulointerstitial nephritis, pyelonephritis, and drug-induced interstitial nephritis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, section on cystitis"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Urine concentration and dilution; the countercurrent mechanism and vasa recta"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity disorders, T cell mediated diseases"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric gram-negative rods (Enterobacteriaceae), Proteus"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal Disease"}]},"UR-12-E":{"unit":"UR-12-E","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-05","title":"Congenital Urinary Disease: Alport Syndrome, Cystic Kidneys, Renal Agenesis, and Vesicoureteral Reflux","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers kidney and urinary tract disease that begins with a gene defect or a developmental error: Alport syndrome, the cystic kidney diseases, renal agenesis, and vesicoureteral reflux. Each condition is explained from the molecule or embryologic step that fails to the lesion it produces and the findings it causes outside the kidney, such as deafness, liver disease, brain aneurysms, or Potter sequence. Board items usually give an age, an inheritance pattern, and one extrarenal clue and expect you to name the disorder and explain why that clue belongs to it.","prerequisites":["Development of the metanephros from the ureteric bud and metanephric mesenchyme (UR-1)","Structure of the glomerular filtration barrier and the basement membrane","Collagen structure and synthesis, including type IV collagen networks","Patterns of Mendelian inheritance, X inactivation, and the two-hit model","Pathophysiology of pyelonephritis and urinary tract infection"],"connections":[{"to":"Renal genetics (UR-9)","how":"The COL4A, PKD1, PKD2, and PKHD1 genes covered there are the starting points for Alport syndrome and the polycystic kidney diseases."},{"to":"Glomerular disease (UR-12-A)","how":"Alport syndrome is a hereditary cause of glomerular hematuria, and its target collagen chain is the same alpha 3 chain attacked in anti-GBM disease."},{"to":"Urinary tract infection and obstruction (UR-12-C, UR-12-D)","how":"Vesicoureteral reflux is the main anatomic risk factor for recurrent pyelonephritis in children and leads to chronic pyelonephritis scarring."},{"to":"Cerebrovascular and cardiac disease (CV)","how":"ADPKD links the kidney to berry aneurysms with subarachnoid hemorrhage and to mitral valve prolapse."},{"to":"Embryology and the respiratory system","how":"Fetal urine makes most of the amniotic fluid, so failure of the kidneys limits fetal lung growth and causes pulmonary hypoplasia."}],"terms":[{"term":"Glomerular basement membrane (GBM)","def":"The fused basement membrane between glomerular endothelium and podocytes, built on a type IV collagen network made of alpha 3, alpha 4, and alpha 5 chains in the mature kidney."},{"term":"Anterior lenticonus","def":"A conical forward bulge of the central lens caused by a thin, weak anterior lens capsule; characteristic of Alport syndrome."},{"term":"Polycystin","def":"Polycystin-1 (a large membrane receptor protein) and polycystin-2 (a calcium-permeable cation channel) form a complex on the primary cilium of tubular cells; mutation causes ADPKD."},{"term":"Fibrocystin","def":"The ciliary protein encoded by PKHD1 in collecting duct and bile duct epithelium; loss causes ARPKD."},{"term":"Primary cilium","def":"A single nonmotile projection on the apical surface of tubular epithelial cells that senses urine flow and signals through calcium entry."},{"term":"Congenital hepatic fibrosis","def":"Portal fibrosis with abnormal bile ducts from a ductal plate malformation, causing presinusoidal portal hypertension; the liver lesion of ARPKD."},{"term":"Renal dysplasia","def":"Abnormal differentiation of the metanephros, recognized by primitive ducts surrounded by undifferentiated mesenchyme, often with cartilage and cysts."},{"term":"Oligohydramnios","def":"Too little amniotic fluid for gestational age; after early pregnancy it usually reflects too little fetal urine or leakage of fluid."},{"term":"Potter sequence","def":"The chain of fetal abnormalities that follows oligohydramnios: pulmonary hypoplasia, flattened facies, and limb deformities."},{"term":"Compensatory hypertrophy","def":"Enlargement of existing nephrons in a remaining kidney, with higher single-nephron GFR, when total nephron mass is reduced."},{"term":"Vesicoureteral reflux (VUR)","def":"Backward flow of urine from the bladder into the ureter and often the renal pelvis, usually during voiding."},{"term":"Intrarenal reflux","def":"Passage of refluxed urine from the pelvis into collecting ducts through the open ducts of compound papillae, carrying bacteria into the parenchyma."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-05-C01","obj":"UR-12-E.1","title":"Alport syndrome: a defective type IV collagen network","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Alport syndrome is a hereditary disease of type IV collagen that damages the glomerular basement membrane (GBM), the cochlea, and the eye. Type IV collagen forms sheets rather than fibrils: three alpha chains wind into a triple helix (a protomer), and protomers join end to end and side by side into a network that gives the basement membrane its strength."},{"type":"h","text":"Why one chain defect breaks the whole network"},{"type":"p","text":"The embryonic GBM uses an alpha 1, alpha 1, alpha 2 network. During glomerular maturation, podocytes switch to producing alpha 3, alpha 4, and alpha 5 chains, which assemble together into one protomer. This mature network has more cross-links and resists proteolysis better, so it lasts under the high filtration pressure of the glomerulus. Because each protomer needs all three chains, loss of any one prevents the mature network from forming. The embryonic network persists in its place, and over years it thins, frays, and splits."},{"type":"table","title":"Genetics of Alport syndrome","cols":["Form","Gene","Share of cases","Who is most affected"],"rows":[["X-linked","COL4A5 (alpha 5 chain)","About 80%","Hemizygous males; carrier females vary from isolated hematuria to renal failure"],["Autosomal recessive","COL4A3 or COL4A4, both alleles","About 15%","Males and females equally, with severe early disease"],["Autosomal dominant","COL4A3 or COL4A4, one allele","Small minority","Milder, later disease in both sexes"]],"caption":"Every form causes the same failure: the alpha 3, 4, 5 protomer cannot assemble. Proportions are approximate and vary by source."},{"type":"p","text":"Males with the X-linked form have no normal alpha 5 chain, so the mature network is absent in every glomerulus. Carrier females are mosaics: random X inactivation leaves some podocytes making normal alpha 5 and others not, so disease ranges from microscopic hematuria alone to renal failure later in life."},{"type":"flow","title":"From collagen gene to renal failure","steps":["COL4A5, COL4A3, or COL4A4 mutation","Alpha 3, 4, 5 protomer fails to assemble","Weaker embryonic network persists in the GBM","GBM thins, then thickens and splits","Red cells leak: hematuria from childhood","Proteinuria, glomerulosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis","End-stage renal disease in young adult males"],"caption":"Hematuria appears first because red cells pass through focal breaks; proteinuria follows as glomeruli scar."},{"type":"h","text":"Clinical course and extrarenal findings"},{"type":"p","text":"Persistent microscopic hematuria is present from early childhood, with episodes of gross hematuria in some children, often after a respiratory infection. The hematuria is glomerular, so red cells are dysmorphic and red cell casts may appear. Proteinuria, hypertension, and declining GFR follow, and most males with X-linked disease reach end-stage renal disease by about 20 to 40 years of age. The same alpha 3, 4, 5 network lines the basement membranes of the cochlea and the lens capsule. Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, beginning in high frequencies, develops in later childhood and is not present at birth. A thin anterior lens capsule lets the lens bulge forward as anterior lenticonus; dot and fleck retinopathy is also seen."},{"type":"p","text":"Light microscopy is nonspecific early and later shows glomerulosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis, and lipid-laden interstitial foam cells. Electron microscopy is diagnostic: the lamina densa is thin in some segments and thickened and split into interweaving layers in others, the basket-weave pattern. Immunostaining shows absent alpha 5 chain in the GBM, and in X-linked males also in the epidermal basement membrane of skin. Thin basement membrane disease, often from one abnormal COL4A3 or COL4A4 allele, causes uniform GBM thinning with benign familial hematuria and no deafness. Anti-GBM disease is the immune mirror image: its antibodies target the alpha 3 chain, and a few Alport patients form anti-GBM antibodies after transplantation because the normal alpha 3, 4, 5 network of the donor kidney is foreign to an immune system that never encountered it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-05-C02","obj":"UR-12-E.2","title":"Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) is the most common inherited kidney disease, affecting about 1 in 400 to 1 in 1,000 people, and a leading genetic cause of end-stage renal disease. About 85% of cases come from mutations in PKD1 on chromosome 16, which encodes polycystin-1, and most of the rest from PKD2 on chromosome 4, which encodes polycystin-2. PKD2 disease is milder, and renal failure arrives about two decades later."},{"type":"h","text":"How a ciliary defect makes a cyst"},{"type":"p","text":"Polycystin-1 and polycystin-2 form a complex on the primary cilium of tubular cells. When urine flow bends the cilium, polycystin-2 lets calcium enter, which holds cyclic AMP (cAMP) low and keeps the cell quiet and the tubule at a fixed diameter. Without this signal, intracellular calcium falls and cAMP rises. In polycystin-deficient cells, cAMP stimulates proliferation and drives chloride secretion through the CFTR channel, with water following. A tubule segment therefore widens, pinches off from its nephron, and keeps filling by secretion. Vasopressin raises cAMP in collecting duct cells, and blocking its V2 receptor slows cyst growth, which supports this mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Every cell carries one mutant allele, yet only a small fraction of nephrons become cysts. The usual explanation is a second, somatic hit that inactivates the normal allele in a single tubular cell, which then expands clonally. Cysts therefore appear focally and accumulate over decades, so kidneys are usually normal in childhood and can reach about 4 kg each by late adulthood."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cyst formation in ADPKD","steps":["Inherited PKD1 or PKD2 mutation in every cell","Somatic second hit in one tubular cell","Ciliary calcium entry lost; cAMP rises","Cell proliferation and CFTR chloride secretion","Segment dilates, detaches, and fills with fluid","Cysts compress parenchyma and intrarenal vessels","Hypertension and progressive loss of GFR"],"caption":"Cysts arise from any segment of the nephron and enlarge for decades."},{"type":"h","text":"Manifestations explained by mechanism"},{"type":"table","title":"ADPKD: findings and their cause","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Bilateral enlarged, lumpy kidneys; palpable flank masses","Thousands of cysts replace the parenchyma"],["Flank or back pain","Capsular stretch, bleeding into a cyst, cyst infection, or stones"],["Hematuria","Rupture of a cyst or of a vessel in its wall into the collecting system"],["Hypertension, often before GFR falls","Cysts compress vessels, local ischemia activates the renin-angiotensin system"],["Nocturia and poor concentrating ability, early","Cysts disrupt the medullary architecture needed for concentration"],["Kidney stones","Urinary stasis and low urinary citrate; uric acid and calcium oxalate stones"],["Progressive renal failure, usually in the fifth or sixth decade","Compression, fibrosis, and loss of normal nephrons"],["Liver cysts (most common extrarenal finding)","Polycystins in bile duct epithelium; cysts larger and more common in women"],["Berry aneurysms, subarachnoid hemorrhage","Polycystins in arterial smooth muscle weaken the wall; hypertension adds stress"],["Mitral valve prolapse; colonic diverticula; abdominal hernias","Abnormal connective tissue and matrix in valve and bowel wall"]],"caption":"Extrarenal findings follow the tissues where polycystins are expressed."},{"type":"p","text":"Intracranial aneurysms occur in roughly 10% of patients, clustering in families with a history of aneurysm, and a ruptured aneurysm presents as a sudden severe headache from subarachnoid hemorrhage. Liver cysts rarely impair liver function because hepatocytes are spared. Cardiovascular disease driven by hypertension is a major cause of death. Ultrasound shows enlarged kidneys with multiple bilateral cysts; the number required for diagnosis rises with age because cysts accumulate."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with ADPKD carry the mutation in every cell from conception yet have normal kidneys at age 10 and huge kidneys at 50?","explain":"A cyst forms only after a second somatic hit inactivates the normal allele in one tubular cell. These hits accumulate randomly over time, and each cyst then grows slowly by proliferation and fluid secretion, so cyst number and size rise with age.","check":"The paragraph on the second hit and the cyst formation flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-05-C03","obj":"UR-12-E.3","title":"ARPKD, multicystic dysplastic kidney, and acquired cystic disease","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD) results from mutations in both alleles of PKHD1 on chromosome 6, which encodes fibrocystin, a ciliary protein of collecting duct and bile duct epithelium. It is uncommon, about 1 in 20,000 births. The collecting ducts dilate into elongated cysts that run at right angles to the capsule, so the cut surface looks spongy with radial channels. Both kidneys are greatly enlarged but keep a smooth surface and their normal shape, unlike the lumpy kidneys of ADPKD."},{"type":"p","text":"Severe disease presents before or at birth. Little urine is made in utero, so oligohydramnios produces Potter sequence, and pulmonary hypoplasia is the usual cause of death in the newborn period. In the liver, the embryonic ductal plate fails to remodel, leaving excess, abnormal bile ducts in fibrotic portal tracts. This congenital hepatic fibrosis blocks portal flow before the sinusoids, so children who survive infancy develop portal hypertension with splenomegaly and varices while hepatocyte function stays normal. Children with milder renal disease present later with hypertension and chronic kidney disease, and the liver may dominate."},{"type":"h","text":"Multicystic dysplastic kidney"},{"type":"p","text":"Multicystic dysplastic kidney is a developmental error, not a single-gene disease, and is usually sporadic. The ureteric bud is often atretic or obstructed, so the metanephric mesenchyme is poorly induced and differentiates abnormally. The result is a nonfunctioning mass of cysts. The diagnostic histology is renal dysplasia: primitive ducts lined by immature epithelium, surrounded by undifferentiated mesenchyme, often with islands of cartilage. It is usually unilateral, and the opposite kidney undergoes compensatory hypertrophy, although that kidney has extra risk of reflux or obstruction. Bilateral disease behaves like bilateral agenesis and is lethal."},{"type":"h","text":"Acquired cystic kidney disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Patients with end-stage kidneys, especially after years of dialysis, develop many cortical and medullary cysts. Cyst number rises with time in renal failure rather than with the dialysis method, which points to uremia and growth stimulation of surviving tubular cells, although the exact signal is not established. The kidneys are usually small or normal in size. Cysts may bleed and cause hematuria, and their lining can transform, so renal cell carcinoma is markedly more common than in the general population."},{"type":"table","title":"Cystic kidney diseases compared","cols":["Feature","ADPKD","ARPKD","Multicystic dysplastic kidney","Acquired cystic disease"],"rows":[["Cause","PKD1 or PKD2, autosomal dominant","PKHD1, autosomal recessive","Developmental, not inherited","Long-standing renal failure and dialysis"],["Usual age","Adults","Fetus, newborn, infant","Fetus or newborn","Adults on dialysis"],["Kidneys","Both, huge, lumpy","Both, huge, smooth","Usually one","Both, usually small to normal"],["Cysts and tissue","From any nephron segment","Radial collecting duct cysts","Cysts with immature ducts, mesenchyme, cartilage","Cortical and medullary cysts in scarred kidney"],["Liver","Cysts, function preserved","Congenital hepatic fibrosis, portal hypertension","Normal","Normal"],["Other key feature","Berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse","Oligohydramnios, Potter sequence","Other kidney hypertrophies","Renal cell carcinoma risk"]],"caption":"Age, laterality, and the liver lesion separate the cystic diseases more reliably than the appearance of the cysts."},{"type":"example","title":"Three patients with cystic kidneys","text":"A newborn with bilateral smooth, enlarged kidneys was born after oligohydramnios and needs ventilation; years later a sibling has splenomegaly and varices with normal liver enzymes. Bilateral disease at birth, a recessive pattern in siblings, and presinusoidal portal hypertension point to ARPKD with congenital hepatic fibrosis. A second newborn has one nonfunctioning cystic flank mass and a normal opposite kidney; one-sided disease with a working partner points to multicystic dysplastic kidney, and the infant survives because the other kidney makes urine. A 58-year-old man on dialysis for 9 years develops hematuria, and imaging shows many cysts and a solid mass in small kidneys: acquired cystic disease with renal cell carcinoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-05-C04","obj":"UR-12-E.4","title":"Renal agenesis, oligohydramnios, and Potter sequence","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The permanent kidney (metanephros) appears in the fifth week, when the ureteric bud grows from the mesonephric duct into the metanephric mesenchyme. The two tissues induce each other. Mesenchymal cells secrete glial cell line-derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF), which acts on the RET receptor of the bud and makes it branch into the ureter, pelvis, calyces, and collecting ducts. The bud tips in turn induce the mesenchyme to form nephrons, from Bowman capsule to the distal convoluted tubule. Renal agenesis follows when the bud fails to form, fails to reach the mesenchyme, or fails to induce it."},{"type":"h","text":"Why bilateral agenesis is lethal"},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal urine production begins late in the first trimester, and from about 16 weeks it supplies most of the amniotic fluid. With no kidneys, almost no fluid accumulates. Oligohydramnios has two effects. The uterine wall presses directly on the fetus, deforming the face and limbs and restricting chest expansion. The lungs also fail to grow, because normal lung development needs fluid retained within the airways and fetal breathing movements against a fluid-filled space. The deformities are therefore a sequence, a cascade from one primary defect, rather than separate malformations."},{"type":"flow","title":"Potter sequence from bilateral renal agenesis","steps":["Ureteric bud fails to form or induce mesenchyme","No kidneys, so no fetal urine","Oligohydramnios from about the second trimester","Uterine compression and restricted lung growth","Flat facies, limb deformities, pulmonary hypoplasia","Respiratory failure and death shortly after birth"],"caption":"Pulmonary hypoplasia, not the absent kidneys, causes death in the first hours or days."},{"type":"table","title":"Features of Potter sequence","cols":["Feature","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Pulmonary hypoplasia","Lack of fluid and chest compression limit airway branching and lung growth"],["Flattened facies: flat nose, receding chin, low-set ears, prominent folds below the eyes","Direct compression of the face against the uterine wall"],["Limb deformities: clubfeet, hip dislocation, joint contractures","Restricted fetal movement in a small space"],["Dry, loose, wrinkled skin","Lack of surrounding fluid"]],"caption":"The same sequence follows any cause of severe early oligohydramnios: bilateral agenesis, ARPKD, bilateral multicystic dysplasia, posterior urethral valves, or prolonged leakage of amniotic fluid."},{"type":"h","text":"Unilateral agenesis and the single kidney"},{"type":"p","text":"Unilateral agenesis is far more common, about 1 in 1,000 births, and usually silent because one kidney makes enough urine to keep amniotic fluid normal. The remaining kidney undergoes compensatory hypertrophy. Nephron number is fixed once nephrogenesis ends at about 34 to 36 weeks of gestation, so the kidney grows by enlarging its existing nephrons: each glomerulus filters more (higher single-nephron GFR), and tubular cells hypertrophy to reabsorb the larger filtered load. Total GFR approaches but usually does not reach that of two kidneys. The same adaptation follows loss of a kidney later in life. Sustained glomerular hyperfiltration can, in some patients over years, cause proteinuria, secondary focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, and hypertension."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the ureteric bud arises from the mesonephric duct, which lies beside the paramesonephric duct, unilateral agenesis is often accompanied by genital anomalies on the same side. Males may lack the vas deferens or seminal vesicle on that side, and females may have a unicornuate uterus or a duplicated uterus with an obstructed hemivagina. Renal hypoplasia differs from agenesis: a small kidney with fewer nephrons but normal structure, which also limits nephron mass."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A fetus has one normal kidney and a contralateral multicystic dysplastic kidney. Will Potter sequence develop?","explain":"No. Amniotic fluid depends on total fetal urine output, and one functioning kidney makes enough, so fluid volume stays normal and the lungs grow. Potter sequence requires failure of both kidneys or blocked outflow from both.","check":"The paragraphs on oligohydramnios and on unilateral agenesis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-05-C05","obj":"UR-12-E.5","title":"Vesicoureteral reflux and reflux nephropathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normally urine moves only downward because the ureterovesical junction works as a flap valve. The distal ureter passes obliquely through the bladder wall and then runs a submucosal tunnel before opening at the trigone. As bladder pressure rises during filling and voiding, that pressure flattens the intravesical segment against the detrusor and seals it. The longer the tunnel relative to the diameter of the ureter, the better the seal."},{"type":"h","text":"Primary and secondary reflux"},{"type":"p","text":"In primary vesicoureteral reflux (VUR), the intravesical segment is congenitally short and the ureteral orifice lies lateral to its usual position, so voiding pressure pushes urine up the ureter instead of closing it. The defect is developmental: a ureteric bud that arises too low on the mesonephric duct is absorbed into the bladder early and ends up with a short tunnel. Primary VUR is found in about 1% of children and in roughly one third of children evaluated after a febrile urinary tract infection, and it is often familial. Secondary VUR occurs when a normal junction is overwhelmed by high bladder pressure, as with posterior urethral valves in boys or a neurogenic bladder."},{"type":"flow","title":"From short intravesical ureter to reflux nephropathy","steps":["Short intravesical ureter; flap valve fails","Voiding pressure drives urine up the ureter","Refluxed urine returns afterward, leaving residual urine","Bladder bacteria are carried to the renal pelvis","Intrarenal reflux through compound papillae at the poles","Recurrent pyelonephritis with parenchymal inflammation","Polar scars over blunted calyces: reflux nephropathy","Hypertension, proteinuria, chronic kidney disease"],"caption":"Infection carried into the parenchyma is the main driver of scarring; high-pressure sterile reflux contributes mainly in secondary VUR."},{"type":"p","text":"Reflux does two harmful things. Urine that rises into the ureter drains back into the bladder after voiding, so the bladder never fully empties, and residual urine lets bacteria multiply. The same flow also carries those bacteria to the renal pelvis, which is why VUR presents as recurrent febrile urinary tract infection and pyelonephritis in young children. Whether infection reaches the parenchyma depends on papillary shape. Simple papillae, which are convex with slit-like duct openings, close under pressure. Compound papillae, which are flat or concave with gaping ducts and cluster at the upper and lower poles, let urine pass into the collecting ducts (intrarenal reflux)."},{"type":"p","text":"Inflammation in these regions heals as broad, coarse cortical scars that sit over dilated, blunted calyces, mostly at the poles. This is reflux nephropathy, the classic form of chronic pyelonephritis. As functioning nephrons are lost, the rest hyperfilter, and renin release from scarred regions raises blood pressure, so children and young adults develop hypertension, proteinuria, and chronic kidney disease. Mild primary reflux often resolves with growth because the intravesical tunnel lengthens."},{"type":"h","text":"Diagnosis and grading"},{"type":"p","text":"The voiding cystourethrogram (VCUG) is the diagnostic study. Contrast is instilled into the bladder through a catheter, and films are taken during filling and voiding, when bladder pressure peaks and reflux is most likely to appear. It shows whether contrast rises into the ureter and pelvis, grades the reflux, and outlines the urethra, which identifies secondary causes such as posterior urethral valves. Renal ultrasound shows dilation but cannot exclude reflux, and a DMSA nuclear scan shows scars rather than reflux itself."},{"type":"table","title":"International grading of reflux on VCUG","cols":["Grade","Findings"],"rows":[["I","Contrast enters the ureter only, without dilation"],["II","Reaches pelvis and calyces without dilation"],["III","Mild to moderate dilation of ureter and pelvis, mild blunting of calyces"],["IV","Moderate dilation and tortuosity; calyceal angles lost, papillary impressions kept"],["V","Gross dilation and tortuosity; papillary impressions lost"]],"caption":"Higher grade predicts more scarring and less spontaneous resolution."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every disorder in this chapter starts at a defined point in development or in a protein, and the extrarenal findings come from the same point. Type IV collagen alpha 3, 4, and 5 chains shared by the GBM, cochlea, and lens explain the triad of Alport syndrome. Ciliary proteins shared by renal tubules, bile ducts, and arteries explain why ADPKD brings liver cysts and aneurysms, and why ARPKD brings congenital hepatic fibrosis. The ureteric bud, which grows from the mesonephric duct, links renal agenesis to ipsilateral genital anomalies and links primary reflux to an orifice placed too far laterally. Amniotic fluid physiology connects any failure of fetal urine output to the lungs, so bilateral agenesis, severe ARPKD, bilateral dysplasia, and urethral obstruction all produce the same Potter sequence."},{"type":"p","text":"The chapter also shows that the kidney responds to lost nephron mass in one way, whatever the cause. A single kidney after agenesis, a kidney opposite a dysplastic one, and the surviving nephrons in reflux nephropathy all hyperfilter, and in some patients this adaptation leads over years to proteinuria, glomerulosclerosis, and hypertension. The limits of these associations matter: carrier females in X-linked Alport syndrome may have only hematuria, many patients with ADPKD never develop an aneurysm, and most children with mild reflux never scar."}],"summary":["Alport syndrome is failure of the alpha 3, 4, 5 type IV collagen network, most often from X-linked COL4A5 mutation, so males are affected most severely.","Alport syndrome causes hematuria progressing to renal failure, high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss, and anterior lenticonus, with a split, basket-weave GBM on electron microscopy.","ADPKD comes from PKD1 or PKD2 mutation; loss of ciliary polycystin signaling lowers intracellular calcium and raises cAMP, which drives cyst proliferation and fluid secretion.","ADPKD causes bilaterally enlarged cystic kidneys in adults, with flank pain, hematuria, early hypertension, and progressive renal failure; liver cysts are the most common extrarenal finding, and berry aneurysms and mitral valve prolapse also occur.","ARPKD (PKHD1, fibrocystin) presents in infancy with bilaterally enlarged smooth kidneys of radial collecting duct cysts, Potter sequence when severe, and congenital hepatic fibrosis with portal hypertension.","Multicystic dysplastic kidney is a sporadic developmental lesion, usually unilateral, with immature ducts, mesenchyme, and cartilage; the other kidney compensates.","Acquired cystic disease develops in end-stage kidneys on long-term dialysis and raises the risk of renal cell carcinoma.","Bilateral renal agenesis removes fetal urine, causing oligohydramnios and Potter sequence; pulmonary hypoplasia causes death.","A single kidney grows by hypertrophy and hyperfiltration of existing nephrons, because no new nephrons form after about 36 weeks of gestation.","Primary VUR results from a short intravesical ureter that defeats the flap valve, causing recurrent pyelonephritis and polar reflux nephropathy scars; the voiding cystourethrogram diagnoses and grades it."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney: cystic diseases of the kidney, hereditary nephritis (Alport syndrome), and chronic pyelonephritis and reflux nephropathy"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System: development of the urinary system and its abnormalities"},{"book":"Moore and Persaud The Developing Human","where":"Urogenital System: development of the kidneys and ureters, renal agenesis"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins: collagen structure and synthesis"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal Disease"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Glomerular filtration and the structure of the filtration barrier"}]},"UR-12-F":{"unit":"UR-12-F","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-06","title":"Vascular Diseases of the Kidney","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how disease of the renal vessels injures the kidney. It covers thrombotic microangiopathy (hemolytic uremic syndrome and its contrast with TTP), hypertensive arteriolar disease, renal artery stenosis, infarction, sickle cell injury to the medulla, and renal vein thrombosis. The kidney receives about 20% of cardiac output through end arteries and a medulla that runs close to hypoxia, so each vessel lesion produces a predictable pattern. Board items describe a blood smear, an arteriolar lesion, a gross kidney, or a bruit and ask you to predict blood pressure, renin, urine findings, and kidney size.","prerequisites":["Renal arterial tree, end-artery supply, and the vasa recta (UR-2, UR-5)","Juxtaglomerular apparatus and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (UR-5, UR-7)","Countercurrent multiplication and urine concentration (UR-6)","Hemostasis: platelets, von Willebrand factor, coagulation tests (PT, aPTT)","Hemoglobin S polymerization and the oxygen dissociation curve"],"connections":[{"to":"Hematology","how":"HUS, TTP, and sickle cell disease are blood disorders whose most important end-organ injury is in the kidney."},{"to":"Cardiovascular pathology (CV-18)","how":"Hyaline and hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus, and endocarditis link heart and vessel disease to renal injury."},{"to":"Glomerular disease (UR-12-A)","how":"Nephrotic syndrome, especially membranous nephropathy, creates the hypercoagulable state behind renal vein thrombosis."},{"to":"Microbiology of enteric pathogens (GI)","how":"Shiga toxin from enterohemorrhagic E. coli O157:H7 is the cause of typical HUS."},{"to":"Papillary necrosis and renal tumors (UR-12-D, UR-12-E)","how":"Sickle cell disease and trait cause papillary necrosis, and renal cell carcinoma can grow into the renal vein."}],"terms":[{"term":"Thrombotic microangiopathy","def":"Injury of small vessels with platelet or platelet-fibrin thrombi that narrow arterioles and capillaries, producing hemolysis and platelet consumption."},{"term":"Schistocyte","def":"A fragmented red cell (helmet or triangle shape) formed when red cells are sheared by strands of fibrin or platelet thrombi in small vessels."},{"term":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia","def":"Intravascular hemolysis caused by mechanical fragmentation of red cells in damaged small vessels, with schistocytes on the smear and a negative direct Coombs test."},{"term":"ADAMTS13","def":"A plasma metalloprotease that cleaves ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers; its deficiency causes TTP."},{"term":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","def":"Homogeneous pink thickening of arteriolar walls from leaked plasma proteins and excess basement membrane matrix, typical of chronic hypertension, diabetes, and aging."},{"term":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","def":"Concentric, laminated (onion-skin) thickening of arteriolar walls by proliferating smooth muscle cells and reduplicated basement membrane, typical of severe hypertension."},{"term":"Fibrinoid necrosis","def":"Necrosis of a vessel wall in which leaked plasma proteins and fibrin give a bright pink, smudgy appearance."},{"term":"Fibromuscular dysplasia","def":"A noninflammatory, nonatherosclerotic thickening of medium-sized artery walls that alternates with thin segments, giving a string of beads pattern."},{"term":"Goldblatt kidney","def":"A kidney rendered ischemic by renal artery narrowing, which secretes renin and causes renovascular hypertension."},{"term":"End artery","def":"An artery whose territory has no effective collateral supply, so its occlusion causes infarction of that territory."},{"term":"Isosthenuria","def":"Excretion of urine whose osmolality stays close to that of plasma (about 300 mOsm/kg) regardless of hydration, from loss of concentrating and diluting ability."},{"term":"Papillary necrosis","def":"Ischemic necrosis of the renal papillary tips, which may slough into the collecting system."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-06-C01","obj":"UR-12-F.1","title":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome and its contrast with TTP","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) is a thrombotic microangiopathy with a defining triad: microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury. Typical HUS is a leading cause of acute kidney injury in young children. It follows bloody diarrhea from Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli, most often serotype O157:H7, acquired from undercooked ground beef, unpasteurized milk or juice, contaminated produce, or person-to-person spread. Renal failure begins about 5 to 10 days after the diarrhea starts, often as the diarrhea is improving."},{"type":"h","text":"How Shiga toxin injures endothelium"},{"type":"p","text":"Shiga toxin is an AB5 toxin. It does not need the bacteria to invade: it crosses the inflamed colonic mucosa into the blood. The five B subunits bind the glycolipid globotriaosylceramide (Gb3), which glomerular endothelial cells express in abundance, so the kidney bears the brunt. After uptake, the A subunit acts as an N-glycosidase that removes one adenine from the 28S rRNA of the 60S ribosomal subunit, halting protein synthesis. The injured endothelium swells, detaches, loses its anticoagulant surface, and releases ultra-large von Willebrand factor, so platelets adhere and platelet-fibrin thrombi form in glomerular capillaries and afferent arterioles."},{"type":"flow","title":"From Shiga toxin to the HUS triad","steps":["E. coli O157:H7 colitis causes bloody diarrhea","Shiga toxin enters the blood","B subunits bind Gb3 on glomerular endothelium","A subunit cleaves 28S rRNA; protein synthesis stops","Endothelial injury exposes a prothrombotic surface","Platelet-fibrin microthrombi narrow glomerular capillaries","Red cells are sheared into schistocytes; platelets are consumed","Glomerular filtration falls: acute kidney injury"],"caption":"Endothelial injury is the starting point; hemolysis, thrombocytopenia, and renal failure all follow from microthrombi in small renal vessels."},{"type":"p","text":"Each part of the triad follows from the microthrombi. Red cells forced through narrowed vessels are fragmented, producing schistocytes, a high LDH, high indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin, and a high reticulocyte count. Because the hemolysis is mechanical, the direct Coombs test is negative. Platelets are consumed in thrombi, but clotting factors are not consumed systemically, so PT and aPTT stay normal, which separates HUS and TTP from disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). Occluded glomeruli cause oliguria, hematuria, proteinuria, and azotemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) produces the same hemolysis and thrombocytopenia by a different route. ADAMTS13 normally trims ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers; when it is deficient, usually from an acquired autoantibody and rarely from inherited mutation, these multimers bind platelets spontaneously and form platelet-rich, fibrin-poor thrombi throughout the circulation. TTP mainly affects adults, especially women, and the brain is the dominant target, so confusion, headache, and focal deficits are common while renal injury is usually milder. Atypical HUS, a third form, results from uncontrolled alternative complement activation (for example factor H mutations) without diarrhea."},{"type":"table","title":"HUS, TTP, and DIC","cols":["Feature","Typical HUS","TTP","DIC"],"rows":[["Usual patient","Young child after bloody diarrhea","Adult, more often women","Sepsis, trauma, obstetric catastrophe, malignancy"],["Primary defect","Shiga toxin injury to endothelium","ADAMTS13 deficiency, ultra-large vWF multimers","Systemic activation of coagulation"],["Thrombi","Platelet-fibrin, mainly kidney","Platelet-rich, widespread, brain prominent","Fibrin-rich, widespread"],["Dominant organ injury","Kidney","Brain","Variable, with bleeding"],["PT and aPTT","Normal","Normal","Prolonged"],["Fibrinogen and D-dimer","Usually near normal","Usually near normal","Fibrinogen low, D-dimer high"],["Schistocytes and thrombocytopenia","Present","Present","Present"]],"caption":"Normal clotting times point to a platelet-driven microangiopathy (HUS or TTP) rather than DIC; the patient and the dominant organ then separate HUS from TTP."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 4-year-old has pallor, petechiae, and low urine output a week after bloody diarrhea. Predict the smear, the direct Coombs test, and the PT.","explain":"Schistocytes from mechanical shearing, a negative Coombs test because no antibody coats the red cells, and a normal PT because clotting factors are not consumed.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the triad and the HUS, TTP, and DIC table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-06-C02","obj":"UR-12-F.2","title":"Benign nephrosclerosis and malignant hypertension","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypertension damages the kidney through its arterioles, and the pattern depends on how high and how fast the pressure rises. The two lesions differ microscopically and grossly, and boards expect you to match each to its setting and its kidney."},{"type":"h","text":"Benign nephrosclerosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Benign nephrosclerosis is the renal lesion of long-standing, moderately elevated pressure. Chronic hemodynamic stress injures arteriolar endothelium, plasma proteins seep into the wall, and smooth muscle cells lay down excess basement membrane matrix. The result is hyaline arteriolosclerosis: a homogeneous pink, glassy wall with a narrowed lumen. Larger interlobular and arcuate arteries show medial hypertrophy and intimal fibroelastic thickening. Because the narrowing is slow and diffuse, nephrons drop out patchily by ischemia: glomeruli collapse and sclerose, and their tubules atrophy and are replaced by fibrosis. Scattered scars pulled down between surviving nephrons give both kidneys a symmetric reduction in size with a finely granular surface and a thinned cortex. The usual clinical picture is mild proteinuria and slowly declining GFR. The same arteriolar lesion occurs with aging and diabetes, so hyaline arteriolosclerosis alone does not prove hypertension."},{"type":"h","text":"Malignant hypertension"},{"type":"p","text":"Malignant hypertension is a severe, often abrupt rise in pressure, commonly with diastolic pressure above 120 mm Hg, accompanied by acute organ damage such as papilledema, retinal hemorrhages, encephalopathy, and acute kidney injury. It can arise on top of benign hypertension or secondary causes such as renal artery stenosis or systemic sclerosis. Two arteriolar lesions appear. Fibrinoid necrosis occurs when the wall gives way and fibrin and plasma proteins flood into it. Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis develops as smooth muscle cells proliferate and lay down concentric layers of basement membrane, producing the onion-skin appearance that nearly occludes the lumen. Ruptured arterioles and capillaries bleed into the cortex, so the kidney surface shows pinpoint petechial hemorrhages: the flea-bitten kidney. Patients have rapidly rising creatinine, hematuria, and proteinuria, and endothelial damage can produce schistocytes and thrombocytopenia as a secondary microangiopathy."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The renin loop of malignant hypertension","steps":["Severe pressure injures arteriolar endothelium","Fibrinoid necrosis and onion-skin narrowing","Glomeruli and juxtaglomerular cells become ischemic","Renin, angiotensin II, and aldosterone rise sharply","Vasoconstriction and sodium retention raise pressure further"],"caption":"Arteriolar narrowing makes the kidney behave as if it were underperfused, so renin release drives the pressure still higher."},{"type":"table","title":"Benign nephrosclerosis versus malignant hypertension","cols":["Feature","Benign nephrosclerosis","Malignant hypertension"],"rows":[["Pressure and tempo","Moderate, sustained for years","Severe, rapid rise with acute organ damage"],["Arteriolar lesion","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","Hyperplastic (onion-skin) arteriolosclerosis and fibrinoid necrosis"],["Gross kidney","Symmetrically small, finely granular","Normal or slightly small, petechial (flea-bitten) surface"],["Renal course","Slow decline, mild proteinuria","Acute kidney injury, hematuria, proteinuria"],["Renin","Usually normal","Markedly elevated"],["Other organs","Left ventricular hypertrophy over time","Papilledema, retinal hemorrhages, encephalopathy"]],"caption":"The words hyaline and granular signal chronic disease; onion skin, fibrinoid necrosis, and flea-bitten signal an emergency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-06-C03","obj":"UR-12-F.3","title":"Renal artery stenosis and renovascular hypertension","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal artery stenosis causes only a small share of hypertension (about 2 to 5%), but it matters because it is potentially curable. Two lesions account for nearly all cases. Atherosclerosis, responsible for about two thirds or more, affects older adults with the usual vascular risk factors (smoking, diabetes, dyslipidemia, other atherosclerotic disease); the plaque sits at the ostium or the proximal third of the artery, often as an extension of aortic plaque. Fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD) affects mainly women under about 50. It is a noninflammatory, nonatherosclerotic thickening of the arterial wall, most often of the media, that alternates with thinned segments, producing a string of beads pattern in the middle and distal artery on angiography. FMD can involve the carotid and vertebral arteries as well."},{"type":"table","title":"Atherosclerotic stenosis versus fibromuscular dysplasia","cols":["Feature","Atherosclerotic","Fibromuscular dysplasia"],"rows":[["Patient","Older adult with vascular risk factors","Young to middle-aged woman"],["Location","Ostium or proximal artery","Mid to distal artery, sometimes bilateral"],["Wall lesion","Lipid-rich atheromatous plaque","Fibrous and muscular wall thickening, usually medial"],["Imaging","Focal proximal narrowing","String of beads"],["Course","Progressive, with other vascular disease","Often found as new hypertension in a young patient"]],"caption":"Age, sex, and the position of the narrowing separate the two causes."},{"type":"h","text":"Why stenosis raises blood pressure"},{"type":"p","text":"The juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole act as intrarenal baroreceptors. A stenosis lowers perfusion pressure beyond it, so they release renin even though systemic pressure is normal or high. Angiotensin II constricts arterioles throughout the body, and aldosterone retains sodium and water, which raises pressure. Aldosterone also increases potassium and hydrogen secretion in the collecting duct, so some patients have hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis (secondary hyperaldosteronism, with high renin and high aldosterone). Turbulent flow across the narrowed lumen produces a systolic or continuous bruit over the abdomen or flank."},{"type":"flow","title":"Renovascular hypertension","steps":["Stenosis lowers afferent arteriolar perfusion pressure","Juxtaglomerular cells release renin","Angiotensin II constricts systemic arterioles","Aldosterone retains sodium; secretes potassium and hydrogen ions","Systemic hypertension, sometimes with hypokalemia"],"caption":"The stenotic kidney reads its low perfusion as low body volume and defends a pressure that the rest of the body does not need."},{"type":"p","text":"The two kidneys then age differently. The stenotic kidney is shielded from the high systemic pressure but chronically underperfused, so its tubules atrophy and it shrinks (ischemic atrophy); a size difference between the kidneys is a clue. The opposite kidney receives the full hypertensive pressure and develops arteriolosclerosis. In the affected kidney, angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole, which holds glomerular capillary pressure and GFR up despite low inflow. Blocking angiotensin II formation removes that support."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a rise in creatinine after an ACE inhibitor","text":"A 70-year-old smoker with resistant hypertension and an abdominal bruit starts an ACE inhibitor, and his creatinine rises from 1.2 to 2.0 mg/dL. Reason it through. GFR depends on glomerular capillary pressure, which sits between afferent inflow and efferent outflow resistance. With proximal stenosis, inflow pressure is low, and the kidney preserves filtration by letting angiotensin II constrict the efferent arteriole. The ACE inhibitor removes angiotensin II, the efferent arteriole relaxes, glomerular pressure falls, and GFR drops. If only one kidney is stenotic, the healthy kidney usually compensates. A sharp rise in creatinine therefore suggests bilateral stenosis or stenosis of a solitary functioning kidney."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-06-C04","obj":"UR-12-F.4","title":"Renal infarction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The renal artery divides into segmental, interlobar, arcuate, and interlobular arteries, and these branches behave as end arteries with no useful collateral flow between them. Occlusion of a branch therefore kills the whole territory it supplies. Because the kidney is a solid organ with a single arterial supply, the dead tissue undergoes coagulative necrosis: cell outlines persist while nuclei disappear, and little blood enters the dead zone, so the infarct is pale (anemic) rather than hemorrhagic. The vessels fan outward from the hilum, so the infarct is wedge-shaped, with its base at the capsule and its apex pointing toward the hilum. Early on, a red hyperemic rim surrounds the pale zone. Over weeks, the necrotic tissue is replaced by fibrous scar, leaving a depressed V-shaped cortical scar."},{"type":"p","text":"Most renal infarcts are embolic. The kidneys receive about 20% of cardiac output straight from the aorta, so thrombi from the left heart reach them easily. The main sources are left atrial thrombi in atrial fibrillation, left ventricular mural thrombi after a myocardial infarction or in dilated cardiomyopathy, and vegetations of infective endocarditis (septic emboli can also cause abscesses). Less often the artery thromboses in place, over an atherosclerotic plaque, after aortic or renal artery dissection or trauma, or in a hypercoagulable state."},{"type":"p","text":"Clinically, many small infarcts are silent. A larger one causes sudden flank or abdominal pain, nausea, fever, and hematuria, sometimes with a sudden rise in blood pressure from renin released by the ischemic margin. Serum LDH rises from the dead tissue, which helps distinguish infarction from kidney stones, a common mimic. Renal function falls substantially only when both kidneys or a solitary kidney are affected."},{"type":"p","text":"Atheroembolic renal disease is a separate entity. Cholesterol crystals released from ulcerated aortic plaque, often after angiography or aortic surgery, lodge in many small arcuate and interlobular arteries. They leave needle-shaped clefts in the vessels, cause many tiny infarcts and a slow decline in GFR, and are often accompanied by livedo reticularis and transient eosinophilia rather than a single wedge."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-06-C05","obj":"UR-12-F.5","title":"Sickle cell nephropathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hemoglobin S (HbS) differs from hemoglobin A by a single substitution, valine for glutamate at position 6 of the beta chain. Deoxygenated HbS polymerizes into long fibers that distort the red cell, and the delay before polymerization shortens steeply as the HbS concentration rises. Sickling is therefore favored by low oxygen, red cell dehydration (which concentrates HbS), and low pH (which, through the Bohr effect, lowers oxygen affinity and increases the deoxygenated fraction). The renal medulla supplies all three at once."},{"type":"p","text":"Countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta lets oxygen diffuse from descending to ascending vessels, so the inner medulla has a low oxygen tension even in health. Its interstitium is strongly hypertonic, reaching about 1,200 mOsm/kg in maximally concentrated states, which draws water out of red cells. Accumulated CO2 and acid keep the pH low. Blood also moves slowly through the long vasa recta, giving polymers time to form. Sickled cells occlude the vasa recta and cause microinfarcts in the medulla and papillae."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why the medulla sickles","steps":["Countercurrent exchange keeps medullary oxygen low","Hypertonic interstitium dehydrates red cells, concentrating HbS","Low pH lowers oxygen affinity (Bohr effect)","Slow vasa recta flow allows polymerization","Sickled cells occlude vasa recta","Medullary microinfarcts and papillary necrosis"],"caption":"The features that let the medulla concentrate urine are the same ones that make it the ideal place for HbS to sickle."},{"type":"p","text":"The consequences follow from what the medulla does. Loss of vasa recta and of the deep loops of Henle destroys the countercurrent gradient, so the urine can no longer be concentrated much above plasma. This defect usually appears in childhood and produces isosthenuria (urine near 300 mOsm/kg, specific gravity about 1.010), polyuria, nocturia, enuresis, and a heightened risk of dehydration, which in turn promotes more sickling. Ischemic papillae can necrose and slough, causing hematuria, colicky pain, or ureteral obstruction. Painless gross or microscopic hematuria from medullary microinfarcts is common, and it occurs more often from the left kidney. Distal tubular dysfunction can impair potassium and hydrogen ion secretion."},{"type":"p","text":"Sickle cell trait (HbAS) carries enough HbS, about 35 to 40%, to sickle under the extreme medullary conditions even though trait carriers are not anemic and rarely sickle elsewhere. Hematuria, a mild concentrating defect, and papillary necrosis therefore occur in trait as well as in disease. Trait is also associated with renal medullary carcinoma, a rare, aggressive tumor of young people. In sickle cell disease (HbSS), anemia also drives cortical hyperfiltration, which over years leads to proteinuria and focal segmental glomerulosclerosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 19-year-old athlete with sickle cell trait has painless hematuria and cannot concentrate urine after overnight water restriction. Why does trait injure the kidney when it spares other organs?","explain":"Only the medulla combines low oxygen, high osmolality, and low pH with slow flow, which together push the modest HbS fraction in trait red cells to polymerize.","check":"Compare with the flow on why the medulla sickles and the paragraph on sickle cell trait."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-06-C06","obj":"UR-12-F.6","title":"Renal vein thrombosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal vein thrombosis follows Virchow's triad: stasis, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability. Nephrotic syndrome supplies hypercoagulability and infant dehydration supplies stasis. Renal cell carcinoma deserves special mention because, unlike most tumors, it characteristically grows as a solid tumor thrombus along the renal vein, sometimes extending through the inferior vena cava into the right atrium."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of renal vein thrombosis","cols":["Setting","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Nephrotic syndrome, especially membranous nephropathy","Hypercoagulability from urinary loss of antithrombin and increased hepatic synthesis of fibrinogen and other clotting factors"],["Renal cell carcinoma","Tumor grows directly into the renal vein and may extend into the inferior vena cava; tumor and attached thrombus obstruct flow"],["Dehydrated infant","Hemoconcentration and low renal venous flow (stasis); risk is higher in infants of diabetic mothers"],["Other causes","Trauma, extrinsic compression, oral contraceptives, inherited thrombophilias"]],"caption":"Nephrotic syndrome is the most common cause in adults, and membranous nephropathy carries the highest risk among nephrotic diseases."},{"type":"p","text":"Nephrotic syndrome is hypercoagulable for several reasons. Antithrombin is a small protein, about the size of albumin, so it leaks through the damaged filtration barrier and its plasma level falls. The liver responds to low plasma oncotic pressure by increasing synthesis of many proteins, including fibrinogen and factors V and VIII, which are too large to be lost in the urine. Platelet aggregability increases, and intravascular volume depletion adds hemoconcentration. The risk rises as serum albumin falls. Why membranous nephropathy carries a higher risk than other nephrotic lesions is not fully explained."},{"type":"flow","title":"From renal vein occlusion to the clinical picture","steps":["Thrombus blocks renal venous outflow","Back pressure congests peritubular and glomerular capillaries","Kidney swells with edema and hemorrhage","Congested glomeruli leak more blood and protein","Flank pain, hematuria, rising proteinuria, enlarged kidney"],"caption":"Venous obstruction enlarges the kidney, the opposite of arterial disease, which shrinks it."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute, complete occlusion causes sudden flank pain, gross hematuria, an enlarged and sometimes palpable kidney, a rise in proteinuria, and a fall in GFR, which is severe when both veins are involved. In a neonate the triad is a flank mass, hematuria, and thrombocytopenia. Gradual occlusion in an adult is often silent because collateral veins (capsular, ureteric, and on the left side gonadal and adrenal) develop, and it may be discovered only after a pulmonary embolism. The left renal vein receives the left gonadal vein, so left renal vein obstruction, by thrombus or by renal cell carcinoma, raises pressure in the left pampiniform plexus and can cause a new left varicocele that does not empty when the patient lies down."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology explain why vessel disease is so damaging here. End arteries make every branch occlusion a complete infarct, the afferent arteriole holds the renin sensor that turns stenosis and arteriolar narrowing into systemic hypertension, and the countercurrent design that concentrates urine also keeps the medulla hypoxic, hypertonic, and acidic, which is why HbS sickles there. The direction of the lesion predicts kidney size: arterial narrowing shrinks the kidney by ischemic atrophy, while venous obstruction swells it with congestion."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology, biochemistry, immunology, and pathology meet in the microangiopathies. A bacterial toxin that cleaves rRNA (HUS), an autoantibody against a protease (TTP), complement dysregulation (atypical HUS), and severe hypertension all converge on endothelial injury with schistocytes and thrombocytopenia, and normal clotting times separate them from DIC. These distinctions have limits: malignant hypertension and systemic sclerosis can also produce a secondary microangiopathy, so the setting and the organ pattern must be read together with the smear."}],"summary":["Typical HUS follows E. coli O157:H7 colitis in a child: Shiga toxin binds Gb3 on glomerular endothelium, cleaves 28S rRNA, and triggers platelet-fibrin microthrombi.","The triad of HUS and TTP is schistocytes with negative Coombs test, thrombocytopenia, and organ injury, with normal PT and aPTT, unlike DIC.","TTP results from ADAMTS13 deficiency and ultra-large vWF multimers, affects mainly adults, and injures the brain more than the kidney.","Benign nephrosclerosis shows hyaline arteriolosclerosis and symmetric, finely granular small kidneys; malignant hypertension shows onion-skin arteriolosclerosis, fibrinoid necrosis, a flea-bitten kidney, and acute kidney injury.","Renal artery stenosis raises renin through low afferent perfusion: atherosclerosis at the ostium in older adults, fibromuscular dysplasia with a string of beads in young women.","The stenotic kidney shrinks, depends on angiotensin II efferent constriction to maintain GFR, and the opposite kidney develops arteriolosclerosis.","Renal infarcts are usually embolic from the left heart and appear as pale, wedge-shaped coagulative necrosis with flank pain, hematuria, and high LDH.","Low oxygen, high osmolality, low pH, and slow flow make the medulla sickle, causing isosthenuria, hematuria, and papillary necrosis in disease and trait.","Renal vein thrombosis complicates nephrotic syndrome (especially membranous), renal cell carcinoma, and infant dehydration, and enlarges the kidney with flank pain and hematuria."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, diseases of blood vessels and thrombotic microangiopathies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, hypertensive vascular disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Role of the kidneys in long-term control of arterial pressure; renin-angiotensin system"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Urine concentration and dilution; the countercurrent mechanism and the vasa recta"},{"book":"Jawetz Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric gram-negative rods, Escherichia coli and Shiga toxin"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal disease and blood disorders, sections on thrombotic microangiopathy and sickle cell anemia"}]},"UR-12-G":{"unit":"UR-12-G","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-07","title":"Neoplasms of the Urinary Tract","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the tumors of the kidney and urinary tract: clear cell renal cell carcinoma and its presentations, the other renal epithelial tumors, Wilms tumor in children, and the urothelial, squamous, and glandular carcinomas of the bladder and collecting system. Each tumor is explained from its cell of origin and its genetic lesion, because those two facts predict the gross and microscopic appearance, the hormones the tumor makes, and the way it spreads. Board questions usually give an age, an exposure, a laboratory abnormality, or a histologic description and ask you to name the tumor or the mechanism behind a finding.","prerequisites":["Histology of the nephron and collecting duct, including proximal tubule and intercalated cells, and of the urothelium","Tumor suppressor genes, oncogenes, the two-hit model, and genomic imprinting","Endocrine functions of the kidney (erythropoietin, renin) and the regulation of plasma calcium by PTH","Venous drainage of the kidneys and gonads into the renal veins and inferior vena cava","Embryology of the metanephros, cloaca, allantois, and ventral body wall"],"connections":[{"to":"Hereditary renal disease (UR-9)","how":"Von Hippel-Lindau disease, tuberous sclerosis, and hereditary papillary renal cancer show how germline mutations create multiple, bilateral, early-onset renal tumors."},{"to":"Vascular diseases of the kidney (UR-12-F)","how":"Renal cell carcinoma growing into the renal vein is a cause of renal vein thrombosis and of a left varicocele."},{"to":"Endocrinology and calcium metabolism","how":"Paraneoplastic erythropoietin, PTHrP, renin, and ACTH explain polycythemia, hypercalcemia, hypertension, and Cushing syndrome in renal cell carcinoma."},{"to":"Microbiology (trematodes)","how":"Chronic Schistosoma haematobium infection of the bladder wall is the leading cause of bladder squamous cell carcinoma worldwide."},{"to":"Pediatric tumors and neural crest derivatives","how":"Wilms tumor must be separated from neuroblastoma, the other common abdominal mass of early childhood."}],"terms":[{"term":"Renal cell carcinoma (RCC)","def":"A malignant tumor arising from renal tubular epithelium, the most common primary kidney cancer in adults."},{"term":"VHL gene","def":"A tumor suppressor on chromosome 3p whose protein targets hypoxia-inducible factor for destruction when oxygen is present."},{"term":"Hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF)","def":"A transcription factor that switches on genes for angiogenesis, glycolysis, and erythropoietin when cells sense low oxygen."},{"term":"Paraneoplastic syndrome","def":"Signs and symptoms caused by substances a tumor secretes rather than by local growth or metastasis."},{"term":"Tumor thrombus","def":"A column of tumor tissue growing within the lumen of a vein, as renal cell carcinoma does in the renal vein and inferior vena cava."},{"term":"Oncocyte","def":"A large epithelial cell with granular pink cytoplasm caused by a great excess of mitochondria."},{"term":"Nephrogenic rest","def":"A focus of persistent embryonic metanephric tissue in the postnatal kidney that can give rise to Wilms tumor."},{"term":"Triphasic histology","def":"The combination of blastemal, stromal, and epithelial elements that characterizes Wilms tumor."},{"term":"Urothelium","def":"The transitional epithelium lining the renal pelvis, ureters, bladder, and proximal urethra."},{"term":"Field effect","def":"Carcinogenic injury of a whole epithelial surface, so that tumors arise at several sites and recur at new ones."},{"term":"Carcinoma in situ (CIS) of the bladder","def":"A flat, high grade urothelial neoplasm confined to the epithelium that has a high risk of invasion."},{"term":"Urachus","def":"The fibrous remnant of the allantois that runs from the bladder dome to the umbilicus as the median umbilical ligament."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-07-C01","obj":"UR-12-G.1","title":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma: origin, genetics, morphology, and venous spread","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) accounts for about 85 to 90% of primary malignant kidney tumors in adults. It peaks in the sixth and seventh decades and is about twice as common in men. The clear cell type makes up about 70 to 80% of cases and arises from the epithelium of the proximal tubule, so it usually forms a solitary cortical mass, often at one pole."},{"type":"h","text":"Molecular mechanism: pseudohypoxia from VHL loss"},{"type":"p","text":"The VHL tumor suppressor gene lies on chromosome 3p25. Its protein is the substrate-recognition part of an E3 ubiquitin ligase. When oxygen is present, prolyl hydroxylases use O2 to hydroxylate HIF-alpha, VHL binds the hydroxylated HIF-alpha, and it is destroyed in the proteasome. Most sporadic clear cell tumors lose both VHL copies, typically by deletion of 3p plus mutation or promoter methylation of the remaining allele. HIF-alpha then accumulates even in well oxygenated cells, pairs with HIF-beta, and drives the genes a hypoxic cell would use. In von Hippel-Lindau disease one defective allele is inherited, so only one further hit is needed and tumors are multiple, bilateral, and appear at a younger age."},{"type":"flow","title":"From VHL loss to a vascular, invasive tumor","steps":["Both VHL alleles inactivated in a proximal tubule cell","HIF-alpha escapes ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation","HIF activates VEGF, PDGF, TGF-alpha, GLUT1, and erythropoietin genes","Angiogenesis, growth signaling, and glycolytic metabolism","Highly vascular tumor that invades the renal vein"],"caption":"Loss of VHL makes the cell behave as if it were hypoxic, which explains the dense vasculature and the growth drive."},{"type":"h","text":"Gross and microscopic appearance"},{"type":"p","text":"The cut surface is golden yellow because the cells store lipid, mottled with gray-white fibrous areas, red-brown hemorrhage, soft necrosis, and cysts. Microscopically, round to polygonal cells have abundant clear cytoplasm, because lipid and glycogen dissolve during tissue processing and leave empty-looking cells. The cells sit in nests and sheets separated by a delicate branching network of thin-walled vessels, the morphologic signature of HIF-driven VEGF, which also explains the frequent hemorrhage. Higher grade tumors show larger nuclei with prominent nucleoli, and sarcomatoid change carries the worst prognosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for renal cell carcinoma","cols":["Risk factor","Note on mechanism or context"],"rows":[["Cigarette smoking","Strongest modifiable risk factor; risk roughly doubles"],["Obesity","Proposed mechanisms include insulin and IGF-1 signaling, adipokines, and lipid peroxidation"],["Hypertension","Independent association; the mechanism is not settled"],["Acquired cystic kidney disease","Cysts that form in end-stage kidneys during long-term dialysis develop renal carcinomas far more often than normal kidneys"],["Von Hippel-Lindau disease","Germline VHL mutation; bilateral multifocal clear cell RCC with cerebellar and retinal hemangioblastomas and pheochromocytoma"],["Other exposures and syndromes","Cadmium and trichloroethylene exposure; tuberous sclerosis"]],"caption":"Smoking, obesity, and hypertension drive most sporadic risk; dialysis cysts and VHL disease are special settings."},{"type":"h","text":"Venous invasion and spread"},{"type":"p","text":"Clear cell carcinoma has a strong tendency to grow into the renal vein as a solid tumor thrombus, which can extend up the inferior vena cava and occasionally into the right atrium. This venous route explains why metastasis is mainly hematogenous: tumor fragments carried in caval blood pass through the right heart and lodge first in the pulmonary capillary bed, so the lung is the most common metastatic site, followed by bone. Regional lymph nodes, adrenal, liver, and brain are also involved."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why might a patient with clear cell RCC have a hematocrit above the reference range even though arterial oxygen saturation is normal?","explain":"The erythropoietin gene is a HIF target. With VHL lost, HIF stays active in tumor cells regardless of oxygen, so the tumor secretes erythropoietin and drives red cell production without any true hypoxia.","check":"The VHL and HIF flow above, then the paraneoplastic table in C02."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-07-C02","obj":"UR-12-G.2","title":"Presentations of renal cell carcinoma: local signs, paraneoplastic hormones, and metastasis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The kidney lies deep in the retroperitoneum, surrounded by perirenal fat, so a tumor can grow large before it causes symptoms. Many renal cell carcinomas are now discovered incidentally on imaging done for another reason. When local signs appear, hematuria is the most common, because the tumor erodes into the calyces and pelvis; it may be gross or microscopic and is often intermittent. Flank pain comes from stretching of the renal capsule, hemorrhage into the tumor, or clots passing down the ureter, and a palpable flank mass means the tumor is already large. The classic triad of hematuria, flank pain, and mass occurs together in only about 10% of patients."},{"type":"p","text":"Systemic features include fever, weight loss, fatigue, and anemia, which is more common than polycythemia. Renal cell carcinoma is known for paraneoplastic syndromes, partly because HIF drives the erythropoietin gene directly and partly because tumor cells secrete hormones ectopically."},{"type":"table","title":"Paraneoplastic products of renal cell carcinoma","cols":["Product","Mechanism","Expected findings"],"rows":[["Erythropoietin","Tumor secretion, partly HIF-driven","Secondary polycythemia with high erythropoietin and normal arterial O2 saturation"],["PTHrP","Binds the PTH1 receptor on bone and kidney, increasing bone resorption and distal Ca2+ reabsorption","Hypercalcemia with suppressed PTH and often low phosphate"],["Renin","Tumor secretion activates angiotensin II and aldosterone","Hypertension, sometimes with hypokalemia"],["ACTH","Ectopic secretion stimulates adrenal cortisol production","Cushing syndrome with hypokalemia and hyperglycemia"]],"caption":"Erythropoietin and renin exaggerate normal renal products; PTHrP and ACTH are ectopic."},{"type":"example","title":"Two abnormal laboratory values in one patient","text":"A 64-year-old man who smokes has hematocrit 57% (men about 41 to 50%), arterial O2 saturation 98%, serum calcium 12.4 mg/dL (about 8.5 to 10.5), and PTH below the reference range. Normal saturation rules out hypoxic polycythemia, so the red cell excess is driven by inappropriate erythropoietin. A high calcium with low PTH means the parathyroids are responding normally and something else is raising calcium: PTHrP or osteolytic bone metastasis. A single renal cell carcinoma secreting erythropoietin and PTHrP explains both findings."},{"type":"h","text":"Metastasis"},{"type":"p","text":"About one quarter of patients have metastases when the tumor is found, sometimes before any local symptom. Because spread follows the venous route described in C01, the lung is involved most often, classically as multiple round nodules sometimes called cannonball metastases. Bone metastases are lytic, so they cause pain, pathologic fracture, and a second mechanism of hypercalcemia."},{"type":"h","text":"The left varicocele"},{"type":"p","text":"The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, while the right gonadal vein enters the inferior vena cava directly. Tumor thrombus in the left renal vein therefore blocks outflow from the left pampiniform plexus. A varicocele produced this way does not empty when the patient lies down, unlike the common idiopathic varicocele, which fills with standing and decompresses when supine."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a left renal tumor produces a left varicocele","steps":["Tumor thrombus grows into the left renal vein","Left gonadal vein cannot drain into the renal vein","Pressure rises in the left pampiniform plexus","Varicocele that persists when the patient is supine"],"caption":"A new varicocele that does not decompress lying down points to an obstructing lesion upstream."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-07-C03","obj":"UR-12-G.3","title":"Papillary and chromophobe carcinoma and oncocytoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal epithelial tumors are classified by the cell they resemble and the genetic change that drives them. Clear cell and papillary carcinomas come from the proximal tubule, while chromophobe carcinoma and oncocytoma come from intercalated cells of the collecting duct. These origins explain why the tumors look different and why they behave differently."},{"type":"table","title":"Renal epithelial tumors compared","cols":["Tumor","Share of renal epithelial tumors","Cell of origin","Genetics","Key morphology","Behavior"],"rows":[["Clear cell RCC","About 70 to 80%","Proximal tubule","VHL loss, 3p deletion","Golden yellow; clear cells; delicate vessels","Malignant; renal vein invasion"],["Papillary RCC","About 10 to 15%","Proximal tubule","Trisomy 7 and 17; MET in hereditary cases","Papillae with foamy macrophages in fibrovascular cores","Malignant; often multifocal and bilateral"],["Chromophobe RCC","About 5%","Intercalated cell","Loss of many whole chromosomes","Pale cells, distinct cell borders, perinuclear halo","Malignant, good prognosis"],["Oncocytoma","About 5 to 10%","Intercalated cell","No VHL loss; no 3p deletion","Mahogany brown; central stellate scar; mitochondria-rich pink cells","Benign"]],"caption":"Origin pairs the tumors: proximal tubule for clear cell and papillary, intercalated cell for chromophobe and oncocytoma."},{"type":"h","text":"Papillary carcinoma"},{"type":"p","text":"Papillary carcinoma is the second most common subtype. Its characteristic cytogenetic changes are trisomy of chromosomes 7 and 17, often with loss of Y in men, and it does not depend on VHL loss. Hereditary papillary renal carcinoma is caused by activating germline mutations of MET on 7q, which encodes the receptor tyrosine kinase for hepatocyte growth factor; extra copies of chromosome 7 increase the dose of the same growth signal in sporadic tumors. Microscopically, tumor cells cover papillae built on fibrovascular cores, and the cores often contain foamy, lipid-laden macrophages and sometimes psammoma bodies. These tumors are frequently multifocal and bilateral and are common in kidneys with dialysis-associated cystic disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Chromophobe carcinoma and oncocytoma"},{"type":"p","text":"Chromophobe carcinoma arises from intercalated cells and shows extensive loss of whole chromosomes, leaving a markedly hypodiploid genome. Its cells have pale to eosinophilic cytoplasm, prominent plant-like cell membranes, and a clear halo around the nucleus. It has a better prognosis than clear cell or papillary carcinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Oncocytoma is a benign epithelial tumor that also comes from intercalated cells. Its cells are oncocytes, packed so densely with mitochondria that the cytoplasm is granular and pink, and the abundant mitochondria give the tumor its tan to mahogany-brown color. A central stellate scar is typical. Because chromophobe carcinoma and oncocytoma share an origin and both can look eosinophilic, they are separated by the distinct membranes and perinuclear halos of chromophobe cells and by the round, uniform nuclei of oncocytes. Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome (FLCN mutation) produces both tumor types, sometimes as hybrid tumors."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A renal mass is composed of granular eosinophilic cells. Which findings would push you toward oncocytoma rather than chromophobe carcinoma?","explain":"Uniform round nuclei without perinuclear halos, no prominent cell membranes, a brown cut surface with a central stellate scar, and no loss of multiple whole chromosomes favor oncocytoma, the benign mitochondria-rich tumor.","check":"The comparison table and the paragraph on chromophobe carcinoma and oncocytoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-07-C04","obj":"UR-12-G.4","title":"Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Wilms tumor is the most common primary renal tumor of childhood, with most cases diagnosed between 2 and 5 years of age. It arises from metanephric blastema, the embryonic tissue that normally forms nephrons. Its precursor lesions are nephrogenic rests, foci of metanephric tissue that persist after birth, which are often found in the kidney next to the tumor. About 5 to 10% of tumors are bilateral, either at the same time or in sequence, and bilateral disease suggests a germline predisposition."},{"type":"h","text":"Morphology: a tumor that copies nephrogenesis"},{"type":"p","text":"Grossly the tumor is large, solitary, well circumscribed, soft, and tan to gray, with areas of hemorrhage and necrosis. The classic triphasic histology recapitulates kidney development: sheets of small round blue blastemal cells, a fibroblastic or myxoid stroma that may contain skeletal muscle, and an epithelial component forming abortive tubules and glomeruli. Diffuse anaplasia, with large bizarre nuclei and abnormal mitoses, is linked to TP53 mutation and a worse prognosis."},{"type":"h","text":"Genetics and associated syndromes"},{"type":"p","text":"WT1 on chromosome 11p13 encodes a zinc finger transcription factor needed for normal development of the kidney and gonads and later expressed in podocytes. Its loss explains why Wilms tumor risk travels with genitourinary malformations. A second locus, WT2 at 11p15.5, is an imprinted region: IGF2 is normally expressed only from the paternal allele, and loss of imprinting or paternal uniparental disomy gives two active copies, which drives overgrowth and tumor risk. Most sporadic Wilms tumors lack WT1 mutations; activating beta-catenin (CTNNB1) mutations are found in a minority."},{"type":"table","title":"Syndromes that predispose to Wilms tumor","cols":["Syndrome","Genetic lesion","Features besides Wilms tumor","Mechanistic link"],"rows":[["WAGR","Germline deletion at 11p13 removing WT1 and PAX6","Aniridia, genitourinary anomalies (cryptorchidism, hypospadias), intellectual disability","Adjacent PAX6 loss adds aniridia; about one third develop Wilms tumor"],["Denys-Drash","Dominant negative missense mutation in the WT1 DNA-binding zinc finger","Early nephrotic syndrome (diffuse mesangial sclerosis), gonadal dysgenesis, often 46,XY with ambiguous genitalia","Mutant protein blocks normal WT1; risk about 90%"],["Beckwith-Wiedemann","Imprinting defects at 11p15.5 (WT2 region), including biallelic IGF2 expression","Macroglossia, organomegaly, hemihyperplasia, omphalocele, neonatal hypoglycemia from islet hyperplasia, adrenal cytomegaly","IGF2 excess drives overgrowth; hepatoblastoma risk too"]],"caption":"WT1 lesions link the tumor to genitourinary and podocyte abnormalities; 11p15.5 lesions link it to overgrowth."},{"type":"h","text":"Presentation"},{"type":"p","text":"The typical child presents with a large, smooth, unilateral flank or abdominal mass, often noticed by a parent while bathing or dressing the child, that usually does not cross the midline. Hematuria occurs when the tumor invades the collecting system, and abdominal pain may follow hemorrhage into the tumor. Hypertension results from renin secreted by the tumor or from compression of renal vessels that makes the kidney ischemic and releases renin. Hematogenous spread goes mainly to the lungs."},{"type":"table","title":"Wilms tumor versus neuroblastoma","cols":["Feature","Wilms tumor","Neuroblastoma"],"rows":[["Origin","Metanephric blastema of the kidney","Neural crest cells of the adrenal medulla or sympathetic ganglia"],["Mass","Smooth, usually does not cross the midline","Irregular, often crosses the midline, often calcified"],["Urine markers","No catecholamine metabolites","Raised vanillylmandelic acid and homovanillic acid"],["Histology","Triphasic: blastema, stroma, epithelium","Small round blue cells with Homer Wright rosettes"],["Genetics","WT1, 11p15.5 imprinting","N-MYC amplification predicts poor prognosis"]],"caption":"Both are abdominal masses of young children made partly of small round blue cells; origin and urine markers separate them."},{"type":"example","title":"Reasoning from associated anomalies","text":"A 2-year-old with ambiguous genitalia and a 46,XY karyotype has heavy proteinuria, low serum albumin, and a rising creatinine; imaging shows a renal mass. Heavy proteinuria in infancy points to a podocyte disorder, ambiguous genitalia to failed gonadal development, and both require WT1. A dominant negative WT1 mutation explains all three findings, so this is Denys-Drash syndrome, with diffuse mesangial sclerosis and Wilms tumor. With aniridia and developmental delay instead, an 11p13 deletion (WAGR) would fit better."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-07-C05","obj":"UR-12-G.5","title":"Urothelial carcinoma of the bladder and collecting system","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Urothelium lines the renal pelvis, ureters, bladder, and proximal urethra, and urothelial carcinoma can arise anywhere along it, although the bladder is by far the most common site. Urothelial carcinoma accounts for about 90% of bladder cancers. It is about three times more common in men, and most patients are older than 50."},{"type":"h","text":"Risk factors and the field effect"},{"type":"p","text":"The common thread in the risk factors is that a carcinogen or its metabolite is excreted into urine and held against the urothelium during bladder storage. Aromatic amines such as 4-aminobiphenyl and 2-naphthylamine are N-hydroxylated in the liver, conjugated, and excreted; in urine they can be released as reactive species that form DNA adducts in urothelial cells. Because the whole urothelial surface is exposed, mutations accumulate at many sites, so tumors are often multifocal and recur at new locations after removal. Seeding of shed tumor cells into new areas of mucosa contributes as well."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for urothelial carcinoma","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism or setting"],"rows":[["Cigarette smoking","Most important risk factor; aromatic amines in smoke are excreted in urine; risk several-fold higher"],["Occupational aromatic amines","2-naphthylamine and benzidine in aniline dye, rubber, leather, and textile work"],["Cyclophosphamide","Metabolite acrolein is concentrated in urine, causes hemorrhagic cystitis, and raises later cancer risk"],["Phenacetin","Long-term analgesic abuse; linked especially to carcinoma of the renal pelvis, along with analgesic nephropathy"],["Other","Aristolochic acid and Lynch syndrome (upper tract tumors), arsenic, pelvic radiation"]],"caption":"Each exposure reaches the urothelium through the urine, which is why tumors are multifocal."},{"type":"flow","title":"Field effect in the urothelium","steps":["Smoking or aromatic amine exposure","Hepatic metabolites excreted into urine","Stored urine bathes the entire urothelium","DNA damage accumulates at many mucosal sites","Multifocal tumors that recur at new sites"],"caption":"A urinary carcinogen exposes the renal pelvis, ureters, and bladder at once."},{"type":"h","text":"Two pathways: papillary tumors and flat carcinoma in situ"},{"type":"table","title":"Papillary urothelial tumors versus flat carcinoma in situ","cols":["Feature","Papillary tumors","Flat carcinoma in situ"],"rows":[["Appearance","Exophytic fronds with fibrovascular cores","Flat, red, velvety mucosa, sometimes invisible on cystoscopy"],["Grade","Most are low grade","High grade by definition"],["Typical molecular change","Activating FGFR3 or HRAS mutations","TP53 and RB loss"],["Behavior","Low grade tumors recur often but seldom invade; high grade ones can invade","High risk of progression to invasive carcinoma"],["Urine cytology","Often negative (cohesive cells)","Usually positive (cells are poorly cohesive and shed)"]],"caption":"The flat lesion looks less dramatic but is the more dangerous one."},{"type":"p","text":"Deletions on chromosome 9, including CDKN2A, are common in both pathways. Prognosis depends mainly on depth of invasion, and invasion of the muscularis propria (detrusor) is the key threshold. Urothelial papilloma (benign) and papillary urothelial neoplasm of low malignant potential sit at the low end of the papillary spectrum."},{"type":"h","text":"Manifestations"},{"type":"p","text":"The classic presentation is painless gross hematuria in an older adult, because fragile vessels in papillary fronds and the friable mucosa of CIS bleed easily. Bleeding is often intermittent, so one normal urinalysis does not exclude a tumor. Carcinoma in situ irritates the bladder and can cause dysuria, frequency, and urgency without infection. A tumor at a ureteral orifice or in the renal pelvis or ureter obstructs urine flow and causes hydronephrosis and flank pain."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An older smoker with intermittent painless gross hematuria has negative urine cytology. Why does this not rule out urothelial carcinoma?","explain":"Low grade papillary tumors are made of cohesive, near-normal cells that shed poorly and look bland, so cytology is often negative. Cytology is most sensitive for high grade lesions such as CIS.","check":"The papillary versus CIS table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-07-C06","obj":"UR-12-G.6","title":"Squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma of the bladder","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Under chronic injury, urothelium can change into another epithelial type. Squamous metaplasia replaces it with keratinizing stratified squamous epithelium, and glandular metaplasia (cystitis glandularis) replaces it with columnar or intestinal-type epithelium. Each metaplastic epithelium can progress through dysplasia to a carcinoma of its own type, which is why the cause of the injury predicts the histology."},{"type":"h","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma and Schistosoma haematobium"},{"type":"p","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma makes up only a few percent of bladder cancers in North America and Europe but is much more frequent where Schistosoma haematobium is endemic, in parts of Africa and the Middle East. This trematode enters through the skin from fresh water, and the adult worms live in the venous plexus around the bladder. They release eggs with a terminal spine that must cross the bladder wall to leave in urine. Many eggs remain trapped and provoke granulomatous inflammation with eosinophils, followed by fibrosis and calcification of the wall, with hematuria from the start. Years of inflammation, cell turnover, reactive oxygen species from inflammatory cells, and N-nitroso compounds formed during bacterial superinfection drive squamous metaplasia, dysplasia, and keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma."},{"type":"flow","title":"Schistosomal squamous cell carcinoma","steps":["S. haematobium eggs lodge in the bladder wall","Granulomatous inflammation with eosinophils","Fibrosis and calcification with ongoing urothelial injury","Squamous metaplasia, then dysplasia","Invasive keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma"],"caption":"Chronic inflammation, not the parasite's genes, drives the change; the same sequence follows other chronic irritants."},{"type":"p","text":"Other sources of chronic irritation follow the same path: long-term indwelling catheters, bladder stones, and recurrent infection in a neurogenic bladder after spinal cord injury. Smoking also contributes. Squamous tumors tend to be found at an advanced stage, and keratin pearls and intercellular bridges identify them microscopically."},{"type":"h","text":"Adenocarcinoma: urachal remnants and exstrophy"},{"type":"p","text":"Primary adenocarcinoma is uncommon, about 2% or less of bladder cancers. In the fetus the urachus, the remnant of the allantois, connects the bladder dome to the umbilicus and normally becomes the fibrous median umbilical ligament. When part of its lumen persists, its glandular epithelium can give rise to urachal adenocarcinoma at the dome or anterior wall, which may grow into the space of Retzius and may secrete mucin into the urine. Bladder exstrophy is a ventral body wall defect: the body wall below the umbilicus fails to close, so the bladder mucosa lies open on the abdomen. Chronic exposure and infection cause glandular metaplasia, and the risk of adenocarcinoma rises."},{"type":"table","title":"Bladder carcinomas by histologic type","cols":["Type","Approximate share","Precursor change","Key associations","Typical findings"],"rows":[["Urothelial carcinoma","About 90%","Papillary neoplasia or flat CIS","Smoking, aromatic amines, cyclophosphamide, phenacetin","Painless hematuria; multifocal, recurrent"],["Squamous cell carcinoma","A few percent (much higher in endemic schistosomiasis)","Squamous metaplasia","S. haematobium, catheters, stones, neurogenic bladder","Keratin pearls; advanced at diagnosis"],["Adenocarcinoma","About 2% or less","Urachal epithelium or glandular metaplasia","Urachal remnant, bladder exstrophy","Dome location for urachal tumors; mucin"]],"caption":"The type of chronic injury predicts the type of carcinoma."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each tumor in this chapter is explained by joining a cell of origin to a genetic lesion. Embryology and histology supply the cell: proximal tubule for clear cell and papillary carcinoma, intercalated cell for chromophobe carcinoma and oncocytoma, metanephric blastema for Wilms tumor, and urothelium, its metaplastic variants, or the urachus for bladder tumors. Biochemistry and genetics supply the driver: VHL loss and pseudohypoxic HIF signaling, MET activation, WT1 loss and IGF2 imprinting, and FGFR3 versus TP53 pathways. Physiology then explains the presentations, since a tumor of renal epithelium can make erythropoietin and renin, and gross anatomy explains the left varicocele and the venous route to the lung."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and immunology enter through Schistosoma haematobium, where Th2-driven granulomas and years of inflammation, rather than the parasite's own genes, produce squamous cancer. The associations in this chapter are probabilities, not rules: smoking raises the risk of both renal and urothelial carcinoma, a single renal mass may be benign, and histologic overlap between eosinophilic tumors means morphology must be read with genetics and the gross appearance."}],"summary":["Clear cell RCC arises from the proximal tubule; biallelic VHL loss on 3p stabilizes HIF and causes VEGF-driven hypervascularity and erythropoietin secretion.","Clear cell RCC is golden yellow because its cells store lipid, and it grows into the renal vein and inferior vena cava, so metastases go most often to lung, then bone.","Smoking is the strongest modifiable risk factor for both RCC and urothelial carcinoma; obesity, hypertension, dialysis-related cysts, and VHL disease add RCC risk.","RCC classically presents with hematuria, and less often with the full triad; paraneoplastic erythropoietin, PTHrP, renin, and ACTH explain polycythemia, hypercalcemia, hypertension, and Cushing syndrome.","A left varicocele that persists when supine suggests left renal vein obstruction because the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein.","Papillary RCC (trisomy 7 and 17, MET, foamy macrophages) shares a proximal tubule origin with clear cell RCC; chromophobe RCC and benign oncocytoma both arise from intercalated cells.","Wilms tumor of children aged 2 to 5 years is a large unilateral flank mass with triphasic histology; WT1 at 11p13 links it to WAGR and Denys-Drash, and 11p15.5 imprinting links it to Beckwith-Wiedemann.","Urothelial carcinoma presents with painless hematuria and is multifocal because urinary carcinogens bathe the whole urothelium; low grade papillary tumors recur, while flat CIS is high grade and often progresses to invasion.","Chronic irritation leads to metaplasia: Schistosoma haematobium and catheters cause squamous cell carcinoma, and urachal remnants and exstrophy cause adenocarcinoma."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, tumors of the kidney"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, tumors of the urinary bladder"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of Infancy and Childhood, tumors of childhood"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital system, development of the kidney, bladder, and urachus"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical parasitology, blood flukes (Schistosoma)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, paraneoplastic syndromes"}]},"UR-12-H":{"unit":"UR-12-H","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-08","title":"Infectious Urinary Diseases: Pyelonephritis, Cystitis, and Urethritis","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows infection up and down the urinary tract: urethritis at the outlet, cystitis in the bladder, and acute pyelonephritis in the kidney. Each level has its own defenses, risk factors, symptoms, and urine findings, and board questions nearly always ask you to place the infection at the correct level from those clues, name the likely organism, and predict what happens if it spreads. The organisms themselves are covered in UR-11; here the focus is on how host anatomy and the inflammatory response explain the presentation, the laboratory findings, and the complications.","prerequisites":["Gross anatomy of the kidney, ureter, bladder, and urethra in both sexes (UR-3, UR-4)","Nephron histology, urothelium, and the renal papillae (UR-2)","Micturition reflex and its afferent and efferent pathways (UR-6)","Urinary pathogens, virulence factors, and urinalysis in infection (UR-11)","Acute inflammation: neutrophil recruitment, cytokines, and fever"],"connections":[{"to":"Urinary pathogens (UR-11)","how":"Fimbriae, urease, pili, and intracellular life cycles explain which organism causes which level of infection and why some escape the Gram stain."},{"to":"Obstructive disease and calculi (UR-12-C)","how":"Obstruction causes urinary stasis that favors infection, and urease-producing infection forms struvite stones that worsen obstruction."},{"to":"Inflammatory and congenital urinary disease (UR-12-D, UR-12-E)","how":"Vesicoureteral reflux and repeated acute pyelonephritis lead to chronic pyelonephritis, and papillary necrosis is shared with analgesic nephropathy."},{"to":"Reproductive system","how":"Gonococcal and chlamydial urethritis ascend along continuous mucosa to cause epididymitis in men and pelvic inflammatory disease in women."},{"to":"Immunology and musculoskeletal disease","how":"Reactive arthritis after Chlamydia and disseminated gonococcal infection with terminal complement deficiency link urethral infection to the joints."}],"terms":[{"term":"Acute pyelonephritis","def":"Suppurative bacterial infection of the renal pelvis and tubulointerstitium, usually reached by ascent from the bladder."},{"term":"Cystitis","def":"Inflammation of the bladder mucosa, in this chapter caused by bacterial infection confined to the lower tract."},{"term":"Urethritis","def":"Inflammation of the urethra, usually from a sexually transmitted infection, classified as gonococcal or nongonococcal."},{"term":"Pyuria","def":"Neutrophils in the urine, detected as leukocyte esterase on dipstick or as white cells on microscopy."},{"term":"Sterile pyuria","def":"Pyuria with a negative routine urine culture, which points to organisms that do not grow on standard media or to noninfectious inflammation."},{"term":"WBC cast","def":"A cylinder of uromodulin matrix packed with leukocytes that forms in a tubule lumen and proves the leukocytes came from the kidney."},{"term":"Costovertebral angle","def":"The angle between the 12th rib and the lateral border of the erector spinae muscles, overlying the kidney, where percussion tenderness suggests renal inflammation."},{"term":"Papillary necrosis","def":"Ischemic death of the renal papillary tips, which may slough into the collecting system."},{"term":"Pyonephrosis","def":"Pus filling an obstructed renal pelvis and calyces, which cannot drain."},{"term":"Perinephric abscess","def":"A collection of pus that has broken through the renal capsule into the perinephric fat."},{"term":"Pelvic inflammatory disease","def":"Ascending infection of the upper female genital tract (endometrium, fallopian tubes, ovaries, pelvic peritoneum), usually from cervical gonococcal or chlamydial infection."},{"term":"Reactive arthritis","def":"A sterile inflammatory arthritis that follows a genitourinary or enteric infection, often with urethritis and conjunctivitis, and is associated with HLA-B27."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-08-C01","obj":"UR-12-H.1","title":"Acute pyelonephritis and its complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute pyelonephritis is suppurative (pus-forming) bacterial infection of the renal pelvis and tubulointerstitium. Most cases are ascending: enteric gram-negative rods, above all uropathogenic Escherichia coli, colonize the periurethral area, enter the bladder, and then climb the ureter. The kidney is normally protected because ureteral peristalsis carries urine downward and the oblique course of the ureter through the bladder wall closes during voiding, acting as a one-way valve. Vesicoureteral reflux, obstruction, and the ureteral dilation of pregnancy defeat this valve, and P fimbriae, which bind glycolipid receptors on upper tract urothelium (UR-11), let bacteria hold on against urine flow."},{"type":"p","text":"Infected urine in the pelvis can be forced into the collecting ducts through the flattened compound papillae at the renal poles (intrarenal reflux), so ascending infection and its later scars favor the poles. Less often, infection is hematogenous, classically Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia seeding the cortex with multiple small abscesses."},{"type":"flow","title":"The ascending route to the kidney","steps":["Fecal flora colonize the periurethral area","Bacteria ascend the urethra into the bladder","Type 1 fimbriae attach bacteria to bladder urothelium","Reflux or obstruction carries infected urine up the ureter","P fimbriae anchor bacteria in the renal pelvis","Intrarenal reflux delivers bacteria into collecting ducts","Neutrophils fill the interstitium and tubules"],"caption":"Each step must overcome a host defense; reflux and obstruction are the usual reasons the kidney is reached."},{"type":"p","text":"Grossly, the kidney is swollen, with discrete yellow-white abscesses raised on the cortical surface. Microscopically the hallmark is patchy suppurative inflammation of the interstitium, with neutrophils packing the tubular lumens and destroying tubules in the most intense foci. Glomeruli are relatively resistant to infection and are spared early. This tubulointerstitial pattern is the key contrast with glomerulonephritis, which produces red cell casts and proteinuria instead."},{"type":"p","text":"The symptoms follow from the inflammation. Cytokines from the infected parenchyma (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha) induce prostaglandin E2 in the hypothalamus, raising the temperature set point and causing fever, usually above 38 °C (100.4 °F), with chills. Edema stretches the renal capsule, producing dull flank pain and tenderness when the costovertebral angle is percussed, often with nausea and vomiting. Because the infection usually passes through the bladder, dysuria and frequency often come first. The blood shows a neutrophilic leukocytosis, and the urine shows pyuria, bacteriuria, and WBC casts. Casts form only inside tubules, where uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) secreted by the thick ascending limb gels around trapped cells, so a WBC cast places the leukocytes in the kidney. WBC casts are not specific for infection, because acute interstitial nephritis also produces them (UR-12-D)."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of acute pyelonephritis","cols":["Complication","Mechanism","Usual setting and clue"],"rows":[["Papillary necrosis","Papillary tips lie at the end of the medullary blood supply; inflammatory edema and microvascular disease cut off flow and infarct them","Diabetes, obstruction, or sickle cell disease; sloughed papillae cause colic, hematuria, or obstruction"],["Renal abscess","Suppurative foci coalesce, or blood-borne S. aureus seeds the cortex","Persistent fever and a focal mass on imaging"],["Pyonephrosis","High-grade obstruction, often a stone, traps pus in the pelvis and calyces","Dilated, pus-filled collecting system; high sepsis risk"],["Perinephric abscess","Suppuration breaks through the capsule into perinephric fat","Fever outlasting the expected course, flank mass"],["Urosepsis","Lipid A of lipopolysaccharide activates TLR4, releasing cytokines that cause vasodilation and capillary leak","Hypotension and organ failure, especially with obstruction"]],"caption":"Obstruction and diabetes underlie most severe complications; repeated infection with reflux or obstruction leads to chronic pyelonephritis (UR-12-D)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with long-standing diabetes is being followed for acute pyelonephritis and suddenly develops colicky flank pain, gross hematuria, and fragments of tissue in the urine. What has happened?","explain":"Papillary necrosis. The papillae are poorly perfused tips of the medulla, and diabetic microvascular disease plus inflammatory edema infarcts them; a sloughed papilla passing down the ureter causes colic, bleeding, and possible obstruction.","check":"Compare with the complications table and the description of papillary blood supply."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-08-C02","obj":"UR-12-H.2","title":"Cystitis: risk factors and how it differs from pyelonephritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The bladder stays sterile mainly because voiding flushes it often and almost completely. In addition, a glycosaminoglycan layer and uroplakin plaques cover the urothelium, uromodulin in the urine binds type 1 fimbriae as a soluble decoy, infected superficial (umbrella) cells are shed, and vaginal lactobacilli keep the introital pH low enough to limit colonization by enteric bacteria. Each risk factor for cystitis defeats one of these defenses."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for cystitis and the defense each one defeats","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Female sex","Short urethra (about 4 cm versus about 20 cm in men) opening near the vagina and anus"],["Sexual intercourse","Pushes periurethral bacteria into the bladder; spermicides disturb vaginal flora"],["Pregnancy","Progesterone relaxes smooth muscle and the uterus compresses the ureters, causing stasis and easy ascent"],["Urinary catheters","Bypass the urethra and carry a biofilm shielded from urine flow; risk rises each day"],["Diabetes mellitus","Glycosuria, impaired neutrophil function, and neuropathic incomplete emptying"],["Obstruction","Prostatic enlargement, stones, strictures, or neurogenic bladder leave residual urine"],["Menopause","Loss of estrogen and vaginal lactobacilli allows enteric colonization"]],"caption":"The long male urethra and antibacterial prostatic secretions make cystitis in men uncommon, so it suggests obstruction or instrumentation."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Obstruction and infection reinforce each other","steps":["Obstruction leaves residual urine","Stasis lets bacteria multiply","Urease organisms such as Proteus alkalinize urine","Struvite precipitates and forms a stone","The stone worsens obstruction and shelters bacteria"],"caption":"Stones and infection sustain each other (UR-12-C)."},{"type":"p","text":"Cystitis causes dysuria (burning as urine passes over the inflamed trigone and urethra), frequency, and urgency, because inflammation sensitizes bladder stretch afferents so that the micturition reflex fires at small volumes. Suprapubic pain and tenderness are common, and the hyperemic mucosa may bleed enough to cause gross hematuria. The infection is confined to the superficial mucosa, so little cytokine reaches the circulation: there is no fever, no flank pain, and no casts. Elderly patients may present only with confusion or new incontinence."},{"type":"p","text":"Urinalysis shows pyuria (positive leukocyte esterase), bacteriuria, and often a positive nitrite test, which requires an organism with nitrate reductase and several hours of urine dwell time (UR-11). Enterococcus and Staphylococcus saprophyticus usually do not form nitrite, and the frequent voiding of cystitis shortens dwell time, so a negative nitrite does not exclude infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Placing the infection at the correct level","cols":["Feature","Urethritis","Cystitis","Acute pyelonephritis"],"rows":[["Main symptoms","Dysuria with urethral discharge","Dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic pain","Fever, chills, flank pain, nausea"],["Fever","Usually absent","Absent","Present, usually above 38 °C (100.4 °F)"],["Costovertebral angle tenderness","Absent","Absent","Present"],["Usual organisms","N. gonorrhoeae, C. trachomatis","E. coli, S. saprophyticus, Proteus, Klebsiella, Enterococcus","E. coli and other enteric gram-negative rods"],["Urine findings","Pyuria with negative routine culture","Pyuria, bacteriuria, often nitrite positive","Pyuria, bacteriuria, WBC casts"],["Blood","Normal","Normal","Neutrophilic leukocytosis"]],"caption":"Fever, flank pain, costovertebral angle tenderness, and WBC casts mark the kidney; discharge and a negative routine culture mark the urethra."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing a urinary infection","text":"A 24-year-old woman has 3 days of dysuria and frequency. Today she has a temperature of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), chills, and right costovertebral angle tenderness. Urinalysis shows positive leukocyte esterase, negative nitrite, bacteria, and WBC casts. Step 1: dysuria and frequency place infection in the bladder. Step 2: fever, flank tenderness, and WBC casts mean the renal parenchyma is now involved, so this is acute pyelonephritis that began as cystitis. Step 3: the negative nitrite does not argue against infection, for the reasons above. Step 4: there is no discharge and bacteriuria is present, so urethritis is not the explanation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does an indwelling catheter raise infection risk even though it keeps the bladder empty?","explain":"Complete emptying is only one defense. The catheter bypasses the urethra, gives bacteria a path along its surfaces, and supports a biofilm protected from urine flow and neutrophils.","check":"Compare with the defenses paragraph and the risk factor table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-12-08-C03","obj":"UR-12-H.3","title":"Gonococcal and nongonococcal urethritis and their complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Urethritis presents with dysuria and urethral discharge and is usually sexually transmitted. It is divided by Gram stain into gonococcal urethritis (Neisseria gonorrhoeae) and nongonococcal urethritis, most often from Chlamydia trachomatis serovars D to K. Their biology (UR-11) explains why the two forms look different."},{"type":"p","text":"Gonococci attach to nonciliated columnar epithelium by pili and Opa proteins, cross it by transcytosis, and release lipooligosaccharide that triggers an intense neutrophil response. The result is a heavy, purulent discharge, and Gram stain shows gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils, a finding highly sensitive in symptomatic men. Women are more often asymptomatic or present with cervicitis. Chlamydia is obligate intracellular: the infectious elementary body enters the cell and becomes a replicating reticulate body inside an inclusion. Few organisms are free in the discharge and inflammation is milder, so discharge is scant or absent and many infections are silent. Gram stain shows neutrophils without organisms and routine culture is negative (sterile pyuria). Nucleic acid amplification testing detects both, and coinfection is common."},{"type":"table","title":"Gonococcal versus nongonococcal urethritis","cols":["Feature","Gonococcal","Nongonococcal (mostly Chlamydia)"],"rows":[["Organism","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","C. trachomatis serovars D to K; also M. genitalium, Ureaplasma, T. vaginalis"],["Gram stain","Gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils","Neutrophils without visible organisms"],["Discharge","Heavy, purulent","Scant, watery or mucoid, or none"],["Onset after exposure","Usually 2 to 7 days","Usually 1 to 3 weeks"],["Asymptomatic infection","Common in women, less so in men","Common in both sexes"],["Distinctive complication","Disseminated gonococcal infection","Reactive arthritis"]],"caption":"Both organisms cause epididymitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, and neonatal conjunctivitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Untreated infection spreads along continuous mucosa. In men it travels through the ejaculatory duct and ductus deferens to the epididymis, causing unilateral scrotal pain and swelling; epididymitis is usually chlamydial or gonococcal in younger men but caused by enteric rods with obstruction or instrumentation in older men. Gonococcal urethritis can also heal with fibrosis and urethral stricture. In women, infection ascends from the cervix through the endometrium to the fallopian tubes. Tubal scarring means even silent chlamydial infection can cause infertility and ectopic pregnancy."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ascending infection in women","steps":["Gonococcal or chlamydial cervicitis","Spread through the endometrium (endometritis)","Tubal infection (salpingitis), sometimes tubo-ovarian abscess","Healing with tubal scarring and adhesions","Infertility, ectopic pregnancy, chronic pelvic pain"],"caption":"Pelvic inflammatory disease; tubal spread to the liver capsule causes perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome)."},{"type":"p","text":"Two complications reach beyond the genital tract. Reactive arthritis follows chlamydial urethritis (or enteric infection) by a few weeks, most often in HLA-B27 positive people. It is an immune reaction to bacterial antigens, not joint infection, so synovial fluid culture is negative; it causes asymmetric lower limb arthritis, enthesitis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis. Disseminated gonococcal infection follows bacteremia, causing tenosynovitis, polyarthralgia, and sparse pustular skin lesions, or a purulent monoarthritis, often of the knee. Because the membrane attack complex is needed to kill Neisseria, deficiency of terminal complement components (C5 to C9) predisposes to recurrent neisserial infection."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading urethritis findings","text":"A 22-year-old man has mild dysuria and a scant mucoid discharge 10 days after a new sexual partner. Gram stain shows neutrophils but no organisms, and routine urine culture is negative. Step 1: discharge with pyuria means urethritis. Step 2: no intracellular diplococci, scant discharge, and onset after more than a week favor nongonococcal urethritis, most likely Chlamydia, which does not grow on routine media. Step 3: three weeks later he develops a swollen knee, heel pain, and red eyes. This is reactive arthritis, and joint fluid culture would be sterile because the arthritis is immune mediated."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can a woman with no symptoms of chlamydial infection still become infertile?","explain":"Chlamydia causes little acute inflammation, so it can ascend to the tubes silently, yet the low-grade inflammation still heals with scarring that blocks or distorts them.","check":"Compare with the ascending infection flow and the description of the chlamydial life cycle."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy sets the risk at every level: a short female urethra near the perineum explains why cystitis is common in women, the vesicoureteral valve explains why most bladder infections stay in the bladder, and compound polar papillae explain where ascending infection and later scars concentrate. Microbiology explains the organism and the stain: adhesive fimbriae for E. coli, urease for Proteus, pili and lipooligosaccharide for gonococci, and an intracellular cycle for Chlamydia that hides it from the Gram stain and routine culture. Immunology and pathology explain the findings: neutrophils produce pyuria, leukocyte esterase, and WBC casts; cytokines produce fever; lipopolysaccharide produces septic shock; and HLA-B27 and terminal complement deficiency explain reactive arthritis and recurrent neisserial infection."},{"type":"p","text":"These distinctions have limits. Fever and flank pain can be blunted in the elderly, in diabetes, and in immunosuppression, WBC casts are often missed on microscopy and also occur in interstitial nephritis, and a negative nitrite or a modest colony count does not exclude infection. Sterile pyuria has a differential beyond urethritis, including renal tuberculosis and interstitial nephritis (UR-11, UR-12-D), so each finding is read with the whole clinical picture."}],"summary":["Most urinary infections ascend from periurethral fecal flora; E. coli with type 1 fimbriae colonizes the bladder and P fimbriae help it reach the kidney.","Vesicoureteral reflux, obstruction, and the ureteral dilation of pregnancy let bladder infection reach the kidney; hematogenous S. aureus infection is less common.","Acute pyelonephritis is patchy neutrophilic inflammation of the interstitium and tubules with glomeruli spared; fever, flank pain, costovertebral angle tenderness, and WBC casts place it in the kidney.","Complications of pyelonephritis are papillary necrosis, renal and perinephric abscess, pyonephrosis behind an obstruction, and urosepsis from lipopolysaccharide.","Risk factors for cystitis each defeat a defense: short female urethra, intercourse, pregnancy, catheters, diabetes, obstruction, and estrogen loss.","Cystitis causes dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic pain without fever, flank pain, or casts because infection stays in the superficial mucosa.","A negative nitrite does not exclude infection: Enterococcus and S. saprophyticus lack nitrate reductase, and frequent voiding shortens dwell time.","Gonococcal urethritis gives a purulent discharge with intracellular gram-negative diplococci; chlamydial urethritis gives scant discharge, no organisms on Gram stain, and sterile pyuria.","Urethral infection ascends to cause epididymitis in men and pelvic inflammatory disease with tubal scarring, infertility, and perihepatitis in women.","Chlamydia can trigger sterile reactive arthritis in HLA-B27 positive people; gonococcal bacteremia causes disseminated infection, favored by terminal complement deficiency."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, section on pyelonephritis and urinary tract infection"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, sections on cystitis, urethritis, and epididymitis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract, section on pelvic inflammatory disease"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Neisseriae; Chlamydia spp.; Enteric Gram-Negative Rods (Enterobacteriaceae)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, kidneys and ureters; Pelvis and Perineum, urinary bladder and urethra"}]},"UR-2":{"unit":"UR-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-02-01","title":"Microscopic Anatomy of the Nephron and Bladder","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the microscopic structure of the kidney and lower urinary tract and ties each structure to its job. It explains how the three layers of the glomerular wall filter by size and by charge, how the juxtaglomerular apparatus links tubular salt delivery to filtration and renin release, how each tubule segment is built for its workload, why only long-looped nephrons can concentrate urine strongly, and how the urothelium stays sealed while the bladder stretches. Board questions usually give a histologic or clinical finding, such as albuminuria, muddy brown casts, or a fixed urine osmolality, and ask you to name the structure that failed and explain why.","prerequisites":["Epithelial types, junctional complexes, and basal lamina composition (general histology)","Order of nephron segments and the renal vascular sequence from afferent arteriole to vasa recta (UR-5)","Starling forces across capillary walls and the meaning of oncotic pressure","Primary and secondary active transport driven by the Na+/K+-ATPase","Basic renin-angiotensin-aldosterone and ADH physiology (UR-6, UR-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Glomerular diseases (UR-12-A)","how":"Each glomerular disease is defined by which layer of the filtration barrier or which mesangial compartment it injures, which predicts nephrotic versus nephritic findings."},{"to":"Tubulointerstitial disease (UR-12-B)","how":"The high energy demand of the proximal straight tubule and medullary thick ascending limb explains where ischemic acute tubular necrosis strikes."},{"to":"Filtration, reabsorption, and micturition (UR-6)","how":"Transporters named here (NKCC2, ENaC, aquaporin 2) are the molecular basis of segment function and of urine concentration."},{"to":"Blood pressure control (UR-7)","how":"Juxtaglomerular cells and the macula densa are the renal sensors that start the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone cascade."},{"to":"Hematology (HE)","how":"Sickling of HbS in the hypoxic, hypertonic medulla destroys vasa recta and long loops, so sickle cell disease and trait impair urine concentration."}],"terms":[{"term":"Glomerular basement membrane (GBM)","def":"The fused basal laminae of glomerular endothelium and podocytes, built from type IV collagen, laminin, and negatively charged heparan sulfate proteoglycans."},{"term":"Podocyte","def":"A visceral epithelial cell of Bowman capsule whose interdigitating foot processes wrap the glomerular capillaries."},{"term":"Slit diaphragm","def":"The thin protein bridge, built mainly from nephrin, that spans the filtration slit between adjacent podocyte foot processes."},{"term":"Effacement","def":"Flattening and fusion of podocyte foot processes, seen by electron microscopy, that disrupts the slit diaphragm."},{"term":"Selective proteinuria","def":"Urinary loss of albumin with relative retention of larger proteins such as IgG, indicating loss of charge or slit diaphragm function rather than gross structural damage."},{"term":"Macula densa","def":"A plaque of tall, crowded tubular cells at the end of the thick ascending limb that senses luminal NaCl."},{"term":"Juxtaglomerular cells","def":"Modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole wall that store and secrete renin."},{"term":"Mesangial cells","def":"Contractile, phagocytic cells in the glomerular stalk that support capillary loops and produce mesangial matrix."},{"term":"Tubuloglomerular feedback","def":"Adjustment of afferent arteriolar tone by the macula densa so that a rise in distal NaCl delivery lowers single nephron GFR."},{"term":"Vasa recta","def":"Long hairpin capillaries from juxtamedullary efferent arterioles that run beside the loops of Henle and act as countercurrent exchangers."},{"term":"Isosthenuria","def":"Urine whose osmolality stays near that of plasma (about 300 mOsm/kg) regardless of hydration, reflecting loss of concentrating and diluting ability."},{"term":"Uroplakins","def":"Membrane proteins that assemble into rigid plaques on the apical surface of umbrella cells and make the urothelium nearly impermeable."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-02-01-C01","obj":"UR-2.1","title":"The glomerular filtration barrier and albuminuria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pressure drives plasma across the glomerular capillary wall into Bowman space, but the wall decides what passes. Its three layers each add a different selectivity, so about 180 L of filtrate forms daily while cells and nearly all protein stay behind."},{"type":"p","text":"The inner layer is fenestrated endothelium with open pores about 70 to 100 nm across. The pores hold back blood cells, and the endothelial surface coat (glycocalyx) adds negative charge. The glomerular basement membrane (GBM), about 300 to 350 nm thick in adults, forms by fusion of the endothelial and podocyte basal laminae. Its meshwork of type IV collagen (alpha3, alpha4, and alpha5 chains) and laminin provides strength and a size filter, and its heparan sulfate proteoglycans give it a dense fixed negative charge. The outer layer is the podocyte, whose primary processes give off interdigitating foot processes. The filtration slits between foot processes, about 30 to 40 nm wide, are bridged by the slit diaphragm, built mainly from nephrin with podocin as a key partner. Podocalyxin coats the foot processes with more negative charge."},{"type":"table","title":"Three layers of the glomerular filtration barrier","cols":["Layer","Key structure","Main contribution","Example of failure"],"rows":[["Fenestrated endothelium","Open fenestrae about 70 to 100 nm; anionic glycocalyx","Holds back blood cells; adds charge","Thrombotic microangiopathy: capillary narrowing, lower GFR"],["Glomerular basement membrane","Type IV collagen (alpha3, alpha4, alpha5), laminin, heparan sulfate","Main charge barrier and a size barrier","Alport syndrome (abnormal collagen); anti-GBM disease (antibody to alpha3)"],["Podocyte foot processes","Slit diaphragm of nephrin and podocin; podocalyxin coat","Final size barrier","Effacement in minimal change disease and FSGS: nephrotic proteinuria"]],"caption":"Injury at each layer gives a different pattern of urinary findings."},{"type":"p","text":"Selectivity works by size and charge together. Neutral molecules with an effective radius below about 2 nm filter almost as freely as water, while those above about 4 nm are nearly excluded. Albumin (about 66 to 69 kDa, radius about 3.5 nm) sits near this cutoff and carries a net negative charge at plasma pH, so the fixed anions of the wall repel it. Its filterability is only about 0.005 of that of water (Guyton and Hall). The small amount that does filter is taken up by proximal tubule endocytosis, so normal urine contains less than 30 mg of albumin and less than 150 mg of total protein per day."},{"type":"p","text":"Losing either the charge barrier or the slit diaphragm therefore lets albumin through. In minimal change disease the foot processes efface (visible only by electron microscopy) and anionic charge is lost, so albumin leaks while larger proteins such as IgG (about 150 kDa) are still retained, giving selective proteinuria. Inherited defects in nephrin (congenital nephrotic syndrome of the Finnish type) or podocin cause heavy proteinuria from birth or childhood, showing that the slit diaphragm is essential. When the wall itself is structurally damaged, as in immune complex glomerulonephritis, larger proteins leak too (nonselective proteinuria) and red cells may pass (hematuria)."},{"type":"flow","title":"From podocyte injury to nephrotic findings","steps":["Foot processes efface and the wall loses negative charge","Albumin crosses the slit into Bowman space","Albuminuria; nephrotic range is above about 3.5 g/day","Plasma albumin and oncotic pressure fall","Fluid shifts to the interstitium as edema"],"caption":"How a podocyte defect produces nephrotic syndrome."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the type of proteinuria","text":"A 5-year-old has heavy proteinuria with abundant urinary albumin but little IgG, and the biopsy is normal by light microscopy. Step 1: albumin is about 66 to 69 kDa and anionic; IgG is about 150 kDa. Step 2: if only albumin escapes, the size barrier and GBM framework are intact. Step 3: albumin is normally held back mainly by charge and the slit diaphragm, so one of these has failed. Step 4: normal light microscopy fits effacement seen only by electron microscopy, the lesion of minimal change disease. Leakage of IgG as well would point to structural wall damage."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-02-01-C02","obj":"UR-2.2","title":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus and mesangial cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) forms at the vascular pole of each glomerulus, where the thick ascending limb of the same nephron returns to touch its own afferent and efferent arterioles. This contact lets each nephron compare what it filtered with the NaCl that reaches the end of its loop, and adjust both its own filtration rate and the release of renin into the circulation. The JGA has three components: juxtaglomerular cells, the macula densa, and extraglomerular mesangial cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Components of the juxtaglomerular apparatus and the glomerular mesangium","cols":["Component","Location","Microscopic features","Function"],"rows":[["Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells","Media of the afferent arteriole next to the glomerulus","Modified smooth muscle with membrane-bound renin granules; sympathetic nerve endings","Secrete renin when perfusion pressure falls, macula densa NaCl falls, or beta1 receptors are stimulated"],["Macula densa","Wall of the distal thick ascending limb, facing the vascular pole","Tall, narrow, crowded cells whose close nuclei form a dark spot","Sense luminal NaCl and signal the afferent arteriole and juxtaglomerular cells"],["Extraglomerular mesangial (lacis) cells","Space between the afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole, and macula densa","Small pale cells joined by gap junctions","Thought to relay signals from the macula densa"],["Intraglomerular mesangial cells","Stalk of the glomerular tuft, between capillary loops","Contractile cells resembling smooth muscle, in their own matrix","Support loops, alter filtering surface, phagocytose trapped macromolecules"]],"caption":"The macula densa is a tubular structure, whereas the renin-secreting cells are part of a blood vessel."},{"type":"p","text":"Macula densa cells read NaCl delivery mainly through Cl- entry by the apical Na-K-2Cl cotransporter (NKCC2). When single nephron GFR rises, more NaCl reaches them, and they release ATP that is converted to adenosine. Adenosine acting on A1 receptors constricts the afferent arteriole, lowering glomerular pressure and returning GFR toward normal. This tubuloglomerular feedback protects the distal nephron from being flooded with filtrate. When NaCl delivery falls, the macula densa raises its output of prostaglandin E2 (made by COX-2) and nitric oxide, which favor afferent dilation and renin release. Renin cleaves angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, starting the cascade whose end product, angiotensin II, constricts the efferent arteriole to support GFR and raises systemic pressure."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Tubuloglomerular feedback","steps":["Single nephron GFR rises","More NaCl reaches the macula densa","Cl- entry through NKCC2 increases","ATP is released and converted to adenosine","A1 receptors constrict the afferent arteriole","Glomerular pressure and GFR fall back toward normal"],"caption":"A negative feedback loop within each nephron that stabilizes filtration and distal NaCl delivery."},{"type":"p","text":"Juxtaglomerular cells also respond on their own to stretch of the afferent arteriole, acting as an intrarenal baroreceptor, and to sympathetic beta1 stimulation through cAMP. The baroreceptor response explains why renal artery stenosis, which lowers afferent pressure, causes juxtaglomerular hyperplasia, high renin, and secondary hyperaldosteronism."},{"type":"p","text":"Intraglomerular mesangial cells sit inside the GBM in the stalk of the tuft. Where the capillary faces the mesangium, the GBM is absent, so fenestrated endothelium lies directly on mesangial matrix. Macromolecules and circulating immune complexes that pass through the fenestrae therefore enter the mesangium without crossing the GBM, and mesangial cells phagocytose them. This explains why IgA immune complexes deposit in the mesangium in IgA nephropathy, where mesangial cells respond by proliferating and making matrix. In diabetes, mesangial matrix expands and can form nodules (Kimmelstiel-Wilson lesions). Mesangial cells also contract in response to angiotensin II, which reduces filtering surface area."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A drug blocks NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb. What happens to renin secretion?","explain":"The macula densa senses NaCl through NKCC2, so blocking it makes the macula densa read low NaCl even though luminal NaCl is high. It then signals through prostaglandin E2 and nitric oxide for more renin release.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on how macula densa cells read NaCl delivery."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-02-01-C03","obj":"UR-2.3","title":"Tubule epithelium and ischemic vulnerability","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Tubule cell structure follows workload. On a slide, look for a brush border, the amount of eosinophilic, mitochondria-rich cytoplasm, and whether lateral cell borders are clear."},{"type":"p","text":"The proximal tubule reabsorbs about two thirds of filtered Na+ and water, nearly all filtered glucose and amino acids, and most filtered bicarbonate. Its simple cuboidal to low columnar cells carry a tall brush border of microvilli that multiplies apical surface area, and their basal membrane is folded deeply around rows of elongated mitochondria, which give a striated look. The mitochondria supply ATP to the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase, whose Na+ gradient powers nearly all proximal secondary active transport. The cells also have an endocytic apparatus (megalin and cubilin receptors feeding lysosomes) that reclaims filtered albumin and small proteins. Lateral interdigitations hide the cell borders, and because it is the longest segment, its pink, fuzzy-edged profiles dominate the cortex."},{"type":"table","title":"Tubule segments under the microscope","cols":["Segment","Epithelium","Slide clues","Main job"],"rows":[["Proximal tubule","Simple cuboidal to low columnar","Brush border, very eosinophilic, many mitochondria, indistinct borders","Bulk reabsorption; protein uptake"],["Thin limbs","Simple squamous","Like a capillary, but no blood cells in the lumen","Descending limb permeable to water (aquaporin 1); ascending limb impermeable to water"],["Thick ascending limb","Simple cuboidal","No brush border, many mitochondria; secretes uromodulin","NaCl uptake by NKCC2 without water (diluting segment)"],["Distal convoluted tubule","Simple cuboidal","No brush border, smaller paler cells, wide clean lumen","NaCl uptake by the thiazide-sensitive cotransporter; Ca2+ reabsorption under PTH"],["Collecting duct","Cuboidal, becoming columnar toward the papilla","Pale cytoplasm, sharp cell borders; principal and intercalated cells","Fine control of Na+, K+, water, and acid-base"]],"caption":"Brush border identifies the proximal tubule; a cuboidal tubule without one is the thick ascending limb or distal tubule."},{"type":"p","text":"Principal cells reabsorb Na+ through ENaC and secrete K+ through ROMK under aldosterone, and insert aquaporin 2 into their apical membrane under ADH. Intercalated cells are darker and mitochondria-rich: type A cells secrete H+ through apical H+-ATPase and H+/K+-ATPase and return bicarbonate to blood, while type B cells secrete bicarbonate through pendrin."},{"type":"p","text":"High transport work means high oxygen use, and the outer medulla is where supply is poorest. Medullary blood flow is a small fraction of renal flow, and oxygen diffuses from descending to ascending vasa recta, bypassing the deeper tissue. The proximal straight tubule (S3 segment) and medullary thick ascending limb sit in this zone, and proximal cells rely almost entirely on oxidative metabolism with little glycolytic reserve. In ischemic acute tubular necrosis (ATN) these two segments fail first. Falling ATP disrupts the actin cytoskeleton and cell polarity, Na+/K+-ATPase drifts to the apical membrane, and cells detach. Shed cells and uromodulin form muddy brown granular casts that block the lumen, and filtrate leaks back across denuded segments. Extra NaCl reaching the macula densa also triggers tubuloglomerular feedback, further lowering GFR. Injured tubules cannot reabsorb Na+ or concentrate urine, so fractional excretion of Na+ rises above about 2% and urine osmolality approaches about 300 mOsm/kg. Necrosis is patchy and surviving cells can regenerate, which is why ischemic ATN is often reversible."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ischemic acute tubular necrosis","steps":["Hypoperfusion lowers oxygen delivery, worst in the outer medulla","ATP falls in the proximal straight tubule and medullary thick limb","Cells lose polarity and detach from the basement membrane","Debris and uromodulin form muddy brown granular casts","Casts obstruct tubules and filtrate leaks back","GFR falls; FENa above 2% and urine osmolality near plasma"],"caption":"The segments with the highest ATP demand and lowest oxygen supply fail first."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does ischemic ATN injure the proximal straight tubule more than the proximal convoluted tubule, even though both have brush borders?","explain":"The straight portion lies in the oxygen-poor outer medulla, whereas the convoluted portion lies in the well-perfused cortex, so the same high ATP demand meets a lower oxygen supply.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on high transport work and outer medullary oxygen supply."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-02-01-C04","obj":"UR-2.4","title":"Cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each human kidney holds roughly one million nephrons, with wide variation between people. Nephrons differ by where the glomerulus sits, and that position sets how deep the loop of Henle reaches and which capillaries follow the efferent arteriole."},{"type":"table","title":"Cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons","cols":["Feature","Cortical nephrons","Juxtamedullary nephrons"],"rows":[["Share of nephrons","Most (about 70 to 85%)","A minority (about 15 to 30%)"],["Glomerulus position","Outer and middle cortex","Inner cortex near the corticomedullary junction"],["Loop of Henle","Short; turns in the outer medulla; no thin ascending limb","Long; reaches the inner medulla, some to the papilla; thin descending and thin ascending limbs"],["Capillaries after the efferent arteriole","Peritubular capillary network around cortical tubules","Vasa recta running beside the loops into the medulla"],["Role in concentrating urine","Small","Large: build and use the inner medullary gradient"]],"caption":"Glomerulus position predicts loop length and capillary supply, and loop length predicts concentrating power."},{"type":"p","text":"Countercurrent multiplication explains why loop length matters. At each level of the medulla, the thick ascending limb pumps NaCl into the interstitium without water, creating a horizontal difference of about 200 mOsm/kg between ascending fluid and surrounding tissue. Fluid in the water-permeable descending limb equilibrates with that tissue, and flow carries the concentrated fluid deeper, where another increment is added. A small single effect repeated along a long axis builds a large vertical gradient, from about 300 mOsm/kg at the corticomedullary junction to about 1200 mOsm/kg at the papillary tip. In the inner medulla, urea recycled from the ADH-responsive inner medullary collecting duct supplies much of the osmolality. Only loops that reach the inner medulla can build and use the deepest part of this gradient."},{"type":"flow","title":"How long loops build the medullary gradient","steps":["Thick ascending limb pumps NaCl out, keeping water in","Interstitium at that level becomes hypertonic","Descending limb water leaves into the interstitium","Concentrated fluid flows deeper to the next level","Increments add along the loop's length","Urea recycling adds inner medullary osmolality","Collecting duct water equilibrates with the deep interstitium under ADH"],"caption":"Longer loops repeat the single effect over more levels, so the papillary tip reaches higher osmolality."},{"type":"p","text":"The vasa recta keep the gradient from being washed away. Their hairpin shape and low flow let solute entering the descending vessel diffuse out of the ascending vessel and water do the reverse (countercurrent exchange), so they remove little more than the water and solute reabsorbed in the medulla. If medullary flow rises, solute is carried off (medullary washout) and maximum urine osmolality falls."},{"type":"p","text":"ADH can only make collecting duct fluid equal in osmolality to the interstitium around it, so damage to the medulla shows early as an inability to concentrate urine, with polyuria and nocturia, often before GFR falls. In sickle cell disease and sickle trait, the hypoxic, hypertonic, and acidic medulla promotes HbS polymerization in the vasa recta. Repeated occlusion destroys vasa recta and long loops, producing isosthenuria and, in severe cases, papillary necrosis. Chronic pyelonephritis, analgesic nephropathy, and obstruction injure the medulla in similar ways. Desert rodents with relatively more long loops and a thicker medulla concentrate urine far beyond the human maximum of about 1200 mOsm/kg."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a concentrating defect from medullary injury","text":"A young adult with sickle cell trait has nocturia. After overnight water deprivation, urine osmolality reaches only about 400 mOsm/kg while plasma ADH is high, and giving exogenous ADH (desmopressin) does not raise it further. Step 1: high ADH rules out central diabetes insipidus. Step 2: aquaporin 2 lets collecting duct water leave only until tubular fluid matches the surrounding interstitium. Step 3: maximum urine osmolality therefore equals maximum papillary interstitial osmolality. Step 4: sickling in the vasa recta has destroyed the long loops and vessels that build that gradient, so the deep interstitium cannot exceed about 400 mOsm/kg, and neither can the urine."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-02-01-C05","obj":"UR-2.5","title":"Urothelium and the bladder wall","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The urinary passages from the minor calyces through the renal pelvis, ureters, bladder, and proximal urethra are lined by urothelium (transitional epithelium), a stratified epithelium built to stretch while staying watertight. Urine may be several times more concentrated than plasma and differ widely in pH, urea, and K+, so a leaky lining would draw water into the urine and let irritating solutes into the bladder wall."},{"type":"p","text":"Urothelium has three cell populations. Small basal cells on the basement membrane serve as a stem cell reservoir. Above them lie several layers of intermediate cells. The surface layer is a single row of large umbrella (dome) cells, many binucleate or polyploid, each covering several cells beneath it. In the empty bladder the epithelium is about five or six cells thick and the umbrella cells bulge into the lumen; in the full bladder it thins to about two or three layers and the umbrella cells flatten."},{"type":"p","text":"Umbrella cells make the barrier. Their apical membrane is covered by rigid plaques of uroplakins (Ia, Ib, II, and IIIa) arranged in hexagonal arrays, forming a thick asymmetric unit membrane with very low permeability to water, urea, and protons. Flexible hinge regions between plaques let the membrane fold. Tight junctions with low-permeability claudins seal the spaces between neighboring umbrella cells, and a glycosaminoglycan coat covers the surface."},{"type":"p","text":"Stretch is handled without breaking that seal. As the bladder fills, the umbrella cells unfold and flatten, and cytoplasmic fusiform vesicles, whose membranes already carry uroplakin plaques, fuse with the apical surface. This adds membrane area that is protected from the start. On emptying, the extra membrane is retrieved by endocytosis. Deeper cells slide past one another, so the epithelium thins without losing contact with the basement membrane."},{"type":"flow","title":"Bladder filling without leaking","steps":["Urine volume rises and stretches the wall","Mucosal folds unfold; umbrella cells flatten","Fusiform vesicles insert uroplakin plaques into the apical membrane","Surface area grows while tight junctions stay sealed","Detrusor relaxes, so bladder pressure stays low","After voiding, excess membrane is retrieved by endocytosis"],"caption":"The urothelium enlarges its surface using membrane that is already sealed by uroplakin plaques."},{"type":"table","title":"Layers of the bladder wall","cols":["Layer","Structure","Function"],"rows":[["Urothelium","Basal, intermediate, and umbrella cells; uroplakin plaques and tight junctions","Impermeable, stretchable lining"],["Lamina propria","Vascular connective tissue with nerves; thin, discontinuous bundles of muscularis mucosae","Supports the epithelium; carries vessels and sensory endings"],["Detrusor (muscularis propria)","Thick smooth muscle in three loosely interwoven layers (inner longitudinal, middle circular, outer longitudinal)","Contracts as a unit through parasympathetic M3 receptors; relaxes during filling through sympathetic beta3 receptors"],["Adventitia or serosa","Connective tissue; peritoneum covers the superior surface","Anchors the bladder to surrounding structures"]],"caption":"Bladder wall layers from lumen outward."},{"type":"p","text":"The interwoven detrusor bundles squeeze the lumen from every direction, which lets the bladder empty from any volume. Mucosal folds disappear on filling except over the trigone, whose mucosa is smooth because it is firmly bound to the underlying muscle. These layers matter in pathology. Most bladder cancers are urothelial carcinomas, and invasion of the detrusor marks muscle-invasive disease, so the thin muscularis mucosae must not be mistaken for it. Chronic irritation, such as from Schistosoma haematobium eggs or long-term catheters, can cause squamous metaplasia that predisposes to squamous cell carcinoma. The ureter has the same urothelium and lamina propria over an inner longitudinal and outer circular muscle coat, with a third, outer longitudinal layer added in its lower third; peristalsis, not gravity, moves urine along it."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Mice lacking a uroplakin gene have umbrella cells without normal apical plaques. What happens to water and urea movement across their urothelium?","explain":"Without uroplakin plaques the apical membrane loses its low permeability, so water and urea cross more readily and the barrier between urine and tissue weakens.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on how umbrella cells make the barrier."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Histology links the disciplines of this system. Embryology sets the plan: the metanephric mesenchyme forms the nephron from glomerulus through distal tubule, and the ureteric bud forms the collecting ducts, which is why the two halves have different cell types. Biochemistry explains the barrier, through type IV collagen chains, heparan sulfate charge, and the slit diaphragm proteins nephrin and podocin, whose gene defects give inherited nephrotic and hematuric diseases. Immunology explains location: immune complexes that pass the fenestrae collect in the mesangium (IgA nephropathy), antibodies against the alpha3 chain bind the GBM itself (anti-GBM disease), and antibodies against podocyte antigens form deposits between the GBM and the podocytes (membranous nephropathy)."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology then follow from structure. Where a cell sits and how hard it works decide how it fails: podocyte injury gives nephrotic proteinuria, outer medullary tubules die in ischemia, medullary vascular injury abolishes concentrating ability, and chronic irritation changes urothelium to squamous epithelium. The limits are worth stating. Light microscopy can look normal when podocytes are injured, the relative roles of charge and size in albumin exclusion are still debated in research literature, and numerical values such as nephron number and loop proportions vary between sources and between people."}],"summary":["The filtration barrier is fenestrated endothelium, a type IV collagen GBM rich in anionic heparan sulfate, and podocyte foot processes joined by nephrin slit diaphragms.","Albumin is near the size cutoff and anionic, so loss of charge or foot process effacement causes selective albuminuria, as in minimal change disease.","Juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole secrete renin; the macula densa in the distal thick ascending limb senses NaCl through NKCC2.","High macula densa NaCl constricts the afferent arteriole through adenosine (tubuloglomerular feedback); low NaCl promotes renin release through prostaglandin E2 and nitric oxide.","Intraglomerular mesangial cells support capillary loops, contract, and clear trapped immune complexes, which is why IgA deposits in the mesangium.","Proximal tubule cells have a brush border and dense basal mitochondria; thin limbs are squamous; distal segments lack a brush border; collecting ducts have principal and intercalated cells.","The proximal straight tubule and medullary thick ascending limb fail first in ischemic ATN because high ATP demand meets low outer medullary oxygen, producing muddy brown granular casts.","Juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops and vasa recta, which build and preserve the medullary gradient that sets maximum urine osmolality.","Medullary injury, as in sickle cell trait, causes early isosthenuria because urine can be concentrated only to the osmolality of the deep interstitium.","Umbrella cells with uroplakin plaques and tight junctions make the urothelium impermeable, and fusiform vesicles add sealed membrane as the bladder fills."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Urinary System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Glomerular Filtration, Renal Blood Flow, and Their Control; Urine Concentration and Dilution"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Organization of the urinary system; glomerular filtration and renal blood flow"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney (glomerular diseases and acute tubular injury); the lower urinary tract"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal Disease"}]},"UR-3":{"unit":"UR-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-03-01","title":"Urinary Organs: Location and Boundaries","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter places the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra in the body and names what surrounds each one. It covers renal position and relations, the renal coverings and hilum, the course and narrow points of the ureter, the bladder trigone and urethral segments, and the anatomy of male urethral injury. Board questions rarely ask for a relation as a bare fact. They describe flank pain moving toward the groin, fluid in a recess on imaging, recurrent cystitis in a young woman, or blood at the urethral meatus after trauma, and ask you to reason back to the structure involved.","prerequisites":["The peritoneum, peritoneal cavity, and the difference between intraperitoneal and retroperitoneal organs","Muscles of the posterior abdominal wall and vertebral level landmarks such as the transpyloric plane (L1)","Internal gross structure of the kidney: cortex, medulla, calyces, and renal pelvis (UR-1)","Urothelium and the layers of the ureter and bladder wall (UR-2)","Layers of the perineum: perineal membrane, deep and superficial perineal pouches, and the membranous layer of superficial fascia"],"connections":[{"to":"Gastrointestinal organs (GI-3)","how":"The duodenum, pancreas, and colic flexures lie directly on the kidneys, so disease and injury cross between the two systems."},{"to":"Urinary system in the abdominopelvic cavity (UR-4)","how":"The relations here set the stage for the renal vessels, the bladder's position in the pelvis, and the peritoneal recesses around the urinary organs."},{"to":"Reproductive anatomy (RE units)","how":"The prostate surrounds the first part of the male urethra, and the ductus deferens and uterine artery cross the pelvic ureter."},{"to":"Neuroanatomy and referred pain","how":"Visceral afferents from the kidney and ureter enter about T11 to L2, which explains why ureteric colic is felt from loin to groin."},{"to":"Microbiology of urinary infection","how":"The short female urethra close to the perineum lets bowel flora such as uropathogenic Escherichia coli reach the bladder."}],"terms":[{"term":"Retroperitoneal","def":"Lying behind the parietal peritoneum on the posterior abdominal wall, outside the peritoneal cavity."},{"term":"Secondarily retroperitoneal","def":"Describing an organ that began with a mesentery but became fused to the posterior wall, such as most of the duodenum, the pancreas, and the ascending and descending colon."},{"term":"Renal hilum","def":"The vertical slit on the medial border of the kidney where the renal vein, renal artery, and renal pelvis enter or leave."},{"term":"Renal sinus","def":"The space inside the kidney, entered through the hilum, that holds the renal pelvis, calyces, branching vessels, nerves, and fat."},{"term":"Renal fascia","def":"The membranous fascial sheath (Gerota fascia) that encloses the kidney, perirenal fat, and suprarenal gland."},{"term":"Hepatorenal recess","def":"The space of the peritoneal cavity between the right lobe of the liver and the right kidney, the lowest part of the upper abdomen in a supine patient."},{"term":"Ureteropelvic junction","def":"The point where the funnel-shaped renal pelvis narrows into the ureter."},{"term":"Ureterovesical junction","def":"The oblique passage of the ureter through the bladder wall, the narrowest part of the ureter."},{"term":"Trigone","def":"The smooth triangular area of the bladder floor between the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice."},{"term":"Perineal membrane","def":"A tough fascial sheet stretched between the ischiopubic rami that forms the floor of the deep perineal pouch and anchors the membranous urethra."},{"term":"Superficial perineal space","def":"The space between the perineal membrane and the membranous layer of superficial fascia (Colles fascia), containing the bulb and crura of the penis or clitoris."},{"term":"Posterior urethra","def":"The prostatic and membranous parts of the male urethra, as distinct from the spongy (anterior) urethra."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-03-01-C01","obj":"UR-3.1","title":"Position of the kidneys and their posterior relations","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The kidneys are retroperitoneal organs that lie in the paravertebral gutters on either side of the vertebral column, behind the parietal peritoneum and against the posterior abdominal wall. They extend from about T12 to L3. Each hilum faces anteromedially at about the level of L1, the transpyloric plane. Because the kidneys rest on the sloping psoas major, their upper poles lie closer to the midline and more posteriorly than their lower poles."},{"type":"p","text":"The right kidney lies slightly lower than the left because the large right lobe of the liver occupies the space above it. As a result the upper pole of the left kidney reaches about the 11th rib, while the upper pole of the right kidney lies at about the 12th rib. Each suprarenal gland caps the superomedial part of the upper pole."},{"type":"p","text":"The posterior relations are the muscles of the posterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm above them. The upper part of each kidney rests on the diaphragm, which separates it from the costodiaphragmatic recess of the pleura. Below the diaphragm the kidney lies on psoas major medially, quadratus lumborum in the middle, and the aponeurosis of transversus abdominis laterally. The subcostal, iliohypogastric, and ilioinguinal nerves run inferolaterally behind the kidney on these muscles."},{"type":"table","title":"Posterior relations and what they explain","cols":["Relation","Anatomical fact","Consequence"],"rows":[["Diaphragm","Upper pole lies on it","Kidney moves down a few centimeters with inspiration; a thin patient's right lower pole may be palpable"],["Pleura (costodiaphragmatic recess)","Extends below the 12th rib medially, behind the diaphragm","A posterior approach or penetrating wound near the 12th rib can enter both pleura and kidney"],["11th and 12th ribs","Lie across the upper posterior kidney","Fractures of the lower ribs can lacerate the kidney"],["Psoas major","Lies medial and posterior","Perinephric inflammation can irritate psoas, causing pain on hip extension"],["Quadratus lumborum and subcostal, iliohypogastric, ilioinguinal nerves","Lie directly behind the kidney","Renal or perinephric disease can refer pain to the flank, lower abdominal wall, and groin"]],"caption":"Each posterior relation predicts a physical finding or an injury pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"These relations explain a standard examination finding. The costovertebral angle, between the 12th rib and the lateral border of the erector spinae, overlies the kidney. A distended, inflamed kidney, as in acute pyelonephritis or obstruction, stretches its capsule, and percussion at this angle jars it and provokes pain. The finding localizes disease to the kidney rather than to an intraperitoneal organ, because nothing intraperitoneal lies between the angle and the kidney."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-03-01-C02","obj":"UR-3.2","title":"Anterior relations of the right and left kidneys","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The organs in front of the kidneys differ on the two sides, and for each one it matters whether peritoneum separates it from the kidney. Secondarily retroperitoneal organs, such as the descending duodenum, the tail of the pancreas, and the colic flexures, lie directly on the kidney with no peritoneum between them. Intraperitoneal organs, such as the liver, stomach, spleen, and jejunum, are separated from the kidney by peritoneum and a potential space."},{"type":"table","title":"Anterior relations of the kidneys","cols":["Kidney","Direct contact (no peritoneum)","Separated by peritoneum"],"rows":[["Right","Suprarenal gland (upper pole), descending duodenum (medially, near the hilum), right colic flexure (lower pole)","Liver (across the hepatorenal recess)"],["Left","Suprarenal gland (upper pole), tail of pancreas with splenic vessels (middle, near the hilum), left colic flexure and upper descending colon (lower lateral)","Stomach (across the omental bursa), spleen (upper lateral), jejunum (lower medial)"]],"caption":"Direct contacts are the routes by which disease or surgical injury passes between kidney and gut."},{"type":"p","text":"On the right, the hepatorenal recess lies between the liver and the peritoneum covering the kidney. In a supine patient it is the lowest point of the upper peritoneal cavity, so free blood or fluid collects there, and it is a standard view for detecting free fluid on bedside abdominal ultrasound. The descending duodenum lies directly on the medial part of the right kidney, close to the hilum, so a right renal mass, abscess, or operation can involve the duodenum."},{"type":"p","text":"On the left, the spleen lies against the upper lateral kidney, and the two are joined by the splenorenal ligament, which contains the splenic vessels and the pancreatic tail. A blow to the left flank therefore often injures the spleen and left kidney together. The pancreatic tail touches the left kidney directly, so pancreatic inflammation or tumor can spread into the anterior renal fascia. The stomach is separated from the kidney by the omental bursa, so disease of the posterior stomach wall spreads into the bursa or the pancreas rather than directly into the kidney."},{"type":"p","text":"The colon also explains a classic examination distinction. An enlarged kidney grows forward but stays behind the colon, so it is usually resonant to percussion over its front and can be balloted between the hands. An enlarged spleen grows forward and downward in front of the colon, so it is dull to percussion and has a palpable notch."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-03-01-C03","obj":"UR-3.3","title":"Renal coverings and the structures at the hilum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Four layers surround each kidney, and they divide the area around it into compartments. These compartments decide where blood, pus, or urine collects."},{"type":"table","title":"Renal coverings, from the kidney outward","cols":["Layer","Description","Significance"],"rows":[["Fibrous capsule","Thin, tough collagenous coat stripped easily from healthy kidney","Limits swelling, so a distended kidney stretches it and hurts; a subcapsular hematoma compresses the parenchyma"],["Perirenal (perinephric) fat","Fat directly around the kidney, continuous with the fat of the renal sinus","Cushions and helps hold the kidney in place; its loss allows the kidney to drop"],["Renal fascia (Gerota fascia)","Anterior and posterior membranous layers enclosing kidney, perirenal fat, and suprarenal gland","Forms the perirenal space that contains early renal bleeding or abscess"],["Pararenal (paranephric) fat","Fat outside the renal fascia, most abundant posteriorly","Separates the renal fascia from the posterior abdominal wall"]],"caption":"The renal fascia is the layer that defines the perirenal compartment."},{"type":"p","text":"Above and laterally, the two layers of renal fascia are fused, so the perirenal space is closed in those directions. Below, the layers are only loosely joined around the ureter. Blood or pus in the perirenal space is therefore held against the kidney at first, may displace it, and tends to track downward along the ureter toward the pelvis rather than upward. A thin septum of fascia separates the suprarenal gland from the kidney, and the gland is attached to the diaphragmatic crura. When rapid loss of perirenal fat lets the kidney descend (nephroptosis), the suprarenal gland stays in place, and the descending kidney can kink its ureter and cause intermittent obstruction."},{"type":"p","text":"The hilum opens into the renal sinus. From anterior to posterior the main hilar structures are the renal vein, the renal artery, and the renal pelvis. The pelvis is the most posterior, so it is the first hilar structure reached in a posterior approach, and the vein is the last. Branches of the artery often divide within the hilum, and a posterior segmental branch passes behind the pelvis, so the simple three-layer order describes the main trunks only."},{"type":"p","text":"The two sides differ in length. The left renal vein is long, crosses in front of the aorta just below the origin of the superior mesenteric artery, and receives the left gonadal and left suprarenal veins. Compression between these two arteries raises pressure in the left gonadal vein, which is one explanation for left varicocele. The right renal artery is the longer artery and passes behind the inferior vena cava."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A perirenal abscess forms around the right kidney. In which direction is the pus most likely to spread?","explain":"Downward toward the pelvis along the ureter. The anterior and posterior layers of renal fascia are fused above and laterally but only loosely joined below, so the inferior side is the path of least resistance.","check":"The paragraph on the closed and open sides of the renal fascia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-03-01-C04","obj":"UR-3.4","title":"Course of the ureter and the sites where stones lodge","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each ureter is a muscular tube about 25 to 30 cm long that carries urine from the renal pelvis to the bladder by peristalsis. It has an abdominal part and a pelvic part of roughly equal length, and it is retroperitoneal throughout, adherent to the peritoneum on its front."},{"type":"p","text":"The abdominal ureter leaves the renal pelvis at the ureteropelvic junction and descends on the anterior surface of psoas major, roughly over the tips of the lumbar transverse processes, which is where it is looked for on a plain radiograph. The gonadal vessels cross in front of it. At the pelvic brim, near the sacroiliac joint, it passes over the bifurcation of the common iliac artery or the start of the external iliac artery. The pelvic ureter then runs down the lateral pelvic wall in front of the internal iliac artery, turns medially at about the level of the ischial spine, and reaches the bladder. In the male the ductus deferens crosses above it near the bladder; in the female the uterine artery crosses above it beside the cervix, where it can be injured during pelvic surgery. Finally it passes obliquely through the bladder wall to open at the ureteric orifice."},{"type":"table","title":"The three narrow points of the ureter","cols":["Site","Why it is narrow","Where pain is felt when a stone lodges"],"rows":[["Ureteropelvic junction","The funnel-shaped pelvis tapers into the ureter","Flank and loin, at the costovertebral angle"],["Pelvic brim (crossing of the iliac vessels)","The ureter angles forward over the vessels","Lower abdomen and groin"],["Ureterovesical junction (narrowest)","Oblique passage through the muscular bladder wall","Groin, scrotum or labia, with urinary urgency and frequency"]],"caption":"A stone moves with peristalsis until the lumen is smaller than the stone."},{"type":"p","text":"The oblique intramural course works as a flap valve. As the bladder fills and contracts, rising pressure flattens the tunnel and stops urine from flowing back up the ureter. When the intramural segment is too short, as in primary vesicoureteral reflux, urine refluxes toward the kidney with each void, carrying bladder bacteria and predisposing to pyelonephritis and renal scarring."},{"type":"flow","title":"From a lodged stone to renal colic","steps":["Stone stops at a narrow point","Urine backs up and pressure rises above it","Ureter and pelvis distend; peristalsis fights the block","Stretch and spasm stimulate visceral afferents","Afferents enter cord at about T11 to L2","Colicky pain referred along those dermatomes","Persistent block dilates pelvis and calyces (hydronephrosis)"],"caption":"Mucosal abrasion by the stone also causes hematuria."},{"type":"example","title":"Following a stone down the ureter","text":"A 40-year-old man has severe colicky pain in the right flank that later moves to the right lower abdomen and groin. Hours later the pain reaches the tip of the penis and he feels urgent and frequent need to void. The first site, flank pain, fits obstruction at the ureteropelvic junction, which stretches the renal pelvis and capsule. Pain in the lower abdomen and groin places the stone near the pelvic brim. Urgency, frequency, and pain referred to the penis place it at the ureterovesical junction, where it irritates the trigone. Because this junction is the narrowest point, it is where a stone that has passed the other two most often stops."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a stone at the ureterovesical junction cause urgency and frequency, while a stone at the pelvic brim does not?","explain":"At the ureterovesical junction the stone sits in the bladder wall beside the trigone. Irritation there stimulates bladder sensory nerves and produces the same sensation as a full bladder. At the pelvic brim the stone is far from the bladder.","check":"The ureterovesical junction row of the narrow points table and the worked example."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-03-01-C05","obj":"UR-3.5","title":"The bladder trigone and the male and female urethra","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"On the floor of the bladder, the trigone is the triangle with the two ureteric orifices at its upper corners and the internal urethral orifice at its lower apex. A ridge, the interureteric fold, joins the two ureteric orifices. The trigone is smooth whether the bladder is full or empty because its mucosa is bound firmly to the underlying muscle. The rest of the bladder lining is loosely attached and folds into rugae when the bladder is empty. The trigone also has a distinct developmental origin, forming where the lower ends of the mesonephric ducts are absorbed into the bladder wall."},{"type":"p","text":"In the male, smooth muscle around the bladder neck forms the internal urethral sphincter. Sympathetic contraction closes it during ejaculation so that semen passes forward and not back into the bladder. Behind the internal urethral orifice, the median lobe of the prostate raises a small bulge, the uvula, which enlarges with prostatic hyperplasia and can obstruct outflow."},{"type":"table","title":"Segments of the male urethra","cols":["Segment","Approximate length","Location","Key features"],"rows":[["Prostatic (posterior urethra)","3 cm","Through the prostate","Widest and most distensible part; ejaculatory ducts and prostatic ducts open here"],["Membranous, or intermediate (posterior urethra)","1 to 2 cm","Through the deep perineal pouch and perineal membrane","Surrounded by the external urethral sphincter; fixed in place; narrowest part apart from the external orifice"],["Spongy (anterior urethra)","15 cm","In the corpus spongiosum","Bulbous part receives the bulbourethral gland ducts; penile part ends at the external urethral orifice"]],"caption":"Total male urethral length is about 18 to 22 cm."},{"type":"p","text":"The female urethra is only about 4 cm long. It runs from the bladder neck in front of the vagina, embedded in the anterior vaginal wall, and opens into the vestibule just in front of the vaginal orifice. This length and position explain why women have far more cystitis than men. Bowel organisms colonize the perineum and vaginal introitus, which sit only a few centimeters from the external urethral orifice, and they need to ascend only a short distance to reach the bladder. Sexual intercourse can push organisms into the urethra. In men the long urethra and the antibacterial activity of prostatic fluid add protection, and cystitis becomes common mainly with instrumentation or with outflow obstruction, such as prostatic enlargement, that leaves residual urine in the bladder."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ascending infection of the female urinary tract","steps":["Uropathogenic Escherichia coli from the bowel colonize the perineum","Organisms colonize the vaginal introitus and periurethral area","Bacteria ascend the short urethra","Type 1 fimbriae bind uroplakins on urothelium","Cystitis: urgency, frequency, dysuria","Reflux or ascent with P fimbriae can cause pyelonephritis"],"caption":"Short distance from the bowel reservoir to the bladder is the anatomical basis of the sex difference."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-03-01-C06","obj":"UR-3.6","title":"Male urethral injury: pelvic fracture versus straddle injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The site of a urethral tear depends on where the urethra is fixed and on which bone it is pressed against. The membranous urethra is anchored by the external urethral sphincter and the perineal membrane, which spans the pubic arch. The prostate above it is attached to the pubis by the puboprostatic ligaments. The bulbous part of the spongy urethra lies below the perineal membrane, directly under the pubic arch."},{"type":"p","text":"In a pelvic fracture, displacement of the bony ring shears the prostate away from the fixed perineal membrane, and the posterior urethra tears at or near the membranous segment. Because the tear lies above the perineal membrane, blood and urine escape into the extraperitoneal retropubic and perivesical spaces of the pelvis rather than into the perineum. The detached prostate and bladder are lifted by the hematoma, so on rectal examination the prostate feels high-riding or cannot be felt. Typical findings are blood at the external meatus and inability to void despite a full bladder."},{"type":"p","text":"In a straddle injury, such as a fall astride a beam or bicycle bar, the bulbous urethra is crushed against the inferior pubic arch below the perineal membrane. If Buck fascia (the deep fascia of the penis) stays intact, blood and urine remain in a sleeve along the penile shaft. If Buck fascia tears, they enter the superficial perineal space beneath Colles fascia. This membranous layer continues into the scrotum, around the penis, and up the anterior abdominal wall as Scarpa fascia, so swelling spreads into those regions. It fuses with the fascia lata just below the inguinal ligament and with the posterior edge of the perineal membrane, so the swelling does not reach the thighs or the anal triangle, and it produces a butterfly-shaped perineal bruise."},{"type":"table","title":"Pelvic fracture and straddle injury compared","cols":["Feature","Pelvic fracture","Straddle injury"],"rows":[["Segment torn","Membranous (posterior urethra)","Bulbous part of spongy (anterior) urethra"],["Relation to perineal membrane","Above or at it","Below it"],["Where blood and urine go","Retropubic and perivesical spaces, deep pelvis","Superficial perineal space, scrotum, penis, lower anterior abdominal wall"],["Distinguishing signs","High-riding prostate, no perineal swelling at first","Perineal butterfly bruise, scrotal and penile swelling, thighs spared"]],"caption":"Blood at the meatus occurs with both and shows only that the urethra is injured."},{"type":"flow","title":"Straddle injury to urine extravasation","steps":["Fall astride crushes bulbous urethra against pubic arch","Urethra and Buck fascia tear","Urine and blood enter the superficial perineal space","Spread under Colles fascia to scrotum and penis","Spread up the abdominal wall under Scarpa fascia","Fusion with fascia lata keeps fluid out of the thighs"],"caption":"The attachments of the membranous layer of superficial fascia set the borders of the swelling."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a urethral injury","text":"Two men have blood at the urethral meatus. The first was crushed in a car and has an unstable pelvis; rectal examination finds a boggy mass and no palpable prostate, and the perineum looks normal. The prostate has been sheared off the fixed membranous urethra and lifted by a pelvic hematoma, so the tear is posterior and above the perineal membrane. The second fell onto a fence rail and has swelling of the scrotum, penis, and lower abdominal wall but none in the thighs. This pattern matches fluid confined by Colles and Scarpa fascia, so the tear is in the bulbous urethra below the perineal membrane, with Buck fascia torn."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Development explains much of this anatomy. The kidneys develop from intermediate mesoderm behind the peritoneum in the pelvis and ascend to the lumbar region, so they remain retroperitoneal on the posterior abdominal wall. A horseshoe kidney, whose fused lower poles are caught under the inferior mesenteric artery, stays low. The trigone arises from the absorbed mesonephric ducts, separately from the rest of the bladder wall. The duodenum, pancreas, and colon become fused to the posterior wall in front of the kidneys, which is why they touch the kidneys directly while the liver, stomach, and spleen stay separated by peritoneum."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows the anatomy. Fascial compartments decide where perirenal blood or pus collects, narrow points decide where stones stop and what the kidney above them suffers, and a short urethra near the perineum decides who gets cystitis and with which organisms. Referred pain from the kidney and ureter follows the T11 to L2 spinal segments that their visceral afferents enter."}],"summary":["The kidneys are retroperitoneal from about T12 to L3; the right kidney lies slightly lower than the left because of the liver.","Posteriorly each kidney rests on the diaphragm, psoas major, quadratus lumborum, and transversus abdominis, with the subcostal, iliohypogastric, and ilioinguinal nerves behind it.","The descending duodenum, pancreatic tail, and colic flexures touch the kidneys directly; the liver, stomach, spleen, and jejunum are separated by peritoneum.","The hepatorenal recess is the lowest part of the supine upper peritoneal cavity, where free fluid first collects.","The renal coverings from inside out are fibrous capsule, perirenal fat, renal fascia, and pararenal fat; the renal fascia is closed above and laterally and open below.","At the hilum, from anterior to posterior, lie the renal vein, renal artery, and renal pelvis.","The ureter runs on psoas major behind the gonadal vessels and crosses the iliac vessels at the pelvic brim; stones lodge at the ureteropelvic junction, pelvic brim, and ureterovesical junction, the narrowest.","The trigone is smooth because its mucosa is bound to the muscle; the male urethra is prostatic, membranous, and spongy, and the 4 cm female urethra explains the higher rate of ascending infection in women.","A pelvic fracture tears the membranous urethra above the perineal membrane, with a high-riding prostate and deep pelvic extravasation.","A straddle injury tears the bulbous urethra below the perineal membrane, with spread to the scrotum, penis, and abdominal wall but not the thighs."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, section on the kidneys, ureters, and suprarenal glands"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum, sections on the urinary bladder, urethra, and perineal fasciae"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System, development of the kidneys and bladder"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney and The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, sections on urinary tract obstruction, urolithiasis, and infection"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric Gram-Negative Rods (Enterobacteriaceae), Escherichia coli"}]},"UR-4":{"unit":"UR-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-04-01","title":"Urinary System in the Abdominopelvic Cavity","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter places the kidneys, ureters, and bladder in their real surroundings: the peritoneum, the posterior abdominal wall, the pelvic organs, and the pelvic floor. Those relations explain most of what happens when the urinary tract is injured, infected, or obstructed. They decide whether a ruptured bladder leaks into the peritoneal cavity, where the pain of a ureteral stone is felt, why the kidney is tender at the costovertebral angle, why the ureter is vulnerable at hysterectomy, and why a woman leaks urine when she coughs. Board questions give the clinical picture and expect you to name the anatomical relation that produces it.","prerequisites":["Gross anatomy of the kidney, ureter, bladder, and urethra (UR-1)","Development of the peritoneal cavity and the meaning of retroperitoneal and subperitoneal","Dermatomes of T10 to L2 and the nerves of the lower abdominal wall and groin","Autonomic innervation of pelvic viscera: sympathetic T11 to L2 and parasympathetic S2 to S4","Bony pelvis and the muscles of the pelvic floor"],"connections":[{"to":"Renal physiology and acute kidney injury","how":"Ureteral obstruction raises tubular pressure and lowers GFR, the basis of postrenal acute kidney injury."},{"to":"Microbiology of urinary tract infection","how":"Ascending uropathogenic Escherichia coli reaches the kidney to cause pyelonephritis, whose tenderness and abscess spread follow the kidney's relations."},{"to":"Female reproductive anatomy (RE units)","how":"The uterus, broad ligament, and vagina shape the female peritoneal pouches and carry the uterine artery across the ureter."},{"to":"Neuroanatomy of the spinal cord and autonomic system (NE units)","how":"Convergence of visceral and somatic afferents explains referred pain, and spinal and pontine centers control continence and voiding."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal trauma (MS units)","how":"Pelvic fracture is the usual cause of extraperitoneal bladder rupture."}],"terms":[{"term":"Retroperitoneal","def":"Lying behind the parietal peritoneum on the posterior abdominal wall, without a mesentery, as the kidneys and ureters do."},{"term":"Extraperitoneal (subperitoneal)","def":"Lying outside the peritoneal cavity beneath its pelvic floor, as the bladder does, with peritoneum covering only its superior surface."},{"term":"Retropubic space","def":"The fat-filled extraperitoneal space between the pubic symphysis and the anterior bladder wall."},{"term":"Rectovesical pouch","def":"The peritoneal recess between bladder and rectum in the male, the lowest point of his peritoneal cavity when upright."},{"term":"Rectouterine pouch","def":"The peritoneal recess between uterus and rectum in the female (pouch of Douglas), the lowest point of her peritoneal cavity when upright."},{"term":"Referred pain","def":"Pain from a viscus felt in the skin or body wall supplied by the same spinal cord segments that receive the visceral afferents."},{"term":"Costovertebral angle","def":"The angle between the 12th rib and the lateral border of the erector spinae, overlying the kidney posteriorly."},{"term":"Renal fascia","def":"The fascial sheath (Gerota fascia) enclosing the kidney, perirenal fat, and suprarenal gland, open loosely below."},{"term":"Hydronephrosis","def":"Dilation of the renal pelvis and calyces caused by obstruction to urine outflow."},{"term":"Urethral closure pressure","def":"The pressure within the urethra that resists flow; continence requires it to exceed bladder pressure."},{"term":"Detrusor overactivity","def":"Involuntary contractions of the bladder wall muscle during filling, the mechanism of urge incontinence."},{"term":"Post-void residual","def":"The volume of urine left in the bladder after voiding; high in overflow incontinence."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-04-01-C01","obj":"UR-4.1","title":"Peritoneal position of the urinary organs and bladder rupture","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The urinary organs have three different relationships to the peritoneum, and those relationships decide where urine, blood, or pus goes after injury. The kidneys and ureters are retroperitoneal: they develop and remain behind the parietal peritoneum on the posterior abdominal wall, embedded in fat and fascia, with peritoneum touching only parts of their anterior surfaces. They have no mesentery and stay fixed in place."},{"type":"p","text":"The urinary bladder is extraperitoneal and lies in the anterior pelvis behind the pubic symphysis, separated from it by the fat of the retropubic space. Peritoneum covers only its superior surface, which becomes the dome as the bladder fills. An empty adult bladder lies within the pelvis. As it fills, it rises above the pubis into the extraperitoneal plane behind the anterior abdominal wall and lifts the parietal peritoneum off that wall, so a distended bladder can be entered just above the pubis without crossing the peritoneal cavity."},{"type":"table","title":"Peritoneal position of the urinary organs","cols":["Organ","Relation to peritoneum","Where fluid goes after injury"],"rows":[["Kidney","Retroperitoneal, inside the renal fascia","Perirenal space, held by the renal fascia"],["Ureter","Retroperitoneal, on psoas major and across the pelvic brim","Retroperitoneum or pelvis (urinoma)"],["Bladder","Extraperitoneal; peritoneum on superior surface only","Dome tear: peritoneal cavity. Low wall tear: retropubic and perivesical space"]],"caption":"Kidneys and ureters lie behind the peritoneum; the bladder lies below it with only its dome covered."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the site of the tear decides where urine goes"},{"type":"p","text":"A full bladder behaves like a thin-walled sphere under tension. A sudden blow to the lower abdomen (seat belt, steering wheel, or a fall, often after drinking) raises intravesical pressure abruptly. The dome is the most mobile and least supported part of the wall, with no bone or dense fascia around it, so it bursts first. Because the dome is the peritoneum-covered surface, urine enters the peritoneal cavity: intraperitoneal rupture."},{"type":"p","text":"Pelvic fracture tears the bladder differently: bone fragments puncture the bladder, or shearing at the ligaments that anchor its base to the pubis tears the anterolateral wall near the bladder neck. That wall lies below the peritoneal reflection, so urine escapes into the retropubic and perivesical tissues and can track along fascial planes into the lower abdominal wall, perineum, or scrotum. Extraperitoneal rupture is the more common type overall because pelvic fracture is the usual cause of bladder injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Intraperitoneal urine causes chemical peritonitis, ileus, and urinary ascites. The peritoneum acts as a large semipermeable membrane, so urea, creatinine, and potassium diffuse from the spilled urine into the blood, and serum urea nitrogen and creatinine rise while the kidneys filter normally (pseudo-azotemia, or reverse autodialysis). Extraperitoneal leakage gives suprapubic pain and swelling without peritoneal signs."},{"type":"flow","title":"Intraperitoneal bladder rupture","steps":["Full bladder rises; peritoneum covers its dome","Blunt lower abdominal blow sharply raises bladder pressure","Dome, the least supported wall, tears","Urine enters the peritoneal cavity","Chemical peritonitis, ileus, urinary ascites","Peritoneum absorbs urea and creatinine; serum values rise"],"caption":"The tear site, not the force, determines whether urine is intraperitoneal."},{"type":"table","title":"Intraperitoneal versus extraperitoneal bladder rupture","cols":["Feature","Intraperitoneal","Extraperitoneal"],"rows":[["Usual cause","Blow to a full bladder","Pelvic fracture"],["Site of tear","Dome","Anterolateral wall near the neck"],["Urine collects in","Peritoneal cavity","Retropubic and perivesical space"],["Peritoneal signs","Present","Absent"],["Serum urea and creatinine","Often rise from peritoneal absorption","Usually little change"],["Cystogram","Contrast around bowel loops","Flame-shaped contrast at the bladder base"]],"caption":"Gross hematuria and difficulty voiding are common to both."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients with normal kidneys are injured: one has a pelvic fracture, the other a blow to a full bladder. Predict which one shows rising serum creatinine over the next day.","explain":"Only urine in the peritoneal cavity contacts a large absorptive membrane, so the intraperitoneal rupture raises creatinine without kidney injury.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on peritoneal absorption and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-04-01-C02","obj":"UR-4.2","title":"Peritoneal reflections over the bladder and the pelvic pouches","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Parietal peritoneum lining the anterior abdominal wall passes back onto the superior surface of the bladder. On each side, between the median and medial umbilical folds, it forms a shallow supravesical fossa. From the bladder the peritoneum continues backward over whatever organs lie in the midline of the pelvis, and a pouch forms wherever it dips down between two organs before climbing onto the next. The pelvic viscera themselves stay below this sheet, in the subperitoneal space."},{"type":"p","text":"In the male nothing lies between bladder and rectum in the midline. Peritoneum covers the superior surface of the bladder, descends a short way down its posterior surface over the upper ends of the seminal vesicles, then reflects backward onto the front of the rectum. The resulting rectovesical pouch is the lowest point of the male peritoneal cavity in the upright position. Below its floor, the prostate and seminal vesicles are separated from the rectum only by the rectovesical septum, a fascial layer with no peritoneum."},{"type":"p","text":"In the female the uterus lies between bladder and rectum and divides the space in two. Peritoneum leaves the bladder and reflects onto the anterior uterus at about the level of the isthmus, forming the shallow vesicouterine pouch, which is usually empty and changes shape as the bladder fills. Peritoneum then covers the fundus and body of the uterus, continues down its posterior surface past the cervix onto the posterior fornix of the vagina, and reflects onto the rectum as the deep rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas). Laterally the uterine peritoneum continues as the broad ligament."},{"type":"p","text":"The rectum relates to these reflections by thirds: its upper third is covered in front and at the sides, its middle third in front only, and its lower third not at all."},{"type":"table","title":"Pelvic peritoneal pouches","cols":["Pouch","Sex","In front","Behind","Significance"],"rows":[["Rectovesical","Male","Bladder and seminal vesicles","Rectum","Lowest point of male peritoneal cavity when upright"],["Vesicouterine","Female","Bladder","Uterus at the isthmus","Shallow, usually empty; plane between bladder and lower uterus"],["Rectouterine (Douglas)","Female","Uterus, cervix, posterior vaginal fornix","Rectum","Lowest point of female peritoneal cavity when upright; close to the vaginal lumen"]],"caption":"A pouch forms wherever the peritoneal sheet dips between two midline pelvic organs."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the pouches matter"},{"type":"p","text":"Because the deepest pouch is the dependent part of the peritoneal cavity in an upright person, free fluid collects there: blood from a ruptured ectopic pregnancy or ovarian cyst, pus from appendicitis or pelvic inflammatory disease, and ascites. In the female only the thin posterior vaginal wall and peritoneum separate the rectouterine pouch from the vagina, so fluid there is seen on transvaginal ultrasound and can be sampled through the posterior fornix (culdocentesis). Tumor cells spreading across the peritoneal cavity, for example from gastric carcinoma, can implant in the pouch and form a firm shelf felt anterior to the rectum on rectal examination (Blumer shelf). When a person lies supine, the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) becomes a dependent recess of the upper abdomen, so where fluid pools depends on posture."},{"type":"p","text":"The bladder dome is the only bladder surface facing the peritoneal cavity, so urine from a dome rupture (C01) settles in these pouches."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-04-01-C03","obj":"UR-4.3","title":"Ureteral colic and referred pain from the upper urinary tract","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The renal pelvis and ureter move urine by peristalsis. Pacemaker cells in the minor calyces and renal pelvis start waves of smooth muscle contraction that sweep down the ureter a few times per minute, and the oblique intramural course of the ureter through the bladder wall acts as a flap valve against reflux. The ureter narrows at three points where stones lodge: the ureteropelvic junction, the crossing of the iliac vessels at the pelvic brim, and the ureterovesical junction, usually the narrowest."},{"type":"p","text":"A stone that blocks the ureter raises pressure above it. The wall and collecting system stretch, the renal capsule may distend, and the smooth muscle contracts forcefully against the obstruction. Visceral afferents in the wall respond to stretch and spasm rather than to cutting or burning, so an obstructed ureter produces severe pain. The pain, called colic, comes in waves on a background ache as peristalsis and pressure change."},{"type":"p","text":"These afferents travel back with sympathetic fibers, through the renal and ureteric plexuses, the lesser, least, and lumbar splanchnic nerves, and the sympathetic trunk, to dorsal root ganglia of about T11 to L2 (the kidney itself about T10 to L1). In the dorsal horn they synapse on second-order neurons that also receive cutaneous afferents from the same segments. The brain normally receives input from these neurons from the skin, so it projects the visceral signal onto the body wall served by those segments. This is referred pain by convergence and projection."},{"type":"flow","title":"Referred pain of ureteral colic","steps":["Stone obstructs the ureter; wall stretches and spasms","Visceral afferents run with sympathetic fibers","Afferents enter cord at about T11 to L2","They converge on dorsal horn neurons with skin input","Brain projects pain to flank, groin, and genital skin","Pain shifts toward the groin as the stone descends"],"caption":"Shared dorsal horn neurons let visceral input be read as body wall pain."},{"type":"table","title":"Where the pain is felt as a stone descends","cols":["Stone position","Pain felt in","Main segments"],"rows":[["Ureteropelvic junction","Flank and loin, below the 12th rib","About T11 to T12"],["Mid ureter, pelvic brim","Lower quadrant and inguinal region","About T12 to L1"],["Ureterovesical junction","Groin, scrotum or labium majus, upper anterior thigh; urgency and frequency","L1 to L2 (ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves)"]],"caption":"Pain is ipsilateral because a paired organ sends afferents to its own side of the cord."},{"type":"p","text":"Visceral pain is not worsened by movement, so a patient with colic is restless and cannot find a comfortable position. A patient with peritonitis lies still, because movement shifts inflamed parietal peritoneum, which has somatic innervation. Nausea and vomiting are common because the same visceral input drives autonomic reflexes, and hematuria, often microscopic, comes from the stone abrading the urothelium. A stone at the ureterovesical junction also irritates the bladder wall, adding urgency and frequency."},{"type":"p","text":"The bladder follows a different rule. Pain from its superior surface, which touches peritoneum, returns with sympathetic fibers to about T11 to L2 and is felt suprapubically. Pain from the bladder neck and urethra, below the pelvic pain line, returns with pelvic splanchnic nerves to S2 to S4 and is felt in the perineum."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A stone moves from the ureteropelvic junction to the ureterovesical junction over two days. Predict how the location of pain and the urinary symptoms change.","explain":"Pain moves from flank to groin and genitalia as lower segments receive the input, and bladder wall irritation adds urgency and frequency.","check":"Compare with the referred pain flow and the stone position table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-04-01-C04","obj":"UR-4.4","title":"Posterior relations of the kidney, costovertebral angle tenderness, and perinephric abscess","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The kidneys lie against the posterior abdominal wall at about the levels of T12 to L3, the right slightly lower than the left because of the liver. Their posterior surfaces rest on the diaphragm above and on the psoas major, quadratus lumborum, and transversus abdominis aponeurosis below. The 12th rib crosses the posterior surface of the upper part of each kidney (the left kidney also reaches the 11th rib), and the subcostal, iliohypogastric, and ilioinguinal nerves run between kidney and muscle. Above the 12th rib only the diaphragm separates the kidney from the costodiaphragmatic recess of the pleura. Each kidney moves about 2 to 3 cm vertically with deep breathing."},{"type":"p","text":"The costovertebral angle is formed by the 12th rib and the lateral border of the erector spinae. The kidney lies directly deep to it, separated from the skin by muscle and fat with no other organ between. In acute pyelonephritis, bacteria (most often uropathogenic Escherichia coli ascending from the bladder) invade the pelvis and interstitium and provoke neutrophilic inflammation and edema. The kidney swells inside its fibrous capsule, which is innervated and pain-sensitive. A percussion blow at the costovertebral angle sends a jolt straight to the stretched capsule and produces sharp pain. Cystitis does not do this because the kidney is not inflamed, so costovertebral angle tenderness, fever, and white cell casts (which form only in renal tubules) localize infection to the kidney."},{"type":"table","title":"Layers around the kidney, inside to outside","cols":["Layer","Description","Clinical relevance"],"rows":[["Fibrous (renal) capsule","Thin, tough, adherent to the cortex","Stretch causes pain in pyelonephritis and obstruction"],["Perirenal (perinephric) fat","Fat around kidney and hilum, continuous into the renal sinus","Space filled by perinephric abscess or hematoma"],["Renal fascia (Gerota)","Encloses kidney, perirenal fat, and suprarenal gland; fused above and laterally, loose below","Confines pus and blood; weak inferiorly along the ureter"],["Pararenal (paranephric) fat","Fat outside the fascia, most marked posteriorly","Separates the fascia from the posterior wall muscles"]],"caption":"The renal fascia defines a closed compartment except at its lower end."},{"type":"p","text":"The anterior and posterior layers of the renal fascia fuse above the suprarenal gland, where they join the fascia of the diaphragm, and fuse laterally. Medially the anterior layer blends with the sheaths of the renal vessels and the posterior layer with the psoas fascia. Inferiorly the layers are only loosely united around the ureter, so this is the open end of the compartment."},{"type":"p","text":"A perinephric abscess usually forms when an intrarenal abscess ruptures through the capsule, typically when pyelonephritis is complicated by obstruction or diabetes, and occasionally from bloodborne Staphylococcus aureus. Pus fills the perirenal space and is held there by the renal fascia, so it stays on one side and spreads poorly upward. Where the fascia is weakest, inferiorly along the ureter, pus tracks down toward the iliac fossa and pelvis. Contact with the posterior wall explains the other signs: a flank mass or fullness, pain on hip extension from psoas irritation (the patient holds the hip flexed), and, through the diaphragm, pleuritic pain or a reactive pleural effusion. The same fascia limits a perirenal hematoma after trauma by tamponade."},{"type":"flow","title":"From pyelonephritis to a spreading perinephric abscess","steps":["Ascending infection reaches the renal pelvis and interstitium","Intrarenal abscess forms, favored by obstruction or diabetes","Abscess ruptures through the fibrous capsule","Pus fills the perirenal fat inside the renal fascia","Fascia blocks upward and medial spread","Pus tracks down the loose lower end toward the pelvis"],"caption":"The shape of the renal fascia sets the direction of spread."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-04-01-C05","obj":"UR-4.5","title":"The ureter at hysterectomy: anatomy of the injury and its consequences","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each ureter (about 25 to 30 cm) descends on the psoas major, is crossed anteriorly by the gonadal vessels, and enters the pelvis by crossing the pelvic brim anterior to the bifurcation of the common iliac artery. It runs down the lateral pelvic wall in front of the internal iliac artery and, at about the level of the ischial spine, turns forward and medially toward the bladder. In the male the ductus deferens crosses above it near the bladder. In the female the ureter runs in the base of the broad ligament, through the connective tissue of the cardinal (transverse cervical) ligament, and passes beneath the uterine artery about 2 cm lateral to the supravaginal cervix, then runs close to the lateral vaginal fornix before entering the bladder. The relation is often summarized as water (urine in the ureter) under the bridge (the uterine artery)."},{"type":"p","text":"During hysterectomy the uterine arteries are clamped and divided beside the cervix, at the point where the ureter passes beneath them, so a clamp placed too far laterally or a suture placed blindly to control bleeding can include the ureter. Other sites at risk are the pelvic brim, where the ureter lies immediately beside the ovarian vessels in the suspensory (infundibulopelvic) ligament, and the vaginal cuff near the ureterovesical junction."},{"type":"p","text":"The ureter's arteries are segmental. Branches of the renal, gonadal, and aortic vessels reach the abdominal part from its medial side, and branches of the internal iliac artery, including the uterine and vesical arteries, reach the pelvic part from its lateral side, anastomosing in the adventitia. Stripping the adventitia or using cautery close to it can devascularize a segment without cutting it, causing ischemic necrosis and a urine leak that typically appears one to three weeks later."},{"type":"h","text":"Predicting the result"},{"type":"p","text":"Ligation blocks outflow. Pressure rises in the ureter and renal pelvis above the tie, the collecting system dilates (hydroureter and hydronephrosis), and the raised hydrostatic pressure in the tubules and Bowman space opposes filtration, so that kidney's GFR falls. Unilateral ligation may cause flank pain, fever, or nothing, and serum creatinine changes little because the other kidney compensates. Bilateral ligation causes anuria immediately after surgery and a rapidly rising creatinine, a postrenal acute kidney injury."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ureteral ligation to hydronephrosis","steps":["Ligature closes the ureter beneath the uterine artery","Urine accumulates; ureteral and pelvic pressure rise","Collecting system dilates: hydroureter and hydronephrosis","Raised tubular and Bowman space pressure opposes filtration","GFR of that kidney falls","Prolonged block: papillary flattening and tubular atrophy"],"caption":"Bilateral ligation produces anuria; unilateral ligation may be silent."},{"type":"p","text":"Transection or ischemic necrosis causes leakage. Urine collects in the retroperitoneum or pelvis as a urinoma, can enter the peritoneal cavity and be reabsorbed (as in C01), or drains through the vaginal cuff as a ureterovaginal fistula."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: which structure is leaking?","text":"Ten days after an abdominal hysterectomy, a woman reports constant watery vaginal discharge day and night, yet she still feels a normal urge and voids normal volumes. Fluid creatinine many times the serum value shows the fluid is urine. A leaking bladder (vesicovaginal fistula) would drain continuously, leaving little to void. Normal voiding means the bladder is filling, so one ureter still drains into it while the other drains into the vagina: a ureterovaginal fistula. The delay of ten days fits ischemic necrosis of a devascularized or crushed segment; an unrecognized clean transection usually leaks within the first few days."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-04-01-C06","obj":"UR-4.6","title":"Urethral support, cystocele, and the mechanisms of incontinence","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Urine stays in the bladder as long as urethral closure pressure exceeds bladder pressure. During filling, sympathetic fibers from about T11 to L2 relax the detrusor through beta-3 receptors, so bladder pressure stays low as volume rises, and contract smooth muscle at the bladder neck and proximal urethra through alpha-1 receptors. Pudendal motor fibers (S2 to S4) keep the striated external urethral sphincter in tone; an estrogen-sensitive mucosa and submucosal vascular cushion help seal the lumen. Voiding begins when the pontine micturition center, released from cortical inhibition, drives parasympathetic pelvic splanchnic fibers (S2 to S4): acetylcholine on M3 receptors contracts the detrusor while the sphincters relax."},{"type":"p","text":"The proximal urethra and bladder neck rest on the anterior vaginal wall and its endopelvic (pubocervical) fascia, anchored to the pelvic sidewall and backed by the levator ani. When a cough raises abdominal pressure, the urethra is pressed against this firm backing, so urethral closure pressure rises along with bladder pressure. Vaginal childbirth can stretch or tear the levator ani and fascia and stretch the pudendal nerve; estrogen loss after menopause thins the mucosa and connective tissue."},{"type":"h","text":"Stress incontinence and cystocele"},{"type":"p","text":"When support fails, the bladder neck and urethra become hypermobile and descend with a cough, so the pressure rise reaches the bladder without equally compressing the urethra. Small spurts of urine escape with coughing, sneezing, laughing, or lifting, without urge. A weak sphincter (intrinsic sphincter deficiency), as after prostate surgery, does the same. A cystocele is descent of the bladder base into the anterior vaginal wall when the pubocervical fascia weakens. The woman feels a bulge or pelvic pressure, and a large cystocele can kink the urethra, causing incomplete emptying that may mask stress leakage."},{"type":"h","text":"Urge and overflow incontinence"},{"type":"p","text":"Urge incontinence results from detrusor overactivity: involuntary detrusor contractions during filling raise bladder pressure above urethral pressure without warning, giving a sudden intense need to void, leakage before reaching a toilet, frequency, and nocturia. It follows loss of cortical inhibition of the micturition reflex, bladder wall irritation, or idiopathic overactivity in older adults."},{"type":"p","text":"Overflow incontinence occurs when the bladder cannot empty. Either the detrusor contracts weakly or the outlet is obstructed. The bladder distends until its pressure passively exceeds urethral resistance and then dribbles continually. The bladder is palpable and the post-void residual is high, whereas it is low in pure stress or urge incontinence."},{"type":"table","title":"Incontinence by mechanism","cols":["Type","Mechanism","Typical causes","Key findings"],"rows":[["Stress","Abdominal pressure exceeds urethral closure pressure; poor support or weak sphincter","Vaginal delivery, menopause, obesity, chronic straining, prostate surgery","Small leaks with cough or lifting; no urge; low residual"],["Urge","Involuntary detrusor contractions (detrusor overactivity)","Stroke, dementia, Parkinson disease, multiple sclerosis, cystitis, stones, idiopathic","Sudden urge, leak on the way, frequency, nocturia; low residual"],["Overflow","Incomplete emptying from weak detrusor or outlet obstruction","Diabetic neuropathy, sacral cord or cauda equina lesions, anticholinergic drugs, prostatic enlargement, urethral stricture","Continuous dribbling, palpable bladder, high residual"]],"caption":"Post-void residual separates overflow from stress and urge."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: sorting three patients","text":"A 48-year-old woman with three vaginal deliveries leaks a little when she coughs; her post-void residual is 20 mL. Poor urethral support means stress incontinence. A 74-year-old man after a stroke feels a sudden urge and wets himself on the way; his residual is low. Lost cortical inhibition lets the micturition reflex fire early: urge incontinence from detrusor overactivity. A 66-year-old man with long-standing diabetes dribbles constantly and has a palpable bladder holding several hundred milliliters after voiding. Autonomic neuropathy weakens the detrusor and blunts bladder sensation, so the bladder overfills until pressure forces urine past the sphincter: overflow incontinence."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict the post-void residual and the trigger for leakage in stress, urge, and overflow incontinence.","explain":"Stress and urge leak with a pressure rise from outside or from the detrusor but empty normally; overflow leaks because the bladder never empties.","check":"The incontinence table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and gross anatomy set the stage. The kidneys and ureters develop on the posterior abdominal wall and stay retroperitoneal, while the bladder descends into the pelvis below a peritoneal sheet that covers only its dome, so injury anywhere in the tract is sorted by its relation to the peritoneum. The renal fascia, the uterine artery crossing, and the pelvic floor are fixed structures, and each one predicts a pattern: where pus spreads, where a clamp catches the ureter, and how a cough is transmitted to the urethra."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology, microbiology, and pathology act on that anatomy. Obstruction lowers GFR through back pressure, ascending E. coli turns the kidney's posterior relations into costovertebral angle tenderness and abscess spread, and segmental afferent and efferent innervation explains both referred colic and the separate failures of storage and emptying. The limits are real: referred pain maps overlap between segments, intraperitoneal and extraperitoneal bladder rupture can coexist in severe pelvic trauma, and many patients have mixed incontinence, so the mechanism is read from the whole picture rather than one sign."}],"summary":["Kidneys and ureters are retroperitoneal; the bladder is extraperitoneal with peritoneum on its superior surface only.","A blow to a full bladder tears the dome and spills urine into the peritoneal cavity, where peritoneal absorption raises serum urea and creatinine.","Pelvic fracture tears the low anterolateral bladder wall and leaks urine into the retropubic and perivesical space.","Peritoneum forms the rectovesical pouch in males and the vesicouterine and deep rectouterine pouches in females; the deepest pouch collects fluid when upright.","Ureteral afferents run with sympathetic fibers to about T11 to L2 and converge with skin afferents, so colic moves from flank to groin and genitalia as the stone descends.","The kidney lies deep to the costovertebral angle against the 12th rib and posterior wall, so percussion jolts the stretched capsule in pyelonephritis.","The renal fascia confines perinephric pus and blood but is loose inferiorly, so abscesses track down toward the pelvis.","The ureter passes beneath the uterine artery about 2 cm lateral to the cervix; ligation causes hydronephrosis, and transection or devascularization causes leakage or fistula.","Stress incontinence comes from poor urethral support or sphincter weakness, urge from detrusor overactivity, and overflow from incomplete emptying with a high residual."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen: kidneys, ureters, and suprarenal glands; renal fascia and fat"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum: urinary organs, pelvic peritoneum, and pelvic floor"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Micturition"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney: pyelonephritis and urinary tract obstruction"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal Disease"}]},"UR-5":{"unit":"UR-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-05-01","title":"Renal Circulation","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows blood through the kidney and explains why its layout matters. Two capillary beds sit in series between two arterioles, so the tone of each arteriole sets renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate (GFR), and filtration fraction, and two intrinsic mechanisms hold GFR steady as blood pressure changes. The same anatomy explains disease: end arteries produce wedge-shaped infarcts, the slow and hypoxic vasa recta favor sickling and papillary necrosis, and the course of the left renal vein links a renal tumor to a left varicocele. Board questions ask you to predict a number or a lesion from the vessel involved, so focus on the reasoning from structure to consequence.","prerequisites":["Gross structure of the kidney: cortex, medulla, pyramids, renal columns, sinus, and hilum (UR-3, UR-4)","Nephron segments and the difference between cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons (UR-2)","Pressure, flow, and resistance relationships and Starling forces across capillaries (CV-11)","Structure of hemoglobin S and the conditions that promote its polymerization (HE-5)","Types of necrosis and the difference between pale and hemorrhagic infarcts"],"connections":[{"to":"Filtration, reabsorption, and secretion (UR-6)","how":"Glomerular and peritubular Starling forces depend directly on the arteriolar resistances described here."},{"to":"Kidney in blood pressure control (UR-7)","how":"The macula densa and juxtaglomerular cells that drive tubuloglomerular feedback also control renin release and the renin-angiotensin system."},{"to":"Hemodynamics and blood flow regulation (CV-11)","how":"Renal blood flow obeys flow equals pressure difference divided by resistance, and autoregulation is a renal case of local flow control."},{"to":"Diseases of blood cell lysis (HE-9-B)","how":"Renal medullary conditions are severe enough to make red cells sickle even in sickle cell trait."},{"to":"Urinary vascular diseases and neoplasms (UR-12-F, UR-12-G)","how":"Renal infarction, renal vein compression, and renal cell carcinoma invading the renal vein follow from the vascular anatomy in this chapter."}],"terms":[{"term":"Renal plasma flow (RPF)","def":"The volume of plasma reaching the kidneys per minute, about 650 mL/min in an adult, equal to renal blood flow multiplied by (1 minus hematocrit)."},{"term":"Filtration fraction (FF)","def":"The fraction of renal plasma flow that is filtered, GFR divided by RPF, normally about 0.2."},{"term":"End artery","def":"An artery that is the only supply to its territory, with no anastomoses large enough to keep the tissue alive if it is blocked."},{"term":"Autoregulation","def":"The intrinsic ability of the kidney to keep renal blood flow and GFR nearly constant across a wide range of arterial pressure."},{"term":"Myogenic response","def":"Contraction of arteriolar smooth muscle in response to stretch of the vessel wall by a rise in pressure."},{"term":"Macula densa","def":"A plaque of specialized cells in the thick ascending limb, where it touches its own glomerulus, that senses the NaCl concentration of tubular fluid."},{"term":"Tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF)","def":"Adjustment of afferent arteriolar tone by the macula densa according to the NaCl delivered to it, which returns that nephron's GFR toward normal."},{"term":"Juxtaglomerular apparatus","def":"The macula densa, the renin-secreting granular cells of the afferent arteriole, and the extraglomerular mesangial cells, grouped at the vascular pole of the glomerulus."},{"term":"Vasa recta","def":"Long hairpin capillaries arising from efferent arterioles of juxtamedullary nephrons that supply the renal medulla."},{"term":"Countercurrent exchange","def":"Passive exchange of water, solutes, and gases between closely apposed vessels carrying blood in opposite directions."},{"term":"Papillary necrosis","def":"Ischemic necrosis of the renal papillae, the tips of the medullary pyramids, where blood supply and oxygen are poorest."},{"term":"Varicocele","def":"Dilation and tortuosity of the veins of the pampiniform plexus of the spermatic cord."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-05-01-C01","obj":"UR-5.1","title":"The path of blood through the kidney","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The kidneys make up about 0.5% of body weight but receive about 20 to 25% of the cardiac output at rest, roughly 1.1 to 1.2 L/min, which gives a renal plasma flow of about 650 mL/min. This flow far exceeds the kidneys' metabolic needs. Its purpose is to deliver plasma for filtration and processing, and about 90% of it goes to the cortex, where the glomeruli are; the medulla receives the remaining 10% or so."},{"type":"p","text":"Each renal artery leaves the aorta at about the level of the L1 to L2 intervertebral disc. Near the hilum it divides into anterior and posterior divisions that give rise to about five segmental arteries. Within the sinus these branch into interlobar arteries, which run through the renal columns between the pyramids. At the corticomedullary junction the interlobar arteries bend over the bases of the pyramids as arcuate arteries, and these send cortical radiate (interlobular) arteries outward toward the capsule. Each cortical radiate artery gives off afferent arterioles, and each afferent arteriole feeds one glomerulus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of blood through the kidney","steps":["Renal artery to segmental arteries at the hilum","Interlobar arteries in the renal columns","Arcuate arteries over the pyramid bases","Cortical radiate arteries toward the capsule","Afferent arteriole to glomerular capillaries","Efferent arteriole","Peritubular capillaries (cortex) or vasa recta (medulla)","Cortical radiate, arcuate, interlobar veins to renal vein"],"caption":"Two capillary beds lie in series, separated by the efferent arteriole."},{"type":"p","text":"The defining feature is two capillary beds in series, each with a resistance vessel on either side. Because the glomerular capillaries lie between the afferent and efferent arterioles, their hydrostatic pressure stays high, about 60 mm Hg, along their whole length, which favors filtration. The efferent arteriole then drops the pressure again, so the peritubular capillaries receive blood at about 13 mm Hg. That blood also has a high oncotic pressure, because about one fifth of the plasma water but almost none of the protein was removed in the glomerulus. Low hydrostatic and high oncotic pressure favor uptake of the fluid that the tubules reabsorb."},{"type":"p","text":"The second bed depends on nephron type. Efferent arterioles of cortical nephrons, which have short loops of Henle, break into peritubular capillaries around the cortical tubules. Efferent arterioles of juxtamedullary nephrons, whose glomeruli lie near the corticomedullary junction and whose long loops reach deep into the medulla, form the vasa recta (C05). Blood returns through cortical radiate, arcuate, and interlobar veins to the renal vein. Unlike the arteries, the intrarenal veins anastomose freely, which matters for infarction (C02)."},{"type":"table","title":"Renal vessels in order","cols":["Vessel","Location","Key feature"],"rows":[["Segmental arteries","Renal sinus, from anterior and posterior divisions","End arteries, each supplying one vascular segment"],["Interlobar arteries","Renal columns, between pyramids","End arteries"],["Arcuate arteries","Corticomedullary junction, over pyramid bases","Arch without forming complete anastomotic arcades"],["Cortical radiate arteries","Cortex, running toward the capsule","Give off afferent arterioles"],["Afferent arteriole","Enters the glomerulus at the vascular pole","Main site of autoregulation; contains renin-secreting granular cells"],["Glomerular capillaries","Inside Bowman's capsule","High pressure, about 60 mm Hg; filtration"],["Efferent arteriole","Leaves the glomerulus at the vascular pole","Second resistance; sets glomerular pressure from downstream"],["Peritubular capillaries","Around cortical tubules","Low pressure, high oncotic pressure; reabsorption"],["Vasa recta","Medulla, beside loops of Henle and collecting ducts","Slow hairpin flow; countercurrent exchange"]],"caption":"Pressures are approximate values from Guyton and Hall for a mean arterial pressure near 100 mm Hg."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-05-01-C02","obj":"UR-5.2","title":"End arteries and wedge-shaped renal infarcts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An end artery is the only arterial supply to its territory, so when it is blocked no neighboring vessel can carry enough blood in to keep the tissue alive. The segmental arteries divide the kidney into vascular segments that share no useful collateral flow, and the plane between the anterior and posterior segments is relatively avascular. The pattern continues inside the kidney: interlobar arteries do not connect with each other, the arcuate arteries arch over the pyramids without forming complete arcades, and cortical radiate arteries supply their own columns of nephrons. Accessory renal arteries, which enter a pole directly, are end arteries as well, so losing one infarcts that pole."},{"type":"p","text":"The usual cause is an embolus from the left side of the heart: thrombus from the left atrium in atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus after a myocardial infarction, or a fragment of an infective endocarditis vegetation. The embolus lodges where the artery becomes narrower than the embolus. Everything that artery supplies lies downstream and fans outward toward the capsule, so the dead tissue forms a cone, seen on the cut surface as a wedge with its base at the capsular surface and its apex pointing toward the medulla and hilum."},{"type":"flow","title":"From embolus to wedge-shaped infarct","steps":["Left atrial or ventricular thrombus fragments","Embolus lodges in a segmental or interlobar artery","No collateral flow reaches the territory","Ischemic coagulative necrosis of a cone of tissue","Pale wedge: base at capsule, apex toward medulla","Healing leaves a depressed cortical scar"],"caption":"The infarct takes the shape of the occluded artery's territory."},{"type":"p","text":"Renal infarcts are pale (anemic). The kidney is a solid organ with a single arterial supply, so little blood seeps into the dead tissue, unlike the hemorrhagic infarcts of the lung, which has a dual supply, or of loose tissues such as bowel. Coagulative necrosis preserves the outlines of tubules and glomeruli for several days while the nuclei disappear. Within a day or two a hyperemic, inflamed border develops at the margin, and the infarct heals as a depressed, V-shaped fibrous scar. A thin rim of subcapsular cortex is often spared because capsular vessels supply it."},{"type":"p","text":"Manifestations follow from the necrosis: sudden flank pain, hematuria, and a rise in serum lactate dehydrogenase released from dead cells, although small infarcts may be silent. Two look-alikes are worth separating. Cholesterol atheroemboli, often after aortic instrumentation, lodge in small arcuate and cortical radiate arteries and cause many tiny patchy infarcts rather than one large wedge. Renal vein thrombosis produces a swollen, congested kidney rather than a pale wedge, because arterial inflow continues while outflow is blocked."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An embolus blocks one cortical radiate artery. Predict the size and shape of the lesion compared with a segmental artery occlusion.","explain":"A small wedge confined to the cortex. The cortical radiate artery is also an end artery, so the tissue it supplies dies, but its territory is a narrow column of cortex rather than a whole segment.","check":"The description of end arteries and the shape of the infarct."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-05-01-C03","obj":"UR-5.3","title":"Afferent and efferent arterioles: RPF, GFR, and filtration fraction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal blood flow equals the pressure difference between renal artery and renal vein divided by total renal vascular resistance, and most of that resistance lies in the afferent and efferent arterioles. GFR equals the filtration coefficient (Kf) multiplied by the net filtration pressure, which is glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman's space pressure minus glomerular oncotic pressure. Because the glomerulus sits between the two arterioles, its hydrostatic pressure behaves like the midpoint of two resistors in series: raising the upstream resistance lowers it, and raising the downstream resistance raises it. Both resistances add to total resistance, so constricting either one lowers flow."},{"type":"table","title":"Effects of arteriolar tone","cols":["Change","Glomerular pressure","RPF","GFR","FF (GFR/RPF)"],"rows":[["Afferent constriction","Down","Down","Down","About unchanged"],["Afferent dilation","Up","Up","Up","About unchanged"],["Efferent constriction (moderate)","Up","Down","Up","Up"],["Efferent dilation","Down","Up","Down","Down"]],"caption":"Afferent changes move RPF and GFR in the same direction; only efferent changes move them apart and so change filtration fraction."},{"type":"p","text":"Afferent constriction lowers both glomerular pressure and plasma flow, so GFR and RPF fall roughly in proportion and filtration fraction changes little. Efferent constriction raises glomerular pressure, so GFR rises while total flow falls, and filtration fraction rises. The effect is biphasic. With strong efferent constriction, flow becomes so slow that plasma proteins concentrate rapidly along the capillary, glomerular oncotic pressure rises until it cancels the hydrostatic gradient, and GFR falls despite the high capillary pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"Filtration fraction also shapes reabsorption. A higher filtration fraction leaves postglomerular blood with a higher oncotic pressure and, after efferent constriction, a lower peritubular hydrostatic pressure, so the peritubular capillaries take up reabsorbed fluid from the proximal tubule more avidly."},{"type":"table","title":"Main mediators of arteriolar tone","cols":["Mediator","Main action","Net effect"],"rows":[["Sympathetic nerves (alpha-1)","Constrict afferent and efferent","RPF and GFR fall, strongly in hemorrhage"],["Angiotensin II","Constricts efferent more than afferent at modest levels","RPF falls, GFR preserved, FF rises"],["Prostaglandins (PGE2, PGI2) and nitric oxide","Dilate mainly the afferent","RPF and GFR preserved when vasoconstrictors are high"],["Adenosine (A1 receptors)","Constricts afferent","GFR falls (tubuloglomerular feedback)"],["Atrial natriuretic peptide","Dilates afferent, constricts efferent","GFR rises"]],"caption":"The balance of these signals sets the two resistances."},{"type":"flow","title":"How GFR is defended when renal perfusion falls","steps":["Renal perfusion pressure falls","Prostaglandins and nitric oxide dilate the afferent arteriole","Renin release raises angiotensin II","Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole","Glomerular pressure and GFR are held up; FF rises","Losing either arm lets GFR fall"],"caption":"Blocking prostaglandin synthesis (NSAIDs) removes the afferent arm; blocking angiotensin II formation or action removes the efferent arm."},{"type":"p","text":"When perfusion is low, as in volume depletion, heart failure, or bilateral renal artery stenosis, GFR depends on both arms. Cyclooxygenase inhibition removes prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilation, and blocking angiotensin II removes efferent constriction; either can then lower glomerular pressure enough to cause acute kidney injury."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: calculating filtration fraction","text":"At baseline, RPF is 650 mL/min and GFR is 125 mL/min, so FF is 125 divided by 650, about 0.19. Angiotensin II then constricts the efferent arteriole moderately: RPF falls to 550 mL/min and GFR rises to 130 mL/min. FF is now 130 divided by 550, about 0.24. Filtration fraction rose for two reasons at once, because the numerator rose and the denominator fell. Compare afferent constriction that lowers RPF to 550 mL/min and GFR to about 106 mL/min: FF stays about 0.19, because both fell by about the same fraction. The illustrative numbers show the rule: only efferent changes move filtration fraction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-05-01-C04","obj":"UR-5.4","title":"Autoregulation: the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal blood flow and GFR stay nearly constant while mean arterial pressure varies between about 75 and 160 mm Hg (some texts extend the upper limit to about 180 mm Hg). Since flow equals pressure difference divided by resistance, constant flow at a higher pressure requires a matching rise in resistance. The kidney adds that resistance mainly at the afferent arteriole, upstream of the glomerulus, so glomerular pressure and GFR are held along with flow. Without this, a 25% rise in pressure would raise GFR by about 25% and deliver far more filtrate than the tubules could reabsorb. Autoregulation is not perfect, and small changes in GFR and sodium excretion with pressure still occur."},{"type":"p","text":"The myogenic response is a property of the arteriolar wall itself. A rise in pressure stretches afferent smooth muscle cells and opens stretch-sensitive nonselective cation channels. The cells depolarize, voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels open, Ca2+ enters, and the arteriole constricts within seconds. A fall in pressure relaxes it. Besides stabilizing flow, this response keeps surges in systemic pressure from reaching the glomerular capillaries."},{"type":"p","text":"Tubuloglomerular feedback works more slowly and senses the result of filtration rather than pressure. The macula densa is a plaque of cells in the wall of the thick ascending limb where it passes between the afferent and efferent arterioles of its own glomerulus. Its cells take up NaCl through the apical Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2), so their signal tracks the NaCl concentration of the fluid reaching them, which rises when GFR rises."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Tubuloglomerular feedback","steps":["GFR rises in one nephron","More NaCl reaches the macula densa","NaCl enters macula densa cells through NKCC2","ATP is released and converted to adenosine","Adenosine on A1 receptors constricts the afferent arteriole","Glomerular pressure and GFR fall back toward normal"],"caption":"A negative feedback loop: the response opposes the original change in GFR."},{"type":"p","text":"When NaCl delivery falls, the loop runs in reverse. Less adenosine is made, and the macula densa produces nitric oxide and prostaglandins that dilate the afferent arteriole and stimulate renin release from the granular cells. The resulting angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole, which also helps restore glomerular pressure. Blocking NKCC2 with a loop diuretic shows the sensing step: the macula densa then behaves as if NaCl delivery were low, and renin secretion rises."},{"type":"table","title":"The two autoregulatory mechanisms","cols":["Feature","Myogenic response","Tubuloglomerular feedback"],"rows":[["What is sensed","Stretch of the afferent arteriolar wall","NaCl concentration at the macula densa"],["Sensor","Afferent smooth muscle","Macula densa cells (via NKCC2)"],["Signal","Depolarization and Ca2+ entry","ATP and adenosine; NO and prostaglandins when NaCl is low"],["Effector","Afferent arteriole","Afferent arteriole; renin affects the efferent"],["Speed","Seconds","Somewhat slower, tens of seconds"]],"caption":"Both act mainly on the afferent arteriole, so both protect glomerular pressure as well as flow."},{"type":"p","text":"Below the lower limit, the arterioles are already maximally dilated, so flow and GFR fall passively with pressure. This is why GFR drops in shock, and why strong sympathetic activity during hemorrhage can override autoregulation and divert blood away from the kidneys."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Mean arterial pressure falls from 100 to 85 mm Hg, then to 55 mm Hg. Predict GFR at each step.","explain":"At 85 mm Hg GFR stays near normal because the afferent arteriole dilates through the myogenic response and reduced tubuloglomerular feedback. At 55 mm Hg pressure is below the autoregulatory range, the arterioles cannot dilate further, and GFR falls with pressure.","check":"The autoregulatory range and the reverse operation of tubuloglomerular feedback."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-05-01-C05","obj":"UR-5.5","title":"Vasa recta, medullary hypertonicity, and medullary hypoxia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The loops of Henle and collecting ducts build an interstitial osmolality that rises from about 300 mOsm/kg at the corticomedullary junction to about 1,200 mOsm/kg at the papillary tip in humans. Blood must supply the medulla without carrying this solute away. The vasa recta do this by two features: their flow is slow, and they are arranged as hairpins. Flow through the vasa recta of the inner medulla is only about 1 to 2% of total renal blood flow."},{"type":"p","text":"The vasa recta are countercurrent exchangers. As descending vessels enter the increasingly hypertonic medulla, their blood loses water and gains NaCl and urea, so its osmolality approaches that of the interstitium near the tip. As the ascending vessels climb back toward the cortex, the blood gives the solute back and regains water. The exchange is passive, so blood leaving the medulla is only slightly hyperosmotic, and little solute is removed. The ascending vessels also carry away more fluid than the descending ones bring in, removing the water reabsorbed from the descending limbs and collecting ducts. The vasa recta do not create the gradient; they preserve it. Because the exchange is passive, it works only when flow is slow, and an increase in medullary blood flow washes solute out and lowers the maximum urine concentration."},{"type":"p","text":"The same arrangement handles oxygen. Oxygen diffuses from descending to ascending vessels and returns toward the cortex without reaching the deep medulla. In the outer medulla, active NaCl transport by the thick ascending limb consumes much of the oxygen that does arrive. As a result the medulla is hypoxic, and the papillary tip, at the far end of the supply line, has the lowest oxygen tension in the kidney. This makes the papilla vulnerable to anything that further reduces its flow, which is why papillary necrosis occurs in sickle cell disease, diabetes, analgesic nephropathy from long-term use of analgesic mixtures and NSAIDs (partly through loss of prostaglandin-mediated medullary vasodilation), and severe pyelonephritis with obstruction."},{"type":"table","title":"Why HbS polymerizes in the vasa recta","cols":["Medullary condition","Effect on HbS"],"rows":[["Low oxygen tension","More deoxyhemoglobin S, the form that polymerizes"],["High osmolality","Water leaves red cells, raising hemoglobin concentration, which speeds polymerization"],["Low pH","Lowers hemoglobin oxygen affinity (Bohr effect), favoring deoxygenation"],["Slow flow","Longer transit time allows polymer to form before cells leave"]],"caption":"These conditions are severe enough to cause sickling even in sickle cell trait."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sickling in the vasa recta and its consequences","steps":["Red cells containing HbS enter the vasa recta","Hypoxia, hypertonicity, and acidosis promote sickling","Sickled cells occlude the vasa recta","Microinfarcts and papillary necrosis","Hematuria; sloughed papilla may obstruct the ureter","Loss of vasa recta and concentrating ability"],"caption":"Damage starts at the papillary tip, where conditions are most extreme."},{"type":"p","text":"In sickle cell disease and sickle cell trait, repeated sickling destroys the vasa recta and the deep parts of the long loops. Countercurrent exchange and multiplication then fail, so urine cannot be concentrated much above plasma osmolality (hyposthenuria). This begins in childhood and can cause nocturia and enuresis. Painless gross hematuria from papillary infarction is a recognized complication of sickle cell trait."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why is the renal medulla one of the few sites where sickle cell trait causes disease?","explain":"Trait cells contain about 40% HbS, which normally does not polymerize enough to sickle. The medulla combines low oxygen, high osmolality that concentrates hemoglobin, low pH, and slow flow, which together push even this amount of HbS past the threshold for polymerization.","check":"The table of medullary conditions."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-05-01-C06","obj":"UR-5.6","title":"The left renal vein, its gonadal tributary, and left varicocele","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The inferior vena cava (IVC) lies to the right of the aorta, so the two renal veins differ. The right renal vein is short and enters the IVC directly. The left renal vein is longer: it runs from the left hilum across the midline, anterior to the aorta and just below the origin of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA), passing through the angle between these two arteries to enter the left side of the IVC. Along the way it receives the left gonadal vein from below and the left suprarenal vein from above, often with a lumbar tributary."},{"type":"table","title":"Left versus right venous drainage","cols":["Feature","Left","Right"],"rows":[["Renal vein","Long; crosses anterior to the aorta below the SMA","Short; enters the IVC directly"],["Gonadal vein drains into","Left renal vein, at about a right angle","IVC, at an oblique angle"],["Suprarenal vein drains into","Left renal vein","IVC"],["Varicocele","Common side","Uncommon; raises concern for IVC obstruction"]],"caption":"The left gonadal vein shares the pressure of the left renal vein; the right gonadal vein does not."},{"type":"p","text":"The testis drains through the pampiniform plexus, which forms the testicular vein near the deep inguinal ring. A varicocele is dilation of this plexus, felt as a soft mass above the testis that enlarges on standing or straining. Most varicoceles are left sided, explained by the longer course of the left testicular vein, its right-angle entry into a higher-pressure renal vein, incompetent or absent valves, and compression of the left renal vein. An ordinary varicocele drains and collapses when the patient lies down, because gravity no longer opposes outflow."},{"type":"p","text":"Renal cell carcinoma, which arises from tubular epithelium, has a strong tendency to grow into the renal vein as a tumor thrombus and can extend into the IVC and even the right atrium. When a left-sided tumor fills the left renal vein beyond the entry of the gonadal vein, testicular drainage is blocked mechanically. The result is a new left varicocele that does not collapse when the patient is supine, because the obstruction persists in any position. In women the same mechanism dilates the left ovarian vein. A right-sided tumor causes a varicocele only if tumor thrombus obstructs the IVC where the right testicular vein drains, so a new right or nondecompressing varicocele also calls for imaging of the kidney and IVC."},{"type":"p","text":"The left renal vein can also be compressed between the aorta and the SMA (nutcracker phenomenon). Raised pressure in the vein causes hematuria from rupture of thin-walled veins into the collecting system, left flank pain, and a left varicocele or pelvic varices."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing a varicocele","text":"A 58-year-old man has a new left scrotal swelling, hematuria, and weight loss. The swelling feels like a bag of veins and stays full when he lies flat. First, ask what drains the left pampiniform plexus: the left testicular vein, into the left renal vein. Second, a varicocele that persists supine points to a fixed obstruction rather than valve failure. Third, hematuria and weight loss point to a renal mass. The most likely explanation is a left renal cell carcinoma with tumor thrombus in the left renal vein. If the same varicocele were on the right, the lesion would have to involve the IVC, because the right testicular vein drains there."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology explain the kidney's vascular behavior together. The series arrangement of afferent arteriole, glomerulus, efferent arteriole, and second capillary bed lets the kidney adjust filtration from either end and links filtration fraction to reabsorption downstream. The juxtaglomerular apparatus ties single nephron GFR to the renin-angiotensin system, so the same cells that autoregulate flow also help set systemic blood pressure. Biochemistry enters through hemoglobin S: polymerization depends on oxygen tension, hemoglobin concentration, and pH, and the renal medulla pushes all three in the wrong direction."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows from the anatomy. End arteries explain pale wedge-shaped infarcts, the medulla's position at the end of a slow, oxygen-poor supply explains papillary necrosis from several different causes, and the course of the left renal vein explains why a renal tumor appears as a scrotal sign. The arteriolar rules hold for moderate changes rather than extreme constriction, and the quoted numbers vary between textbooks."}],"summary":["The kidneys receive about 20 to 25% of cardiac output, mostly to the cortex, so that plasma can be filtered, not to meet metabolic need.","Blood passes renal, segmental, interlobar, arcuate, and cortical radiate arteries, then afferent arteriole, glomerulus, efferent arteriole, and peritubular capillaries or vasa recta.","Two capillary beds in series give a high-pressure filtering bed and a low-pressure, high-oncotic reabsorbing bed.","Segmental and interlobar arteries are end arteries, so emboli cause pale, wedge-shaped infarcts with the base at the capsule.","Afferent changes move RPF and GFR together; moderate efferent constriction raises GFR and filtration fraction while RPF falls.","Prostaglandin afferent dilation and angiotensin II efferent constriction defend GFR when perfusion is low.","Autoregulation holds flow and GFR between about 75 and 160 mm Hg through the myogenic response and macula densa tubuloglomerular feedback using adenosine.","Slow countercurrent flow in the vasa recta preserves medullary hypertonicity but leaves the papilla hypoxic.","Hypoxia, hypertonicity, acidosis, and slow flow cause sickling in the vasa recta, papillary necrosis, hematuria, and hyposthenuria, even in sickle cell trait.","The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, so renal cell carcinoma in that vein causes a left varicocele that does not collapse when supine."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Glomerular Filtration, Renal Blood Flow, and Their Control"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Urine concentration and dilution, section on the vasa recta"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Glomerular filtration and renal blood flow"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, kidneys and renal vessels, testicular veins"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, sections on renal infarcts, papillary necrosis, and renal cell carcinoma"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders, sickle cell disease"}]},"UR-6":{"unit":"UR-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-06-01","title":"Filtration, Reabsorption, Secretion, and Micturition","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows plasma through the nephron and urine out of the body. It explains how Starling forces set the glomerular filtration rate (GFR), how clearance measures renal function, how the proximal tubule and thick ascending limb do the bulk of reabsorption, and how aldosterone, antidiuretic hormone (ADH), and parathyroid hormone (PTH) make the final adjustments. It then covers urine concentration, body fluid shifts, and the neural control of the bladder. Board questions in this area are mostly calculations and predictions: work a clearance, predict a GFR change, sort out polyuria or hyponatremia, or place a neurologic lesion from the bladder pattern.","prerequisites":["Starling forces across capillary walls and the meaning of oncotic pressure (CV-11)","Nephron segments, juxtaglomerular apparatus, and tubular cell types (UR-2)","Renal blood flow and afferent and efferent arteriolar control (UR-5)","Osmosis, osmolality, and primary and secondary active transport","Autonomic receptors and G protein signaling (NE-9)"],"connections":[{"to":"Renal circulation (UR-5)","how":"Afferent and efferent arteriolar tone sets glomerular capillary pressure and plasma flow, the two variables that most often change GFR."},{"to":"Kidney in acid-base and blood pressure control (UR-7)","how":"Proximal bicarbonate reclamation, aldosterone, and the renin-angiotensin system described here are the effectors of acid-base and blood pressure regulation."},{"to":"Hypothalamic and limbic function (NE-12)","how":"Hypothalamic osmoreceptors, thirst, and ADH release from the posterior pituitary link plasma osmolality to collecting duct water permeability."},{"to":"Obstructive urinary diseases (UR-12-C)","how":"Raised Bowman space pressure explains why obstruction lowers GFR, and bladder outlet failure explains retention and overflow."},{"to":"Autonomic nervous system (NE-9)","how":"Bladder storage and voiding are opposite sympathetic and parasympathetic states coordinated by the pons."}],"terms":[{"term":"Glomerular filtration rate (GFR)","def":"The volume of plasma filtered into Bowman space per minute by both kidneys, normally about 125 mL/min (about 180 L/day)."},{"term":"Net filtration pressure","def":"Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space hydrostatic pressure minus glomerular capillary oncotic pressure."},{"term":"Filtration coefficient (Kf)","def":"The product of glomerular capillary permeability and surface area, which converts net filtration pressure into GFR."},{"term":"Clearance","def":"The volume of plasma completely cleared of a substance per minute, calculated as urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration."},{"term":"Effective renal plasma flow (ERPF)","def":"Renal plasma flow estimated from para-aminohippurate (PAH) clearance, about 600 mL/min, roughly 10 percent below true renal plasma flow (about 650 mL/min)."},{"term":"Filtration fraction","def":"The fraction of renal plasma flow that is filtered, GFR divided by renal plasma flow, normally about 0.2."},{"term":"Transport maximum (Tm)","def":"The highest rate at which a carrier-mediated system can reabsorb or secrete a solute once all carriers are saturated."},{"term":"Countercurrent multiplication","def":"The process by which the loop of Henle turns a small transverse osmotic difference into a large corticomedullary gradient through opposite flow in its two limbs."},{"term":"Effective osmole","def":"A solute that cannot freely cross cell membranes, such as Na+ or glucose without insulin, and therefore moves water between compartments."},{"term":"Pontine micturition center","def":"A brainstem nucleus that switches the bladder from storage to voiding by coordinating detrusor contraction with sphincter relaxation."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-06-01-C01","obj":"UR-6.1","title":"Starling forces and the glomerular filtration rate","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Filtration across the glomerular capillary is governed by the same Starling forces as any capillary, but in humans the balance favors filtration along the whole capillary length. Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure (PGC, about 60 mm Hg) pushes fluid out. Bowman space hydrostatic pressure (PBS, about 18 mm Hg) and glomerular capillary oncotic pressure (piGC, about 32 mm Hg on average) oppose it. The oncotic pressure of the filtrate is near zero because normal filtrate is almost protein free, so the net filtration pressure is about 60 minus 18 minus 32, or about 10 mm Hg."},{"type":"p","text":"GFR equals Kf times net filtration pressure. The glomerular Kf is very high because the fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms form a large, permeable surface, so a small net pressure produces about 125 mL/min. Because filtration removes protein-free fluid, plasma proteins become more concentrated along the capillary, and piGC rises from about 28 to about 36 mm Hg. Higher plasma flow slows that rise and keeps net filtration pressure positive over more of the capillary, which is why GFR rises with renal plasma flow even when PGC is unchanged."},{"type":"p","text":"Arteriolar tone sets PGC. Afferent constriction lowers both PGC and plasma flow, so GFR falls. Efferent constriction, the main action of angiotensin II, raises PGC and GFR while lowering plasma flow, so filtration fraction rises; very strong efferent constriction eventually lowers GFR because oncotic pressure climbs steeply. Myogenic contraction of the afferent arteriole and tubuloglomerular feedback keep GFR nearly constant over a mean arterial pressure of about 75 to 160 mm Hg."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Tubuloglomerular feedback","steps":["GFR rises","More NaCl reaches the macula densa","Macula densa releases adenosine and ATP","Afferent arteriole constricts","PGC and GFR fall back toward normal"],"caption":"The macula densa senses NaCl delivery in the thick ascending limb and adjusts afferent tone of its own nephron."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting GFR from the Starling terms","cols":["Condition","Term changed","GFR","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Ureteral obstruction","PBS rises","Falls","Urine backs up into the tubules and Bowman space and opposes filtration"],["Hypoalbuminemia","piGC falls","Tends to rise","Less oncotic opposition; in nephrotic syndrome a lower Kf often offsets this"],["Dehydration or hypovolemia","piGC rises; plasma flow and PGC fall","Falls","Proteins concentrate and sympathetic afferent constriction lowers flow; angiotensin II efferent constriction limits the fall and raises filtration fraction"],["Afferent constriction","PGC and plasma flow fall","Falls","Less pressure and flow reach the glomerulus"],["Efferent constriction (moderate)","PGC rises; plasma flow falls","Rises","Pressure builds behind the efferent arteriole"]],"caption":"Each condition shifts one or more terms of the Starling equation; the direction of GFR follows from which term moves."},{"type":"p","text":"In dehydration the fall in GFR raises plasma urea and creatinine. Because slow tubular flow also increases passive urea reabsorption, the blood urea nitrogen (BUN) to creatinine ratio typically exceeds about 20 to 1 in this prerenal state, a mechanistic clue that the kidney itself is intact."},{"type":"stop","predict":"What happens to GFR and filtration fraction when angiotensin II levels rise during mild volume depletion?","explain":"Efferent constriction raises PGC, so GFR is preserved or falls only slightly, while renal plasma flow falls. Filtration fraction (GFR divided by plasma flow) therefore rises, which also raises peritubular oncotic pressure and favors proximal reabsorption.","check":"The paragraph on arteriolar tone and the dehydration row of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-06-01-C02","obj":"UR-6.2","title":"Clearance, GFR, renal plasma flow, and filtration fraction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Clearance (C) is the volume of plasma per minute that would have to be completely emptied of a substance to supply the amount appearing in the urine. It is calculated as C = (U x V) / P, where U is urine concentration, V is urine flow rate, and P is plasma concentration. The product U x V is the excretion rate, and dividing it by P converts an amount per minute into a plasma volume per minute."},{"type":"p","text":"A substance that is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed, secreted, nor metabolized by the tubules has a clearance equal to GFR, because every milliliter filtered is cleared and no other milliliter is. Inulin, a fructose polymer, meets these conditions and is the reference standard. Creatinine, made at a fairly steady rate from muscle creatine, is used clinically; a small amount is secreted by the proximal tubule, so creatinine clearance slightly overestimates GFR. At steady state, plasma creatinine varies inversely with GFR: halving GFR roughly doubles plasma creatinine, so early losses of GFR cause only small rises in creatinine."},{"type":"p","text":"Para-aminohippurate (PAH) is filtered and then secreted by proximal tubule organic anion transporters so efficiently that about 90 percent is removed in one pass at low plasma levels. Its clearance therefore approximates renal plasma flow, called effective renal plasma flow (ERPF, about 600 mL/min) because some blood perfuses tissue without secreting tubules. True renal plasma flow, about 650 mL/min, is ERPF divided by the PAH extraction ratio, and renal blood flow is renal plasma flow divided by (1 minus hematocrit). Filtration fraction is GFR divided by renal plasma flow, about 125/650, or roughly 0.2."},{"type":"p","text":"Comparing a clearance with GFR shows net tubular handling. Filtered load is GFR x P. If the clearance is below GFR, less appeared in urine than was filtered, so the tubules reabsorbed it (net reabsorption equals filtered load minus excretion). If the clearance is above GFR, the tubules added it (net secretion). Glucose clearance is normally zero, but once plasma glucose exceeds a threshold of about 200 mg/dL some nephrons saturate their carriers and glucose appears in urine; full saturation (Tm) is reached at a higher level, and the gradual curve between them is called splay."},{"type":"table","title":"Clearance compared with GFR","cols":["Substance","Tubular handling","Clearance relative to GFR"],"rows":[["Inulin","Filtered only","Equal to GFR"],["Creatinine","Filtered, slightly secreted","Slightly above GFR"],["PAH","Filtered and strongly secreted","Far above GFR, about ERPF"],["Urea","Filtered, partly reabsorbed","Below GFR, about half"],["Na+","Filtered, more than 99 percent reabsorbed","Far below GFR"],["Glucose (normal plasma level)","Filtered, completely reabsorbed","Zero"]],"caption":"The ratio of a clearance to GFR tells whether the tubules reabsorb or secrete a substance overall."},{"type":"example","title":"Working a clearance problem","text":"Plasma inulin is 1 mg/mL, urine inulin 125 mg/mL, and urine flow 1 mL/min, so GFR = (125 x 1)/1 = 125 mL/min. Plasma PAH is 0.02 mg/mL and urine PAH 13 mg/mL, so ERPF = (13 x 1)/0.02 = 650 mL/min. Taking ERPF as renal plasma flow, filtration fraction = 125/650, about 0.19. With a hematocrit of 0.45, renal blood flow is about 650/0.55, or 1,180 mL/min. Substance X has plasma 2 mg/mL and urine 100 mg/mL: clearance = 100 mL/min, below GFR, so X is reabsorbed. Filtered load is 125 x 2 = 250 mg/min, excretion is 100 mg/min, and net reabsorption is 150 mg/min."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-06-01-C03","obj":"UR-6.3","title":"Proximal tubule and thick ascending limb transport","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Nearly all tubular reabsorption is powered by the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase, which keeps intracellular Na+ low and the cell interior negative. Apical carriers then use the inward Na+ gradient to move other solutes by secondary active transport. The proximal tubule is a leaky, high-capacity epithelium rich in mitochondria and brush border, and it reabsorbs about two thirds of filtered Na+, water, K+, and Cl-, about 80 percent of filtered bicarbonate, most filtered phosphate, and nearly all filtered glucose and amino acids."},{"type":"p","text":"Glucose enters through the Na+-glucose cotransporter SGLT2 in the early proximal tubule and SGLT1 later, then leaves across the basolateral membrane through GLUT2 by facilitated diffusion. Amino acids use similar Na+-coupled carriers. The apical Na+/H+ exchanger (NHE3) secretes H+, which combines with filtered bicarbonate; luminal carbonic anhydrase converts the carbonic acid to CO2 and water, CO2 enters the cell, and intracellular carbonic anhydrase regenerates bicarbonate, which exits with Na+ into blood. Water follows the reabsorbed solute through aquaporin-1 and between cells, so proximal reabsorption is isosmotic and tubular fluid stays at about 300 mOsm/kg. The proximal tubule also secretes organic anions and cations, including PAH, urate, and many drugs."},{"type":"p","text":"Generalized proximal tubule failure (Fanconi syndrome, from causes such as cystinosis, light chains in multiple myeloma, or heavy metals) shows the segment's roles by their loss: glucose in urine with normal plasma glucose, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and bicarbonate wasting with proximal renal tubular acidosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The thick ascending limb reabsorbs about 25 percent of filtered Na+ through the apical Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2) but is impermeable to water, so it dilutes tubular fluid to about 100 mOsm/kg and builds the medullary gradient. Most of the K+ taken up leaks back into the lumen through ROMK channels while Cl- exits basolaterally, which leaves the lumen positive. That voltage drives Ca2+ and Mg2+ between the cells through tight junction claudins. The thick ascending limb reabsorbs about 25 percent of filtered Ca2+ and is the main site of Mg2+ reabsorption."},{"type":"flow","title":"Paracellular cation reabsorption in the thick ascending limb","steps":["NKCC2 brings in Na+, K+, and 2 Cl-","Na+/K+-ATPase moves Na+ out to blood","Cl- exits through basolateral Cl- channels","K+ recycles to the lumen through ROMK","Lumen becomes positive relative to blood","Ca2+ and Mg2+ move between cells into blood"],"caption":"Blocking NKCC2 or ROMK removes the positive lumen, so Ca2+ and Mg2+ are lost along with NaCl."},{"type":"table","title":"Proximal tubule compared with thick ascending limb","cols":["Feature","Proximal tubule","Thick ascending limb"],"rows":[["Share of filtered Na+","About 65 percent","About 25 percent"],["Main apical Na+ entry","NHE3, SGLT2, Na+-coupled carriers","NKCC2"],["Water permeability","High (aquaporin-1)","Impermeable"],["Fluid leaving the segment","About 300 mOsm/kg (isosmotic)","About 100 mOsm/kg (dilute)"],["Special solutes","Glucose, amino acids, bicarbonate, phosphate","Paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+"],["Loss-of-function disorder","Fanconi syndrome","Bartter syndrome"]],"caption":"Bulk isosmotic reabsorption happens proximally; the water-tight thick ascending limb separates salt from water."},{"type":"p","text":"Bartter syndrome, inherited loss of NKCC2, ROMK, or the basolateral Cl- channel, reproduces loop diuretic action: salt wasting with low or normal blood pressure, high renin and aldosterone, hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis from increased distal Na+ delivery under aldosterone, and hypercalciuria."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-06-01-C04","obj":"UR-6.4","title":"Hormonal control in the distal nephron: aldosterone, ADH, and PTH","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"By the time fluid reaches the distal convoluted tubule, about 90 percent of filtered Na+ and about 80 percent of filtered water have been reabsorbed. The distal nephron handles small fractions, but it is where hormones set final excretion, so modest changes there decide Na+, K+, water, and Ca2+ balance."},{"type":"h","text":"Aldosterone and the principal cell"},{"type":"p","text":"Aldosterone, released from the zona glomerulosa in response to angiotensin II and to a rise in plasma K+, is a steroid that binds the intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor in principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct. The hormone-receptor complex increases transcription of genes that add open ENaC channels to the luminal membrane, more Na+/K+-ATPase to the basolateral membrane, and more ROMK channels. Na+ entering through ENaC without an accompanying anion makes the lumen negative, and that voltage, together with high intracellular K+ from the pump, drives K+ secretion through ROMK. Aldosterone also stimulates H+ secretion by alpha-intercalated cells. Cortisol binds the same receptor with equal affinity, but the enzyme 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 converts it to inactive cortisone in these cells; inhibiting the enzyme (for example by glycyrrhetinic acid in licorice) lets cortisol act as a mineralocorticoid."},{"type":"p","text":"The mechanism predicts the disorders. Aldosterone excess gives Na+ retention with hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis; edema is usually absent because pressure natriuresis and natriuretic peptides limit volume gain (aldosterone escape). Aldosterone deficiency gives Na+ wasting, hyperkalemia, and a mild metabolic acidosis. Blocking ENaC with amiloride removes the lumen-negative voltage and so reduces K+ secretion, and gain-of-function ENaC mutations (Liddle syndrome) mimic aldosterone excess with low renin and low aldosterone."},{"type":"h","text":"ADH and aquaporin-2"},{"type":"flow","title":"ADH action on the collecting duct","steps":["Plasma osmolality rises or blood volume falls","Hypothalamic neurons release ADH from the posterior pituitary","ADH binds V2 receptors on principal cells","Gs activates adenylyl cyclase; cAMP activates protein kinase A","Aquaporin-2 vesicles fuse with the luminal membrane","Water exits through basolateral aquaporin-3 and aquaporin-4 into the hypertonic interstitium"],"caption":"ADH controls water permeability only; the medullary gradient supplies the driving force."},{"type":"p","text":"Osmoreceptors respond to a change in plasma osmolality of about 1 percent, whereas a volume or pressure stimulus must be considerably larger (on the order of 10 percent or more) but then produces strong ADH release that can override osmotic control. With sustained ADH, aquaporin-2 synthesis also rises, and in the inner medullary collecting duct ADH increases urea transporter activity."},{"type":"h","text":"PTH and distal calcium reabsorption"},{"type":"p","text":"In the distal convoluted tubule, Ca2+ enters across the luminal membrane through TRPV5 channels, binds calbindin in the cytosol, and leaves through the basolateral Na+/Ca2+ exchanger and Ca2+-ATPase. PTH, acting through its Gs-coupled receptor, increases this transcellular pathway and so lowers urinary Ca2+. In the proximal tubule PTH reduces Na+-phosphate cotransporters, causing phosphaturia, and stimulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase to form calcitriol. Thiazide block of the distal NaCl cotransporter also lowers urinary Ca2+, because lower intracellular Na+ speeds basolateral Na+/Ca2+ exchange."},{"type":"table","title":"Distal nephron hormones","cols":["Hormone","Main stimulus","Receptor and signal","Transport effect"],"rows":[["Aldosterone","Angiotensin II, high plasma K+","Mineralocorticoid receptor, gene transcription","More ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase; Na+ reabsorption and K+ and H+ secretion"],["ADH","High plasma osmolality, low blood volume","V2 receptor, Gs, cAMP, protein kinase A","Aquaporin-2 insertion; water reabsorption; urea transport in inner medulla"],["PTH","Low plasma ionized Ca2+","PTH receptor, Gs, cAMP","More distal Ca2+ reabsorption; less proximal phosphate reabsorption"]],"caption":"Each hormone changes the number or activity of specific transporters in a specific cell."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-06-01-C05","obj":"UR-6.5","title":"Urine concentration and the disorders of water balance","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"To excrete concentrated urine, the kidney needs a hypertonic medullary interstitium and ADH to let collecting duct water equilibrate with it. The loop of Henle builds the gradient by countercurrent multiplication. The thick ascending limb pumps NaCl out without water and can create about a 200 mOsm/kg difference between its lumen and the interstitium at any level. Water then leaves the water-permeable descending limb, concentrating its fluid, and because flow carries that concentrated fluid around the bend into the ascending limb, the single step is repeated and stacked along the loop."},{"type":"flow","title":"Countercurrent multiplication","steps":["Thick ascending limb pumps NaCl into the interstitium without water","Interstitial osmolality rises around the loop","Water leaves the descending limb; its fluid concentrates","Concentrated fluid flows around the bend into the ascending limb","More NaCl is pumped out at a higher starting osmolality","Repetition builds a gradient of about 300 to 1,200 mOsm/kg"],"caption":"The gradient runs from cortex to papilla; longer loops of juxtamedullary nephrons build the deepest part."},{"type":"p","text":"Urea adds a large share of the inner medullary osmolality. The cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts are urea impermeable, so urea concentrates as water is removed, and ADH-regulated urea transporters in the inner medullary collecting duct then let it diffuse into the interstitium. The vasa recta preserve the gradient because they are also hairpin loops with low flow: solute entering the descending vessel leaves the ascending one (countercurrent exchange)."},{"type":"p","text":"Fluid leaving the thick ascending limb is hypotonic (about 100 mOsm/kg) whether or not ADH is present. With maximal ADH, collecting duct fluid equilibrates with the papilla and urine reaches about 1,200 mOsm/kg; without ADH action, further solute reabsorption makes urine as dilute as about 50 mOsm/kg. Because a typical diet requires excreting about 600 mOsm of solute daily, the minimum obligatory urine volume is about 0.5 L/day."},{"type":"table","title":"Polyuria and hyponatremia by mechanism","cols":["Condition","Defect","Serum Na+","Urine osmolality","Water deprivation","Desmopressin"],"rows":[["Central diabetes insipidus","Too little ADH released (pituitary surgery, trauma, tumor, idiopathic)","High-normal or high","Low","Urine stays dilute","Urine concentrates"],["Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Collecting duct does not respond (lithium, hypercalcemia, hypokalemia, V2 receptor or aquaporin-2 mutation)","High-normal or high","Low","Urine stays dilute","Little or no rise"],["Primary polydipsia","Excess water intake suppresses ADH","Low-normal or low","Low","Urine concentrates","Little further rise; endogenous ADH is already acting"],["SIADH","ADH released despite low osmolality (small cell lung carcinoma, CNS disease, lung disease, some drugs)","Low","Inappropriately high","Not performed; diagnosis rests on low serum osmolality with urine osmolality above about 100 mOsm/kg","Not performed; ADH effect is already present"]],"caption":"Water deprivation tests whether the kidney can concentrate; desmopressin, a V2-selective ADH analog, tests whether the kidney responds to ADH."},{"type":"p","text":"In SIADH, water retention expands volume slightly, which suppresses renin and aldosterone and raises natriuretic peptides, so urine Na+ stays above about 40 mEq/L and the patient appears euvolemic rather than edematous. In primary polydipsia, chronic high urine flow washes urea and NaCl out of the medulla, so maximal concentration may be blunted even though ADH and its receptor are normal."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with polyuria has a serum Na+ of 146 mEq/L. After water deprivation, urine osmolality stays at 150 mOsm/kg; after desmopressin it rises to 480 mOsm/kg. Where is the defect?","explain":"The kidney cannot concentrate without help but responds well to exogenous V2 stimulation, so the collecting duct is normal and ADH is missing: central diabetes insipidus.","check":"The table of water balance disorders and the role of ADH in the collecting duct."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-06-01-C06","obj":"UR-6.6","title":"Body fluid compartments and fluid shifts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Total body water is about 60 percent of body weight in a lean adult man (less in women, older adults, and people with more adipose tissue). About two thirds is intracellular fluid (ICF) and one third extracellular fluid (ECF); ECF is about three quarters interstitial fluid and one quarter plasma. Plasma osmolality is normally about 285 to 295 mOsm/kg and can be estimated as 2 x [Na+] + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8, with glucose and BUN in mg/dL."},{"type":"p","text":"Two rules predict every shift. First, water crosses cell membranes freely, so ICF and ECF always reach the same osmolality. Second, Na+ is held outside cells by the Na+/K+-ATPase and K+ inside, so NaCl added to the ECF stays there and only water crosses into or out of cells. Urea crosses membranes and is therefore an ineffective osmole: it raises measured osmolality without moving water. Glucose is effective when insulin is lacking, so marked hyperglycemia pulls water out of cells and lowers serum Na+ by dilution."},{"type":"table","title":"Fluid gain and loss","cols":["Change","Example","ECF volume","ICF volume","Osmolality"],"rows":[["Isotonic gain","0.9 percent saline infusion","Up","No change","No change"],["Isotonic loss","Hemorrhage, diarrhea","Down","No change","No change"],["Hypertonic gain","Hypertonic saline, high salt intake","Up","Down","Up"],["Hypotonic loss (water loss)","Sweating, diabetes insipidus, poor water intake","Down","Down","Up"],["Hypotonic gain (water gain)","SIADH, excess water intake","Up","Up","Down"],["Hypertonic loss (salt loss exceeds water loss)","Adrenal insufficiency","Down","Up","Down"]],"caption":"Only a change in ECF osmolality moves water across cell membranes."},{"type":"p","text":"These shifts separate terms that are often blurred. Dehydration in the strict sense is loss of water in excess of Na+: osmolality and serum Na+ rise, cells shrink, thirst and ADH are stimulated, and brain cell shrinkage causes confusion and, if severe, can tear bridging veins. Hypovolemia is loss of ECF volume, mainly Na+ with water; osmolality may be normal, and the signs are those of low effective circulating volume: tachycardia, orthostatic hypotension, flat neck veins, and a prerenal fall in GFR."},{"type":"p","text":"Volume overload is an excess of total body Na+ that expands the ECF, as in heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, or renal failure. Expansion of the interstitial compartment appears as edema and pulmonary congestion. When low effective arterial volume drives nonosmotic ADH release, water is retained in excess of Na+ and hyponatremia follows. Low ECF osmolality swells brain cells, giving headache, confusion, and seizures; brain cells adapt over about 48 hours by exporting K+ and organic osmolytes, which is why rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia can shrink them and cause osmotic demyelination."},{"type":"example","title":"Distributing a water load","text":"A 70 kg man has total body water of 42 L (ICF 28 L, ECF 14 L) at 290 mOsm/kg, so total body osmoles are 42 x 290 = 12,180 mOsm. He drinks 2 L of water that is retained. New total water is 44 L, so new osmolality is 12,180/44, about 277 mOsm/kg. Osmoles in each space are unchanged: ICF = (28 x 290)/277, about 29.3 L, and ECF = (14 x 290)/277, about 14.7 L. Pure water distributes in proportion to the compartments, about two thirds into cells. By contrast, 2 L of isotonic saline would stay entirely in the ECF."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After several days of severe watery diarrhea treated only with plain water, what happens to ECF volume, ICF volume, and serum Na+?","explain":"The diarrhea causes isotonic loss and shrinks the ECF. Replacing it with water, retained under volume-driven ADH, lowers osmolality, so water shifts into cells: ECF remains contracted, ICF expands, and serum Na+ falls.","check":"The two rules for water movement and the table of fluid gain and loss."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-06-01-C07","obj":"UR-6.7","title":"The micturition reflex and neurogenic bladder","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The bladder wall is the detrusor, a smooth muscle syncytium. At the bladder neck the smooth muscle internal sphincter is under involuntary control, and below it the striated external urethral sphincter is under voluntary control. Storage and voiding are opposite states, and the nervous system must switch between them while keeping the detrusor and sphincters coordinated."},{"type":"table","title":"Nerves of the bladder","cols":["Pathway","Origin","Receptor","Action"],"rows":[["Sympathetic (hypogastric nerve)","About T11 to L2","Beta-3 on detrusor; alpha-1 on bladder neck","Relaxes detrusor, closes internal sphincter: storage"],["Parasympathetic (pelvic splanchnic nerves)","S2 to S4","M3 muscarinic on detrusor","Contracts detrusor: voiding"],["Somatic (pudendal nerve)","S2 to S4 (Onuf nucleus)","Nicotinic on external sphincter","Keeps external sphincter closed: continence"],["Afferent (mainly pelvic nerve)","Stretch receptors in bladder wall","Not applicable","Signals filling to sacral cord and pons"]],"caption":"Sympathetic and somatic output store urine; parasympathetic output empties the bladder."},{"type":"p","text":"During filling the bladder accommodates volume at low pressure because smooth muscle shows stress relaxation as it stretches and sympathetic beta-3 tone keeps the detrusor relaxed. A first urge is usually felt at about 150 mL, and a strong urge at roughly 300 to 400 mL. Stretch afferents enter the sacral cord and ascend to the periaqueductal gray and the pontine micturition center. The frontal cortex inhibits the pons until voiding is appropriate."},{"type":"flow","title":"Micturition reflex","steps":["Bladder stretch afferents fire through the pelvic nerve","Signals ascend to the pontine micturition center","Cortex releases its inhibition of the pons","Pons inhibits sympathetic and pudendal output; sphincters relax","Sacral parasympathetic neurons release acetylcholine on M3 receptors","Detrusor contracts and the bladder empties"],"caption":"The pons makes detrusor contraction and sphincter relaxation happen together."},{"type":"p","text":"Lesion level predicts the bladder. Damage above the pons, as in frontal lobe stroke or dementia, removes cortical inhibition: the reflex is intact and coordinated but triggered early, giving urge incontinence. A spinal cord lesion between the pons and the sacral segments first produces spinal shock with an areflexic bladder and retention. As the sacral reflex recovers without pontine control, a reflex spastic bladder develops: small capacity, involuntary reflex emptying, and detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia, in which the external sphincter contracts while the detrusor contracts. The high pressures that result cause incomplete emptying, vesicoureteral reflux, and hydronephrosis. With lesions above about T6, bladder distention can trigger autonomic dysreflexia, a massive sympathetic discharge below the lesion with severe hypertension and reflex bradycardia."},{"type":"p","text":"A lesion of the sacral cord, cauda equina, or pelvic nerves destroys the reflex arc itself. The detrusor cannot contract and the bladder becomes flaccid and overdistended, emptying only by overflow dribbling, usually with saddle anesthesia and reduced anal tone. Afferent loss alone, as in diabetic autonomic neuropathy, also produces a large, insensate bladder with overflow."},{"type":"table","title":"Bladder pattern by lesion level","cols":["Lesion","Bladder","Main finding"],"rows":[["Above the pons (cortex)","Uninhibited, coordinated","Urge incontinence"],["Spinal cord above the sacral segments","Reflex spastic, small capacity","Reflex emptying with detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia"],["Sacral cord, cauda equina, or pelvic nerves","Flaccid, large capacity","Retention with overflow incontinence"]],"caption":"An upper motor neuron pattern above the sacral cord and a lower motor neuron pattern at it."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Structure explains function throughout this chapter. The fenestrated glomerular filter with its large surface gives a high Kf, so GFR follows small pressure changes; the brush border and mitochondria of the proximal tubule support bulk isosmotic transport; the water-tight thick ascending limb and hairpin loops of juxtamedullary nephrons make concentration possible; and the sacral and pontine wiring of the bladder decides what each spinal lesion does. Biochemistry appears in the Na+/K+-ATPase that powers nearly every transporter, in carbonic anhydrase chemistry for bicarbonate, and in steroid receptor transcription for aldosterone."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology tests the same mechanisms from the other side. Single transporter mutations (Bartter, Liddle, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from V2 receptor or aquaporin-2 defects) behave like drug blockade or activation of that transporter, and ectopic ADH from small cell lung carcinoma links oncology to hyponatremia. Measured values have limits: creatinine clearance overestimates GFR, and plasma creatinine rises late in early kidney disease."}],"summary":["Net filtration pressure is PGC minus PBS minus piGC (about 60 minus 18 minus 32 mm Hg); GFR is Kf times net filtration pressure, about 125 mL/min.","Ureteral obstruction lowers GFR by raising Bowman space pressure; hypoalbuminemia tends to raise it by lowering oncotic pressure; dehydration lowers it through higher oncotic pressure and lower renal flow.","Clearance is UV/P; inulin clearance equals GFR, creatinine slightly overestimates it, and PAH clearance equals effective renal plasma flow (about 600 mL/min, about 90 percent of true plasma flow).","Filtration fraction is GFR divided by renal plasma flow, about 0.2; clearance below GFR means net reabsorption and above GFR means net secretion.","The proximal tubule reabsorbs about two thirds of filtered Na+ and water isosmotically, nearly all glucose and amino acids, and most bicarbonate through Na+/H+ exchange.","The thick ascending limb reabsorbs about 25 percent of Na+ through NKCC2, is water impermeable, and uses a lumen-positive voltage from K+ recycling to reabsorb Ca2+ and Mg2+ between cells.","Aldosterone adds ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase in principal cells, increasing Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion; ADH inserts aquaporin-2 through V2, cAMP, and protein kinase A; PTH increases distal Ca2+ reabsorption.","Countercurrent multiplication and urea recycling build a 300 to 1,200 mOsm/kg medullary gradient that ADH lets the collecting duct use.","Central diabetes insipidus responds to desmopressin, nephrogenic does not, primary polydipsia concentrates with water deprivation, and SIADH shows low serum Na+ with inappropriately concentrated urine.","Only changes in ECF osmolality move water into or out of cells; a suprasacral cord lesion gives a spastic dyssynergic bladder and a sacral lesion a flaccid overflow bladder."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Glomerular Filtration, Renal Blood Flow, and Their Control"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Renal Tubular Reabsorption and Secretion"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Concentration and Dilution of Urine; Regulation of Extracellular Fluid Osmolarity and Sodium Concentration"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The urinary system: glomerular filtration, transport of sodium, chloride, and water, and urine concentration"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Body Fluid Compartments: Extracellular and Intracellular Fluids; Edema, and the section on micturition"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal disease and disorders of the hypothalamus and pituitary gland"}]},"UR-7":{"unit":"UR-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-07-01","title":"Kidney in Acid-Base and Blood Pressure Control","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the kidney holds plasma pH and arterial pressure steady over days to weeks. It covers bicarbonate reclamation and new bicarbonate generation, blood gas interpretation and compensation, the anion gap, the renal tubular acidoses, vomiting alkalosis, the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and its counterweight atrial natriuretic peptide, and pressure natriuresis. Board questions usually give a blood gas or an electrolyte panel and ask you to name the disorder, judge compensation, find the cause, or explain hypertension with low potassium. Most answers follow from one idea: the kidney trades Na+ for H+ and K+ in predictable ways, and volume needs often override pH needs.","prerequisites":["Henderson-Hasselbalch equation and the bicarbonate buffer system","Nephron segments and their transporters: proximal tubule, thick ascending limb, principal and intercalated cells (UR-6)","Glomerular filtration, filtration fraction, and peritubular capillary forces (UR-6)","Effective circulating volume, ECF volume, and osmolality (UR-6)","G protein receptor signaling (Gs, Gq, cAMP, cGMP) and steroid hormone receptors"],"connections":[{"to":"Respiratory physiology","how":"The lungs set PaCO2 within minutes, so every metabolic disorder has a respiratory compensation and every respiratory disorder a renal one."},{"to":"Endocrine system: adrenal cortex","how":"Aldosterone from the zona glomerulosa links the renin-angiotensin system to K+ and H+ secretion, so adrenal disease appears as acid-base and K+ disorders."},{"to":"Cardiovascular system: blood pressure regulation","how":"Baroreflexes buffer pressure moment to moment, but pressure natriuresis by the kidney sets the long-term level and explains most forms of hypertension."},{"to":"Biochemistry: amino acid and energy metabolism","how":"Glutamine breakdown makes ammonium and new bicarbonate, and lactate, ketoacids, and toxic alcohol metabolites are the acids that open the anion gap."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal system","how":"Vomiting removes HCl and diarrhea removes HCO3-, giving the two most common gut-driven acid-base disorders."}],"terms":[{"term":"Nonvolatile acid","def":"Acid produced by metabolism that cannot be exhaled as CO2, mainly sulfuric acid from sulfur amino acids, about 1 mEq/kg per day in adults on a typical diet."},{"term":"Titratable acid","def":"H+ excreted bound to filtered urinary buffers, chiefly phosphate, measured by titrating urine back to plasma pH."},{"term":"Net acid excretion","def":"Titratable acid plus ammonium minus any urinary bicarbonate; it equals the new bicarbonate the kidney adds to blood."},{"term":"Compensation","def":"The secondary change in PaCO2 or HCO3- that moves pH back toward normal without fully correcting it."},{"term":"Mixed disturbance","def":"Two or more primary acid-base disorders present at the same time, recognized when compensation falls outside its expected range."},{"term":"Anion gap","def":"Plasma Na+ minus the sum of Cl- and HCO3-, an estimate of unmeasured anions, mostly albumin."},{"term":"Renal tubular acidosis (RTA)","def":"A normal anion gap metabolic acidosis caused by a tubular defect in reclaiming HCO3- or excreting H+, with near-normal GFR."},{"term":"Paradoxical aciduria","def":"Excretion of acidic urine during metabolic alkalosis because volume and K+ depletion drive H+ secretion."},{"term":"Juxtaglomerular apparatus","def":"The renin-secreting granular cells of the afferent arteriole, the macula densa of the thick ascending limb, and extraglomerular mesangial cells at the vascular pole."},{"term":"Pressure natriuresis","def":"The rise in renal Na+ and water excretion that follows a rise in arterial pressure."},{"term":"Aldosterone escape","def":"The return of Na+ excretion to match intake despite continued aldosterone excess, which limits volume gain and prevents edema."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-07-01-C01","obj":"UR-7.1","title":"Reclaiming filtered bicarbonate and making new bicarbonate","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Metabolism adds nonvolatile acid to the body every day, about 1 mEq/kg (roughly 50 to 100 mEq in an adult). Plasma HCO3- buffers it immediately, so each H+ consumes one HCO3-. The kidney has two separate jobs: it must first reclaim essentially all the HCO3- that is filtered, and then it must make new HCO3- equal to the acid produced. Only the second job corrects an acid load."},{"type":"h","text":"Reclamation in the proximal tubule"},{"type":"p","text":"At a GFR of 180 L/day and a plasma HCO3- of 24 mEq/L, about 4,300 mEq of HCO3- is filtered daily. The proximal tubule reclaims about 80%, the thick ascending limb about 10 to 15%, and the distal nephron the rest, so normal urine contains almost none. Filtered HCO3- cannot cross the apical membrane directly. Instead, the Na+/H+ exchanger (NHE3), driven by the low intracellular Na+ that the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase creates, secretes H+ into the lumen, where it reconverts HCO3- to CO2 and water."},{"type":"flow","title":"Proximal reclamation of filtered HCO3-","steps":["NHE3 secretes H+ into the lumen in exchange for Na+","H+ combines with filtered HCO3- to form H2CO3","Brush border carbonic anhydrase forms CO2 and water","CO2 diffuses into the tubule cell","Cytosolic carbonic anhydrase re-forms H+ and HCO3-","HCO3- exits to blood with Na+; H+ is secreted again"],"caption":"The secreted H+ is consumed in the lumen, so this step recovers filtered base but adds no new HCO3- and excretes no acid."},{"type":"p","text":"Blocking carbonic anhydrase, as acetazolamide does, shows why the enzyme matters: luminal H2CO3 accumulates, H+ secretion stalls, and HCO3- with Na+ and K+ spills into the urine, producing a mild proximal acidosis."},{"type":"h","text":"New bicarbonate: titratable acid and ammonium"},{"type":"p","text":"New HCO3- is made whenever secreted H+ leaves in the urine on a buffer other than HCO3-. Urine pH cannot fall much below about 4.5, so free H+ carries almost nothing; buffers are essential. Filtered HPO4 2- (pKa about 6.8) accepts H+ to become H2PO4-, which is titratable acid. Its amount is fixed by the filtered phosphate load, so it cannot rise much in acidosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Ammonium is the adaptable route. Proximal cells take up glutamine and split it into 2 NH4+ and alpha-ketoglutarate, whose metabolism yields 2 new HCO3-. NH4+ is secreted by NHE3, reabsorbed in the thick ascending limb in place of K+ on the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, and builds up in the medullary interstitium. NH3 then diffuses into the collecting duct, where H+ secreted by alpha-intercalated cells (via the H+-ATPase and H+/K+-ATPase) traps it as NH4+. The HCO3- counts as new only if NH4+ is excreted, because NH4+ returned to the liver is made into urea, a process that consumes HCO3-. Acidosis and hypokalemia stimulate ammoniagenesis; hyperkalemia suppresses it."},{"type":"table","title":"Two renal tasks in acid-base balance","cols":["Feature","HCO3- reclamation","New HCO3- generation"],"rows":[["Main site","Proximal tubule","Proximal tubule (ammoniagenesis) and alpha-intercalated cells"],["Fate of secreted H+","Consumed in the lumen as CO2 and water","Excreted bound to phosphate or as NH4+"],["Net effect on body base","Prevents loss; adds none","Adds 1 HCO3- per H+ excreted"],["Typical daily amount","About 4,300 mEq reclaimed","About 50 to 100 mEq: titratable acid about 20 to 30, NH4+ about 30 to 50"],["Response to chronic acidosis","Proximal H+ secretion increases","NH4+ excretion can rise several-fold, to a few hundred mEq/day"]],"caption":"Net acid excretion (titratable acid plus NH4+ minus urinary HCO3-) equals the new HCO3- returned to blood."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In chronic metabolic acidosis, which urinary component rises the most, titratable acid or ammonium, and why?","explain":"Ammonium. Titratable acid is limited by the filtered phosphate load, while glutamine uptake and ammoniagenesis are induced by acidosis and can increase several-fold over days.","check":"The ammonium paragraph and the last row of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-07-01-C02","obj":"UR-7.2","title":"Interpreting arterial blood gases and compensation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, pH = 6.1 + log [HCO3- / (0.03 x PaCO2)], shows that pH depends on the ratio of HCO3- to dissolved CO2, not on either value alone. With HCO3- 24 mEq/L and PaCO2 40 mm Hg the ratio is 20 to 1 and pH is 7.40. Normal arterial values are pH 7.35 to 7.45, PaCO2 35 to 45 mm Hg, and HCO3- about 22 to 26 mEq/L."},{"type":"p","text":"A primary metabolic disorder changes HCO3-; a primary respiratory disorder changes PaCO2. Compensation changes the other variable in the same direction, which restores the ratio partly. It is driven by the pH deviation itself, so it never fully normalizes pH and never overshoots."},{"type":"flow","title":"Reading a blood gas","steps":["Check pH: below 7.35 acidemia, above 7.45 alkalemia","Find the variable that explains the pH change","HCO3- moved with pH: primary metabolic","PaCO2 moved opposite to pH: primary respiratory","Compare compensation with its expected range","Outside the range: a second primary disorder","In metabolic acidosis, calculate the anion gap"],"caption":"Applying the steps in order prevents mistaking a compensation for a primary disorder."},{"type":"p","text":"The lungs compensate within minutes to hours because chemoreceptors change ventilation as soon as pH changes. Winters formula predicts the PaCO2 in metabolic acidosis: 1.5 x HCO3- + 8, plus or minus 2. Respiratory compensation for metabolic alkalosis is weaker, because hypoventilation is limited by falling PaO2."},{"type":"p","text":"Compensation for a respiratory disorder has two phases. In acute respiratory acidosis, HCO3- rises only slightly, because non-bicarbonate buffers, mainly hemoglobin and proteins, take up H+ from the hydrated CO2. Over 3 to 5 days, the high PaCO2 increases tubular H+ secretion and ammoniagenesis, the kidney adds new HCO3-, and the rise becomes about three to four times larger. The reverse occurs in respiratory alkalosis. Comparing measured HCO3- with the acute and chronic expectations therefore indicates how long a respiratory disorder has been present."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary disorders and expected compensation","cols":["Disorder","pH","Primary change","Expected compensation"],"rows":[["Metabolic acidosis","Low","HCO3- down","PaCO2 = 1.5 x HCO3- + 8, plus or minus 2"],["Metabolic alkalosis","High","HCO3- up","PaCO2 up about 0.7 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in HCO3-"],["Acute respiratory acidosis","Low","PaCO2 up","HCO3- up about 1 per 10 mm Hg rise"],["Chronic respiratory acidosis","Low, nearer normal","PaCO2 up","HCO3- up about 3.5 per 10 mm Hg rise"],["Acute respiratory alkalosis","High","PaCO2 down","HCO3- down about 2 per 10 mm Hg fall"],["Chronic respiratory alkalosis","High, nearer normal","PaCO2 down","HCO3- down about 4 to 5 per 10 mm Hg fall"]],"caption":"Values are consensus approximations; a measured value clearly outside the range indicates a second primary process."},{"type":"p","text":"Mixed disturbances are recognized in three ways: compensation outside the expected range, PaCO2 and HCO3- moving in opposite directions (for example both acidosis types, with high PaCO2 and low HCO3-), or a normal pH with clearly abnormal PaCO2 and HCO3-. Salicylate toxicity in adults is the classic mixed respiratory alkalosis and high anion gap metabolic acidosis."},{"type":"example","title":"A metabolic acidosis with inadequate ventilation","text":"A patient with diabetic ketoacidosis who has received a sedative has pH 7.20, PaCO2 32 mm Hg, and HCO3- 12 mEq/L. The pH is low and HCO3- is low, so the primary disorder is metabolic acidosis. Winters formula predicts PaCO2 = 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, range 24 to 28 mm Hg. The measured 32 is higher than expected, so ventilation is not compensating fully and a concurrent respiratory acidosis is present. Check the ratio: 0.03 x 32 = 0.96, and 12 / 0.96 = 12.5, whose log is about 1.10, so pH is about 7.20, consistent with the gas. Had PaCO2 been 20, a concurrent respiratory alkalosis would be present instead."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-07-01-C03","obj":"UR-7.3","title":"The anion gap and the causes of metabolic acidosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Plasma is electrically neutral, but routine panels measure only some ions. The anion gap, Na+ minus (Cl- + HCO3-), estimates the unmeasured anions, mostly albumin with smaller amounts of phosphate, sulfate, and organic anions. The normal range is about 8 to 12 mEq/L, though it depends on the laboratory method. Because albumin carries most of the gap, each 1 g/dL fall in albumin below about 4 g/dL lowers the expected gap by about 2.5 mEq/L, and hypoalbuminemia can hide an added acid."},{"type":"p","text":"The gap separates metabolic acidoses by mechanism. When an acid HA is added, its H+ is buffered by HCO3-, which falls, while its anion A- stays in plasma as an unmeasured anion, so the gap rises and Cl- stays normal. When HCO3- itself is lost from the gut or kidney, or not regenerated, the kidney retains Cl- with Na+ to keep neutrality, so Cl- rises and the gap stays normal (hyperchloremic acidosis)."},{"type":"table","title":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis","cols":["Cause","Acid that accumulates","Mechanism and distinguishing findings"],"rows":[["Lactic acidosis","L-lactate","Anaerobic glycolysis in shock or hypoxia (type A) or impaired lactate clearance or mitochondrial function (type B)"],["Ketoacidosis","Beta-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate","Low insulin with high glucagon drives hepatic fatty acid oxidation (diabetic, alcoholic, starvation)"],["Uremia","Sulfate, phosphate, urate, organic anions","Retained when GFR falls very low; earlier kidney disease gives a normal gap from low NH4+ excretion"],["Methanol","Formic acid","Alcohol dehydrogenase forms formaldehyde, and aldehyde dehydrogenase forms formate; formate injures the retina and optic nerve; high osmolal gap"],["Ethylene glycol","Glycolic and oxalic acids","Calcium oxalate crystals in urine and tubules cause acute kidney injury; hypocalcemia; high osmolal gap"],["Salicylates","Salicylic acid, lactate, ketoacids","Uncoupled oxidative phosphorylation; early direct respiratory center stimulation causes respiratory alkalosis"]],"caption":"Propylene glycol, iron, and isoniazid are less common causes. The osmolal gap (measured minus calculated osmolality, normally under about 10 mOsm/kg) is high in toxic alcohol ingestion before the alcohol is fully metabolized."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal gap acidosis has two main sources. Diarrhea removes HCO3- secreted by pancreatic and intestinal cells, along with K+, so hypokalemia is common. Renal tubular acidosis (C04) and early chronic kidney disease fail to reclaim HCO3- or to excrete NH4+. Carbonic anhydrase inhibition and large volumes of saline also produce a hyperchloremic acidosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The urine anion gap, urine Na+ + K+ minus Cl-, distinguishes gut from renal causes. NH4+ is excreted with Cl-, so when the kidney responds appropriately to diarrhea, urine Cl- exceeds Na+ + K+ and the gap is negative. In distal and type 4 RTA, NH4+ excretion is low and the urine gap is positive."},{"type":"p","text":"In a high gap acidosis, comparing the rise in the gap with the fall in HCO3- (the delta ratio) reveals a hidden second disorder. A rise in gap much larger than the fall in HCO3- means HCO3- was also being added, as in vomiting, whereas a fall in HCO3- much larger than the rise in gap means a normal gap acidosis coexists."},{"type":"example","title":"A gap that rises more than HCO3- falls","text":"A patient with diabetic ketoacidosis has vomited for two days. Na+ is 136, Cl- 90, and HCO3- 16 mEq/L; albumin is normal. The anion gap is 136 - (90 + 16) = 30 mEq/L, high, so an unmeasured acid has been added. Taking 12 as the upper normal gap and 24 as normal HCO3-, the gap rose by 18 while HCO3- fell by only 8. If ketoacids alone were acting, HCO3- should have fallen by roughly the same 18, to about 6. The extra HCO3- comes from a concurrent metabolic alkalosis, which the low Cl- from vomiting supports."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-07-01-C04","obj":"UR-7.4","title":"Renal tubular acidosis types 1, 2, and 4","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal tubular acidosis (RTA) is a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis in which the tubule fails at one of the two tasks in C01 while GFR is preserved. Asking which task failed, and where, predicts the urine pH, the plasma K+, and the associated findings."},{"type":"h","text":"Type 1 (distal)"},{"type":"p","text":"Alpha-intercalated cells cannot secrete enough H+, because of a defective H+-ATPase or basolateral Cl-/HCO3- exchanger, autoimmune injury (Sjogren syndrome, systemic lupus erythematosus), or back-leak of secreted H+ through a damaged membrane (amphotericin B). The collecting duct cannot lower urine pH, so urine pH stays above 5.5 even in severe acidemia, little NH3 is trapped as NH4+, and plasma HCO3- may fall very low. Hypokalemia follows because Na+ reabsorbed by principal cells is balanced by K+ secretion instead of H+, and mild volume loss raises aldosterone. Calcium phosphate stones and nephrocalcinosis have three causes: bone buffering of acid raises urinary calcium, acidosis increases proximal citrate reabsorption so urinary citrate, a calcium chelator, falls, and alkaline urine favors calcium phosphate precipitation."},{"type":"h","text":"Type 2 (proximal)"},{"type":"p","text":"The proximal tubule reclaims HCO3- poorly, lowering the plasma level at which HCO3- appears in urine. HCO3- is wasted until plasma HCO3- falls to that new threshold, often about 15 to 20 mEq/L, after which the filtered load is small enough to be reclaimed and the intact distal nephron acidifies urine below 5.5. The acidosis is therefore self-limited. Hypokalemia results from high delivery of Na+ and HCO3- to the distal nephron together with aldosterone from volume loss. The defect may be isolated (carbonic anhydrase inhibitors) or part of Fanconi syndrome, generalized proximal failure that also wastes glucose, amino acids, phosphate, and urate. Causes include light chains in multiple myeloma, cystinosis, Wilson disease, lead, and some drugs such as tenofovir and ifosfamide. Phosphate loss causes rickets or osteomalacia, while citrate excretion is preserved, so stones are uncommon."},{"type":"h","text":"Type 4 (hyperkalemic)"},{"type":"p","text":"Aldosterone deficiency or resistance reduces Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion, so K+ rises. Hyperkalemia suppresses proximal ammoniagenesis, and less aldosterone means less H+-ATPase activity, so NH4+ excretion falls. The cells can still acidify urine below 5.5, but there is little buffer to carry acid. The classic setting is hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism in diabetic nephropathy; adrenal insufficiency and drugs that block the renin-angiotensin system, the mineralocorticoid receptor, or ENaC (for example spironolactone at the receptor and trimethoprim at ENaC) produce the same physiology. The acidosis is usually mild."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparing the renal tubular acidoses","cols":["Feature","Type 1 (distal)","Type 2 (proximal)","Type 4"],"rows":[["Defect","Distal H+ secretion","Proximal HCO3- reclamation","Aldosterone deficiency or resistance"],["Urine pH in acidemia","Above 5.5","Below 5.5 once HCO3- is low; high during wasting","Usually below 5.5"],["Plasma K+","Low","Low","High"],["Plasma HCO3-","Can be below 10 mEq/L","Often about 15 to 20 mEq/L","Often above 17 mEq/L"],["Urine anion gap","Positive","Variable","Positive"],["Associations","Calcium phosphate stones, nephrocalcinosis, autoimmune disease, amphotericin B","Fanconi syndrome, myeloma, rickets or osteomalacia","Diabetic nephropathy, adrenal insufficiency, drugs blocking aldosterone"]],"caption":"Types 1 and 2 cause hypokalemia; type 4 is the RTA with hyperkalemia. Type 3, a rare mixed form from carbonic anhydrase II deficiency with osteopetrosis, is omitted."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-07-01-C05","obj":"UR-7.5","title":"Vomiting and hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Metabolic alkalosis needs two processes: one that generates extra HCO3- and one that stops the kidney from excreting it. Normally the kidney can excrete a large HCO3- load quickly, so persistent alkalosis always means the kidney is being forced to reclaim HCO3-."},{"type":"p","text":"Generation in vomiting begins in the parietal cell. Each H+ secreted into the stomach is matched by one HCO3- released to blood (the alkaline tide). Normally pancreatic HCO3- later neutralizes that acid in the duodenum, so the net effect is zero. Vomiting removes the HCl, so the matching HCO3- stays in blood and Cl- is lost."},{"type":"p","text":"Maintenance comes from the kidney's response to the losses. Volume depletion lowers GFR, which lowers the filtered HCO3- load, and raises angiotensin II, which stimulates proximal Na+/H+ exchange. Aldosterone increases Na+ reabsorption through ENaC in principal cells, making the lumen more negative and favoring K+ secretion, and also stimulates the H+-ATPase of alpha-intercalated cells. Chloride depletion matters independently: beta-intercalated cells excrete HCO3- through the apical Cl-/HCO3- exchanger pendrin, which needs luminal Cl-. Hypokalemia adds further: H+ shifts into cells, and H+/K+-ATPase activity and ammoniagenesis rise."},{"type":"flow","title":"From vomiting to maintained alkalosis","steps":["Vomiting removes HCl, Na+, and water","Parietal cell HCO3- stays in blood","Volume loss raises angiotensin II and aldosterone","Low luminal Cl- blocks distal HCO3- secretion","Kidney reclaims HCO3-; secretes K+ and H+","Hypochloremic, hypokalemic alkalosis with acidic urine"],"caption":"Generation is in the stomach; maintenance is in the kidney."},{"type":"p","text":"Most K+ is lost in urine, not vomitus, because gastric fluid contains only about 5 to 10 mEq/L of K+. Early on, filtered HCO3- exceeds what the tubule can reclaim, so some HCO3- reaches the urine with Na+ and K+, and urine pH is alkaline. As volume depletion deepens, the kidney reclaims all filtered HCO3- and secretes H+ to save Na+, so urine becomes acidic despite alkalemia, the paradoxical aciduria. Throughout, urine Cl- is low (often below about 20 mEq/L), which marks a chloride-responsive alkalosis. The ventilatory response is hypoventilation, raising PaCO2 about 0.7 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in HCO3-."},{"type":"p","text":"Urine Cl- separates this pattern from mineralocorticoid excess (C07) and ongoing diuretic use, in which urine Cl- is high because Cl- is not being conserved."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In a patient who has been vomiting for several days, why would the urine pH be acidic despite a plasma pH of 7.52?","explain":"Volume depletion and aldosterone make Na+ retention the kidney's priority. Proximal Na+/H+ exchange and distal Na+ reabsorption require H+ and K+ secretion, low luminal Cl- blocks HCO3- excretion, and hypokalemia further stimulates H+ secretion.","check":"The maintenance paragraph and the flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-07-01-C06","obj":"UR-7.6","title":"The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and atrial natriuretic peptide","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renin release is the rate-limiting step of the system. Renin is stored in granular juxtaglomerular cells, modified smooth muscle in the wall of the afferent arteriole. Three inputs release it, and each signals low effective circulating volume. First, falling perfusion pressure reduces stretch of the afferent arteriole, which the juxtaglomerular cells sense directly. Second, the macula densa, specialized cells at the end of the thick ascending limb, senses less NaCl through its Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter and signals renin release through prostaglandin E2 and nitric oxide; high NaCl does the reverse through adenosine, which also constricts the afferent arteriole (tubuloglomerular feedback). Third, renal sympathetic nerves act on beta-1 receptors, raising cAMP in juxtaglomerular cells."},{"type":"flow","title":"Renin, angiotensin, and aldosterone","steps":["Low perfusion, low macula densa NaCl, or beta-1 input","Juxtaglomerular cells release renin","Renin cleaves liver angiotensinogen to angiotensin I","ACE, mainly in lung endothelium, forms angiotensin II","Angiotensin II acts on AT1 receptors","Aldosterone and ADH release; thirst","Na+ and water retention restore volume and pressure"],"caption":"Angiotensin II also inhibits renin release, a short negative feedback loop."},{"type":"p","text":"Angiotensin II, acting on Gq-coupled AT1 receptors, restores volume and pressure in several ways. It constricts systemic arterioles. In the kidney it constricts the efferent arteriole more than the afferent at physiological levels, which supports GFR when perfusion is low and raises filtration fraction; the resulting lower hydrostatic and higher oncotic pressure in peritubular capillaries increases proximal reabsorption. It also stimulates NHE3 directly, which is why volume depletion favors HCO3- reclamation (C05). It stimulates aldosterone from the zona glomerulosa, ADH from the posterior pituitary, thirst, and sympathetic transmission. ACE also inactivates bradykinin."},{"type":"p","text":"Aldosterone, released in response to angiotensin II and to high plasma K+, binds cytosolic mineralocorticoid receptors in principal cells and increases ENaC, ROMK K+ channels, and Na+/K+-ATPase. Na+ reabsorption makes the lumen negative, which drives K+ secretion from principal cells and supports H+ secretion from alpha-intercalated cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) is released by atrial myocytes when they are stretched by high volume and acts through a guanylyl cyclase receptor that raises cGMP. Its actions oppose those of angiotensin II at nearly every level, but it is weaker, so it limits volume expansion rather than driving large changes. Brain natriuretic peptide, released mainly by ventricular myocytes, acts similarly."},{"type":"table","title":"Opposing actions of angiotensin II and ANP","cols":["Target","Angiotensin II","ANP"],"rows":[["Stimulus","Low volume or pressure","Atrial stretch from high volume"],["Receptor and messenger","AT1, Gq, IP3 and Ca2+","Guanylyl cyclase receptor, cGMP"],["Arterioles","Constriction","Dilation"],["GFR","Preserved by efferent constriction","Raised by afferent dilation and efferent constriction"],["Tubular Na+","More proximal reabsorption; aldosterone increases distal reabsorption","Less collecting duct reabsorption"],["Renin and aldosterone","Aldosterone stimulated; renin inhibited by feedback","Both inhibited"],["ADH and thirst","Stimulated","Inhibited"]],"caption":"Angiotensin II conserves salt and water; ANP promotes natriuresis and diuresis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-07-01-C07","obj":"UR-7.7","title":"Pressure natriuresis and primary hyperaldosteronism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Baroreceptor reflexes correct pressure within seconds but reset to a new level within one to two days, so they cannot set long-term pressure. The kidney can. When arterial pressure rises, Na+ and water excretion rise (pressure natriuresis and pressure diuresis). Higher pressure is transmitted to peritubular capillaries and the renal interstitium, which reduces proximal reabsorption and moves NHE3 and other transporters away from the brush border; medullary blood flow rises and reduces the medullary gradient; and renin and angiotensin II fall."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Pressure natriuresis as long-term feedback","steps":["Arterial pressure rises above the set point","Renal Na+ and water excretion exceed intake","ECF and blood volume fall","Venous return and cardiac output fall","Arterial pressure returns toward the set point"],"caption":"Excretion exceeds intake until pressure returns to the level where they are equal."},{"type":"p","text":"The long-term pressure therefore sits where the renal output curve, plotting Na+ excretion against pressure, crosses the line of salt intake. Because the kidney keeps adjusting until output equals intake, this feedback returns pressure fully to that point, unlike reflexes. It follows that sustained hypertension requires the renal output curve to shift toward higher pressures, so that balance is reached only at a higher pressure. Anything that increases tubular Na+ reabsorption or reduces nephron function produces that shift, and if the renin-angiotensin system cannot be suppressed, pressure becomes salt sensitive."},{"type":"h","text":"Primary hyperaldosteronism"},{"type":"p","text":"In primary hyperaldosteronism, the adrenal cortex secretes aldosterone autonomously, from an aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn syndrome) or bilateral zona glomerulosa hyperplasia. Excess ENaC activity retains Na+ and shifts the renal output curve, so pressure rises. The expanded volume and high pressure suppress renin, so the hallmark is high aldosterone with low renin. The same lumen-negative potential drives K+ secretion, causing hypokalemia with weakness and polyuria from impaired concentrating ability, and H+ secretion plus hypokalemia-driven ammoniagenesis generate metabolic alkalosis with high urine Cl-. Plasma Na+ is normal to high-normal."},{"type":"p","text":"Edema does not develop. After a modest rise in extracellular volume, higher pressure and ANP raise Na+ delivery beyond the aldosterone-sensitive segment until excretion again matches intake (aldosterone escape), yet K+ wasting continues because distal Na+ delivery remains high."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypertension with hypokalemia: renin and aldosterone patterns","cols":["Condition","Mechanism","Renin","Aldosterone"],"rows":[["Primary hyperaldosteronism","Autonomous adrenal aldosterone","Low","High"],["Renovascular hypertension","Renal artery stenosis lowers perfusion, releasing renin","High","High"],["Liddle syndrome","Gain-of-function ENaC mutation","Low","Low"],["Apparent mineralocorticoid excess or licorice","Deficient or inhibited 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 lets cortisol activate mineralocorticoid receptors","Low","Low"]],"caption":"Measuring renin and aldosterone together locates the source of mineralocorticoid activity."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with an aldosterone-producing adenoma has gained only a modest amount of extracellular fluid. Why does volume stop rising while K+ loss continues?","explain":"The rise in pressure and ANP increases Na+ excretion through pressure natriuresis until output equals intake. More Na+ then reaches the collecting duct, where aldosterone still drives Na+ reabsorption in exchange for K+ secretion.","check":"The pressure natriuresis cycle and the aldosterone escape paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Acid-base and blood pressure control share one set of renal transporters, which is why their disorders travel together. Angiotensin II drives proximal Na+/H+ exchange, and aldosterone couples distal Na+ reabsorption to K+ and H+ secretion, so states of high aldosterone produce hypokalemic alkalosis and states of low aldosterone produce hyperkalemic acidosis. Biochemistry supplies the acids (lactate from anaerobic glycolysis, ketoacids from unopposed fatty acid oxidation, formate and oxalate from alcohol dehydrogenase metabolism) and the buffer, ammonium from glutamine. The lungs and kidneys share compensation, on a time scale of hours for one and days for the other."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology places these ideas in disease: autoimmune injury to intercalated cells, light chain injury to proximal cells in myeloma, diabetic nephropathy with hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism, and adrenal adenomas. The formulas in this chapter are approximations, and a lab value must be read in its clinical setting. A normal pH or a normal anion gap does not exclude a disorder; hypoalbuminemia and mixed disturbances can hide one."}],"summary":["The proximal tubule reclaims about 80% of filtered HCO3- through NHE3 and carbonic anhydrase, but reclamation adds no new base.","New HCO3- is made only when H+ is excreted as titratable acid (mainly phosphate) or as NH4+ from glutamine; ammonium is the adaptable route.","pH depends on the HCO3- to PaCO2 ratio; compensation moves the second variable in the same direction but never fully corrects pH.","Winters formula (PaCO2 = 1.5 x HCO3- + 8, plus or minus 2) tests respiratory compensation; chronic renal compensation is several times larger than acute.","Anion gap = Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-); an added acid raises it, while HCO3- loss from gut or kidney gives a normal gap, hyperchloremic acidosis.","Type 1 RTA: distal H+ secretion fails, urine pH above 5.5, hypokalemia, calcium phosphate stones. Type 2: proximal HCO3- wasting, hypokalemia, often Fanconi. Type 4: hypoaldosteronism, hyperkalemia, low NH4+.","Vomiting generates alkalosis by removing HCl; volume, Cl-, and K+ depletion maintain it, with low urine Cl- and paradoxical aciduria.","Renin release responds to low afferent arteriolar pressure, low macula densa NaCl, and beta-1 input; angiotensin II and aldosterone retain Na+, and ANP opposes them.","Pressure natriuresis sets long-term arterial pressure; hypertension requires the renal output curve to shift to higher pressures.","Primary hyperaldosteronism: hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, high aldosterone, low renin, and no edema because of aldosterone escape."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Acid-Base Regulation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Role of the kidneys in long-term control of arterial pressure and hypertension"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Renal transport of acids and bases; integration of salt and water balance"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Disposal of nitrogen: glutamine and ammonia metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney; The Endocrine System, adrenal cortex"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal disease; disorders of the adrenal cortex"}]},"UR-8":{"unit":"UR-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-08-01","title":"Urinary Biochemistry and Nutrition","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the kidney as a metabolic organ: how it reclaims glucose and filtered proteins, activates vitamin D, sets the distribution of potassium, senses oxygen to make erythropoietin, and is injured by the metabolites of ethylene glycol and by the lipid changes of nephrotic syndrome. Board questions in this area usually hand you a lab panel, a urinalysis, or an ECG and ask you to predict the next value or name the failed step. If you can trace each abnormal value back to a transporter, an enzyme, or a feedback loop, you can work out patterns you have never memorized.","prerequisites":["Glomerular filtration, filtered load, and proximal tubule transport (UR-6)","Secondary active transport driven by the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase","Resting membrane potential and the Nernst equation for K+","Anion gap and the classification of metabolic acidosis (UR-7)","Lipoprotein classes and the LDL receptor pathway"],"connections":[{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"Potassium sets the resting potential and repolarization of cardiac cells, so its disorders appear as predictable changes in T waves, the QRS, and U waves."},{"to":"Metabolic endocrine disease (EN-12-D)","how":"Diabetic glucosuria and the parathyroid and vitamin D disorders are endocrine diseases whose laboratory signature is written by renal transport."},{"to":"Glomerular diseases (UR-12-A)","how":"Damage to the filtration barrier explains glomerular proteinuria, the hyperlipidemia of nephrotic syndrome, and lipiduria."},{"to":"Hematopoiesis and hemostasis (HE-4)","how":"Erythropoietin from the kidney is the main hormonal signal that sets red cell production in the marrow."},{"to":"Neoplasms of the urinary tract (UR-12-G)","how":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma loses VHL function and can secrete erythropoietin, causing secondary polycythemia."}],"terms":[{"term":"Filtered load","def":"The amount of a solute filtered per minute, equal to GFR multiplied by its plasma concentration (for freely filtered solutes)."},{"term":"Transport maximum (Tm)","def":"The highest rate at which a saturable carrier system can reabsorb a solute; for glucose about 375 mg/min in an adult."},{"term":"Renal threshold","def":"The plasma concentration at which a solute first appears in the urine; for glucose about 200 mg/dL."},{"term":"Splay","def":"The rounded portion of the titration curve between threshold and Tm, caused by nephrons saturating at different plasma levels."},{"term":"Osmotic diuresis","def":"Increased urine flow caused by an unreabsorbed solute that holds water in the tubular lumen."},{"term":"Bence Jones protein","def":"Free immunoglobulin light chains in the urine, produced in excess by a plasma cell clone."},{"term":"Calcitriol","def":"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, the active hormone formed by 1-alpha-hydroxylation in the proximal tubule."},{"term":"FGF23","def":"A phosphaturic hormone from osteocytes that lowers proximal phosphate reabsorption and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase."},{"term":"Internal potassium balance","def":"The distribution of potassium between intracellular and extracellular fluid, as distinct from external balance between intake and excretion."},{"term":"Hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF)","def":"A transcription factor degraded in normal oxygen and stabilized in hypoxia, where it switches on the erythropoietin gene."},{"term":"Osmolal gap","def":"Measured serum osmolality minus calculated osmolality; a gap above about 10 mOsm/kg signals an unmeasured osmole such as a toxic alcohol."},{"term":"Oval fat body","def":"A degenerated tubular epithelial cell or macrophage filled with lipid droplets, shed into the urine in heavy proteinuria."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-08-01-C01","obj":"UR-8.1","title":"Glucose reabsorption, the renal threshold, and osmotic diuresis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Glucose is small and uncharged, so it is freely filtered, and its filtered load equals GFR times plasma glucose. At a GFR of 125 mL/min and a plasma glucose of 100 mg/dL (1 mg/mL), about 125 mg is filtered each minute, and normally all of it is reclaimed in the proximal tubule, leaving urine essentially glucose free."},{"type":"p","text":"Reabsorption is secondary active transport. The basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase keeps intracellular Na+ low and the cell interior negative, and apical sodium-glucose cotransporters use that gradient to move glucose uphill into the cell. SGLT2 in the early proximal tubule (S1 and S2 segments) is a high capacity, low affinity carrier with a 1 Na+ to 1 glucose ratio and reclaims about 90% of filtered glucose. SGLT1 in the late proximal tubule (S3) is a low capacity, high affinity carrier with a 2 Na+ to 1 glucose ratio that scavenges the remainder. Glucose then leaves the cell down its gradient by facilitated diffusion through basolateral GLUT2 (GLUT1 in S3)."},{"type":"h","text":"Saturation, threshold, and splay"},{"type":"p","text":"Because the number of carriers is fixed, reabsorption rises with filtered load only until the carriers saturate. The whole-kidney transport maximum is about 375 mg/min. Glucose nevertheless appears in urine at a plasma level of about 200 mg/dL, when the filtered load (about 250 mg/min) is still below Tm, because nephrons differ in glomerular size and carrier density; those with high filtered load relative to their carriers saturate first. This gap between threshold and full saturation is splay. Above Tm, every additional milligram filtered is excreted, so excretion rises linearly with plasma glucose."},{"type":"example","title":"How much glucose reaches the urine","text":"A patient with uncontrolled diabetes has a plasma glucose of 400 mg/dL (4 mg/mL) and a GFR of 125 mL/min. Filtered load is 4 mg/mL times 125 mL/min, or 500 mg/min. The tubules can reabsorb at most about 375 mg/min, so about 125 mg/min is excreted, roughly 180 g/day if sustained. At 150 mg/dL the filtered load is only 188 mg/min, below threshold for nearly all nephrons, so the dipstick (glucose oxidase) is negative. Between about 200 mg/dL and the level at which filtered load reaches Tm (about 300 mg/dL), simple subtraction predicts no excretion, yet some glucose appears because low-capacity nephrons saturate early (splay)."},{"type":"flow","title":"From hyperglycemia to volume depletion","steps":["Plasma glucose rises above about 200 mg/dL","Filtered load exceeds carrier capacity in some nephrons","Unreabsorbed glucose stays in the tubular lumen as an osmole","Luminal osmoles hold water; luminal Na+ is diluted","Less water and Na+ are reabsorbed downstream","Polyuria, thirst, and extracellular volume depletion"],"caption":"Glucosuria drives an osmotic diuresis that loses water in excess of sodium, so volume depletion and rising plasma osmolality follow."},{"type":"p","text":"Osmotic diuresis explains the classic triad of diabetes: polyuria, polydipsia, and, with ongoing calorie loss as urinary glucose, weight loss. Water is lost in excess of sodium, so plasma osmolality rises and the falling GFR of volume depletion further raises plasma glucose, a cycle that underlies the hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state."},{"type":"p","text":"Glucosuria with a normal plasma glucose means the transport system, not the filtered load, has changed. Causes include generalized proximal tubule dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome, with glucose, amino acids, phosphate, and bicarbonate wasted together), isolated familial renal glucosuria from SLC5A2 (SGLT2) mutations, and pregnancy, in which a higher GFR raises filtered load and the threshold falls. Pharmacologic SGLT2 inhibition confirms the model: blocking the early carrier produces glucosuria and a mild osmotic natriuresis at normal plasma glucose."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-08-01-C02","obj":"UR-8.2","title":"Glomerular, overflow, and tubular proteinuria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal urine contains less than about 150 mg of protein per day, of which less than 30 mg is albumin. Two systems keep it that low. The glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, the glomerular basement membrane with its negatively charged heparan sulfate, and the podocyte slit diaphragm built on nephrin) restricts molecules by size and charge. Albumin (about 69 kDa, anionic) is large enough and negative enough to be held back almost completely. Smaller proteins, such as beta-2 microglobulin (about 12 kDa), free light chains (about 22 kDa as monomers), lysozyme, and retinol-binding protein, pass the filter and are reclaimed by the proximal tubule, where the megalin and cubilin receptors bind them for endocytosis and lysosomal breakdown to amino acids."},{"type":"p","text":"Each type of proteinuria breaks one step of this system. In glomerular proteinuria the barrier is damaged, so albumin crosses in large amounts; loss of charge selectivity alone (as in minimal change disease) leaks mainly albumin (selective proteinuria), while structural damage leaks larger proteins such as IgG as well (nonselective). Excretion often exceeds 3.5 g/day, the nephrotic range. In overflow proteinuria the barrier and tubule are normal but plasma carries so much of a small protein that the filtered load exceeds the proximal reabsorptive capacity, which is limited like that of glucose. The classic example is Bence Jones light chains from a plasma cell clone in multiple myeloma; myoglobin in rhabdomyolysis and hemoglobin in intravascular hemolysis behave the same way. In tubular proteinuria the filter and plasma are normal, but injured proximal cells fail to reclaim the small proteins filtered every day, so beta-2 microglobulin and retinol-binding protein appear, usually in amounts below about 2 g/day."},{"type":"table","title":"Three mechanisms of proteinuria","cols":["Feature","Glomerular","Overflow","Tubular"],"rows":[["Failed step","Filtration barrier","Plasma level exceeds tubular capacity","Proximal reabsorption"],["Main urinary protein","Albumin (plus IgG if nonselective)","Free light chains, myoglobin, or hemoglobin","Beta-2 microglobulin, retinol-binding protein"],["Usual amount","Often above 3.5 g/day","Variable, can be large","Usually below 2 g/day"],["Dipstick","Strongly positive","Often weak or negative for light chains","Weak or negative"],["Examples","Diabetic nephropathy, minimal change disease, membranous nephropathy","Multiple myeloma","Fanconi syndrome, heavy metal or drug injury, interstitial nephritis"]],"caption":"Locate the failed step first; the protein type and the dipstick result follow from it."},{"type":"p","text":"The urine dipstick uses a pH indicator dye that changes color when protein binds it, and it reacts mainly with albumin. A negative or weak dipstick with a high measured total urine protein (by sulfosalicylic acid precipitation or a quantitative protein to creatinine ratio) therefore points to light chains or tubular proteins, and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation identifies a monoclonal light chain."},{"type":"p","text":"The categories interact. Light chains overloading the proximal tubule are directly toxic to it and can cause an acquired Fanconi syndrome, adding tubular proteinuria, and in the distal nephron they bind uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) to form obstructing casts (myeloma cast nephropathy). Heavy glomerular proteinuria also overwhelms and injures proximal cells, so most glomerular diseases carry some tubular damage."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-08-01-C03","obj":"UR-8.3","title":"Vitamin D activation and calcium, phosphate, and PTH patterns","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) forms in skin when ultraviolet B light converts 7-dehydrocholesterol; D3 and plant-derived D2 are also absorbed from the diet. The liver adds a 25-hydroxyl group to form 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol), the main circulating and storage form and the best measure of vitamin D status because its production is largely unregulated. The regulated step is in mitochondria of proximal tubule cells, where 1-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) forms 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol)."},{"type":"p","text":"1-alpha-hydroxylase is stimulated by PTH and by low phosphate, and suppressed by FGF23, high phosphate, and calcitriol itself, which also induces 24-hydroxylase (CYP24A1) to inactivate it. PTH, acting through its Gs-coupled receptor, raises plasma calcium by three routes: bone resorption (indirectly, through RANKL from osteoblasts), increased calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule, and calcitriol synthesis. At the same time it removes NaPi-IIa cotransporters from the proximal tubule brush border, causing phosphaturia, so the phosphate released from bone does not accumulate. Calcitriol increases intestinal absorption of both calcium and phosphate and suppresses PTH gene transcription."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Calcium, PTH, and calcitriol feedback","steps":["Plasma ionized calcium falls","Calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid cells signals less","PTH secretion rises","Proximal tubule 1-alpha-hydroxylase makes more calcitriol","Gut calcium absorption, bone release, and distal reabsorption rise","Plasma calcium returns toward normal and PTH falls"],"caption":"Negative feedback through the calcium-sensing receptor; PTH-driven phosphaturia keeps phosphate from rising."},{"type":"p","text":"In chronic kidney disease, a falling GFR retains phosphate. FGF23 rises early to excrete it and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase, and loss of proximal tubule mass removes the enzyme itself, so calcitriol falls. Less calcitriol means less intestinal calcium, phosphate binds ionized calcium, and the parathyroid glands, now lacking calcitriol suppression, enlarge and secrete more PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism), leading to renal osteodystrophy. In primary hyperparathyroidism, usually a single adenoma, PTH is autonomous: calcium is high, phosphate is low from phosphaturia, and calcitriol is high. In vitamin D deficiency, low calcium absorption drives PTH up; PTH restores calcium toward normal at the cost of phosphaturia, so phosphate is low."},{"type":"table","title":"Mineral patterns (normal total calcium about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL, phosphate about 2.5 to 4.5 mg/dL)","cols":["Condition","Calcium","Phosphate","PTH","Vitamin D"],"rows":[["Chronic kidney disease","Low or normal","High","High (secondary)","Calcitriol low"],["Primary hyperparathyroidism","High","Low or low normal","High or inappropriately normal","Calcitriol high or high normal"],["Vitamin D deficiency","Low or low normal","Low","High (secondary)","25-hydroxyvitamin D low; calcitriol may be normal"],["Severe hypomagnesemia","Low","Normal or variable","Inappropriately low or normal","Calcitriol low"]],"caption":"Phosphate separates the two secondary forms: high in CKD (failed excretion) and low in vitamin D deficiency (PTH phosphaturia)."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypomagnesemia, usually below about 1 mg/dL (normal about 1.7 to 2.2 mg/dL), causes hypocalcemia that does not correct with calcium alone. Low intracellular magnesium enhances signaling by the calcium-sensing receptor, so PTH release is suppressed as if calcium were high, and magnesium-dependent adenylyl cyclase in bone and kidney responds poorly to the PTH that is released. PTH is therefore inappropriately low for the calcium, and calcitriol falls with it. The same deficiency causes renal potassium wasting, so hypokalemia often travels with it."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has calcium 7.2 mg/dL, low magnesium, and a PTH in the normal range. Is the parathyroid response normal?","explain":"No. Hypocalcemia should raise PTH well above normal. A normal value is inappropriately low because magnesium deficiency impairs PTH release and action, and calcium will stay low until magnesium is restored.","check":"The paragraph on hypomagnesemia and the last row of the mineral patterns table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-08-01-C04","obj":"UR-8.4","title":"Potassium shifts and the ECG","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"About 98% of body potassium is intracellular (about 140 mEq/L in cells versus 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L in plasma), and the gradient is maintained by the Na+/K+-ATPase. A potassium load is first moved into cells (internal balance) and then excreted over hours (external balance, mainly by principal cells under aldosterone). A small shift across cell membranes therefore changes the serum value a great deal, without any change in total body potassium."},{"type":"table","title":"Shifts of potassium across cell membranes","cols":["Direction","Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Into cells (lowers serum K+)","Insulin","Stimulates Na+/K+-ATPase (and Na+/H+ exchange, which supplies Na+ for the pump)"],["Into cells","Beta-2 adrenergic stimulation","Gs and cAMP increase Na+/K+-ATPase activity"],["Into cells","Alkalosis","H+ leaves cells to buffer the ECF; K+ enters to preserve electroneutrality"],["Out of cells (raises serum K+)","Acidosis","H+ enters cells to be buffered; K+ leaves"],["Out of cells","Cell lysis","Rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, hemolysis, or crush injury release cell contents"],["Out of cells","Insulin deficiency","Loss of tonic pump stimulation, as in diabetic ketoacidosis"],["Out of cells","Hyperosmolality","Water leaves cells, concentrating intracellular K+ and carrying it out by solvent drag"]],"caption":"These shifts change serum K+ without changing total body K+."},{"type":"p","text":"Two refinements matter on exams. First, mineral (non-anion gap) acidosis shifts potassium far more than organic acidosis, because lactate and ketoanions can enter cells with H+, so less K+ must leave. Second, diabetic ketoacidosis shows how shifts can hide depletion: insulin deficiency and hyperosmolality raise serum K+ even though osmotic diuresis has depleted total body potassium, so serum K+ falls sharply once insulin drives it back into cells. A falsely high value (pseudohyperkalemia) comes from hemolysis in the sample tube or from potassium released by very high platelet or white cell counts during clotting."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the ECG changes"},{"type":"p","text":"The resting membrane potential of cardiac cells lies near the K+ equilibrium potential, which depends on the ratio of intracellular to extracellular K+. In hyperkalemia this ratio falls, so the resting potential becomes less negative. Partial depolarization inactivates some fast Na+ channels, slowing phase 0 and conduction. At the same time, high extracellular K+ increases the conductance of the delayed rectifier K+ channels, so repolarization is faster. Faster repolarization appears first as peaked T waves; slowing conduction then produces the later changes in the table below. Skeletal muscle shows the same partial depolarization as weakness."},{"type":"p","text":"In hypokalemia the resting potential becomes more negative, but K+ conductance falls, so repolarization is delayed. The T wave flattens and a U wave (a small deflection after the T wave, best seen in V2 and V3) becomes prominent. Delayed repolarization and increased automaticity favor early afterdepolarizations and ectopic beats, and the risk is greater when Na+/K+-ATPase is also inhibited, as by digoxin. Skeletal muscle weakness, cramps, and ileus follow from hyperpolarization, which moves the resting potential further from threshold."},{"type":"diagram","name":"ecg-waveform","caption":"Normal ECG for reference against the potassium changes described above.","img":"notes-web/fig/UR-8-3.png"},{"type":"table","title":"ECG findings in order of severity","cols":["Disorder","Early","Later"],"rows":[["Hyperkalemia (above about 5.0 mEq/L)","Peaked, narrow T waves; shortened QT","Flat or absent P waves, long PR, wide QRS, sine wave, ventricular fibrillation or asystole"],["Hypokalemia (below about 3.5 mEq/L)","Flattened T waves, ST depression","Prominent U waves, long QU interval, ectopy, torsades de pointes"]],"caption":"ECG changes correlate only loosely with the serum value; the rate of change matters as well."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with lactic acidosis and one with an equal fall in pH from diarrhea (hyperchloremic acidosis): which has the larger rise in serum potassium from the shift alone?","explain":"The patient with diarrhea-associated mineral acidosis. Chloride cannot follow H+ into cells, so K+ must leave; lactate enters cells with H+, so less K+ is displaced.","check":"The paragraph on refinements to the shift rules."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-08-01-C05","obj":"UR-8.5","title":"Erythropoietin, the anemia of CKD, and secondary polycythemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In adults about 90% of erythropoietin (EPO) is made by fibroblast-like peritubular interstitial cells in the renal cortex and outer medulla; most of the rest comes from the liver, which is the main source in the fetus. The kidney is a good oxygen sensor because its oxygen consumption is tied to sodium reabsorption, which tracks GFR and renal blood flow. Local tissue PO2 therefore reflects oxygen delivery (hemoglobin concentration times saturation times flow) rather than blood flow alone, so anemia and hypoxemia both raise EPO, while a fall in renal blood flow alone has relatively little effect."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypoxia to red cell production","steps":["Oxygen delivery to renal interstitial cells falls","Prolyl hydroxylases lack O2 and stop hydroxylating HIF-alpha","VHL cannot tag HIF-alpha for proteasomal degradation","HIF-alpha pairs with HIF-beta and activates the EPO gene","EPO binds receptors on erythroid progenitors; JAK2 signaling","Progenitors survive and proliferate; reticulocytes rise in days","Oxygen delivery recovers and EPO falls"],"caption":"Oxygen-dependent prolyl hydroxylation is the sensor; HIF-2 alpha is the main isoform driving renal EPO."},{"type":"p","text":"The prolyl hydroxylases need oxygen, iron, and 2-oxoglutarate, which is why oxygen is the controlling variable. EPO acts mainly on CFU-E and proerythroblasts, where its receptor signals through JAK2 and STAT5 to prevent apoptosis and drive proliferation and differentiation. Without EPO, most committed progenitors die."},{"type":"h","text":"Anemia of chronic kidney disease"},{"type":"p","text":"As CKD destroys renal interstitium, EPO-producing cells are lost or transformed into scar-forming myofibroblasts, so EPO stays inappropriately low for the degree of anemia. The result is a hypoproliferative, normocytic, normochromic anemia with a low reticulocyte count. It usually becomes significant as GFR falls below about 30 to 60 mL/min. Contributing factors include shortened red cell survival in uremia, iron restriction by hepcidin (raised by inflammation and reduced renal clearance), and blood loss. Iron deficiency superimposed on CKD can make the anemia microcytic."},{"type":"h","text":"Secondary polycythemia"},{"type":"p","text":"Secondary polycythemia is a raised red cell mass driven by EPO. It is appropriate when tissue hypoxia is real (high altitude, chronic lung disease, cyanotic heart disease) and inappropriate when EPO is made without hypoxia. Clear cell renal cell carcinoma is the key renal example: loss of VHL function stabilizes HIF even in normal oxygen, so the tumor can secrete EPO autonomously while arterial saturation is normal. Other renal causes are cysts and hydronephrosis, which compress tissue and cause local hypoxia."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing causes of erythrocytosis","cols":["Type","Example","Arterial O2 saturation","EPO"],"rows":[["Primary","Polycythemia vera (JAK2 V617F)","Normal","Low"],["Secondary, appropriate","High altitude, COPD, right-to-left shunt","Low","High"],["Secondary, inappropriate","Renal cell carcinoma, hepatocellular carcinoma, hemangioblastoma","Normal","High"],["Relative","Dehydration (plasma volume loss)","Normal","Normal"]],"caption":"EPO level and oxygen saturation together locate the cause."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-08-01-C06","obj":"UR-8.6","title":"Ethylene glycol: from alcohol to acid, gaps, and crystals","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ethylene glycol, a sweet-tasting antifreeze solvent, is itself only mildly toxic, acting much like ethanol on the brain. Its danger lies in its metabolites. Hepatic alcohol dehydrogenase, using NAD+ as the electron acceptor, oxidizes it to glycolaldehyde, the first and rate-limiting step. Aldehyde dehydrogenase, also NAD+-dependent, then forms glycolic acid. Conversion of glycolic acid to glyoxylic acid is slow, so glycolate accumulates and is the main cause of the acidosis. Glyoxylate is oxidized to oxalic acid, or diverted to glycine (a pyridoxine-dependent transamination) and to other nontoxic products (a thiamine-dependent pathway)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Metabolism of ethylene glycol","steps":["Ethylene glycol: intact alcohol, raises measured osmolality","Alcohol dehydrogenase with NAD+ forms glycolaldehyde","Aldehyde dehydrogenase with NAD+ forms glycolic acid","Glycolate accumulates: high anion gap metabolic acidosis","Glycolate to glyoxylate to oxalic acid","Oxalate binds calcium: hypocalcemia and calcium oxalate crystals","Crystals and toxic metabolites injure proximal tubules: acute kidney injury"],"caption":"The parent alcohol causes the osmolal gap; its acid products cause the anion gap and the organ injury."},{"type":"p","text":"The NADH produced by both dehydrogenases raises the cytosolic NADH to NAD+ ratio, which shifts pyruvate toward lactate and adds a lactic component to the acidosis. Oxalate chelates ionized calcium, causing hypocalcemia that can prolong the QT interval and produce tetany, and it precipitates as calcium oxalate in the proximal tubule. Crystals and the direct toxicity of glycolaldehyde and glycolate damage tubular cells, causing acute tubular injury with oliguria. In urine, calcium oxalate monohydrate crystals are needle or prism shaped, and dihydrate crystals have an envelope shape; their absence does not exclude poisoning."},{"type":"h","text":"The two gaps and their time course"},{"type":"p","text":"Calculated serum osmolality is 2 times Na+ plus glucose divided by 18 plus BUN divided by 2.8 (glucose and BUN in mg/dL). A measured value more than about 10 mOsm/kg above this signals an unmeasured osmole. The anion gap, Na+ minus the sum of Cl- and HCO3-, is normally about 8 to 12 mEq/L. Early after ingestion the intact alcohol produces an osmolal gap with little acidosis. Over hours, as it is converted to acids, the osmolal gap falls and the anion gap rises, so a late presentation may show a large anion gap with a nearly normal osmolal gap. Blocking alcohol dehydrogenase, either by the competitive substrate ethanol or by fomepizole, confirms the model: the osmolal gap persists, the acids do not form, and the parent compound is excreted unchanged."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading both gaps","text":"A confused man has Na+ 138 mEq/L, Cl- 102 mEq/L, HCO3- 8 mEq/L, glucose 90 mg/dL, BUN 14 mg/dL, and a measured osmolality of 316 mOsm/kg. Anion gap: 138 minus 110 is 28 mEq/L, a high anion gap acidosis. Calculated osmolality: 276 plus 5 plus 5 is 286, so the osmolal gap is 30 mOsm/kg. Both gaps together point to a toxic alcohol caught partway through metabolism. Ethylene glycol has a molecular weight of 62, so each 1 mOsm/kg of gap corresponds to about 6.2 mg/dL, about 186 mg/dL here if ethylene glycol is the only unmeasured osmole. Calcium oxalate crystals in the urine and a falling calcium would confirm it."},{"type":"table","title":"Toxic alcohols compared","cols":["Alcohol","Toxic product","Anion gap acidosis","Distinctive injury"],"rows":[["Ethylene glycol","Glycolic and oxalic acids","Yes","Calcium oxalate crystals, acute tubular injury, hypocalcemia"],["Methanol","Formic acid","Yes","Optic nerve and retinal injury, putaminal necrosis"],["Isopropanol","Acetone","No","Osmolal gap and ketosis without acidosis"]],"caption":"All three are oxidized first by alcohol dehydrogenase and all raise the osmolal gap."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-08-01-C07","obj":"UR-8.7","title":"Hyperlipidemia and lipiduria in nephrotic syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Nephrotic syndrome is defined by proteinuria above 3.5 g/day (in adults), hypoalbuminemia (below about 3 g/dL), edema, hyperlipidemia, and lipiduria. The lipid changes follow from heavy glomerular protein loss through two broad mechanisms: the liver makes more lipoprotein, and the body clears lipoprotein more slowly."},{"type":"p","text":"On the synthetic side, the liver responds to low plasma oncotic pressure and urinary protein loss by increasing synthesis of many proteins at once, including albumin and apolipoprotein B. Hepatic cholesterol synthesis (HMG-CoA reductase activity) rises, and more VLDL is secreted, which is converted to LDL. Lipoprotein(a) also rises. On the catabolic side, lipoprotein lipase activity in capillary endothelium falls, so triglyceride-rich VLDL and chylomicron remnants are cleared slowly, and hepatic LDL receptor activity is reduced, so LDL is removed slowly. Urinary loss of lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT) impairs maturation of HDL. The typical result is high total and LDL cholesterol, with triglycerides rising as albumin falls further; the severity of hyperlipidemia is roughly inversely related to serum albumin."},{"type":"flow","title":"From proteinuria to lipiduria","steps":["Heavy glomerular albumin loss lowers plasma albumin","Low oncotic pressure stimulates hepatic protein and apoB synthesis","More VLDL and LDL are secreted","Lipoprotein lipase and LDL receptor activity fall; clearance slows","Plasma cholesterol and LDL rise; triglycerides rise in severe cases","Lipoproteins cross the damaged filter into tubular fluid","Lipid-laden tubular cells and casts appear in urine"],"caption":"Increased production and reduced clearance together raise plasma lipids; the leaky barrier carries them into urine."},{"type":"h","text":"Lipiduria under the microscope"},{"type":"p","text":"Proximal tubule cells take up the filtered lipid and protein, become engorged, degenerate, and are shed. A shed tubular cell (or macrophage) packed with lipid droplets is an oval fat body. When such cells or free droplets are trapped in a uromodulin matrix in the tubule, they form a fatty cast. Minimal change disease was historically called lipoid nephrosis because of lipid in tubular cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Cholesteryl esters in these droplets are anisotropic, so under polarized light each droplet shows a bright Maltese cross pattern (birefringence). Triglycerides are isotropic and do not polarize, but stain orange-red with fat stains such as Sudan III or Oil Red O, so the two methods are complementary. Starch granules from glove powder also show a Maltese cross; they are an artifact, recognized by their irregular, faceted shape and their location outside cells and casts."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A urine sediment from a patient with 5 g/day proteinuria shows refractile droplets that do not polarize. Does this exclude lipiduria?","explain":"No. Triglyceride droplets are isotropic and do not form Maltese crosses; only cholesteryl esters do. A fat stain such as Sudan III or Oil Red O would reveal them.","check":"The paragraph on polarized light and fat stains."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each topic in this chapter is a transport or enzyme step read through a lab value. Physiology supplies the rules: saturable carriers explain glucosuria and overflow proteinuria, the Nernst relationship explains the potassium ECG, and oxygen-dependent hydroxylation explains why erythropoietin tracks oxygen delivery. Biochemistry supplies the enzymes: 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the proximal tubule, alcohol dehydrogenase and its NAD+ requirement, and the lipoprotein enzymes whose activity falls in nephrotic syndrome. Pathology shows where these steps fail: diabetic nephropathy, myeloma kidney, Fanconi syndrome, CKD with secondary hyperparathyroidism and anemia, VHL-deficient renal cell carcinoma, and oxalate crystal injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Several findings depend on a second variable, so they must be read in context. Glucosuria depends on GFR as well as plasma glucose, serum potassium on acid-base status and insulin as well as total body stores, PTH on magnesium as well as calcium, and the two toxic alcohol gaps on the time since ingestion. Reference ranges differ slightly between laboratories, and ECG changes correlate only loosely with the potassium value, so the trend and the whole clinical picture carry more weight than a single number."}],"summary":["Glucose is reclaimed by SGLT2 (about 90%) and SGLT1 with Na+, then exits by GLUT2; carriers saturate at a Tm of about 375 mg/min.","Glucosuria starts at about 200 mg/dL because of splay; unreabsorbed glucose causes an osmotic diuresis with polyuria and volume depletion.","Glomerular proteinuria is albumin through a damaged barrier, overflow proteinuria is excess small protein such as light chains, and tubular proteinuria is failed proximal reclamation of beta-2 microglobulin; the dipstick detects mainly albumin.","PTH and low phosphate stimulate, and FGF23 and high phosphate suppress, proximal tubule 1-alpha-hydroxylase.","CKD: high phosphate, low calcitriol, low or normal calcium, high PTH. Primary hyperparathyroidism: high calcium, low phosphate. Vitamin D deficiency: low phosphate, high PTH.","Severe hypomagnesemia suppresses PTH release and action, so hypocalcemia persists until magnesium is replaced.","Insulin, beta-2 stimulation, and alkalosis move K+ into cells; acidosis, lysis, insulin deficiency, and hyperosmolality move it out.","Hyperkalemia gives peaked T waves, then wide QRS and sine wave; hypokalemia gives flat T waves and U waves.","Hypoxia stabilizes HIF in renal interstitial cells to make EPO; CKD causes normocytic anemia with low EPO, and renal cell carcinoma can cause polycythemia with high EPO and normal saturation.","Ethylene glycol gives an early osmolal gap, a later high anion gap acidosis, oxalate crystals, and hypocalcemia; nephrotic lipiduria shows oval fat bodies and fatty casts with Maltese crosses from cholesteryl esters."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Renal Tubular Reabsorption and Secretion"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of glucose, potassium, calcium, and phosphate by the kidney"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamin D and lipoprotein metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney; Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal Disease; Disorders of the Parathyroids and Calcium and Phosphorus Metabolism"}]},"UR-9":{"unit":"UR-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-09-01","title":"Genetic Diseases of the Kidney","subject":"Renal and Urinary System (RENL)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the single-gene disorders that most often reach the kidney: polycystic kidney disease, type IV collagen disease, the tumor suppressor syndromes behind renal carcinoma, Wilms tumor, and angiomyolipoma, and two metabolic disorders, cystinuria and Fabry disease. For each, it follows the chain from gene to protein to cell to the renal and extrarenal findings. Board questions usually give a pedigree, a biopsy or urine finding, or a cluster of extrarenal signs and ask for the gene, the protein, the inheritance, or the risk to a relative. If you understand what the normal protein does, the findings become predictable.","prerequisites":["Mendelian inheritance, pedigree symbols, and X inactivation","Tumor suppressor genes, oncogenes, and the Knudson two-hit hypothesis","Genomic imprinting and uniparental disomy","Nephron structure, the glomerular filtration barrier, and proximal tubule transport (UR-2, UR-6)","Development of the metanephros from ureteric bud and metanephric mesenchyme (UR-1)"],"connections":[{"to":"Kidney embryology (UR-1)","how":"WT1 controls the conversion of metanephric mesenchyme into nephron epithelium, so its loss links Wilms tumor to genitourinary malformation."},{"to":"Renal immunology (UR-10)","how":"Anti-GBM disease targets the alpha-3 NC1 domain of type IV collagen, part of the same network that is missing or defective in Alport syndrome."},{"to":"Inherited and developmental kidney disease (UR-12-E)","how":"The cystic diseases and Alport syndrome are taught there as pathology; this chapter supplies the gene and protein mechanisms."},{"to":"Kidney tumors (UR-12-G)","how":"VHL loss, WT1 loss, 11p15 imprinting defects, and TSC loss explain clear cell carcinoma, Wilms tumor, and angiomyolipoma."},{"to":"Obstruction and stones (UR-12-C)","how":"Cystinuria is the inherited stone disease, and its solubility rules are the same ones that govern other stones."}],"terms":[{"term":"Primary cilium","def":"A single nonmotile projection from the apical surface of a tubular cell that houses signaling proteins, including the polycystins and fibrocystin."},{"term":"Ciliopathy","def":"A disease caused by defective structure or signaling of the primary cilium, such as polycystic kidney disease."},{"term":"Two-hit hypothesis","def":"The model in which both alleles of a tumor suppressor gene must be inactivated for a lesion to form; in hereditary syndromes the first hit is inherited."},{"term":"Loss of heterozygosity","def":"Loss of the remaining normal allele in a cell that carries one mutant allele, often by deletion of a chromosome segment."},{"term":"Haploinsufficiency","def":"A state in which one functional copy of a gene is not enough for normal function."},{"term":"Dominant negative mutation","def":"A mutation whose abnormal product interferes with the protein made from the normal allele."},{"term":"Genomic imprinting","def":"Parent-of-origin gene expression set by DNA methylation in the germline, so only the maternal or only the paternal allele is active."},{"term":"Uniparental disomy","def":"Inheritance of both copies of a chromosome or chromosome segment from one parent."},{"term":"Hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF)","def":"A transcription factor whose alpha subunit is destroyed in normal oxygen and that activates angiogenic and metabolic genes in hypoxia."},{"term":"Hamartoma","def":"A benign, disorganized overgrowth of tissue types normally found at that site."},{"term":"Lysosomal storage disease","def":"A disorder in which deficiency of a lysosomal enzyme lets its undegraded substrate accumulate inside cells."},{"term":"Lyonization","def":"Random inactivation of one X chromosome in each female cell early in development, which makes heterozygous females mosaics."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-09-01-C01","obj":"UR-9.1","title":"Polycystic kidney disease: polycystins, fibrocystin, and pedigrees","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Polycystic kidney disease (PKD) replaces renal parenchyma with fluid-filled cysts derived from tubular epithelium. Autosomal dominant PKD (ADPKD), about 1 in 400 to 1,000 births, is the most common inherited kidney disease and presents in adults. Autosomal recessive PKD (ARPKD), about 1 in 20,000 births, presents in the fetus or infant. Both are ciliopathies: the defective proteins work at the primary cilium that each tubular cell extends into the lumen."},{"type":"p","text":"Polycystin-1, encoded by PKD1 on chromosome 16 (about 85% of families), is a large receptor-like membrane protein. Polycystin-2, encoded by PKD2 on chromosome 4 (about 15%), is a Ca2+-permeable cation channel. Together they are thought to sense a ciliary signal such as luminal flow and admit Ca2+. When the complex fails, intracellular Ca2+ falls and cAMP rises. In cyst epithelium cAMP drives proliferation through the B-Raf and ERK pathway and opens CFTR Cl- channels, so the cells secrete Cl- and water into the lumen; vasopressin acting on V2 receptors is a major source of this cAMP."},{"type":"flow","title":"ADPKD pathogenesis","steps":["Germline PKD1 or PKD2 mutation; somatic hit in one tubular cell","Polycystin complex fails at the primary cilium","Intracellular Ca2+ falls; cAMP rises","Proliferation and CFTR-driven Cl- and fluid secretion","Cyst detaches from its nephron and enlarges for decades","Compression of nephrons and vessels: renin release, nephron loss","Hypertension and progressive kidney failure"],"caption":"Every cell carries one mutant allele, but a cyst forms only where a second somatic event drops polycystin function below a threshold."},{"type":"p","text":"Although every cell carries the mutation, cysts arise from only a small fraction of nephrons, because each cyst requires a second, somatic hit to the normal allele. Compression of neighboring tissue causes regional ischemia and renin release, so hypertension often precedes a fall in GFR. A concentrating defect appears early, and flank pain, hematuria from cyst rupture, cyst infection, and stones are common. PKD1 disease is more severe, reaching kidney failure about two decades earlier than PKD2 disease. Polycystins also act in biliary epithelium, vascular smooth muscle, and connective tissue, which explains liver cysts (the most common extrarenal finding), intracranial berry aneurysms with subarachnoid hemorrhage, mitral valve prolapse, and colonic diverticula."},{"type":"p","text":"ARPKD requires mutation of both alleles of PKHD1 on chromosome 6, which encodes fibrocystin, a ciliary protein of collecting duct and bile duct epithelium. Dilated collecting ducts form elongated cysts that run radially toward a smooth capsule. Poor fetal urine output causes oligohydramnios and the Potter sequence (pulmonary hypoplasia, flattened facies, limb deformities), and malformation of the embryonic ductal plate causes congenital hepatic fibrosis with later portal hypertension."},{"type":"table","title":"ADPKD and ARPKD compared","cols":["Feature","ADPKD","ARPKD"],"rows":[["Gene, chromosome, protein","PKD1, 16, polycystin-1; PKD2, 4, polycystin-2","PKHD1, 6, fibrocystin"],["Usual onset","Adulthood (30s to 50s)","Fetus, newborn, or infancy"],["Kidneys","Bilaterally enlarged, cysts from all segments, bumpy surface","Bilaterally enlarged, radial collecting duct cysts, smooth surface"],["Liver","Hepatic cysts","Congenital hepatic fibrosis, portal hypertension"],["Other","Berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse, diverticula","Oligohydramnios, Potter sequence"],["Pedigree","Vertical: every generation, both sexes, male-to-male transmission, 50% risk per child","Horizontal: unaffected carrier parents, 25% risk per child, consanguinity raises risk"]],"caption":"About 10% of ADPKD patients have no affected parent because of a new mutation or a mildly affected parent."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: risk in a PKD family","text":"A healthy woman's brother had ARPKD. Her parents must both be carriers. She is unaffected, so she is one of three equally likely genotypes (two carrier, one noncarrier): her carrier risk is 2/3, not 1/2. Her unrelated husband has the population carrier risk, about 1 in 70. Their child's risk is 2/3 x 1/70 x 1/4, about 1 in 420. Now suppose instead that the husband's mother has ADPKD and he has not been imaged. His risk of carrying the mutation is 1/2, and an affected parent passes it to half of his children, so each child's risk is 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/4."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-09-01-C02","obj":"UR-9.2","title":"Type IV collagen disease: Alport syndrome and thin basement membrane disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Type IV collagen forms the sheet-like network of basement membranes. Each molecule is a triple helix of three alpha chains joined head to head through C-terminal noncollagenous (NC1) domains. The embryonic glomerular basement membrane (GBM) is built from alpha-1, alpha-1, alpha-2 molecules; as glomeruli mature, podocytes switch to an alpha-3, alpha-4, alpha-5 network that is more cross-linked and more resistant to proteases. The same alpha-3, 4, 5 network occurs in the cochlea, the lens capsule, and the retina. COL4A3 and COL4A4 lie together on chromosome 2, and COL4A5 lies on the X chromosome."},{"type":"p","text":"The three chains must assemble together, so a defect in any one prevents the whole alpha-3, 4, 5 network from forming. The GBM keeps the embryonic network, which is adequate at first but degrades under years of filtration pressure. It becomes irregularly thinned and thickened, and on electron microscopy the lamina densa splits into layers with a basket-weave pattern. Red cells leak through the breaks, so persistent microscopic hematuria, with episodes of gross hematuria, is the earliest sign, followed by proteinuria, glomerulosclerosis, and progressive loss of GFR. Defective basement membranes in the ear and eye cause sensorineural hearing loss, starting at high frequencies in late childhood, and anterior lenticonus (a conical bulge of the thin lens capsule) with dot-and-fleck retinopathy."},{"type":"p","text":"About 80% of families are X-linked (COL4A5), about 15% autosomal recessive (both alleles of COL4A3 or COL4A4), and a few autosomal dominant. Hemizygous males with X-linked disease have no normal alpha-5 chain and usually reach kidney failure as young adults. Heterozygous females are mosaics because of random X inactivation: GBM segments made by podocytes using the normal X are intact, so most have hematuria and only a minority progress. A normal transplanted kidney presents alpha-3, 4, 5 chains the recipient has never made, so a few recipients make anti-GBM alloantibodies, mainly against alpha-5 in X-linked disease and alpha-3 in recessive disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Thin basement membrane disease (TBMD) results from a heterozygous COL4A3 or COL4A4 mutation. The normal allele supplies enough chain to build a complete but uniformly thin GBM (about 150 to 250 nm, against about 300 to 400 nm in adults) without splitting. It follows an autosomal dominant pattern and causes persistent isolated microscopic hematuria with normal kidney function and no ear or eye disease, although a minority of carriers later develop proteinuria and declining GFR."},{"type":"table","title":"Alport syndrome and thin basement membrane disease","cols":["Feature","Alport syndrome","Thin basement membrane disease"],"rows":[["Genes","COL4A5 (X); both alleles of COL4A3 or COL4A4","One allele of COL4A3 or COL4A4"],["GBM on electron microscopy","Split, laminated basket weave; thin and thick areas","Uniformly thin, not split"],["Urine","Hematuria, then proteinuria","Isolated microscopic hematuria"],["Kidney course","Progressive to kidney failure, earliest in males","Usually normal function"],["Ear and eye","Sensorineural hearing loss, anterior lenticonus","Absent"]],"caption":"Hematuria alone does not separate the two; progression, splitting of the GBM, and extrarenal findings do."},{"type":"table","title":"Reading an X-linked recessive pedigree","cols":["Affected parent","Sons","Daughters"],"rows":[["Hemizygous father","None affected (they receive his Y)","All heterozygous"],["Heterozygous mother","Each has a 50% risk of being affected","Each has a 50% risk of being heterozygous"]],"caption":"No male-to-male transmission; affected males are linked through heterozygous females. The same rules apply to Fabry disease (C07)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with X-linked Alport syndrome and an unaffected woman have children. What is the risk for each son and each daughter?","explain":"Sons receive his Y chromosome and are unaffected. Every daughter receives his mutant X and is heterozygous; most will have hematuria, and some will progress, depending on X inactivation in their podocytes.","check":"The X-linked pedigree table and the paragraph on heterozygous females."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-09-01-C03","obj":"UR-9.3","title":"VHL loss, HIF, and clear cell renal carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF) has an oxygen-regulated alpha subunit and a constant beta subunit. In normal oxygen, prolyl hydroxylases use O2, Fe2+, and alpha-ketoglutarate to hydroxylate prolines on HIF-alpha. The VHL protein (pVHL), the substrate-recognition part of an E3 ubiquitin ligase, binds hydroxylated HIF-alpha and polyubiquitinates it, and the proteasome destroys it within minutes. In hypoxia hydroxylation stops, HIF-alpha pairs with HIF-beta, and the dimer activates VEGF, PDGF-beta, TGF-alpha, erythropoietin (EPO), the glucose transporter GLUT1, and glycolytic enzymes."},{"type":"flow","title":"From VHL loss to a vascular clear cell tumor","steps":["First hit: 3p deletion or VHL mutation","Second hit removes the remaining VHL allele","Hydroxylated HIF-alpha is no longer ubiquitinated","HIF accumulates as if the cell were hypoxic","VEGF and PDGF build dense, fragile vessels","TGF-alpha drives growth; EPO may raise hematocrit","Glycogen and lipid storage give clear cytoplasm"],"caption":"The tumor cell senses normal oxygen as hypoxia; every feature of the tumor follows from constant HIF activity."},{"type":"p","text":"VHL, on chromosome 3p25, is a tumor suppressor: one functional copy is enough, so a tumor requires loss of both copies (the Knudson two-hit model). Without pVHL the cell behaves as though permanently hypoxic. VEGF and PDGF build a dense network of thin-walled vessels, so clear cell carcinoma is highly vascular, bleeds (hematuria and a hemorrhagic yellow cut surface), and tends to grow into the renal vein. TGF-alpha stimulates proliferation through the EGF receptor, tumor EPO can cause paraneoplastic polycythemia, and the shift toward glycolysis and lipid storage contributes to cytoplasm full of glycogen and lipid that dissolve during processing. The tumor arises from proximal tubule epithelium and accounts for about 70 to 80% of renal cell carcinomas."},{"type":"p","text":"In sporadic clear cell carcinoma both hits are somatic and must occur in the same cell, usually deletion of one 3p copy plus mutation or promoter hypermethylation of the other, so the typical patient is an older adult (about 60s) with a single unilateral tumor; smoking, obesity, and hypertension raise the risk. In von Hippel-Lindau syndrome (autosomal dominant, about 1 in 36,000) the first hit is germline. Every cell already lacks one copy, and one somatic event anywhere produces a tumor, so renal cysts and carcinomas are multiple, bilateral, and appear decades earlier. The extrarenal tumors form the same way in other tissues; hemangioblastomas, like clear cell carcinoma, are richly vascular and can secrete EPO."},{"type":"table","title":"Sporadic clear cell carcinoma and von Hippel-Lindau syndrome","cols":["Feature","Sporadic","VHL syndrome"],"rows":[["First hit","Somatic","Germline (inherited or new)"],["Second hit","Somatic, same cell","Somatic, any cell"],["Age","About 60s","Often before 40"],["Kidney","One unilateral tumor","Multiple bilateral cysts and carcinomas"],["Other lesions","None","Hemangioblastomas of cerebellum, spinal cord, retina; pheochromocytoma; pancreatic cysts and neuroendocrine tumors; endolymphatic sac tumors"]],"caption":"The gene and pathway are identical; only the timing of the first hit differs."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A biopsy from a normal kidney region of a patient with a germline VHL mutation is examined. Would its tubular cells overexpress VEGF?","explain":"No. Each cell still has one normal VHL allele, which makes enough pVHL to destroy HIF-alpha. VEGF rises only in cells that lose the second allele, which is why tumors are discrete even though every cell carries the mutation.","check":"The two-hit paragraph and the first steps of the flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-09-01-C04","obj":"UR-9.4","title":"Wilms tumor genetics: WT1 at 11p13 and imprinting at 11p15","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma) is the most common primary renal malignancy of children, usually presenting at 2 to 5 years as a large unilateral flank mass. It arises from persistent embryonic metanephric blastema (nephrogenic rests) and recapitulates nephrogenesis: the classic triphasic histology is blastema, stroma, and epithelium forming abortive tubules and glomeruli. Most cases are sporadic; about 10% occur in a syndrome, and the syndromes identify the genes."},{"type":"p","text":"WT1, on 11p13, encodes a zinc-finger transcription factor of the intermediate mesoderm. It is required for metanephric mesenchyme to convert into nephron epithelium in response to signals from the ureteric bud, for podocyte differentiation and maintenance, and for early gonadal development, including regulation of SRY. Without WT1, blastema fails to differentiate and keeps proliferating, and podocytes and gonads are also affected. WT1 acts as a tumor suppressor but is mutated in only a minority (about 10 to 20%) of sporadic Wilms tumors."},{"type":"p","text":"WAGR syndrome is a germline deletion of 11p13 that removes one copy of WT1 and the neighboring PAX6. PAX6 haploinsufficiency causes aniridia, WT1 loss causes genitourinary anomalies (cryptorchidism, hypospadias) and a Wilms tumor risk of about 50%, and loss of other genes in the deletion contributes to intellectual disability. The tumor itself needs a second, somatic hit in the remaining WT1 allele, a two-hit pattern like VHL. Denys-Drash syndrome is caused instead by a heterozygous missense mutation in a WT1 zinc finger whose product interferes with the normal protein (a dominant negative effect). Because function falls further than with simple loss of one copy, podocytes fail early (diffuse mesangial sclerosis with nephrotic syndrome in infancy and progression to kidney failure), gonadal development fails (46,XY disorder of sex development), and Wilms tumor risk is about 90%."},{"type":"p","text":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (BWS) involves a different locus, the imprinted cluster at 11p15.5. IGF2, a fetal growth factor, is expressed only from the paternal allele; H19 (a noncoding RNA) and CDKN1C (the cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor p57) are expressed only from the maternal allele. Defects that raise IGF2 or lower CDKN1C cause overgrowth: macrosomia, macroglossia, omphalocele, hemihyperplasia, visceromegaly including large kidneys, and neonatal hypoglycemia from islet hyperplasia and hyperinsulinism. Embryonal tumor risk (Wilms tumor, hepatoblastoma) is about 5 to 10% overall, highest with paternal uniparental disomy or gain of methylation at the H19 and IGF2 imprinting center."},{"type":"table","title":"Syndromes that predispose to Wilms tumor","cols":["Syndrome","Lesion","Mechanism","Key findings","Wilms risk"],"rows":[["WAGR","11p13 deletion (WT1 and PAX6)","Haploinsufficiency; second WT1 hit in tumor","Aniridia, genitourinary anomalies, intellectual disability","About 50%"],["Denys-Drash","WT1 missense mutation","Dominant negative WT1","Diffuse mesangial sclerosis, early nephrotic syndrome, 46,XY disorder of sex development","About 90%"],["Beckwith-Wiedemann","11p15.5 imprinting defect","Excess IGF2, reduced CDKN1C","Macroglossia, omphalocele, hemihyperplasia, organomegaly, neonatal hypoglycemia","About 5 to 10% (embryonal tumors overall)"]],"caption":"11p13 lesions damage a differentiation gene; 11p15 lesions change the dose of growth genes."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: imprinting dosage in Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","text":"Normally a cell expresses one dose of IGF2 (from the paternal 11p15) and one dose of CDKN1C (from the maternal 11p15). Suppose a postzygotic error leaves some cells with two paternal copies of 11p15 and no maternal copy (paternal uniparental disomy). Those cells express two doses of IGF2 and no CDKN1C: growth signaling doubles and a brake on the cell cycle is lost. Because the error happens after fertilization, only some cells carry it, so overgrowth is patchy, which explains hemihyperplasia. No DNA sequence is mutated, yet expression is abnormal, which is why imprinting disorders need methylation testing rather than sequencing alone."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-09-01-C05","obj":"UR-9.5","title":"Tuberous sclerosis: hamartin, tuberin, mTOR, and angiomyolipoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"mTOR complex 1 (mTORC1) is a serine/threonine kinase complex that couples growth factor and nutrient availability to cell growth. Active mTORC1 phosphorylates S6 kinase and 4E-BP1 to increase protein synthesis, promotes lipid synthesis, and suppresses autophagy. Its direct activator is Rheb, a small GTPase that stimulates mTORC1 only when bound to GTP."},{"type":"p","text":"Hamartin (TSC1, chromosome 9q34) and tuberin (TSC2, chromosome 16p13.3) form a complex in which hamartin stabilizes tuberin and tuberin acts as a GTPase-activating protein (GAP) for Rheb, converting active Rheb-GTP to inactive Rheb-GDP. The complex integrates signals: growth factors acting through PI3K and Akt phosphorylate and inhibit tuberin, releasing mTORC1, whereas low cellular energy activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK), which activates tuberin and slows growth. Loss of either protein leaves Rheb in the GTP state and mTORC1 constitutively active, so cells grow and proliferate without the normal signals."},{"type":"flow","title":"Loss of the TSC brake on mTOR","steps":["Germline TSC1 or TSC2 mutation; somatic second hit","Hamartin-tuberin complex lost","No GAP activity: Rheb stays bound to GTP","mTORC1 constitutively active","Protein and lipid synthesis rise; autophagy falls","Hamartomas: angiomyolipomas, tubers, angiofibromas"],"caption":"Tuberin normally switches Rheb off; without it, mTORC1 runs regardless of growth factor or energy status."},{"type":"p","text":"Tuberous sclerosis complex (TSC) is autosomal dominant with high penetrance but very variable expression, and about two-thirds of cases are new mutations; TSC2 mutations generally cause more severe disease. Lesions follow the two-hit pattern, forming where a somatic event removes the second allele. Hamartomas appear in the brain (cortical tubers, subependymal nodules and giant cell astrocytomas, with seizures and intellectual disability), skin (hypomelanotic ash-leaf macules, facial angiofibromas, shagreen patches, ungual fibromas), heart (rhabdomyomas that often regress after birth), and lung (lymphangioleiomyomatosis in women)."},{"type":"p","text":"Renal angiomyolipomas develop in most adults with TSC (about 70 to 80%) and are usually multiple and bilateral. They belong to the perivascular epithelioid cell tumor family and contain thick-walled dysplastic blood vessels with poor elastic tissue, smooth muscle, and mature fat. The abnormal vessel walls form aneurysms that can rupture into the retroperitoneum, and enlarging tumors replace parenchyma, so renal disease is a leading cause of death in adults with TSC. Fat is a diagnostic clue because it is rare in renal cell carcinoma. Renal cysts are also common, and because TSC2 lies directly beside PKD1 on 16p13.3, a contiguous deletion of both genes causes severe polycystic disease in infancy. Sporadic angiomyolipomas are usually single and occur most often in middle-aged women."},{"type":"table","title":"Angiomyolipoma and clear cell carcinoma","cols":["Feature","Angiomyolipoma","Clear cell carcinoma"],"rows":[["Nature","Benign mesenchymal tumor (perivascular epithelioid cell)","Malignant epithelial tumor of proximal tubule"],["Pathway","mTORC1 overactivity (TSC1 or TSC2)","HIF stabilization (VHL)"],["Tissue","Abnormal vessels, smooth muscle, mature fat","Clear cells rich in glycogen and lipid, delicate vessels"],["Fat on imaging","Characteristic","Rare"],["Main danger","Hemorrhage from aneurysmal vessels","Invasion and metastasis"]],"caption":"Both are vascular and both may be bilateral in a syndrome, but their pathways and composition differ."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-09-01-C06","obj":"UR-9.6","title":"Cystinuria: a dibasic amino acid transport defect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The proximal tubule reabsorbs about 99% of filtered amino acids through apical transporters grouped by substrate class. Cystine, the disulfide-linked dimer of two cysteines, shares one transporter with the dibasic amino acids ornithine, lysine, and arginine. This transporter, system b0,+, is a heterodimer of a heavy subunit, rBAT (gene SLC3A1, chromosome 2), and a light subunit, b0,+AT (gene SLC7A9, chromosome 19). It takes up luminal cystine and dibasic amino acids in exchange for intracellular neutral amino acids, and the same transporter works in the small intestinal brush border."},{"type":"p","text":"Cystinuria is usually autosomal recessive: loss of both alleles of either gene abolishes the transporter, so all four amino acids appear in the urine in large amounts. Ornithine, lysine, and arginine are soluble and are lost harmlessly, and intestinal malabsorption causes no deficiency because these amino acids are still absorbed as di- and tripeptides by the separate peptide transporter PepT1. Cystine is the problem because it is the least soluble amino acid, about 250 mg/L at pH 7, lower in acidic urine, and rising steeply only above about pH 7.5. A homozygous patient excretes several hundred to more than 1,000 mg/day, compared with normal excretion under about 30 mg/day, which exceeds solubility in an ordinary urine volume. Some SLC7A9 heterozygotes excrete moderately increased cystine but rarely form stones."},{"type":"flow","title":"From transporter defect to cystine stones","steps":["Both alleles of SLC3A1 or SLC7A9 defective","Apical b0,+ transporter absent in proximal tubule","Cystine, ornithine, lysine, arginine stay in tubular fluid","Cystine exceeds its solubility, especially in acidic urine","Hexagonal crystals nucleate and aggregate","Recurrent radiopaque stones, sometimes staghorn"],"caption":"Four amino acids are lost, but only cystine precipitates."},{"type":"p","text":"Stones begin in childhood or young adulthood and recur. They are radiopaque because of their sulfur content, though less dense than calcium stones, and may fill the collecting system as staghorn calculi. Urine microscopy shows flat hexagonal crystals, which are pathognomonic and are best seen in concentrated, acidic first-morning urine. The cyanide-nitroprusside screen detects excess cystine: cyanide reduces each cystine to two cysteines, and their free sulfhydryl groups react with nitroprusside to give a red-purple color. The screen is also positive in homocystinuria, whose urine contains the disulfide homocystine, so a positive result is confirmed by quantitative urine amino acid measurement."},{"type":"table","title":"Cystinuria and look-alike disorders","cols":["Disorder","Defect","Main consequence"],"rows":[["Cystinuria","Apical b0,+ transporter (cystine and dibasic amino acids), kidney and gut","Cystine stones; no Fanconi syndrome"],["Cystinosis","Lysosomal cystine efflux carrier (cystinosin, CTNS)","Cystine crystals inside cells; Fanconi syndrome, corneal crystals, kidney failure"],["Hartnup disease","Neutral amino acid transporter (B0AT1), kidney and gut","Tryptophan loss; pellagra-like rash and ataxia"],["Homocystinuria","Usually cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency","Positive nitroprusside test without a transport defect; thrombosis, lens dislocation"]],"caption":"Cystinuria is a membrane transport defect; cystinosis is a lysosomal storage defect."},{"type":"stop","predict":"One child has cystinuria and another has cystinosis. Which would you expect to have glycosuria, phosphaturia, and generalized aminoaciduria, and why?","explain":"The child with cystinosis. Cystine trapped in proximal tubule lysosomes injures the cells and impairs all their reabsorptive transporters (Fanconi syndrome). In cystinuria only one transporter is missing, so glucose, phosphate, and other amino acids are reabsorbed normally.","check":"The look-alike table and the description of system b0,+."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RENL-09-01-C07","obj":"UR-9.7","title":"Fabry disease: alpha-galactosidase A deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Glycosphingolipids are ceramide-based membrane lipids carrying sugar chains. Lysosomes degrade them by removing sugars one at a time from the outer end, each step catalyzed by a specific acid hydrolase. Alpha-galactosidase A, encoded by GLA on Xq22, removes the terminal alpha-linked galactose from globotriaosylceramide (Gb3, also called ceramide trihexoside), leaving lactosylceramide."},{"type":"p","text":"When the enzyme is deficient, Gb3 and its deacylated form, lyso-Gb3, accumulate in lysosomes of vascular endothelium and smooth muscle, podocytes and tubular cells, cardiomyocytes, dorsal root ganglion neurons and small nerve fibers, sweat glands, and corneal epithelium. On electron microscopy the stored lipid forms concentric lamellated inclusions (zebra bodies), and on light microscopy podocytes look foamy and vacuolated. Lyso-Gb3 also promotes endothelial dysfunction, smooth muscle proliferation, and fibrosis, so tissue injury extends beyond the space the lipid occupies."},{"type":"table","title":"Fabry manifestations explained by storage site","cols":["Storage site","Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Small sensory fibers, dorsal root ganglia","Burning pain in hands and feet (acroparesthesias) from childhood, crises with fever, heat, or exercise","Small-fiber neuropathy"],["Sweat glands, autonomic fibers","Hypohidrosis, heat and exercise intolerance","Gland and autonomic nerve storage"],["Dermal capillaries","Angiokeratomas, clustered between umbilicus and knees","Dilated, damaged superficial vessels"],["Cornea","Whorled corneal opacities (cornea verticillata)","Epithelial lipid deposits"],["Podocytes, tubules, glomerular vessels","Proteinuria, concentrating defect, progressive kidney failure","Podocyte injury, glomerulosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis"],["Heart and cerebral vessels","Left ventricular hypertrophy, arrhythmias, early stroke","Cardiomyocyte storage, vasculopathy"]],"caption":"One enzyme defect produces findings in skin, nerve, eye, kidney, heart, and brain because Gb3 accumulates wherever it is made."},{"type":"p","text":"Kidney disease follows a sequence. Podocyte storage causes proteinuria, often beginning in the 20s in males with classic disease; tubular storage impairs urine concentration; and progressive glomerulosclerosis and interstitial fibrosis lead to kidney failure, classically in the 30s to 50s. The urine sediment may contain lipid-laden cells and fat droplets that show a Maltese cross under polarized light."},{"type":"p","text":"Fabry disease is X-linked. Hemizygous males with classic mutations have little or no enzyme activity and the full picture from childhood. Heterozygous females are mosaics because of random X inactivation, and many develop symptoms, usually later and milder but sometimes severe when inactivation is skewed toward the mutant X. For this reason the inheritance is better described as X-linked than strictly recessive, and the pedigree rules match those in C02. Enzyme activity confirms the diagnosis in males but may be normal in heterozygous females, who need GLA testing. Later-onset variants with residual enzyme activity can present only with cardiac or renal disease in adulthood. Fabry disease and Hunter syndrome are the classic X-linked lysosomal storage diseases."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These disorders fall into four mechanistic groups. Structural protein defects (polycystins, fibrocystin, type IV collagen) damage the tubule or basement membrane and show the same lesion wherever the protein is used, which is why PKD brings liver cysts and aneurysms and Alport brings deafness and lens defects. Tumor suppressor syndromes (VHL, WT1, TSC) follow the two-hit model, so inherited forms give early, multiple, bilateral lesions and sporadic forms give single lesions later in life; the exceptions are dominant negative WT1 in Denys-Drash, where one mutant allele already impairs podocytes and gonads, and BWS, where gene dosage changes through imprinting without any sequence mutation. Transport defects (cystinuria) act through solubility, and lysosomal storage (Fabry) acts through progressive cell injury."},{"type":"p","text":"The kidney links each group to other Part I disciplines: cilia and cAMP signaling (cell biology), basement membrane collagen and anti-GBM immunity (biochemistry and immunology), HIF and EPO (physiology of oxygen sensing), mTOR and growth signaling (biochemistry), amino acid transport (renal physiology), and sphingolipid degradation (biochemistry). The limits are real: penetrance and expression vary, many sporadic tumors carry other mutations, and quoted percentages differ between sources, so pedigree risks assume complete penetrance unless stated otherwise."}],"summary":["ADPKD: PKD1 (chromosome 16, polycystin-1, about 85%, more severe) or PKD2 (chromosome 4, polycystin-2); ciliary Ca2+ falls, cAMP rises, cysts proliferate and secrete fluid; liver cysts, berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse.","ARPKD: PKHD1 (chromosome 6, fibrocystin); infant collecting duct cysts, oligohydramnios with Potter sequence, and congenital hepatic fibrosis.","Dominant pedigrees are vertical with male-to-male transmission and 50% risk per child; recessive pedigrees are horizontal with 25% risk, and an unaffected sibling of an affected child has a 2/3 carrier risk.","Alport syndrome: a defect in any alpha-3, 4, or 5 chain (usually X-linked COL4A5) prevents the mature GBM network, giving a split basket-weave GBM, hematuria to kidney failure, hearing loss, and anterior lenticonus.","Thin basement membrane disease: one mutant COL4A3 or COL4A4 allele gives a uniformly thin GBM and isolated hematuria with usually normal function.","VHL (3p25) targets hydroxylated HIF-alpha for destruction; loss of both copies stabilizes HIF and drives VEGF, PDGF, TGF-alpha, and EPO in clear cell carcinoma; a germline first hit gives early bilateral tumors, hemangioblastomas, and pheochromocytoma.","WT1 (11p13) loss causes Wilms tumor with genitourinary defects: WAGR by deletion with PAX6 (aniridia), Denys-Drash by dominant negative mutation (mesangial sclerosis, 46,XY disorder of sex development).","Beckwith-Wiedemann: 11p15.5 imprinting defects increase IGF2 and reduce CDKN1C, causing overgrowth and embryonal tumors including Wilms tumor.","TSC1 (hamartin) or TSC2 (tuberin) loss removes GAP control of Rheb, so mTORC1 stays active; angiomyolipomas (vessels, smooth muscle, fat) and renal cysts follow.","Cystinuria: autosomal recessive loss of the b0,+ transporter for cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine gives hexagonal cystine stones and a positive cyanide-nitroprusside test; Fabry: X-linked alpha-galactosidase A deficiency stores Gb3, causing angiokeratomas, painful neuropathy, hypohidrosis, and progressive kidney failure."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, sections on cystic diseases, hereditary nephritis, and tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic disorders: lysosomal storage diseases and genomic imprinting"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, section on tumor suppressor genes and the two-hit hypothesis"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Glycosphingolipid degradation and the sphingolipidoses"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Proximal tubule transport of organic solutes, including amino acids"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System"}]}});
